window.NPLEX_NOTES=window.NPLEX_NOTES||{};Object.assign(window.NPLEX_NOTES,{"PU-1":{"unit":"PU-1","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-01","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-01-01","title":"Respiratory Embryology","subject":"Respiratory System (RESP)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows the lower respiratory tract from a small outgrowth of the foregut in week 4 to the alveolar lung of early childhood. It explains which germ layer builds each tissue, how the trachea separates from the esophagus, how the airway tree and its capillaries mature in stages, and when type II pneumocytes make enough surfactant for air breathing. Board questions usually give a newborn in respiratory distress and ask for the developmental step that failed: septation in tracheoesophageal fistula, surfactant production in neonatal respiratory distress syndrome, or lung growth in pulmonary hypoplasia. Knowing the timeline and the mechanism behind each stage lets you predict the finding at birth.","prerequisites":["Trilaminar disc and germ layers: endoderm, splanchnic and somatic mesoderm","Folding of the embryo and formation of the primitive gut tube","Pharyngeal arches and their cranial nerves","Alveolar structure: type I and type II pneumocytes, blood-air barrier","Surface tension and the law of Laplace"],"connections":[{"to":"Gastrointestinal embryology (GI chapters)","how":"The lung bud and esophagus come from the same foregut tube, so septation defects join the airway to the gut."},{"to":"Diaphragm and body cavity development","how":"Failure of a pleuroperitoneal membrane to close lets bowel enter the chest and compress the growing lung."},{"to":"Renal embryology (UR chapters)","how":"Fetal urine supplies most amniotic fluid in later pregnancy, so absent or obstructed kidneys starve the lungs of the fluid they need to grow."},{"to":"Respiratory mechanics and gas exchange (PU chapters)","how":"Surfactant, compliance, and shunt explain why an immature lung fails once the infant must breathe air."},{"to":"Fetal circulation (CV chapters)","how":"A small or hypoxic pulmonary vascular bed keeps pulmonary resistance high after birth and drives right-to-left shunting through fetal channels."}],"terms":[{"term":"Respiratory diverticulum","def":"The lung bud, a ventral outgrowth of foregut endoderm in week 4 that forms the larynx lining, trachea, bronchi, and lungs."},{"term":"Tracheoesophageal septum","def":"The partition formed by fusion of the tracheoesophageal ridges that separates the ventral trachea from the dorsal esophagus."},{"term":"Tracheoesophageal fistula","def":"An abnormal channel between the trachea and esophagus, usually combined with esophageal atresia."},{"term":"Branching morphogenesis","def":"Repeated division of the endodermal airway tubes, directed by signals from the surrounding mesenchyme."},{"term":"Respiratory bronchiole","def":"The first airway generation whose wall contains alveoli, marking the start of the gas exchange zone."},{"term":"Type II pneumocyte","def":"A cuboidal alveolar epithelial cell that synthesizes, stores, and secretes surfactant and can replace type I cells."},{"term":"Surfactant","def":"A lipoprotein mixture, mostly dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine, that lowers surface tension at the alveolar air-liquid interface."},{"term":"Lecithin to sphingomyelin (L/S) ratio","def":"The ratio of these two phospholipids in amniotic fluid, used as an index of fetal lung maturity."},{"term":"Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome","def":"Respiratory failure of the newborn caused by surfactant deficiency, with diffuse alveolar collapse and hyaline membranes."},{"term":"Oligohydramnios","def":"An abnormally small volume of amniotic fluid for gestational age."},{"term":"Pulmonary hypoplasia","def":"Incomplete lung growth with too few airway branches, alveoli, and pulmonary vessels for body size."},{"term":"Congenital diaphragmatic hernia","def":"Herniation of abdominal organs into the thorax through a defect in the diaphragm, usually posterolateral and left-sided."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-01-01-C01","obj":"PU-1.1","title":"The lung bud and the germ layer origins of the respiratory tract","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In week 4 the respiratory diverticulum, or lung bud, grows out of the ventral wall of the foregut just caudal to the pharynx. Its position is set by signals from the surrounding mesoderm: retinoic acid from that mesoderm induces the transcription factor TBX4 in the overlying endoderm, which marks the site where the bud forms. At first the bud opens freely into the foregut; it is separated from the esophagus by the septation described in the next section and keeps one permanent connection with the pharynx, the laryngeal inlet."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the bud is an outpouching of endoderm, every surface that air touches, from the larynx to the alveolus, is endodermal. Endoderm forms the airway lining epithelium, the submucosal glands, and both type I and type II pneumocytes. The bud grows into the splanchnic mesoderm that surrounds the foregut, and that mesoderm forms everything in the wall: cartilage, smooth muscle, connective tissue, blood vessels, and the visceral pleura covering the lung. The parietal pleura comes from somatic mesoderm lining the body wall. The larynx is the exception in the wall: its cartilages and muscles come from the mesenchyme of pharyngeal arches 4 and 6, so they are supplied by the nerves of those arches, the superior laryngeal and recurrent laryngeal branches of the vagus nerve."},{"type":"table","title":"Germ layer origins of the respiratory tract","cols":["Source","Structures formed"],"rows":[["Foregut endoderm","Epithelium of larynx, trachea, bronchi, bronchioles, and alveoli (type I and II pneumocytes); airway glands"],["Splanchnic mesoderm","Cartilage, smooth muscle, connective tissue, and vessels of the trachea and lungs; visceral pleura"],["Somatic mesoderm","Parietal pleura"],["Pharyngeal arch 4 mesenchyme","Thyroid cartilage and cricothyroid muscle; superior laryngeal nerve"],["Pharyngeal arch 6 mesenchyme","Cricoid and arytenoid cartilages and the other intrinsic laryngeal muscles; recurrent laryngeal nerve"]],"caption":"Lining and glands are endodermal; the supporting wall is mesodermal. Arch assignments of the smaller laryngeal cartilages differ slightly between texts."},{"type":"p","text":"Branching is a conversation between the two layers. The mesenchyme secretes signals such as fibroblast growth factor 10 (FGF10) that tell the endodermal tip where to grow and divide, and endoderm grafted next to tracheal mesenchyme fails to branch. Early in week 5 the bud splits into right and left bronchial buds. The right forms three secondary (lobar) bronchi and the left forms two, which fixes three lobes on the right and two on the left. Each lobar bronchus then gives tertiary (segmental) bronchi, about ten on the right and eight to ten on the left, which define the bronchopulmonary segments. As the lungs enlarge they grow laterally into the pericardioperitoneal canals, which become the pleural cavities."},{"type":"p","text":"Errors in budding explain several anomalies. An abnormal extra bud from the foregut or airway can pinch off as a bronchogenic cyst, usually in the mediastinum near the carina, lined by respiratory epithelium and containing cartilage and smooth muscle because both layers took part. Abnormal division of the bronchial tree produces accessory lobes or atypical segments, which can mimic disease on imaging."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-01-01-C02","obj":"PU-1.2","title":"Tracheoesophageal septation and esophageal atresia with fistula","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"When the lung bud first appears, the future trachea and esophagus share one lumen. Longitudinal tracheoesophageal ridges grow in from each side of the foregut and fuse in the midline, forming the tracheoesophageal septum. The septum divides the foregut into a ventral laryngotracheal tube and a dorsal esophagus, which is why the trachea lies anterior to the esophagus throughout the neck and upper thorax. Septation stops at the larynx, where the airway keeps its opening into the pharynx."},{"type":"p","text":"If the septum deviates posteriorly or the ridges fail to fuse completely, a channel between the airway and the gut persists, and the esophagus often fails to remain continuous. Fistula and atresia therefore usually occur together. Esophageal atresia with tracheoesophageal fistula occurs in about 1 in 3,000 to 4,500 births and is part of the VACTERL association (vertebral, anal, cardiac, tracheoesophageal, renal, and limb anomalies) in a substantial minority of cases, so finding one anomaly should prompt a search for the others."},{"type":"table","title":"Types of esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula","cols":["Anatomy","Approximate frequency","Key findings explained by the anatomy"],"rows":[["Blind upper esophageal pouch; lower esophagus joins trachea (distal fistula)","About 85 to 90%","Polyhydramnios, drooling, choking and cyanosis with first feed, tube coils in pouch, gas-filled stomach and bowel"],["Esophageal atresia without fistula","About 5 to 8%","Marked polyhydramnios, drooling, gasless abdomen because no air can reach the stomach"],["H-type fistula without atresia","About 4%","Feeds pass normally, so diagnosis is delayed; recurrent aspiration pneumonia and coughing with feeds"],["Proximal fistula, or fistulas to both segments","Rare","Immediate aspiration of feeds from the upper pouch into the airway"]],"caption":"Frequencies are approximate and vary between series; the distal fistula type is consistently the most common."},{"type":"flow","title":"How the common type produces its findings","steps":["Tracheoesophageal septum forms abnormally","Upper esophagus ends blindly; lower esophagus opens into trachea","Fetus cannot swallow amniotic fluid: polyhydramnios","Newborn cannot clear saliva: drooling, choking","Feeds overflow the pouch into the larynx: cough, cyanosis","Air enters stomach through the fistula: gastric distension","Gastric acid refluxes up the fistula: chemical pneumonitis"],"caption":"Each sign follows from the blind upper pouch or from the open path between trachea and lower esophagus."},{"type":"p","text":"Polyhydramnios is the prenatal clue because the fetus normally swallows large volumes of amniotic fluid and absorbs it in the intestine; a blind pouch blocks that route while urine output continues. The excess is greatest without a distal fistula, since a fistula lets some fluid reach the gut through the trachea. After birth, a feeding tube that will not pass into the stomach and instead coils in the upper pouch on a radiograph confirms the atresia, and gas below the diaphragm shows that a distal fistula exists. The distal fistula also harms the lungs from below: a crying infant pumps air into the stomach, distension splints the diaphragm, and acid gastric content can reflux into the trachea."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two newborns have esophageal atresia. One has a distended, gas-filled abdomen on radiograph; the other has no gas below the diaphragm. Which anatomy does each have?","explain":"Air reaches the stomach only through a fistula from the trachea to the lower esophagus, so a gas-filled abdomen means a distal fistula (the common type). A gasless abdomen means atresia without a distal fistula.","check":"The table of fistula types and the flow figure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-01-01-C03","obj":"PU-1.3","title":"Stages of lung development and the limit of viability","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"After the embryonic period lays down the lung bud and the lobar and segmental bronchi, the lung matures through four overlapping stages. Each stage adds a structure that the next one needs. The key to timing questions is that gas exchange requires two things side by side: an airspace with thin epithelium and a capillary pressed against it."},{"type":"table","title":"Stages of lung development","cols":["Stage","Approximate timing","What forms","Gas exchange possible"],"rows":[["Embryonic","Weeks 4 to 7","Lung bud, main, lobar, and segmental bronchi","No"],["Pseudoglandular","Weeks 5 to 16","Branching down to terminal bronchioles; cartilage, glands, and smooth muscle appear","No"],["Canalicular","Weeks 16 to 26","Respiratory bronchioles and alveolar ducts; capillaries invade and approach thinning epithelium; first type I and II cells","Becomes possible only near the end, about weeks 24 to 26"],["Saccular (terminal sac)","Week 26 to birth","Thin-walled terminal sacs (primitive alveoli) lined by type I and II cells; capillaries bulge into them","Yes, limited by surfactant"],["Alveolar","About week 36 to about age 8","Mature alveoli with thin septa; alveolar number rises steeply after birth","Yes"]],"caption":"Stages overlap because branching proceeds at different rates in different regions; boundaries are approximate."},{"type":"p","text":"In the pseudoglandular stage the lung looks like a gland: blind epithelial tubes in thick mesenchyme. By about week 16 all conducting airways down to the terminal bronchioles exist, so the airway branching pattern is set before mid-gestation. No breathing is possible at this stage regardless of support, because there are no respiratory bronchioles and the capillaries lie far from the epithelium."},{"type":"p","text":"The canalicular stage is named for the vascular canals that grow into the mesenchyme. Terminal bronchioles divide into respiratory bronchioles, which give alveolar ducts, the lumens widen, the epithelium flattens, and capillaries move up against it. Near the end of this stage, at about weeks 24 to 26, enough thin interface exists for an infant to exchange gas with intensive support. That is why the limit of viability falls here: before this point the problem is structural and cannot be overcome, whereas after it the main limits are surfactant and barrier thickness, which improve week by week."},{"type":"p","text":"In the saccular stage the airspaces become terminal sacs, type I cells thin to form a barrier only a fraction of a micrometer thick, and type II cells increase. The alveolar stage adds true alveoli by secondary septation, in which new walls grow out from the sac walls and subdivide them. Most adult alveoli form after birth, mainly in the first two to three years, which gives an injured or premature lung some capacity to catch up. Before birth the airspaces are filled mainly with fluid secreted by the airway epithelium rather than with amniotic fluid, and fetal breathing movements move this fluid and stretch the lung."},{"type":"example","title":"Predicting outcome from gestational age","text":"Infant A is born at 22 weeks; infant B at 27 weeks. At 22 weeks the lung is early in the canalicular stage: respiratory bronchioles are only beginning to form and most capillaries are still separated from the airspaces by thick mesenchyme, so even full support delivers little oxygen to blood. At 27 weeks the lung is entering the saccular stage: terminal sacs and close capillaries exist, so gas exchange is structurally possible, but type II cells are few and surfactant is far below the level reached near week 35. Infant B can survive but will very likely develop neonatal respiratory distress syndrome, while infant A faces a structural barrier rather than a surfactant problem."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-01-01-C04","obj":"PU-1.4","title":"Surfactant production and fetal lung maturity","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Surfactant is about 90% lipid and 10% protein. Its main lipid is dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine (DPPC), a lecithin; phosphatidylglycerol and surfactant proteins A, B, C, and D complete the mixture. Type II pneumocytes synthesize it, store it in lamellar bodies, and secrete it by exocytosis onto the alveolar lining fluid. SP-B and SP-C help spread the lipid into a film; SP-A and SP-D are collectins that bind microbes and aid innate defense."},{"type":"p","text":"By the law of Laplace (P = 2T/r), the pressure that tends to collapse an alveolus rises as its radius falls. Surfactant lowers surface tension, and it lowers it most in small alveoli, where the film is compressed and more concentrated. This keeps small alveoli from emptying into large ones, raises compliance, and reduces the force that would otherwise draw fluid from capillaries into the airspaces."},{"type":"p","text":"Type II cells appear in the canalicular stage and begin secreting surfactant in the second trimester, but output rises steeply only late in gestation and becomes adequate at about week 35. Fetal cortisol, which surges near term, induces the enzymes of phospholipid synthesis and the surfactant proteins; thyroid hormone also accelerates maturation. Insulin opposes the glucocorticoid effect, so in maternal diabetes fetal hyperglycemia leads to fetal hyperinsulinemia and delayed maturation. Male sex and cesarean delivery without labor (less stress cortisol and catecholamine) also raise risk."},{"type":"p","text":"Fetal lung fluid flows up the trachea into the amniotic fluid, carrying surfactant with it, so amniotic fluid samples the lung. Sphingomyelin stays roughly constant through late gestation while lecithin rises sharply after about weeks 32 to 34. An L/S ratio above 2 indicates mature lungs and a low risk of respiratory distress; a ratio below about 1.5 indicates high risk. Diabetic pregnancy is the classic exception, because distress can occur despite a ratio above 2; the appearance of phosphatidylglycerol, a late component of mature surfactant, is more reliable there. Blood or meconium in the sample can distort the ratio."},{"type":"flow","title":"From surfactant deficiency to neonatal respiratory distress syndrome","steps":["Birth before type II cells make enough surfactant","High surface tension in small alveoli","Alveoli collapse at end expiration: diffuse atelectasis","Low compliance raises work of breathing: retractions, grunting","Perfused, unventilated alveoli act as shunt: hypoxemia","Epithelial and endothelial injury leaks protein: hyaline membranes"],"caption":"Collapse, shunt, and protein leak explain the clinical and pathologic findings."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Self-sustaining injury in respiratory distress syndrome","steps":["Surfactant deficiency causes atelectasis","Shunt produces hypoxemia and acidosis","Hypoxia and acidosis impair type II cell surfactant synthesis","Hypoxic vasoconstriction raises pulmonary resistance","Right-to-left shunting through ductus and foramen ovale worsens hypoxemia"],"caption":"Each step deepens the deficit that started the loop."},{"type":"p","text":"Grunting is expiration against a partly closed glottis, which holds pressure in the airways and keeps alveoli open. The radiograph shows diffuse ground-glass opacity with air bronchograms, because air-filled bronchi stand out against collapsed alveoli. Hyaline membranes, made of fibrin and necrotic epithelial debris, line the alveolar ducts and further thicken the barrier."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 36-week fetus of a mother with poorly controlled diabetes has an amniotic L/S ratio of 2.2 and no phosphatidylglycerol. Is the lung safely mature?","explain":"Not reliably. Fetal hyperinsulinemia blunts the glucocorticoid drive on type II cells, so the surfactant may be functionally immature despite a ratio above 2. Absent phosphatidylglycerol supports that concern.","check":"The paragraph on the L/S ratio and its exceptions."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-01-01-C05","obj":"PU-1.5","title":"Pulmonary hypoplasia from oligohydramnios and diaphragmatic hernia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Normal lung growth depends on mechanical stretch. The fetal airway epithelium secretes fluid by active chloride transport, and the larynx and upper airway resist its outflow, so the lung stays distended at a pressure slightly above that of the amniotic cavity. Fetal breathing movements, which need an intact diaphragm and phrenic nerve, add cyclic stretch. Three conditions must therefore be met: fluid held in the airways, adequate room in the chest, and fetal breathing. Loss of any one, especially during the pseudoglandular and canalicular stages, leaves a lung with fewer airway generations, fewer alveoli, and a smaller vascular bed."},{"type":"p","text":"From the second trimester on, fetal urine is the main source of amniotic fluid. In bilateral renal agenesis there is almost no urine, so oligohydramnios develops. The uterus then compresses the fetal chest, and the lost external fluid pressure lets lung fluid drain out, removing the distending force. The resulting Potter sequence combines pulmonary hypoplasia with compression features: flattened nose, low-set ears, receding chin, and limb positioning deformities such as clubfeet. Urinary tract obstruction (posterior urethral valves in males), autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease, and prolonged rupture of membranes cause the same lung outcome by the same route."},{"type":"p","text":"Congenital diaphragmatic hernia results from failure of a pleuroperitoneal membrane to close the pleuroperitoneal canal, which normally happens around week 7. The defect is usually posterolateral (Bochdalek) and about 85% are left-sided, because the left canal is larger and closes later and the liver shields the right. When the midgut returns from the umbilical cord at about week 10, bowel can pass into the chest during the pseudoglandular stage. The ipsilateral lung is most affected, but mediastinal shift compresses the other lung too, so both are often hypoplastic."},{"type":"flow","title":"Two routes to pulmonary hypoplasia","steps":["Renal agenesis or obstruction, or a diaphragmatic defect","Oligohydramnios compresses chest, or bowel fills hemithorax","Lung fluid and stretch lost during branching","Fewer airways, alveoli, and pulmonary arteries","Small gas exchange area and high vascular resistance at birth"],"caption":"Both causes converge on reduced stretch while the airway tree is forming."},{"type":"p","text":"At birth the small lung cannot exchange enough gas, and the pulmonary arteries are few and abnormally muscular far into the periphery, so pulmonary vascular resistance fails to fall. Blood shunts right to left through the ductus arteriosus and foramen ovale (persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn), causing profound hypoxemia, often with higher oxygen saturation in the right arm than in the legs. Stiff, small lungs also rupture easily, giving pneumothorax. In diaphragmatic hernia the abdomen is scaphoid because its contents are in the chest, bowel sounds may be heard over the hemithorax, and heart sounds shift away from the hernia, usually to the right."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of pulmonary hypoplasia","cols":["Category","Example","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Too little amniotic fluid","Bilateral renal agenesis, posterior urethral valves, prolonged membrane rupture","External compression and loss of distending lung fluid"],["Too little space in the chest","Diaphragmatic hernia, large pleural effusion, small bony thorax","Direct compression of the growing lung"],["Too little fetal breathing","Severe neuromuscular disease, phrenic nerve injury","Loss of cyclic stretch"]],"caption":"Any cause that removes stretch during branching reduces lung growth."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does polyhydramnios accompany esophageal atresia, while oligohydramnios accompanies renal agenesis, and which of the two causes pulmonary hypoplasia?","explain":"Atresia blocks fetal swallowing, which removes fluid, so fluid accumulates; agenesis removes urine, the main fluid source, so fluid falls. Only oligohydramnios compresses the chest and drains lung fluid, so only renal agenesis causes hypoplasia.","check":"The tracheoesophageal flow in C02 and the oligohydramnios paragraph here."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and embryology set the stage. The lung is a foregut derivative, so its anomalies cluster with gut, cardiac, renal, and vertebral defects, and the timing of the diaphragm, the return of the midgut, and the onset of fetal urine production all intersect with the pseudoglandular and canalicular stages. Biochemistry explains maturity: surfactant synthesis is a glucocorticoid-regulated lipid pathway opposed by insulin, which links maternal diabetes to delayed lung maturity. Immunology enters through SP-A and SP-D, which act as collectins, and through the infection risk of a lung that aspirates gastric content through a fistula."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology and pathology then explain the newborn. Surface tension, compliance, and shunt account for respiratory distress syndrome, while a small vascular bed accounts for the pulmonary hypertension of hypoplasia. The limits are real: gestational age boundaries are approximate, the L/S ratio can mislead in diabetic pregnancy, and several conditions can coexist, so the newborn's findings are read against the whole developmental history."}],"summary":["The lung bud arises from ventral foregut endoderm in week 4; endoderm forms all airway and alveolar epithelium and glands, while splanchnic mesoderm forms cartilage, smooth muscle, connective tissue, vessels, and visceral pleura.","Laryngeal cartilages and muscles come from pharyngeal arches 4 and 6, supplied by the superior and recurrent laryngeal branches of the vagus nerve.","Fusion of the tracheoesophageal ridges forms the septum that places the trachea anterior to the esophagus; faulty septation gives esophageal atresia with fistula.","The most common type is a blind upper pouch with a distal fistula: polyhydramnios, drooling, choking with feeds, a coiled tube, and a gas-filled stomach.","Branching to terminal bronchioles is complete by about week 16; the canalicular stage adds respiratory bronchioles and close capillaries, so survival first becomes possible near weeks 24 to 26.","Most alveoli form after birth, and alveolarization continues into childhood.","Type II pneumocytes begin surfactant secretion in the second trimester, reaching adequate levels near week 35; cortisol speeds and insulin delays maturation.","An L/S ratio above 2 indicates lung maturity, but phosphatidylglycerol is more reliable in diabetic pregnancy.","Surfactant deficiency causes atelectasis, shunt, low compliance, and hyaline membranes, a loop worsened by hypoxia and acidosis.","Oligohydramnios and diaphragmatic hernia cause pulmonary hypoplasia by removing stretch during branching, leading to respiratory failure and persistent pulmonary hypertension at birth."],"sources":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Respiratory System"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Body Cavities, formation of the diaphragm"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax, lungs and pleurae; neck, larynx"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pulmonary Ventilation, surfactant and surface tension"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of Infancy and Childhood, neonatal respiratory distress syndrome; The Lung, congenital anomalies"}]},"PU-10":{"unit":"PU-10","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-10","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-10-01","title":"Pulmonary Genetics","subject":"Respiratory System (RESP)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the inherited lung diseases that boards test most, cystic fibrosis, alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, and primary ciliary dyskinesia, and the acquired mutations that turn bronchial epithelium into lung cancer. Each disease is taught as a chain from gene to protein to cell to organ, because exam questions usually give the organ findings and ask for the defect, or give the defect and ask you to predict the findings. The cancer sections explain how the p53 and Rb brakes work, how tobacco carcinogens damage DNA, and why adenocarcinoma, small cell carcinoma, smokers, and never smokers carry different driver mutations.","prerequisites":["Mendelian inheritance: autosomal recessive and codominant patterns, carrier probability","Protein folding, endoplasmic reticulum quality control, and the ubiquitin proteasome pathway","Epithelial ion transport: apical channels, Na+/K+ ATPase, and water movement by osmosis","Airway histology: ciliated pseudostratified epithelium, goblet cells, and mucociliary clearance","Obstructive lung physiology: elastic recoil, FEV1/FVC, and lung volumes"],"connections":[{"to":"Obstructive lung disease and emphysema","how":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency is the clearest example of the protease and antiprotease imbalance that underlies all emphysema."},{"to":"Exocrine pancreas and malabsorption (GI)","how":"The same CFTR defect that dehydrates airway mucus blocks pancreatic ducts and causes fat malabsorption and fat-soluble vitamin deficiency."},{"to":"Liver pathology (GI)","how":"Retained Z alpha-1 antitrypsin is a cause of neonatal hepatitis, cirrhosis, and hepatocellular carcinoma."},{"to":"Embryology of body axes and the heart","how":"Nodal cilia set left-right asymmetry, which explains situs inversus and heterotaxy in primary ciliary dyskinesia."},{"to":"Neoplasms of the pulmonary system (PU-14-F)","how":"The driver mutations in this chapter define the molecular subtypes of adenocarcinoma, squamous cell carcinoma, and small cell carcinoma."}],"terms":[{"term":"CFTR","def":"Cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator, a cAMP and ATP regulated apical anion channel that conducts chloride and bicarbonate."},{"term":"F508del","def":"Deletion of the phenylalanine at position 508 of CFTR, the most common cystic fibrosis allele, which causes misfolding and degradation."},{"term":"Codominance","def":"Expression of both alleles of a gene in the heterozygote, so the phenotype reflects the contribution of each."},{"term":"Serpin","def":"Serine protease inhibitor that traps its target enzyme in an irreversible complex after the enzyme cleaves the serpin's reactive loop."},{"term":"Panacinar emphysema","def":"Uniform destruction of the whole acinus, from respiratory bronchiole to alveolar sac, typical of alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency."},{"term":"Axoneme","def":"The microtubule core of a cilium or flagellum, usually nine outer doublets around a central pair (9+2)."},{"term":"Situs inversus","def":"A complete mirror image reversal of thoracic and abdominal organ position."},{"term":"Restriction point","def":"The point in late G1 after which a cell is committed to enter S phase without further growth factor signaling."},{"term":"Tumor suppressor gene","def":"A gene whose product restrains growth, so cancer requires loss of function, usually of both alleles."},{"term":"DNA adduct","def":"A carcinogen molecule covalently bound to a DNA base, which distorts the helix and causes miscoding during replication."},{"term":"Transversion","def":"A point mutation that replaces a purine with a pyrimidine or the reverse, such as guanine to thymine."},{"term":"Driver mutation","def":"A somatic alteration that gives a tumor cell a growth advantage and is selected during tumor evolution."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-10-01-C01","obj":"PU-10.1","title":"Cystic fibrosis: CFTR, F508del, and dehydrated mucus","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"CFTR (cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator) is an apical anion channel in airway, pancreatic, intestinal, biliary, sweat duct, and male reproductive epithelium. It is an ATP-binding cassette (ABC) protein that works as a channel, not a pump. Protein kinase A phosphorylates its regulatory (R) domain, and ATP binding at two nucleotide-binding domains opens the pore. CFTR conducts chloride and bicarbonate, and in the airway it probably restrains sodium absorption through the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC)."},{"type":"p","text":"Cystic fibrosis is autosomal recessive. CFTR lies on the long arm of chromosome 7, and the carrier frequency is about 1 in 25 in people of Northern European ancestry. More than 2,000 variants are known; those blocking synthesis, folding, or gating cause the most severe disease. The most common, F508del, removes three base pairs, deleting phenylalanine 508 without a frameshift. The protein misfolds, endoplasmic reticulum (ER) quality control retains it, and it is ubiquitinated and destroyed by the proteasome, so very little CFTR reaches the apical membrane. What escapes also opens poorly."},{"type":"flow","title":"F508del to bronchiectasis","steps":["Phenylalanine 508 deleted; CFTR misfolds in the ER","Ubiquitin tags the protein for proteasomal degradation","Little CFTR reaches the apical membrane","Chloride and bicarbonate secretion fall; ENaC uptake continues","Water leaves the airway surface liquid","Thick mucus traps bacteria; neutrophils accumulate","Repeated infection destroys bronchial walls: bronchiectasis"],"caption":"A processing defect ends as structural lung damage."},{"type":"p","text":"Cilia can beat only within a periciliary liquid layer about 7 micrometers deep. When chloride secretion fails and sodium absorption continues, water follows sodium out of the airway surface liquid, the layer collapses, and mucus sticks to the epithelium. Lost bicarbonate secretion also acidifies the liquid, impairing mucin expansion and antimicrobial peptides. Trapped bacteria recruit neutrophils, whose elastase and DNA thicken the mucus and damage the bronchial wall. Staphylococcus aureus and Haemophilus influenzae dominate in childhood; mucoid Pseudomonas aeruginosa, which forms alginate biofilms, dominates in adults."},{"type":"p","text":"The sweat gland shows that one defect can change ion flux in opposite directions. The secretory coil produces a near-isotonic fluid, and the duct normally reabsorbs NaCl, with CFTR carrying the chloride. Without CFTR, chloride stays in the lumen and sodium cannot be absorbed without its counterion, so sweat is salty. A sweat chloride of 60 mmol/L or more supports the diagnosis; 30 to 59 mmol/L is intermediate and below 30 mmol/L makes it unlikely. Salt loss in hot weather can cause hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis in infants."},{"type":"table","title":"CFTR loss outside the lower airway","cols":["Organ","Mechanism","Finding"],"rows":[["Pancreas","Low duct fluid and bicarbonate; enzymes plug ducts","Exocrine insufficiency (about 85%): steatorrhea, vitamin A, D, E, K deficiency; high immunoreactive trypsinogen on newborn screen"],["Intestine","Dehydrated meconium","Meconium ileus in about 15 to 20% of affected newborns"],["Biliary tree","Thick bile obstructs small ducts","Focal biliary cirrhosis"],["Male tract","Vas deferens obstructed and lost in fetal life","Absent vas deferens; azoospermia, normal spermatogenesis"],["Upper airway","Same dehydration of mucus","Chronic sinusitis, nasal polyps"]],"caption":"Each finding traces to too little fluid and bicarbonate secretion."},{"type":"stop","predict":"CFTR secretes chloride in the airway but reabsorbs it in the sweat duct. Predict what its loss does to chloride in each fluid.","explain":"Airway secretion fails, so the surface liquid is depleted; sweat duct reabsorption fails, so chloride stays in sweat. The gradient, not the channel, sets the direction.","check":"The airway and sweat gland paragraphs."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: carrier risk in a family","text":"A healthy woman has a brother with cystic fibrosis; her partner, of Northern European ancestry, has no family history. Her parents are both carriers, so her genotype odds are 1/4 normal, 1/2 carrier, 1/4 affected. Because she is unaffected, the affected quarter is removed: her carrier probability is (1/2) divided by (3/4), or 2/3. Her partner carries the population risk, about 1/25. Two carriers have a 1/4 chance per child. The child's risk is 2/3 x 1/25 x 1/4 = 1/150."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-10-01-C02","obj":"PU-10.2","title":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency: emphysema and cirrhosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin (AAT) is a serpin made mainly by hepatocytes and secreted into plasma, from which it diffuses into the lung interstitium and alveolar lining fluid. Its main target is neutrophil elastase. Elastase cleaves the AAT reactive loop at methionine 358 and is trapped in an irreversible complex. AAT is an acute phase protein and the main component of the alpha-1 band on serum protein electrophoresis."},{"type":"p","text":"The SERPINA1 gene is on chromosome 14. Alleles are named by their protease inhibitor (Pi) electrophoretic type and are codominant: both alleles are expressed, so the serum level is roughly the sum of the two contributions. M is the normal allele. S (glutamate 264 to valine) is mildly reduced. Z (glutamate 342 to lysine) is severely reduced, and null alleles make no protein."},{"type":"table","title":"Genotype, serum level, and organ risk","cols":["Genotype","Serum AAT (about, % of normal)","Emphysema risk","Liver disease risk"],"rows":[["PiMM","100","Baseline","Baseline"],["PiMZ","60","Slightly increased, mainly in smokers","Slightly increased with other liver injury"],["PiSZ","40","Increased, especially in smokers","Low"],["PiZZ","10 to 15","High; early onset in smokers","High: neonatal hepatitis, cirrhosis, hepatocellular carcinoma"],["Null/null","0","High and very early","None, because no protein is retained"]],"caption":"Lung risk tracks low serum AAT; liver risk tracks retained Z protein."},{"type":"p","text":"Emphysema results from imbalance between proteases and antiproteases. With little AAT, elastase released by neutrophils digests elastin in alveolar walls evenly from the respiratory bronchiole to the alveolar sac, so the emphysema is panacinar, and it is worst at the lung bases, probably because blood flow, and therefore neutrophil delivery, is greatest there. Ordinary smoking-related emphysema is instead centriacinar and worst in the upper lobes. Smoking accelerates AAT deficiency in two ways: smoke recruits neutrophils and macrophages, and oxidants oxidize methionine 358, inactivating the AAT that remains. PiZZ smokers may be dyspneic by their 30s or 40s. Lost elastic recoil removes the radial traction that holds small airways open, so they collapse in expiration: FEV1/FVC falls, total lung capacity and residual volume rise, and diffusing capacity (DLCO) falls with the lost alveolar surface."},{"type":"flow","title":"PiZZ to lower lobe panacinar emphysema","steps":["Z protein polymerizes and is retained in hepatocyte ER","Serum AAT falls to about 10 to 15% of normal","Neutrophil elastase in the alveoli acts unopposed","Elastin destroyed across the whole acinus, worst at the bases","Lost recoil: expiratory airway collapse, air trapping, low DLCO"],"caption":"The lung consequence is a loss of function: too little inhibitor where elastase is released."},{"type":"p","text":"The liver disease has the opposite mechanism, a toxic gain of function. The Z substitution lets the reactive loop of one molecule insert into the beta sheet of the next, forming polymers that cannot leave the ER. Accumulated polymer causes ER stress and hepatocyte death, presenting as neonatal cholestatic hepatitis or later as cirrhosis with a higher risk of hepatocellular carcinoma. On biopsy the polymers are round eosinophilic globules in periportal hepatocytes that are PAS-positive and diastase-resistant: diastase removes glycogen but not this glycoprotein. Null alleles produce no protein, so they cause emphysema without liver disease. Serum electrophoresis shows a reduced alpha-1 band, and because AAT rises with inflammation, an acute illness can push a PiMZ level into the normal range."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: codominance in a PiMZ family","text":"Two PiMZ parents each pass M or Z with equal probability. Their children are 1/4 PiMM, 1/2 PiMZ, and 1/4 PiZZ. Because the alleles are codominant, each genotype has its own serum level: about 100%, 60%, and 10 to 15% of normal. A PiMZ child therefore has a measurable deficiency but usually no disease unless other injury such as smoking is added, while a PiZZ child is at risk for neonatal cholestasis now and panacinar emphysema later. A level alone can mislead, because inflammation and S or null alleles also change it, so Pi typing settles the genotype."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-10-01-C03","obj":"PU-10.3","title":"Primary ciliary dyskinesia and Kartagener syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Motile cilia line the airways, paranasal sinuses, middle ear, fallopian tubes, and brain ventricles, and the sperm flagellum is built the same way. All share the 9+2 axoneme: nine outer microtubule doublets around a central pair. Each doublet carries inner and outer dynein arms, ATPase motors that walk along the neighboring doublet. Nexin links tie the doublets together so that sliding is converted into bending, and radial spokes coordinate the beat. Airway cilia beat about 10 to 15 times per second in coordinated waves that carry the mucus layer toward the pharynx."},{"type":"p","text":"Primary ciliary dyskinesia (PCD) is usually autosomal recessive and genetically heterogeneous, with more than 50 genes identified. The most common defects are in outer dynein arm genes such as DNAH5 and DNAI1, and electron microscopy most often shows absent or shortened outer dynein arms, sometimes with inner arm loss as well. Defects of radial spokes or the central pair also cause PCD, and some forms look nearly normal on electron microscopy, so genetic testing and high-speed video of ciliary beating complement it. Without working dynein, cilia are immotile or beat stiffly and out of phase."},{"type":"p","text":"Most affected term newborns have unexplained respiratory distress, and a daily wet cough and nasal congestion begin in infancy. Mucus composition is normal, but with cough as the only clearance, retained secretions and repeated infection cause chronic rhinosinusitis, otitis media with effusion and conductive hearing loss, and bronchiectasis, typically in the middle and lower lobes. Men are infertile because sperm, though normal in number, cannot swim. Women have reduced fertility and more ectopic pregnancies because oviduct cilia help move the ovum. Nasal nitric oxide is characteristically very low, a useful screening finding whose cause is not fully understood. Sweat chloride is normal, which separates PCD from cystic fibrosis."},{"type":"table","title":"Cystic fibrosis and primary ciliary dyskinesia compared","cols":["Feature","Cystic fibrosis","Primary ciliary dyskinesia"],"rows":[["Defect","Dehydrated mucus (CFTR channel)","Immotile cilia (dynein motor)"],["Inheritance","Autosomal recessive","Usually autosomal recessive"],["Bronchiectasis","Upper lobe predominant","Middle and lower lobe predominant"],["Sweat chloride","60 mmol/L or more","Normal"],["Pancreas","Usually insufficient","Normal"],["Male infertility","Absent vas deferens; azoospermia","Immotile sperm; normal count"],["Organ position","Normal","Situs inversus in about half"]],"caption":"Both diseases fail mucociliary clearance, so their lung disease looks alike; the extrapulmonary findings separate them."},{"type":"p","text":"Left-right asymmetry is set during gastrulation at the primitive node. Node cilia have a 9+0 axoneme but still contain dynein, so they rotate, and because they tilt posteriorly their rotation drives a leftward flow of fluid across the node. This flow triggers expression of Nodal, Lefty, and the transcription factor PITX2 on the left side, which directs heart looping and organ position. If node cilia cannot move, there is no directional cue and sidedness is decided by chance: about half of patients have situs inversus totalis, a full mirror image, and a smaller group has heterotaxy, often with congenital heart disease. Kartagener syndrome is PCD with situs inversus, giving the triad of situs inversus, chronic sinusitis, and bronchiectasis. Dextrocardia appears on the ECG as an inverted P wave, QRS, and T wave in lead I with R waves that shrink rather than grow from V1 to V6."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two siblings carry the same pair of DNAH5 mutations. One has situs inversus and the other has normal organ position. Predict why, and whether normal anatomy argues against PCD.","explain":"Without nodal ciliary flow, sidedness is random, so each embryo has about an even chance of either arrangement. Normal organ position does not exclude PCD; only about half of patients have situs inversus.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on the primitive node and left-right asymmetry."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-10-01-C04","obj":"PU-10.4","title":"p53, Rb, and how tobacco carcinogens mutate TP53","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The cell cycle runs from G1 through S (DNA synthesis) and G2 to mitosis (M). The restriction point in late G1 commits the cell to divide without further growth factor signal. Cyclin-dependent kinases (CDKs) drive progression; each is active only when bound to its cyclin and is opposed by CDK inhibitors. The G1 to S transition matters most in cancer because a cell that copies damaged DNA turns the damage into a permanent mutation in both daughter cells."},{"type":"p","text":"Rb, the product of RB1 on chromosome 13, is the gatekeeper. Hypophosphorylated Rb binds the transcription factor E2F and recruits histone deacetylases, silencing genes needed for S phase. Growth factors induce cyclin D, which activates CDK4 and CDK6; these phosphorylate Rb and partly release E2F. E2F then induces cyclin E, which with CDK2 hyperphosphorylates Rb, a positive feedback loop that makes the switch irreversible. Freed E2F drives transcription of S phase genes such as DNA polymerase and dihydrofolate reductase. Rb is dephosphorylated after mitosis, resetting the brake."},{"type":"table","title":"Regulators of the G1 to S transition","cols":["Molecule","Normal role","Change in cancer"],"rows":[["Cyclin D with CDK4/6","Phosphorylates Rb in response to growth factors","Overexpressed or amplified (gain)"],["Rb (RB1, 13q)","Binds E2F; blocks S phase genes","Deleted or mutated (loss)"],["p16 (CDKN2A)","Inhibits CDK4/6, keeping Rb active","Deleted or silenced by methylation (loss)"],["p53 (TP53, 17p)","Senses DNA damage; arrests G1 or triggers apoptosis","Mutated (loss); the most often mutated gene in cancer"],["p21 (CDKN1A)","CDK inhibitor induced by p53","Absent when p53 is lost"]],"caption":"All converge on Rb phosphorylation, so tumors usually disable this checkpoint at one point."},{"type":"p","text":"p53, from TP53 on chromosome 17p, is a transcription factor that responds to stress rather than driving the cycle. MDM2 normally ubiquitinates p53, keeping it short-lived. DNA damage activates the ATM and ATR kinases, which phosphorylate p53 so MDM2 cannot bind it. p53 accumulates and induces p21, which keeps Rb hypophosphorylated and holds the cell in G1, and GADD45, which aids repair. If repair fails, p53 induces BAX and PUMA and triggers mitochondrial apoptosis. RB1 and TP53 are tumor suppressors, so usually both alleles must be lost (Knudson two-hit model); missense p53 can also act as a dominant negative because p53 is a tetramer."},{"type":"cycle","title":"p53 and MDM2 feedback","steps":["DNA damage activates ATM and ATR kinases","Phosphorylated p53 escapes MDM2 and accumulates","p53 induces p21: G1 arrest for repair","p53 also induces MDM2 transcription","After repair, MDM2 degrades p53"],"caption":"Negative feedback: p53 induces its own inhibitor, ending arrest after repair."},{"type":"p","text":"Tobacco smoke carries more than 60 carcinogens, mainly polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons such as benzo[a]pyrene and nitrosamines such as NNK. Benzo[a]pyrene is a procarcinogen: cytochrome P450 1A1, induced by smoke through the aryl hydrocarbon receptor, and epoxide hydrolase convert it to benzo[a]pyrene diol epoxide, an electrophile that binds the N2 position of guanine. Nucleotide excision repair normally removes these bulky adducts. If they persist, the polymerase tends to place adenine opposite the damaged guanine, producing a guanine to thymine transversion in the next round of replication. In smokers' lung tumors these transversions cluster at TP53 hotspot codons such as 157, 248, and 273, the same sites where the diol epoxide preferentially forms adducts. Without p53, damaged cells keep dividing, so smokers' lung cancers carry very high mutation burdens."},{"type":"flow","title":"Benzo[a]pyrene to loss of the G1 checkpoint","steps":["Benzo[a]pyrene inhaled in smoke","CYP1A1 forms the diol epoxide","Epoxide binds guanine: bulky DNA adduct","Unrepaired adduct; adenine inserted opposite","Guanine to thymine transversion at a TP53 hotspot","No arrest or apoptosis; damaged cells divide"],"caption":"Activation makes the carcinogen; failed repair and replication fix the mutation."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A tumor has lost both copies of RB1. Would restoring p16 arrest it in G1?","explain":"No. p16 acts upstream by inhibiting CDK4/6; with Rb gone, E2F is free regardless. p16 loss and Rb loss are alternative hits on one switch.","check":"The Rb paragraph and regulator table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-10-01-C05","obj":"PU-10.5","title":"Driver alterations in adenocarcinoma and small cell carcinoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A driver alteration gives a tumor cell a growth advantage and is selected during tumor evolution, while passenger mutations ride along. Lung cancers are divided into non-small cell carcinomas (adenocarcinoma, squamous cell carcinoma, large cell carcinoma) and small cell carcinoma, and their drivers differ. Adenocarcinoma is the most common type overall and the most common in never smokers, and its drivers are mostly activating changes in the receptor tyrosine kinase to RAS pathway."},{"type":"p","text":"EGFR is a receptor tyrosine kinase. Ligand binding normally causes dimerization and autophosphorylation, which recruits the adaptor GRB2 and the exchange factor SOS; SOS loads RAS with GTP. RAS-GTP then activates the RAF, MEK, ERK cascade toward proliferation and the PI3K, AKT, mTOR pathway toward survival and growth. Lung cancer EGFR mutations lie in the kinase domain, mostly exon 19 deletions and the exon 21 L858R point mutation, and they keep the kinase active without ligand. In the EML4-ALK fusion, created by an inversion within chromosome 2p, the EML4 coiled-coil domain holds the ALK kinase domain in a permanently dimerized, active state. KRAS mutations, most often at codon 12, prevent GTPase-activating proteins from stimulating GTP hydrolysis, so RAS stays locked in the active GTP-bound form."},{"type":"flow","title":"Where the adenocarcinoma drivers act","steps":["Growth factor binds EGFR (mutant EGFR skips this step)","Kinase dimerizes and autophosphorylates (EML4-ALK does this constitutively)","GRB2 and SOS load RAS with GTP","RAS-GTP stays on (mutant KRAS resists GAP-driven GTP hydrolysis)","RAF, MEK, ERK and PI3K, AKT signal","Proliferation and survival without external signal"],"caption":"EGFR, ALK, and KRAS activate one pathway at different levels, which explains why they rarely occur together."},{"type":"p","text":"EGFR mutations occur in about 10 to 15% of adenocarcinomas in Western populations and about 40 to 50% in East Asian populations, and are most frequent in never smokers and women. Without the tobacco mutation load, these tumors are driven by a single potent kinase mutation, which is why EGFR-mutant adenocarcinoma is the classic lung cancer of a woman who never smoked. KRAS mutations, in about 25 to 30% of adenocarcinomas in Western series, are concentrated in smokers; the common smoker variant, G12C, is a guanine to thymine transversion, the benzo[a]pyrene signature from C04. ALK fusions, in about 3 to 7%, occur in younger never or light smokers. Because these three activate the same pathway, a second one adds little advantage, and they are nearly mutually exclusive. A tumor comes to depend on its driver, termed oncogene addiction."},{"type":"p","text":"Small cell carcinoma, a neuroendocrine tumor almost confined to heavy smokers, is defined by loss of tumor suppressors rather than kinase activation. Biallelic inactivation of TP53 and RB1 is found in nearly all tumors, often with MYC family amplification. With both G1 brakes gone, the cells divide rapidly, with high mitotic counts and early metastasis. Squamous cell carcinoma, also strongly linked to smoking, typically shows TP53 mutation, CDKN2A (p16) loss, and gains on chromosome 3q that include SOX2."},{"type":"table","title":"Major driver alterations in lung cancer","cols":["Alteration","Type of change","Tumor","Typical patient"],"rows":[["EGFR exon 19 deletion or L858R","Activating kinase mutation (oncogene gain)","Adenocarcinoma","Never smoker, woman, East Asian ancestry"],["EML4-ALK","Fusion from inversion in 2p (oncogene gain)","Adenocarcinoma","Younger never or light smoker"],["KRAS codon 12","GTPase-impaired RAS (oncogene gain)","Adenocarcinoma","Smoker"],["TP53 and RB1","Biallelic inactivation (suppressor loss)","Small cell carcinoma","Heavy smoker"],["TP53, CDKN2A loss, 3q (SOX2) gain","Suppressor loss plus amplification","Squamous cell carcinoma","Smoker"]],"caption":"Adenocarcinoma drivers turn the growth pathway on; small cell carcinoma removes the brakes."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Cystic fibrosis and alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency are both protein folding diseases, but they end differently. Misfolded CFTR is destroyed, so disease comes only from lost channel function in every epithelium that uses it. Misfolded Z antitrypsin escapes degradation and polymerizes, so the lung suffers a loss of function while the liver suffers a toxic gain of function. Primary ciliary dyskinesia fails the same clearance system as cystic fibrosis from the other side, the motor rather than the fluid, and its embryology explains organ reversal. All three lead to chronic neutrophil inflammation, and neutrophil elastase is the common final pathway of tissue destruction in bronchiectasis and emphysema."},{"type":"p","text":"Lung cancer joins biochemistry, cell biology, and pathology. Smoke carcinogens need P450 activation, their adducts need excision repair, and failed repair leaves a recognizable guanine to thymine signature in TP53 and KRAS. Tumors arising this way carry many mutations and lose the p53 and Rb brakes, whereas never-smoker adenocarcinomas usually depend on a single kinase driver. These are statistical associations, not rules: EGFR mutations occur in some smokers, KRAS mutations in some never smokers."}],"summary":["Cystic fibrosis is autosomal recessive (CFTR, chromosome 7); F508del misfolds and is degraded by the proteasome, so little channel reaches the membrane.","Lost chloride and bicarbonate secretion with continued ENaC sodium absorption dehydrates airway mucus, leading to infection and bronchiectasis.","In the sweat duct CFTR reabsorbs chloride, so its loss raises sweat chloride to 60 mmol/L or more.","CFTR loss also causes pancreatic insufficiency, meconium ileus, focal biliary cirrhosis, and absent vas deferens with obstructive azoospermia.","Alpha-1 antitrypsin alleles are codominant; PiZZ gives about 10 to 15% of normal serum levels, lower lobe panacinar emphysema, and cirrhosis.","Z polymers retained in the hepatocyte ER form PAS-positive, diastase-resistant globules; null alleles cause emphysema without liver disease.","Primary ciliary dyskinesia removes dynein arm function, causing bronchiectasis, sinusitis, otitis, and infertility; situs is random, so about half have situs inversus.","Rb holds E2F until cyclin D with CDK4/6 phosphorylates it; p53 senses DNA damage and induces p21 to keep Rb active and arrest G1.","Benzo[a]pyrene is activated by CYP1A1 to a diol epoxide whose guanine adducts cause guanine to thymine transversions in TP53.","EGFR and ALK drive adenocarcinoma in never smokers, KRAS drives it in smokers, and small cell carcinoma loses both TP53 and RB1."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Genetic and Pediatric Diseases, section on cystic fibrosis"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lung, sections on emphysema, bronchiectasis, and lung carcinomas"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Neoplasia, sections on cell cycle regulation, tumor suppressor genes, and chemical carcinogenesis"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Liver and Gallbladder, section on alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Gastrulation and establishment of the body axes, left-right asymmetry"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Airway epithelium, airway surface liquid, and mucociliary clearance"}]},"PU-11":{"unit":"PU-11","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-11","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-11-01","title":"Energy Production and the Respiratory System","subject":"Respiratory System (RESP)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains why the body needs oxygen at all. O2 is the final electron acceptor that lets mitochondria make most of the cell's ATP, so failure of O2 delivery or O2 use forces cells onto anaerobic glycolysis and produces lactic acidosis. The chapter then applies that idea to smoke inhalation (cyanide and carbon monoxide), to the respiratory quotient and the CO2 load that different fuels create, to red cell 2,3-BPG and hemoglobin O2 affinity, and to the energy cost of breathing in severe emphysema. Board questions in this area hinge on reading a blood gas, a venous saturation, or a lactate level and working back to the step in the pathway that failed.","prerequisites":["Glycolysis, the citric acid cycle, and the electron transport chain","Oxygen content, the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve, and P50 (PU-8)","Alveolar ventilation and the relationship between PaCO2 and CO2 production (PU-6)","Anion gap and the classification of metabolic acidosis","Lung mechanics, compliance, and airway resistance (PU-5 and PU-6)"],"connections":[{"to":"Blood gas transport (PU-8)","how":"Carbon monoxide and 2,3-BPG both act by changing hemoglobin O2 content or affinity, which this chapter links to cellular energy supply."},{"to":"Gas exchange and tissue perfusion (PU-7)","how":"O2 delivery and extraction at the tissue level decide when a cell runs short of usable O2 and switches to lactate production."},{"to":"Pulmonary biochemistry and nutrition (PU-9)","how":"The respiratory quotient ties the fuel a patient burns to the CO2 the lungs must remove."},{"to":"Obstructive pulmonary diseases (PU-14-B)","how":"Hyperinflation and airflow limitation in emphysema raise the work of breathing, which explains weight loss in advanced disease."},{"to":"Hematology","how":"Pyruvate kinase and hexokinase deficiencies are red cell glycolytic defects whose effect on 2,3-BPG changes how well patients tolerate anemia."}],"terms":[{"term":"Cytochrome c oxidase (complex IV)","def":"The last enzyme of the electron transport chain, which transfers electrons from cytochrome c to O2 to form water while pumping protons."},{"term":"Oxidative phosphorylation","def":"ATP synthesis by ATP synthase driven by the proton gradient that electron transport builds across the inner mitochondrial membrane."},{"term":"Lactic acidosis","def":"A high anion gap metabolic acidosis caused by accumulation of lactate and H+, most often from inadequate tissue O2 delivery or use."},{"term":"Histotoxic hypoxia","def":"Failure of cells to use O2 that is delivered normally, as in cyanide poisoning."},{"term":"Carboxyhemoglobin","def":"Hemoglobin with carbon monoxide bound to its heme iron, which cannot carry O2 and raises the O2 affinity of the remaining hemes."},{"term":"Respiratory quotient (RQ)","def":"The ratio of CO2 produced to O2 consumed by metabolism, which depends on the fuel being oxidized."},{"term":"2,3-Bisphosphoglycerate (2,3-BPG)","def":"A red cell glycolytic intermediate that binds deoxyhemoglobin and lowers its O2 affinity."},{"term":"P50","def":"The PO2 at which hemoglobin is 50% saturated, normally about 26 to 27 mmHg; a higher P50 means a right shift and lower affinity."},{"term":"Rapoport-Luebering shunt","def":"The red cell side path of glycolysis in which bisphosphoglycerate mutase converts 1,3-BPG to 2,3-BPG."},{"term":"Work of breathing","def":"The energy the respiratory muscles spend to overcome elastic recoil and airway resistance during ventilation."},{"term":"Dynamic hyperinflation","def":"Progressive air trapping that occurs when expiration is too slow to finish before the next breath begins."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-11-01-C01","obj":"PU-11.1","title":"Oxygen as the final electron acceptor and the switch to lactate","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"NADH and FADH2 from glycolysis, beta-oxidation, and the citric acid cycle hand their electrons to the electron transport chain in the inner mitochondrial membrane. Electrons pass from complex I (or II) to coenzyme Q, complex III, cytochrome c, and finally complex IV, cytochrome c oxidase. At complex IV, four electrons and four matrix protons reduce one O2 to two water molecules. Complexes I, III, and IV use the energy of this downhill electron flow to pump protons into the intermembrane space, and ATP synthase lets them flow back to make ATP."},{"type":"p","text":"O2 is the only acceptor that can take electrons off the end of the chain. Without it, cytochrome c oxidase stays reduced, electrons back up through every complex, proton pumping stops, and ATP synthase runs down. The backlog also means NADH can no longer be reoxidized, so the mitochondrial and cytosolic NADH to NAD+ ratio rises. Complete oxidation of one glucose yields about 30 to 32 ATP; glycolysis alone yields a net 2."},{"type":"h","text":"The anaerobic switch"},{"type":"p","text":"Glycolysis needs NAD+ at the glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase step. When mitochondria cannot accept NADH, lactate dehydrogenase reduces pyruvate to lactate, regenerating NAD+ so glycolysis can keep running. At the same time, falling ATP and rising AMP activate phosphofructokinase-1, so glycolytic flux increases (the Pasteur effect). The cell survives briefly on 2 ATP per glucose, but it must burn glucose many times faster to approach its normal ATP supply."},{"type":"flow","title":"Tissue hypoxia to lactic acidosis","steps":["O2 supply to mitochondria falls below demand","Complex IV lacks its electron acceptor; the chain stops","Proton pumping and oxidative ATP synthesis fall","NADH accumulates and AMP rises","PFK-1 activated; glycolytic flux increases","LDH reduces pyruvate to lactate, regenerating NAD+","Lactate and H+ accumulate: high anion gap acidosis"],"caption":"Each step follows from the one before: no terminal acceptor, no oxidative ATP, then lactate production to keep glycolysis supplied with NAD+."},{"type":"p","text":"Lactate and H+ accumulate together, bicarbonate is consumed in buffering, and the unmeasured lactate anion widens the anion gap. Normal blood lactate is below about 2 mmol/L. Because the redox state shifts toward NADH, the lactate to pyruvate ratio rises above its normal value of about 10. The liver (Cori cycle) and kidney normally clear lactate, so liver failure worsens accumulation."},{"type":"table","title":"Ways tissue O2 supply or use can fail","cols":["Mechanism","Example","PaO2","Arterial O2 content"],"rows":[["Hypoxemic","Hypoventilation, V/Q mismatch, high altitude","Low","Low"],["Anemic","Severe anemia, carbon monoxide","Normal","Low"],["Circulatory (stagnant)","Shock, heart failure, arterial occlusion","Normal","Normal"],["Histotoxic","Cyanide","Normal","Normal"]],"caption":"O2 delivery equals cardiac output times arterial O2 content. Any of the first three lowers delivery; the fourth blocks O2 use at complex IV. All four can produce lactic acidosis (type A, tissue hypoxia)."},{"type":"p","text":"Tissues normally extract only about 25% of delivered O2, so delivery can fall a long way before consumption suffers. Once extraction reaches its maximum, consumption becomes supply dependent and lactate rises. Red cells, which lack mitochondria, make lactate even when O2 is plentiful, which is why a small baseline lactate is normal. Lactic acidosis without tissue hypoxia (type B), as in liver failure or with metformin, follows the same chemistry through a different cause."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-11-01-C02","obj":"PU-11.2","title":"Cyanide and carbon monoxide in smoke inhalation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Fires that burn nitrogen-containing polymers (polyurethane foam, nylon, acrylic fibers) or wool and silk release hydrogen cyanide through incomplete combustion, and every fire releases carbon monoxide (CO), so a smoke victim may carry both poisons."},{"type":"h","text":"Cyanide: a pure block of O2 use"},{"type":"p","text":"Cyanide binds the ferric (Fe3+) iron of heme a3 in cytochrome c oxidase and stops electron transfer to O2. The lungs still oxygenate blood normally, so PaO2 and arterial O2 content are normal, but cells cannot use the O2 they receive. ATP falls, cells switch to anaerobic glycolysis, and lactate rises sharply (histotoxic hypoxia). Because tissues extract little O2, venous blood returns almost as saturated as arterial blood: venous O2 saturation is high, the arteriovenous O2 difference narrows, and venous blood looks bright red. The organs with the highest O2 use, brain and heart, fail first, so headache, confusion, seizures, coma, hypotension, and arrhythmias follow."},{"type":"p","text":"A small detoxifying pathway exists: the mitochondrial enzyme rhodanese transfers sulfur from thiosulfate to cyanide, forming thiocyanate for renal excretion, but it is limited by the supply of sulfur donors and is quickly overwhelmed."},{"type":"flow","title":"Cyanide poisoning from smoke inhalation","steps":["Burning plastics, foam, wool, or silk release hydrogen cyanide","Cyanide binds Fe3+ of heme a3 in complex IV","Electron transport stops; O2 cannot be used","ATP falls; anaerobic glycolysis and lactate rise","Unused O2 returns in venous blood: high venous saturation","Brain and heart fail first: seizures, coma, arrhythmias"],"caption":"PaO2 stays normal throughout because the lesion is in the mitochondria, not the lung or the blood."},{"type":"h","text":"Carbon monoxide: two lesions"},{"type":"p","text":"CO binds the ferrous iron of hemoglobin with an affinity about 200 to 250 times that of O2, forming carboxyhemoglobin. This lowers O2 content, and CO bound at one heme raises the O2 affinity of the remaining hemes, shifting the curve left so tissues unload less of what is left. CO also binds the reduced heme a3 of cytochrome c oxidase and myoglobin, adding a direct block of cellular respiration. PaO2 is normal because PaO2 measures dissolved O2, which CO does not change. Standard pulse oximetry reads carboxyhemoglobin as oxyhemoglobin and gives a falsely normal SpO2; co-oximetry is needed to measure true O2 saturation and the carboxyhemoglobin fraction."},{"type":"table","title":"Telling the causes of tissue hypoxia apart","cols":["Condition","PaO2","Arterial O2 content","Venous O2 saturation","Lactate"],"rows":[["Hypoxemia from lung disease","Low","Low","Low","High if severe"],["Severe anemia","Normal","Low","Low","High if severe"],["Carbon monoxide","Normal","Low (true SaO2 low; SpO2 falsely normal)","Low","High"],["Cyanide","Normal","Normal","High","High"]],"caption":"A normal PaO2 with lactic acidosis points to a problem in O2 carriage or O2 use. A high venous saturation points to failed O2 use, the signature of cyanide."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: O2 content in carbon monoxide poisoning","text":"A fire victim has hemoglobin 15 g/dL, PaO2 95 mmHg, and carboxyhemoglobin 30%. Pulse oximetry reads 98%, but co-oximetry shows oxyhemoglobin about 68%. Arterial O2 content is 1.34 x Hb x SaO2 plus 0.003 x PaO2: 1.34 x 15 x 0.68 = 13.7, plus 0.3, about 14 mL/dL against a normal of about 20 mL/dL. Content has fallen by almost a third while PaO2 and the oximeter look normal, and the left shift means tissues release less of what remains."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two smoke victims both have a normal PaO2 and marked lactic acidosis. Central venous O2 saturation is 90% in one and 50% in the other. Which is more likely dominated by cyanide?","explain":"The patient at 90%. Cyanide blocks O2 use, so little is extracted and venous blood stays nearly arterial. CO lowers O2 content and unloading, so extraction is high and venous saturation falls.","check":"Compare with the table on telling the causes of tissue hypoxia apart."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-11-01-C03","obj":"PU-11.3","title":"The respiratory quotient and ventilatory demand","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The respiratory quotient (RQ) is the ratio of CO2 produced to O2 consumed by cellular metabolism, VCO2 divided by VO2. A resting adult consumes about 250 mL O2/min and produces about 200 mL CO2/min, so RQ is about 0.8 on a mixed diet. RQ is a property of the fuel: the more oxygen a fuel already contains, the less inhaled O2 it needs for each CO2 it releases."},{"type":"p","text":"Glucose oxidation is C6H12O6 + 6 O2 giving 6 CO2 + 6 H2O, so RQ is 6/6 = 1.0; carbohydrate supplies enough of its own oxygen to make the water. Fat is highly reduced, mostly carbon and hydrogen. Palmitate (C16H32O2) needs 23 O2 to make 16 CO2, an RQ of 16/23, about 0.7, because much of the inhaled O2 goes to forming water from its hydrogen. Protein falls between, at about 0.8. When excess carbohydrate is converted to fat, an oxygen-rich substrate becomes an oxygen-poor product, so CO2 is released without matching O2 use and whole-body RQ rises above 1.0. In fasting or a ketogenic state, RQ falls toward 0.7."},{"type":"table","title":"Respiratory quotient by fuel","cols":["Fuel or state","RQ","CO2 produced per O2 consumed"],"rows":[["Fat oxidation (fasting, ketosis)","About 0.7","Lowest"],["Protein oxidation","About 0.8","Intermediate"],["Mixed diet","About 0.8","Intermediate"],["Carbohydrate oxidation","1.0","Highest of the oxidized fuels"],["Net lipogenesis from excess carbohydrate","Above 1.0","CO2 rises without matching O2 use"]],"caption":"An RQ above 1.0 cannot come from burning carbohydrate alone; it means net fat synthesis."},{"type":"h","text":"Why fuel mix changes ventilation"},{"type":"p","text":"Arterial PCO2 is set by the balance between CO2 production and alveolar ventilation: PaCO2 = 0.863 x VCO2 / VA, with VCO2 in mL/min and VA in L/min. For the same energy yield, carbohydrate generates more CO2 than fat, so a shift toward carbohydrate raises VCO2 and the lungs must increase alveolar ventilation to hold PaCO2 near 40 mmHg. A healthy person does this easily, but a patient with severe COPD, respiratory muscle weakness, or a limited ventilatory reserve may not, and PaCO2 rises."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: CO2 load and PaCO2","text":"At rest, VCO2 is 200 mL/min and alveolar ventilation is 4.3 L/min, so PaCO2 = 0.863 x 200 / 4.3 = about 40 mmHg. A heavy carbohydrate load raises RQ toward 1.0 and lifts VCO2 to 250 mL/min. If alveolar ventilation cannot rise, PaCO2 = 0.863 x 250 / 4.3 = about 50 mmHg, an acute respiratory acidosis. To keep PaCO2 at 40 mmHg, alveolar ventilation must rise by the same 25%, to about 5.4 L/min, a demand a patient with severe emphysema may not be able to meet."},{"type":"p","text":"RQ also enters the alveolar gas equation, PAO2 = FiO2 x (PB minus PH2O) minus PaCO2/R. At sea level on room air with PaCO2 40 mmHg, an R of 0.8 gives PAO2 of about 100 mmHg, while an R of 1.0 gives about 110 mmHg. Keep RQ, which describes cell metabolism, separate from the respiratory exchange ratio (RER) measured at the mouth. They match in a steady state, but RER rises above 1.0 during hyperventilation or intense exercise, when bicarbonate buffering of lactic acid releases extra CO2 that metabolism did not produce."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-11-01-C04","obj":"PU-11.4","title":"2,3-BPG, hemoglobin O2 affinity, altitude, and pyruvate kinase deficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Mature red cells have no mitochondria, so glycolysis is their only source of ATP. A side path of red cell glycolysis, the Rapoport-Luebering shunt, makes 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate (2,3-BPG). Bisphosphoglycerate mutase converts 1,3-BPG to 2,3-BPG, and a phosphatase activity of the same bifunctional enzyme later converts 2,3-BPG to 3-phosphoglycerate, which rejoins glycolysis. The shunt skips the phosphoglycerate kinase step, so the red cell gives up one ATP for every molecule routed through it. Red cells hold about 4 to 5 mmol/L of 2,3-BPG, roughly one molecule per hemoglobin tetramer."},{"type":"flow","title":"The red cell 2,3-BPG shunt","steps":["Red cell glycolysis produces 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate","Bisphosphoglycerate mutase converts it to 2,3-BPG","The phosphoglycerate kinase ATP step is bypassed","2,3-BPG binds the central cavity of deoxyhemoglobin","T state stabilized; O2 affinity falls","P50 rises; curve shifts right; tissues receive more O2"],"caption":"The shunt trades one ATP for a molecule that improves O2 unloading."},{"type":"h","text":"How 2,3-BPG lowers affinity"},{"type":"p","text":"2,3-BPG carries several negative charges and fits the positively charged central cavity between the two beta chains of deoxyhemoglobin. Binding cross-links the beta chains and stabilizes the tense (T) state, which has low O2 affinity. When O2 binds, the molecule shifts to the relaxed (R) state, the cavity narrows, and 2,3-BPG is expelled. Raising 2,3-BPG therefore raises P50 above its normal value of about 26 to 27 mmHg and shifts the oxyhemoglobin curve right, which matters most at tissue PO2, where more O2 is released. Fetal hemoglobin binds 2,3-BPG poorly because its gamma chains replace a positively charged histidine in the cavity with serine, which is why fetal blood has higher O2 affinity."},{"type":"h","text":"Conditions that change 2,3-BPG"},{"type":"p","text":"At high altitude, hypoxemia triggers hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis. A higher red cell pH stimulates phosphofructokinase and bisphosphoglycerate mutase and inhibits the phosphatase, and more deoxyhemoglobin binds free 2,3-BPG and relieves product inhibition, so 2,3-BPG rises within hours to days. This counteracts the left shift that alkalosis alone would cause and improves unloading. The same rise occurs in chronic anemia and chronic hypoxemic lung disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Pyruvate kinase deficiency, an autosomal recessive defect and the most common glycolytic enzyme cause of hemolytic anemia, blocks the last step of glycolysis. Intermediates above the block, including 1,3-BPG, accumulate and more flux enters the shunt, so 2,3-BPG rises. ATP falls, the membrane cannot maintain its ion gradients and shape, and rigid cells are removed in the spleen (extravascular hemolysis). The right shift lets tissues extract more O2 from each gram of hemoglobin, so patients tolerate their anemia better than the hemoglobin level suggests. Hexokinase deficiency blocks glycolysis above the shunt, lowers 2,3-BPG, and has the opposite effect."},{"type":"table","title":"Factors that change red cell 2,3-BPG","cols":["Condition","2,3-BPG","Curve shift","Reason"],"rows":[["High altitude, chronic hypoxemia","Up","Right","Alkalosis and deoxyhemoglobin increase shunt flux"],["Chronic anemia","Up","Right","More deoxyhemoglobin binds 2,3-BPG, relieving inhibition"],["Pyruvate kinase deficiency","Up","Right","Block below the shunt backs intermediates into it"],["Hexokinase deficiency","Down","Left","Block above the shunt starves it of substrate"],["Stored banked blood","Down","Left","Acidic storage lowers synthesis over days to weeks"],["Chronic acidosis","Down","Left (offsets the Bohr effect)","Low pH inhibits PFK and the mutase"]],"caption":"A block below the shunt raises 2,3-BPG; a block above it lowers it."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two patients have the same hemoglobin of 8 g/dL from hemolysis, one from pyruvate kinase deficiency and one from hexokinase deficiency. Which tolerates exercise better?","explain":"The pyruvate kinase deficient patient. The block lies below the shunt, so 2,3-BPG rises, the curve shifts right, and more O2 is unloaded per gram of hemoglobin. Hexokinase deficiency lowers 2,3-BPG and shifts the curve left.","check":"Compare with the table of factors that change red cell 2,3-BPG."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-11-01-C05","obj":"PU-11.5","title":"Work of breathing, energy expenditure, and weight loss in emphysema","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Work of breathing is the energy the respiratory muscles spend to stretch the lungs and chest wall against elastic recoil and to move air against airway resistance. In a healthy person at rest, quiet breathing uses only a few percent of total body O2 consumption, because expiration is passive and inspiration requires little force. Several features of severe emphysema raise this cost."},{"type":"p","text":"Loss of alveolar walls destroys elastic recoil and removes the radial traction that holds small airways open. Airways collapse during expiration, so air is trapped and expiration is not complete before the next breath begins. This dynamic hyperinflation leaves positive end-expiratory pressure in the alveoli (intrinsic PEEP), which the inspiratory muscles must overcome before any air flows. Hyperinflation also flattens and shortens the diaphragm, placing it at a poor point on its length-tension curve and reducing the pressure it generates for a given effort, so accessory muscles are recruited. Destroyed capillary beds raise dead space, so a larger minute ventilation is needed to clear the same CO2. Each breath costs more, and more breaths are needed."},{"type":"table","title":"Why each breath costs more in severe emphysema","cols":["Change","Mechanical consequence","Energy effect"],"rows":[["Loss of elastic recoil","Expiratory airway collapse and air trapping","Expiration may become active"],["Dynamic hyperinflation","Intrinsic PEEP must be overcome first","Added inspiratory threshold load"],["Flattened, shortened diaphragm","Poor length-tension position","More effort per unit of pressure"],["Accessory muscle use","Less efficient chest wall movement","Higher O2 cost of breathing"],["Increased dead space","Higher minute ventilation for the same CO2","More breaths per minute"]],"caption":"Several independent mechanisms multiply the O2 cost of ventilation."},{"type":"p","text":"The result is a resting energy expenditure that is commonly above predicted. Intake often falls at the same time: dyspnea worsens during eating, chewing and swallowing interrupt breathing, and the low, flat diaphragm compresses the stomach and causes early fullness. Systemic inflammation, with cytokines such as tumor necrosis factor alpha and interleukin 6, adds protein breakdown, and hypoxemia and inactivity add further muscle loss. The energy gap is filled by breaking down fat and skeletal muscle, including the respiratory muscles themselves."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The energy cycle of pulmonary cachexia","steps":["Hyperinflation and airflow limitation raise work of breathing","Resting energy expenditure rises","Dyspnea and early fullness reduce intake","Fat and muscle, including respiratory muscle, are catabolized","Weaker respiratory muscles raise relative effort per breath"],"caption":"Each turn of the cycle makes the next breath relatively more costly."},{"type":"p","text":"This mechanism underlies the thin, dyspneic patient with predominant emphysema, in contrast to the patient with predominant chronic bronchitis, who is more often normal weight or heavier. Low body mass index and loss of fat-free mass independently predict worse survival in COPD, and body mass index is one component of the BODE index. Fuel choice links back to the respiratory quotient: a high carbohydrate load raises CO2 production, adding ventilatory work for a patient who has little reserve."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"The chapter rests on one biochemical fact: O2 is consumed only at complex IV, so every route to cellular energy failure ends in the same place. Pulmonary disease lowers PaO2, anemia and CO lower O2 content, shock lowers flow, and cyanide blocks use, yet all of them push cells into anaerobic glycolysis and produce the same high anion gap lactic acidosis. The blood gas, co-oximetry, and venous saturation distinguish them by showing which link in the chain from air to mitochondrion has failed. The brain and heart, with the highest O2 use, show injury first."},{"type":"p","text":"Metabolism and ventilation are coupled in both directions. The fuel being burned sets CO2 production and therefore the ventilation needed, and in emphysema the act of ventilating becomes a large enough energy cost to cause weight loss. Red cell glycolysis, through 2,3-BPG, tunes hemoglobin so that delivery adapts to altitude and anemia. The limit of these models is that real patients combine mechanisms, such as a smoke victim with CO, cyanide, and lung injury at once, so findings must be read together rather than one value at a time."}],"summary":["O2 is the final electron acceptor at complex IV; without it the electron transport chain stops, oxidative ATP synthesis fails, and NADH accumulates.","Lactate dehydrogenase reduces pyruvate to lactate to regenerate NAD+ for glycolysis, producing a high anion gap metabolic acidosis (lactate normally below about 2 mmol/L).","Hypoxemic, anemic, circulatory, and histotoxic hypoxia all end in lactic acidosis; PaO2 is low only in the hypoxemic type.","Cyanide binds Fe3+ of heme a3, so PaO2 and O2 content are normal, venous O2 saturation is high, and lactate rises.","CO forms carboxyhemoglobin, lowers O2 content, shifts the curve left, and also binds complex IV; PaO2 stays normal and pulse oximetry reads falsely normal.","RQ is VCO2/VO2: 1.0 for carbohydrate, about 0.7 for fat, about 0.8 for protein and a mixed diet, and above 1.0 during net lipogenesis.","PaCO2 is proportional to VCO2 divided by alveolar ventilation, so a higher carbohydrate load demands more ventilation.","2,3-BPG from the Rapoport-Luebering shunt stabilizes the T state and shifts the curve right; it rises with altitude, anemia, and pyruvate kinase deficiency and falls with hexokinase deficiency and stored blood.","In severe emphysema, hyperinflation, intrinsic PEEP, a flattened diaphragm, and increased dead space raise the work of breathing and resting energy expenditure, causing weight loss and muscle wasting."],"sources":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Bioenergetics and Oxidative Phosphorylation"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Glycolysis, including the red cell 2,3-BPG pathway and pyruvate kinase deficiency"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Transport of Oxygen and Carbon Dioxide in Blood and Tissue Fluids"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Aviation, High Altitude, and Space Physiology"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Respiratory physiology: gas exchange, oxygen transport, and mechanics of breathing"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lung, section on chronic obstructive pulmonary disease"}]},"PU-12":{"unit":"PU-12","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-12","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-12-01","title":"Immune Responses in Pulmonary Disease","subject":"Respiratory System (RESP)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"The lung exchanges gas across about 70 square meters of alveolar surface while breathing in about 10,000 L of air a day, so it must clear microbes and particles without inflaming the alveoli. This chapter explains the layered innate defenses, then shows how each hypersensitivity type produces a distinct lung disease: IgE in asthma, antibody to basement membrane in Goodpasture syndrome, immune complexes and T cells in hypersensitivity pneumonitis, and Th1 granulomas in tuberculosis and sarcoidosis. It closes with how respiratory vaccines work and how lung cancers escape immunity. Board questions usually give a clinical picture and ask for the hypersensitivity type, cytokine, antibody target, or defense that failed.","prerequisites":["Innate and adaptive immunity, antigen presentation on MHC class I and II (IM units)","The four Gell and Coombs hypersensitivity types","Airway and alveolar histology: ciliated epithelium, goblet cells, type I and II pneumocytes","Obstructive versus restrictive patterns on pulmonary function tests","B cell class switching and the germinal center reaction"],"connections":[{"to":"Immunology (IM units)","how":"Every disease here is a standard hypersensitivity or immune evasion mechanism expressed in lung tissue."},{"to":"Respiratory pathogens (PU-13)","how":"Loss of a specific defense predicts which organisms cause pneumonia, such as encapsulated bacteria when opsonizing antibody is lacking."},{"to":"Obstructive and restrictive lung disease (PU-14)","how":"Asthma is the prototype of reversible obstruction, while hypersensitivity pneumonitis and sarcoidosis produce restriction and a low DLCO."},{"to":"Renal pathology (UR units)","how":"Goodpasture syndrome and granulomatosis with polyangiitis are pulmonary renal syndromes with crescentic glomerulonephritis."},{"to":"Neuromuscular physiology (NE units)","how":"Lambert-Eaton syndrome is explained by reduced presynaptic Ca2+ entry and acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction."}],"terms":[{"term":"Mucociliary escalator","def":"The layer of mucus moved toward the pharynx by the coordinated beating of airway cilia, which carries trapped particles out of the lower airways."},{"term":"Secretory IgA","def":"Dimeric IgA joined by a J chain and carried across epithelium by the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor, a fragment of which remains as secretory component."},{"term":"Collectins","def":"Soluble C-type lectins, including surfactant proteins A and D, that bind microbial carbohydrates and promote their clearance."},{"term":"Opsonin","def":"A molecule that coats a particle and makes it easier for phagocytes to bind and ingest."},{"term":"Th2 cell","def":"A CD4 helper T cell subset that secretes IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13 and drives IgE, eosinophil, and mucus responses."},{"term":"Late phase reaction","def":"The second wave of a type I response, several hours after exposure, caused by recruited eosinophils, Th2 cells, and other leukocytes."},{"term":"Granuloma","def":"An organized collection of activated macrophages (epithelioid cells), often with multinucleated giant cells, surrounded by lymphocytes."},{"term":"Epithelioid cell","def":"An IFN-gamma activated macrophage with abundant pink cytoplasm and an elongated nucleus that resembles an epithelial cell."},{"term":"Precipitins","def":"Serum IgG antibodies that form visible precipitates with a specific antigen, used as a marker of exposure in hypersensitivity pneumonitis."},{"term":"ANCA","def":"Antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies, directed against neutrophil granule enzymes such as proteinase 3 (PR3) and myeloperoxidase (MPO)."},{"term":"Conjugate vaccine","def":"A vaccine in which a bacterial capsular polysaccharide is covalently linked to a carrier protein so that helper T cells can assist the antibody response."},{"term":"Immune checkpoint","def":"An inhibitory receptor pathway, such as PD-1 binding PD-L1, that limits T cell activation and can be exploited by tumors."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-12-01-C01","obj":"PU-12.1","title":"Innate lung defenses and how their failure causes pneumonia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The lower respiratory tract below the larynx is kept nearly sterile by defenses arranged in layers. Which layer handles an inhaled particle depends mainly on its size, because size determines where it deposits: by impaction in the nose and large airways, by sedimentation in small airways, and by diffusion in the alveoli."},{"type":"table","title":"Layers of lung defense","cols":["Site","Defense","How it works"],"rows":[["Nose and nasopharynx","Hairs, turbinates, mucus","Turbulent flow makes particles larger than about 10 micrometers impact on the mucosa"],["Larynx and airways","Cough and glottic closure","Vagal afferents trigger forceful expiration that expels material; the epiglottis and glottis close during swallowing"],["Trachea to bronchioles","Mucociliary escalator","Particles of about 2 to 10 micrometers stick to mucus that cilia carry up to the pharynx to be swallowed or coughed out"],["Mucosal surface","Secretory IgA, lysozyme, lactoferrin, defensins","IgA blocks microbial attachment without inflammation; the others kill or starve bacteria"],["Alveoli","Alveolar macrophages","Ingest particles of about 0.5 to 5 micrometers and microbes; recruit neutrophils if the load is large"],["Alveolar lining fluid","Surfactant proteins A and D","Bind microbial surface carbohydrates, aggregate organisms, and opsonize them for macrophages"]],"caption":"Size cutoffs are approximate and overlap; smaller particles travel deeper."},{"type":"p","text":"The mucociliary escalator has two parts. Goblet cells and submucosal glands make a gel layer of mucus, which floats on a thin watery periciliary layer. Ciliated cells, each with a few hundred cilia built on a 9 + 2 microtubule axoneme, beat in coordinated waves; dynein arms between the outer microtubule doublets use ATP to slide them and bend the cilium. The beat moves the gel layer toward the pharynx, so a defect in mucus hydration, in cilia, or in cough leaves material to stagnate."},{"type":"p","text":"Secretory IgA is made by plasma cells in the lamina propria as a dimer joined by a J chain. The polymeric immunoglobulin receptor on the basal surface of the epithelium carries it across, and part of the receptor stays bound as secretory component, which protects IgA from proteases. IgA neutralizes toxins and blocks adhesion but activates complement poorly, so it protects without inflaming the mucosa. Surfactant proteins A and D are hydrophilic collectins secreted by type II pneumocytes and club cells. Unlike SP-B and SP-C, which lower surface tension, SP-A and SP-D bind sugars on bacteria, viruses, and fungi and present them to macrophages."},{"type":"h","text":"Why smoking, alcohol, and ciliary defects raise pneumonia risk"},{"type":"p","text":"Cigarette smoke slows ciliary beating, causes goblet cell hyperplasia with thicker mucus, replaces ciliated cells with squamous metaplasia, and impairs the killing ability of alveolar macrophages, so organisms that should be carried out are retained. Alcohol depresses consciousness, cough, and the gag reflex, which allows aspiration of oropharyngeal flora, including anaerobes and Klebsiella pneumoniae, and it also impairs neutrophil and macrophage function. In primary ciliary dyskinesia, an autosomal recessive defect most often in dynein arm proteins, cilia are immotile or beat abnormally, causing chronic sinusitis, otitis media, and bronchiectasis from childhood. About half of patients have situs inversus (Kartagener syndrome) because embryonic nodal cilia set left and right asymmetry, and men are usually infertile because sperm flagella share the axoneme. Cystic fibrosis is a different mechanism: the cilia are normal, but dehydrated mucus cannot be moved."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with immotile cilia and a patient with cystic fibrosis both develop bronchiectasis. Would situs inversus or a nasal biopsy with abnormal cilia help separate them?","explain":"Yes. Primary ciliary dyskinesia is a structural ciliary defect, so electron microscopy shows missing dynein arms and half of patients have situs inversus. In cystic fibrosis the CFTR defect dehydrates the periciliary layer; cilia are structurally normal and organ position is normal.","check":"The mucociliary escalator paragraph and the final paragraph on ciliary defects."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-12-01-C02","obj":"PU-12.2","title":"Type I hypersensitivity in allergic asthma and rhinitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Allergic asthma and allergic rhinitis are type I (immediate) hypersensitivity reactions: IgE bound to mast cells reacts with an inhaled allergen such as house dust mite, pollen, animal dander, or cockroach protein. The same mechanism affects the nasal mucosa in rhinitis and the bronchi in asthma, and the two often occur together in atopic people, who have an inherited tendency to make IgE responses."},{"type":"flow","title":"Sensitization and the allergic asthma attack","steps":["Dendritic cells carry inhaled allergen to lymph nodes","Epithelial alarmins and IL-4 push naive CD4 cells to Th2","Th2 IL-4 and CD40 ligand switch B cells to IgE","IgE binds Fc epsilon RI on mast cells (sensitized)","Re-exposure cross-links bound IgE; mast cells degranulate","Minutes: bronchoconstriction, mucus, edema (early phase)","Hours: IL-5 recruited eosinophils and Th2 cells (late phase)"],"caption":"The first exposure sensitizes without symptoms; later exposures trigger the reaction."},{"type":"p","text":"Sensitization begins when the injured or stimulated airway epithelium releases alarmins (IL-25, IL-33, and thymic stromal lymphopoietin), which favor Th2 differentiation. Th2 cytokines then each produce one feature of the disease. IL-4, with CD40 ligand on the T cell, drives B cell class switching to IgE. IL-5 drives eosinophil production in the marrow, their activation, and their survival. IL-13 acts on airway epithelium and smooth muscle to cause goblet cell metaplasia, mucus hypersecretion, and airway hyperresponsiveness, and it also supports IgE switching. IgE binds the high-affinity receptor Fc epsilon RI on mast cells and basophils, where it remains for weeks."},{"type":"p","text":"When allergen cross-links two or more receptor-bound IgE molecules, the mast cell degranulates within minutes. Preformed mediators (histamine, tryptase, heparin) are released at once, and arachidonic acid is converted to newly synthesized mediators: the cysteinyl leukotrienes C4, D4, and E4 and prostaglandin D2, which are potent bronchoconstrictors and increase vascular permeability and mucus secretion. Mast cells also release cytokines and chemokines that set up the late phase."},{"type":"table","title":"Early and late phase responses","cols":["Feature","Early phase","Late phase"],"rows":[["Onset","Within minutes; fades in about 1 hour","About 4 to 8 hours; may last a day or more"],["Main cells","Resident mast cells","Recruited eosinophils, Th2 cells, basophils, neutrophils"],["Mediators","Histamine, leukotrienes C4, D4, E4, prostaglandin D2","Major basic protein, eosinophil cationic protein, leukotrienes, Th2 cytokines"],["Airway effect","Smooth muscle contraction, edema, mucus","Epithelial damage, persistent inflammation, increased hyperresponsiveness"],["In the nose","Sneezing, itching, watery rhinorrhea","Nasal congestion and blockage"]],"caption":"Repeated late phase reactions lead to chronic airway remodeling."},{"type":"p","text":"Eosinophil granule proteins injure the epithelium, exposing sensory nerves and removing epithelial barrier function, which explains why hyperresponsiveness rises after the late phase. Repeated cycles remodel the airway: a thickened subepithelial basement membrane from collagen deposition, smooth muscle hypertrophy and hyperplasia, goblet cell and submucosal gland hyperplasia, and mucus plugs. Sputum may show Curschmann spirals (shed epithelium in mucus casts of small airways) and Charcot-Leyden crystals (crystallized eosinophil protein). Narrowed airways produce wheeze and an obstructive pattern with a reduced FEV1/FVC ratio that improves substantially after a bronchodilator, and blood eosinophils and total IgE are often raised. In rhinitis, histamine stimulates sensory nerves (itch, sneezing) and dilates leaky vessels (rhinorrhea, congestion), and the mucosa looks pale and swollen."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-12-01-C03","obj":"PU-12.3","title":"Granulomas in tuberculosis and sarcoidosis and the role of TNF-alpha","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A granuloma is a type IV (delayed, cell-mediated) response that walls off an antigen macrophages cannot destroy. Mycobacterium tuberculosis is the classic example: inhaled bacilli are ingested by alveolar macrophages, but cell wall lipids such as sulfatides and lipoarabinomannan, together with bacterial proteins, block fusion of the phagosome with the lysosome, so the organism survives and multiplies inside the cell."},{"type":"flow","title":"How a granuloma forms and is maintained","steps":["Macrophage ingests an organism it cannot kill","Macrophage presents antigen on MHC class II and secretes IL-12","IL-12 drives naive CD4 cells to become Th1 cells","Th1 cells secrete IFN-gamma","IFN-gamma activates macrophages: phagolysosome fusion, nitric oxide","Activated macrophages become epithelioid and giant cells","TNF-alpha recruits cells and holds the granuloma together"],"caption":"The same Th1 pathway is used in tuberculosis and in sarcoidosis; only the antigen differs."},{"type":"p","text":"IFN-gamma is the key activating signal. It enables phagolysosome fusion, induces inducible nitric oxide synthase, and increases antigen presentation, turning macrophages into epithelioid cells that may fuse into multinucleated giant cells (Langhans giant cells, not Langerhans cells, with nuclei in a horseshoe). A cuff of CD4 T cells surrounds them. TNF-alpha, made mainly by macrophages and T cells, induces chemokines and endothelial adhesion molecules that keep recruiting cells, and it acts with IFN-gamma to keep macrophages activated. In tuberculosis the intense response to mycobacterial lipids produces caseous necrosis, a cheesy center of dead cells, and the granuloma contains rather than eliminates the organism; this contained state is latent infection. The tuberculin skin test and interferon-gamma release assay detect this Th1 memory, not active disease. People with inherited defects in the IL-12 or IFN-gamma receptors develop disseminated mycobacterial infection, which confirms the pathway."},{"type":"table","title":"Tuberculosis and sarcoidosis granulomas","cols":["Feature","Tuberculosis","Sarcoidosis"],"rows":[["Antigen","Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Unknown; exaggerated Th1 response to a persistent antigen"],["Necrosis","Caseating","Noncaseating"],["Typical lung picture","Ghon complex in primary; apical cavities in reactivation","Bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, with or without interstitial disease"],["Inclusions","Acid-fast bacilli in the granuloma","Asteroid bodies and Schaumann bodies (nonspecific)"],["Laboratory clues","Positive culture or nucleic acid test","Raised serum ACE; hypercalcemia; high CD4 to CD8 ratio in lavage fluid"]],"caption":"Neither inclusion nor ACE is diagnostic; sarcoidosis is a diagnosis of exclusion."},{"type":"p","text":"Sarcoidosis granulomas are formed by the same Th1 mechanism, driven by CD4 T cells concentrated in the lung while peripheral skin test responses are often reduced (cutaneous anergy). Activated granuloma macrophages produce angiotensin converting enzyme and express 1-alpha-hydroxylase, converting 25-hydroxyvitamin D to calcitriol without normal feedback control, which explains hypercalciuria and hypercalcemia."},{"type":"example","title":"Predicting tuberculosis reactivation under TNF-alpha blockade","text":"A patient with a positive interferon-gamma release assay and a normal chest radiograph has latent tuberculosis: live bacilli are contained inside granulomas. A monoclonal antibody that neutralizes TNF-alpha is started for an autoimmune disease. Without TNF-alpha, chemokine and adhesion molecule signals that recruit and retain cells fall, macrophage activation weakens, and the granuloma loses its organized structure. Bacilli are released and multiply, usually within the first months. Because containment fails everywhere at once, the resulting disease is often extrapulmonary or disseminated rather than typical apical cavitation. The same reasoning explains why granulomatous fungal infections such as histoplasmosis can also reactivate."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-12-01-C04","obj":"PU-12.4","title":"Hypersensitivity pneumonitis and how it differs from asthma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hypersensitivity pneumonitis (extrinsic allergic alveolitis) is an immune reaction in the alveolar walls and terminal bronchioles to an inhaled organic antigen. Farmer lung is the prototype: thermophilic actinomycetes, such as Saccharopolyspora rectivirgula, grow in warm, moldy hay and release spores small enough (under about 5 micrometers) to reach the alveoli. Bird fancier lung (avian proteins in droppings and feathers) and hot tub lung (nontuberculous mycobacteria in aerosols) work the same way. Asthma antigens, by contrast, act mainly in the conducting airways."},{"type":"p","text":"Two hypersensitivity types combine. Repeated exposure induces precipitating IgG. When a large dose of antigen is inhaled again, antigen and IgG form immune complexes in the alveolar walls (type III), activate complement, and attract neutrophils. Complex formation and neutrophil recruitment take hours, which is why fever, chills, cough, and dyspnea begin about 4 to 8 hours after exposure and settle within a day or two once exposure stops. With continued exposure the response shifts to T cells (type IV): lymphocytes, many of them CD8 cells, infiltrate the interstitium around bronchioles, and macrophages form small, loose, poorly formed noncaseating granulomas. Over months to years this can progress to interstitial fibrosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the inflammation thickens and later scars the alveolar walls, the lung becomes stiff and gas transfer falls. Pulmonary function tests show a restrictive pattern (reduced total lung capacity and FVC with a normal or high FEV1/FVC ratio) and a reduced DLCO, and lavage fluid shows a marked lymphocytosis. Serum precipitins prove exposure but not disease, because many exposed people who stay well also have them."},{"type":"table","title":"Hypersensitivity pneumonitis versus allergic asthma","cols":["Feature","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Allergic asthma"],"rows":[["Hypersensitivity type","III (acute) and IV (subacute and chronic)","I"],["Antibody","Precipitating IgG","IgE on mast cells"],["Antigen","Organic dust: actinomycetes, avian proteins, fungi","Mite, pollen, dander, mold, cockroach"],["Site","Alveolar walls and terminal bronchioles","Conducting airways (bronchi and bronchioles)"],["Timing after exposure","About 4 to 8 hours","Minutes, with a late phase at 4 to 8 hours"],["Systemic features","Fever, chills, malaise","Usually none"],["Cells","Neutrophils early, then lymphocytes (often CD8)","Mast cells, eosinophils, Th2 cells"],["Histology","Interstitial lymphocytes, poorly formed granulomas","Mucus plugs, eosinophils, thick basement membrane"],["Pulmonary function","Restrictive with low DLCO","Obstructive, reversible, normal or high DLCO"]],"caption":"The late phase of asthma also starts hours after exposure, so timing alone does not separate them; site, cell type, fever, and the PFT pattern do."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A dairy farmer has fever, dyspnea, and crackles every evening after forking hay in winter. Would you expect wheeze, eosinophilia, and a low FEV1/FVC ratio?","explain":"No. This is hypersensitivity pneumonitis. The reaction is immune complex and T cell inflammation in the alveolar walls, not IgE in the bronchi, so it causes crackles, fever, neutrophils then lymphocytes, and a restrictive pattern with low DLCO.","check":"The comparison table and the paragraph on timing."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-12-01-C05","obj":"PU-12.5","title":"Goodpasture syndrome versus granulomatosis with polyangiitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Both diseases cause a pulmonary renal syndrome, bleeding into the alveoli together with rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis, but by different immune mechanisms. Recognizing the mechanism explains the immunofluorescence pattern, the upper airway findings, and the serology."},{"type":"p","text":"Goodpasture syndrome (anti-glomerular basement membrane disease) is type II hypersensitivity: IgG binds a fixed tissue antigen, the noncollagenous (NC1) domain of the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen. This chain is found in glomerular and alveolar basement membranes, which is why these two organs are hit. The antibody binds evenly along the basement membrane, giving smooth linear IgG and C3 staining on immunofluorescence. Complement activation and Fc receptor engagement recruit neutrophils and monocytes, which break the capillary walls, so blood enters the alveoli and Bowman space and crescents form. Glomerular endothelium is fenestrated, giving circulating antibody direct access to the basement membrane, whereas alveolar capillary endothelium is continuous, so lung hemorrhage is more likely when the alveolar membrane is already injured, especially by smoking or inhaled hydrocarbons. HLA-DR15 increases susceptibility. Patients present with hemoptysis, iron deficiency anemia, hematuria with red cell casts, and rising creatinine; lavage fluid shows hemosiderin-laden macrophages."},{"type":"p","text":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (formerly Wegener granulomatosis) is a necrotizing vasculitis of small and medium vessels with necrotizing granulomatous inflammation of the upper and lower respiratory tract. Most patients have antibodies to proteinase 3 (PR3-ANCA), which give a cytoplasmic (c-ANCA) pattern. Cytokines such as TNF-alpha, released during infection, prime neutrophils so that PR3 moves to the cell surface. ANCA then bind it and engage Fc receptors, causing neutrophils to adhere to endothelium, degranulate, release reactive oxygen species, and form extracellular traps that destroy the vessel wall. Because injury is caused by activated neutrophils rather than deposited antibody, the kidney shows few or no immune deposits (pauci-immune crescentic glomerulonephritis)."},{"type":"table","title":"Two pulmonary renal syndromes","cols":["Feature","Goodpasture syndrome","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis"],"rows":[["Mechanism","Type II: IgG to alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen","ANCA-activated neutrophils damage vessel walls"],["Serology","Anti-GBM antibody","PR3-ANCA (c-ANCA) in most"],["Kidney immunofluorescence","Linear IgG and C3","Pauci-immune (little or no deposit)"],["Granulomas","None","Necrotizing granulomas"],["Upper airway","Spared","Sinusitis, otitis, nasal ulcers, saddle nose"],["Lung","Diffuse alveolar hemorrhage","Nodules that cavitate; may also bleed"],["Other organs","Lung and kidney only","Eyes, skin, joints, nerves possible"]],"caption":"Microscopic polyangiitis resembles granulomatosis with polyangiitis but has MPO-ANCA (p-ANCA) and no granulomas."},{"type":"p","text":"The upper airway is the most useful distinction: chronic sinusitis, crusting nasal ulcers, and collapse of the nasal cartilage (saddle nose) point to granulomatosis with polyangiitis. About a third of patients with anti-GBM antibody also have ANCA (double positive)."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-12-01-C06","obj":"PU-12.6","title":"How toxoid, acellular pertussis, and conjugate vaccines protect","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A vaccine protects only if it induces the effector that blocks the organism's main weapon. Diphtheria and tetanus cause disease mainly through exotoxins, Hib and pneumococcus through polysaccharide capsules that resist phagocytosis, and pertussis through adhesins plus toxins, so each needs a different antigen."},{"type":"p","text":"A toxoid is a toxin inactivated chemically, usually with formaldehyde, so it loses toxicity but keeps its epitopes. Diphtheria toxin and tetanospasmin are A-B toxins: the B subunit binds a host receptor and the A subunit acts inside the cell (diphtheria toxin ADP-ribosylates elongation factor 2 and stops protein synthesis; tetanospasmin cleaves synaptobrevin in inhibitory interneurons). Because toxoids are proteins, they are T cell dependent antigens, and helper T cells drive class switching to high-affinity neutralizing IgG and memory. Antitoxin IgG blocks binding of the toxin to its receptor. The immunity is antitoxic, not antibacterial, so a vaccinated person can still carry Corynebacterium diphtheriae while being protected from toxin-mediated disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Acellular pertussis vaccine contains purified Bordetella pertussis proteins instead of killed whole bacteria: pertussis toxoid with, depending on the product, filamentous hemagglutinin, pertactin, and fimbrial antigens. Antibody to the toxoid neutralizes pertussis toxin, while antibodies to the adhesins hinder attachment to ciliated respiratory epithelium. Antibody levels and protection after acellular vaccination wane over several years."},{"type":"p","text":"Capsular polysaccharides behave differently. Their long chains of repeating sugar units cross-link many B cell receptors at once and activate B cells without T cell help (T cell independent antigens). Because carbohydrates cannot be processed into peptides for MHC class II, helper T cells are never engaged, so the response is mostly IgM with little class switching, affinity maturation, or memory, and a repeat dose gives no booster rise. Children under about 2 years respond poorly because their marginal zone B cells, which carry most of these responses, are immature. This is why infants are especially susceptible to encapsulated bacteria."},{"type":"flow","title":"Linked recognition in a conjugate vaccine","steps":["Polysaccharide-specific B cell binds the conjugate by its receptor","B cell internalizes and processes the carrier protein","Carrier peptides are displayed on MHC class II","Carrier-specific helper T cell recognizes the peptide","CD40 ligand and cytokines drive germinal center reaction","IgG to the polysaccharide, affinity maturation, memory B cells"],"caption":"The antibody is against the polysaccharide even though the T cell recognizes the protein."},{"type":"table","title":"Plain polysaccharide versus conjugate vaccine","cols":["Feature","Plain polysaccharide","Polysaccharide-protein conjugate"],"rows":[["T cell help","No (T cell independent)","Yes (T cell dependent)"],["Main antibody","IgM, some IgG2","High-affinity IgG"],["Memory and booster response","Little or none","Yes"],["Response under about 2 years","Poor","Good"],["Effect on nasopharyngeal carriage","Little","Reduced, which also protects unvaccinated contacts"],["Examples","23-valent pneumococcal polysaccharide vaccine","Hib (PRP capsule linked to tetanus toxoid or other carrier), pneumococcal conjugate"]],"caption":"Conjugation converts a T cell independent antigen into a T cell dependent one."},{"type":"example","title":"Why the conjugate antibody targets the sugar","text":"The Hib capsule is polyribosylribitol phosphate (PRP). In the conjugate vaccine PRP is linked to a carrier such as tetanus toxoid. A B cell whose receptor recognizes PRP binds the conjugate and swallows the whole molecule. It cannot present PRP to T cells, but it does present tetanus toxoid peptides on MHC class II. A helper T cell that recognizes those peptides delivers CD40 ligand and cytokines to that B cell, and the B cell, still specific for PRP, class switches and enters a germinal center. The resulting memory B cells and plasma cells make IgG against PRP, which opsonizes the bacterium and activates complement. Help from the carrier is the only change; the specificity comes from the B cell receptor."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-12-01-C07","obj":"PU-12.7","title":"Immune evasion by lung cancer and paraneoplastic neurologic syndromes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Lung cancers, especially those caused by smoking, carry many mutations, so they make many abnormal proteins (neoantigens). Tumor cells process these into peptides presented on MHC class I, where CD8 cytotoxic T cells can recognize and kill them. A tumor that grows to clinical size has therefore usually acquired ways to avoid this response."},{"type":"table","title":"How lung cancers escape T cells","cols":["Mechanism","How it works","Result"],"rows":[["Reduced MHC class I","Loss or mutation of HLA genes or beta-2 microglobulin, or reduced antigen processing (for example TAP)","CD8 T cells cannot see tumor peptides"],["PD-L1 expression","PD-L1 on tumor cells binds PD-1 on activated T cells; PD-1 recruits a phosphatase that dampens T cell receptor and CD28 signaling","T cells in the tumor are present but exhausted"],["Suppressive environment","Regulatory T cells, TGF-beta, IL-10, myeloid suppressor cells","Local T cell activation is blunted"]],"caption":"Tumors often use several mechanisms at once."},{"type":"p","text":"Loss of MHC class I should make tumor cells targets for natural killer cells, whose inhibitory receptors recognize class I (missing self), so tumors that escape this way must also evade NK cells, for example by shedding activating NK ligands. PD-L1 expression is often induced by IFN-gamma released from the T cells attacking the tumor, a self-protective response called adaptive resistance. Antibodies that block PD-1 or PD-L1 remove this brake, which is the mechanistic proof that the pathway restrains antitumor T cells; the same release explains why such antibodies can cause autoimmune inflammation."},{"type":"h","text":"Paraneoplastic neurologic syndromes"},{"type":"p","text":"Small cell lung carcinoma is a neuroendocrine tumor that expresses proteins normally restricted to neurons. An immune response against the tumor therefore also attacks the nervous system, and the neurologic syndrome often appears before the cancer is found. Anti-Hu antibodies target HuD, an RNA-binding protein inside neuronal nuclei; because the antigen is intracellular, the antibody is mainly a marker, and cytotoxic T cells cause the neuronal loss. The results are paraneoplastic encephalomyelitis, limbic encephalitis, and sensory neuronopathy from destruction of dorsal root ganglion cells. Antibodies to P/Q-type voltage-gated calcium channels are directly pathogenic: they reduce the number of presynaptic channels, so less Ca2+ enters the nerve terminal and fewer acetylcholine quanta are released, producing Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome."},{"type":"example","title":"Why Lambert-Eaton weakness improves with effort","text":"In Lambert-Eaton syndrome each action potential lets too little Ca2+ into the nerve terminal, so acetylcholine release, and the end plate potential, falls below threshold at many fibers. Reflexes are reduced and proximal limbs are weak. If the patient contracts a muscle hard for about 10 seconds, impulses arrive faster than Ca2+ can be cleared, so residual Ca2+ accumulates and each later impulse releases more transmitter. Strength and reflexes briefly improve, and high-frequency repetitive nerve stimulation shows an increment in the muscle action potential. Myasthenia gravis is the opposite: postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors are lost, release is normal, and weakness worsens with repeated use. Reduced acetylcholine release at autonomic synapses also explains the dry mouth and orthostatic symptoms of Lambert-Eaton."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"The lung shows every hypersensitivity type because it is constantly exposed to foreign material and has a thin, richly vascular basement membrane. Antigen size and site decide the disease: proteins deposited in conducting airways of atopic people give IgE responses (type I), organic dusts reaching alveoli give immune complex and T cell injury (types III and IV), persistent intracellular organisms give Th1 granulomas (type IV), and an autoantigen shared by alveolar and glomerular basement membranes gives antibody-mediated injury (type II). Histology confirms the mechanism: eosinophils and mucus plugs, loose interstitial granulomas, organized caseating or noncaseating granulomas, linear IgG, or pauci-immune necrosis."},{"type":"p","text":"The same immunology explains protection and its limits. Innate barriers fail mechanically (cilia, cough, mucus) before adaptive immunity is needed; vaccines work only if they engage T cell help when lasting IgG is required; and cancer exploits the checkpoints that normally prevent autoimmunity, while cross-reaction between tumor and neuron shows how an antitumor response can itself cause disease. Hypersensitivity labels are simplifications: most real diseases, including asthma and hypersensitivity pneumonitis, involve more than one mechanism."}],"summary":["Particle size sets where defenses act: the nose traps large particles, the mucociliary escalator clears mid-sized ones, and alveolar macrophages aided by SP-A and SP-D opsonins clear the smallest.","Smoking (ciliary and macrophage damage), alcohol (aspiration, weak cough and phagocytes), and dynein arm defects (primary ciliary dyskinesia) each remove a defense layer and raise pneumonia risk.","Allergic asthma is type I: IL-4 switches B cells to IgE, IL-5 drives eosinophils, IL-13 drives mucus and hyperresponsiveness; mast cell mediators cause the early phase and eosinophils the late phase.","Granulomas form when macrophage IL-12 drives Th1 cells whose IFN-gamma activates macrophages; TNF-alpha maintains the granuloma, so blocking it can reactivate latent tuberculosis.","Tuberculosis granulomas caseate; sarcoidosis granulomas do not and are linked to hilar adenopathy, raised ACE, and calcitriol-driven hypercalcemia.","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis is a type III and IV reaction to organic dust in the alveolar walls, with fever hours after exposure, lymphocytes, poorly formed granulomas, and a restrictive pattern with low DLCO.","Goodpasture syndrome is type II anti-alpha-3 type IV collagen IgG with linear staining, alveolar hemorrhage, and crescentic glomerulonephritis; granulomatosis with polyangiitis is PR3-ANCA vasculitis with upper airway disease and pauci-immune glomerulonephritis.","Toxoids induce neutralizing antitoxin IgG; plain polysaccharides give T cell independent IgM without memory; conjugates add carrier-driven T cell help, giving IgG and memory even in infants.","Lung cancers evade CD8 T cells by lowering MHC class I and expressing PD-L1; small cell carcinoma shares neuronal antigens, causing anti-Hu syndromes and anti-calcium channel Lambert-Eaton syndrome."],"sources":[{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity disorders; Immunity to tumors; Immunity to microbes"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lung, sections on asthma, granulomatous diseases, hypersensitivity pneumonitis, and diffuse alveolar hemorrhage syndromes"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of the Immune System; Blood Vessels, section on vasculitis"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg Medical Microbiology","where":"Sections on Corynebacterium diphtheriae, Bordetella, Haemophilus, pneumococcus, and mycobacteria"},{"book":"Junqueira Basic Histology","where":"Respiratory System, respiratory epithelium and alveoli"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Pulmonary Disease"}]},"PU-13":{"unit":"PU-13","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-13-01","title":"Pulmonary Pathogens","subject":"Respiratory System (RESP)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the bacteria, viruses, and fungi that infect the lungs, with the structural features, laboratory traits, and virulence factors that identify each and explain the disease it causes. Board questions usually give a host or exposure clue (asplenia, alcohol use, cystic fibrosis, a cooling tower, a cave, a CD4 count) and ask for the organism, its toxin, its culture requirement, or how it escapes immunity. If you can connect each organism's biology to the defense it defeats, you can reason through these questions instead of memorizing lists, and you will recognize why particular patients get particular infections.","prerequisites":["Gram-positive and gram-negative cell wall structure and the Gram stain","Complement, opsonization, and splenic clearance of bacteria (IM-7, IM-8)","Th1 cell-mediated immunity, macrophage activation, and granuloma formation (IM-5, IM-13)","G protein signaling: Gs, Gi, adenylyl cyclase, and cAMP","Respiratory tract defenses: mucociliary clearance and alveolar macrophages (PU-2, PU-12)"],"connections":[{"to":"Infectious diseases of the pulmonary system (PU-14-H)","how":"The pneumonias, tuberculosis, and fungal diseases taught there are the clinical expression of the organisms described here."},{"to":"Acquired immunodeficiency diseases (IM-15-B)","how":"Falling CD4 counts in HIV infection predict which lung pathogens appear, with Pneumocystis below 200 cells/mm3."},{"to":"Complement and lymphatic organ function (IM-7, IM-8)","how":"Encapsulated bacteria such as S. pneumoniae are cleared by opsonization and the spleen, so asplenia and complement defects raise risk."},{"to":"Pulmonary genetics (PU-10)","how":"CFTR mutations thicken airway mucus, which explains chronic Pseudomonas colonization in cystic fibrosis."},{"to":"Cell signaling (IM-6)","how":"Pertussis and cholera toxins both raise cAMP but act on opposite G proteins, a common comparison on board questions."}],"terms":[{"term":"Opsonization","def":"Coating of a microbe with antibody or complement C3b so phagocytes can bind and ingest it."},{"term":"Polysaccharide capsule","def":"A loose carbohydrate layer outside the cell wall that hides the bacterium from complement and phagocytes."},{"term":"Lysogenic conversion","def":"Acquisition of a new trait, such as a toxin gene, from a bacteriophage integrated into the bacterial chromosome."},{"term":"ADP-ribosylation","def":"Enzymatic transfer of ADP-ribose from NAD+ onto a host protein, which changes or abolishes that protein's function."},{"term":"Acid-fast","def":"Retaining carbolfuchsin stain after decolorizing with acid alcohol, a property of bacteria with mycolic acid-rich walls."},{"term":"Droplet nuclei","def":"Dried respiratory particles about 1 to 5 um across that stay airborne and reach the alveoli when inhaled."},{"term":"Granuloma","def":"A compact collection of activated macrophages (epithelioid cells), giant cells, and lymphocytes that walls off a persistent antigen."},{"term":"Antigenic drift","def":"Gradual change in viral surface antigens from point mutations, producing seasonal epidemics."},{"term":"Antigenic shift","def":"Abrupt replacement of an influenza A surface antigen by reassortment of genome segments, which can produce a pandemic."},{"term":"Syncytium","def":"A multinucleated cell formed when a viral fusion protein on an infected cell fuses it with neighboring cells."},{"term":"Dimorphic fungus","def":"A fungus that grows as a mold in the environment and converts to a yeast or spherule at body temperature."},{"term":"Angioinvasion","def":"Growth of fungal hyphae into blood vessel walls, causing thrombosis and hemorrhagic infarction of tissue."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-13-01-C01","obj":"PU-13.1","title":"Streptococcus pneumoniae","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Streptococcus pneumoniae (the pneumococcus) is a gram-positive coccus that grows in pairs of lancet-shaped cells, called diplococci. It colonizes the nasopharynx of many healthy people, and disease follows when it reaches a site where it is not promptly cleared: the alveoli after aspiration of nasopharyngeal secretions, or the middle ear, sinuses, blood, or meninges. It is the most common bacterial cause of community-acquired pneumonia and the classic cause of lobar pneumonia, in which consolidation fills an entire lobe."},{"type":"h","text":"Laboratory identification"},{"type":"p","text":"Like all streptococci it is catalase negative. On blood agar it is alpha-hemolytic: hydrogen peroxide made by the organism oxidizes hemoglobin to a green product, leaving a zone of partial hemolysis. Viridans streptococci look the same, so two tests separate them. Optochin inhibits pneumococcal growth around a disk, while viridans streptococci are resistant. In the bile solubility test, bile salts activate the pneumococcal autolysin (LytA), which digests the cell wall and lyses the colony; viridans streptococci do not lyse. The capsule also swells visibly when mixed with type-specific antiserum (the quellung reaction)."},{"type":"table","title":"Virulence factors of S. pneumoniae","cols":["Factor","Mechanism","Consequence"],"rows":[["Polysaccharide capsule (about 100 serotypes)","Hydrophilic coat limits C3b deposition and hides deposited C3b and antibody from phagocyte receptors","Resists phagocytosis; clearance needs type-specific antibody and the spleen"],["IgA protease","Cleaves secretory IgA1 at its hinge region","Allows colonization of the nasopharyngeal mucosa"],["Pneumolysin","Cholesterol-dependent pore-forming toxin; also activates complement","Damages alveolar epithelium and endothelium, slows cilia, intensifies inflammation"],["Autolysin (LytA)","Lyses the bacterium, releasing pneumolysin and cell wall fragments","Cell wall teichoic acid and peptidoglycan trigger strong innate inflammation"]],"caption":"The capsule is the essential virulence factor; unencapsulated strains are rarely invasive."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the capsule blocks complement-mediated uptake, defense depends on anticapsular IgG that opsonizes the organism and on splenic macrophages that remove poorly opsonized bacteria from the blood. Capsular polysaccharide is a T cell-independent antigen, so children under about 2 years respond poorly to it. People with asplenia, whether surgical or functional as in sickle cell disease, and people with antibody or C3 deficiency are at risk for rapid, overwhelming pneumococcal sepsis."},{"type":"h","text":"Lobar pneumonia"},{"type":"p","text":"Bacteria multiply in the alveoli and the protein-rich edema fluid they provoke carries them through the pores of Kohn to neighboring alveoli, so infection spreads through a lobe until stopped by the pleura of the fissures. The pathologic stages follow the inflammatory response."},{"type":"flow","title":"Stages of lobar pneumonia","steps":["Congestion: vascular engorgement, edema fluid, many bacteria (about days 1 to 2)","Red hepatization: neutrophils, red cells, and fibrin fill alveoli","Gray hepatization: red cells lyse; fibrinosuppurative exudate remains","Resolution: enzymes digest exudate, macrophages clear debris"],"caption":"Approximate timing follows Robbins; with resolution the alveolar architecture is usually restored."},{"type":"p","text":"The manifestations follow from these stages. Abrupt fever and rigors reflect cytokine release, and rust-colored sputum reflects red cells and degraded hemoglobin in the exudate. Consolidated lung is airless, so it is dull to percussion, and it transmits sound well, producing bronchial breath sounds and increased tactile fremitus. Alveoli that are perfused but filled with exudate act as a shunt, causing hypoxemia, and extension to the pleura causes pleuritic pain. Bacteremia can seed the meninges or joints."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 19-year-old with sickle cell disease develops fever and becomes hypotensive within hours. Which organism should you think of first, and why is she vulnerable?","explain":"Repeated splenic infarction leaves her functionally asplenic, so poorly opsonized encapsulated bacteria such as S. pneumoniae are not removed from the blood.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on capsule and host defense."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-13-01-C02","obj":"PU-13.2","title":"Gram-negative respiratory pathogens","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Four gram-negative organisms account for most board questions on gram-negative pneumonia. Each has a growth requirement or structural trait that identifies it in the laboratory and a host or exposure setting that explains who gets it."},{"type":"table","title":"Gram-negative lung pathogens compared","cols":["Organism","Morphology and metabolism","Laboratory clue","Key virulence","Typical host or setting"],"rows":[["Haemophilus influenzae","Small pleomorphic coccobacillus","Needs factor X (hemin) and factor V (NAD+); grows on chocolate agar; satellite growth around S. aureus","Type b capsule (polyribosylribitol phosphate); IgA protease","Type b: invasive disease in unvaccinated young children; nontypeable: otitis, sinusitis, COPD exacerbations"],["Klebsiella pneumoniae","Nonmotile rod; lactose fermenter (mucoid pink colonies on MacConkey agar)","Very mucoid colonies; urease positive","Large polysaccharide capsule","Aspiration in alcohol use disorder and diabetes; hospital-acquired infection"],["Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Aerobic rod; non-lactose fermenter","Oxidase positive; blue-green pigment (pyocyanin); grape-like odor","Exotoxin A (ADP-ribosylates EF-2); alginate biofilm; type III secretion","Cystic fibrosis, mechanical ventilation, neutropenia, burns"],["Legionella pneumophila","Rod that stains poorly with Gram stain; facultative intracellular","Buffered charcoal yeast extract agar with iron and cysteine; urine antigen (serogroup 1)","Multiplies inside macrophages in a vacuole that avoids lysosomes","Aerosols from contaminated warm water; older smokers, chronic lung disease, immunosuppression"]],"caption":"Each organism favors a setting in which a specific host defense is weakened or bypassed."},{"type":"p","text":"H. influenzae cannot synthesize heme (factor X), which it needs for its cytochromes, or NAD+ (factor V). Chocolate agar supplies both because heating lyses the red cells to release them and inactivates enzymes in blood that degrade NAD+. On ordinary blood agar it grows only beside a hemolytic S. aureus streak, which supplies NAD+ and releases heme (satellite growth). The type b capsule, like the pneumococcal capsule, resists complement and allows bloodstream invasion, causing meningitis, epiglottitis, and bacteremia in young children who lack anticapsular antibody. Nontypeable strains lack a capsule, stay on mucosal surfaces, and exploit damaged mucociliary clearance in COPD."},{"type":"p","text":"Klebsiella pneumonia usually follows aspiration of oral secretions colonized by gram-negative rods in people whose cough and gag reflexes are blunted by alcohol. Its thick capsule resists phagocytosis, and the infection is destructive, producing necrosis, abscesses, and thick, bloody, mucoid sputum described as currant jelly."},{"type":"p","text":"Pseudomonas thrives in moist environments, including respiratory equipment. An endotracheal tube bypasses upper airway defenses and carries a biofilm, which explains ventilator-associated pneumonia. In cystic fibrosis, dehydrated mucus from defective CFTR is poorly cleared; Pseudomonas colonizes it, switches to a mucoid alginate-producing form, and grows in biofilms that resist antibodies and neutrophils. The persistent neutrophil response damages bronchial walls and causes bronchiectasis. Exotoxin A kills cells by the same reaction as diphtheria toxin (C03)."},{"type":"p","text":"Legionella lives inside free-living amoebae in warm water systems such as cooling towers, hot water plumbing, and whirlpools, and infects people who inhale aerosols; it is not spread person to person. In alveolar macrophages it uses a secretion system to build a vacuole that escapes lysosomal fusion, so control requires cell-mediated immunity. Legionnaires disease is a severe pneumonia with high fever and frequent diarrhea, confusion, and hyponatremia; Pontiac fever is a self-limited influenza-like illness without pneumonia. Because it stains poorly, a gram stain of sputum shows neutrophils but few organisms."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-13-01-C03","obj":"PU-13.3","title":"Diphtheria and pertussis toxins","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Diphtheria toxin and pertussis toxin are both AB toxins: a binding (B) portion attaches to host cells and delivers an active (A) portion that is an ADP-ribosyltransferase. The A portion transfers ADP-ribose from NAD+ onto a specific host protein. The disease each causes follows from which protein is modified."},{"type":"h","text":"Corynebacterium diphtheriae"},{"type":"p","text":"C. diphtheriae is a gram-positive, club-shaped, pleomorphic rod that often lies in V and L shapes and contains metachromatic (polyphosphate) granules. It forms black colonies on tellurite-containing agar. Only strains lysogenized by a bacteriophage (corynephage beta) carrying the tox gene make toxin, an example of lysogenic conversion. A bacterial repressor (DtxR) blocks tox transcription when iron is plentiful, so toxin is made when iron is scarce. Toxin production by an isolate is confirmed with the Elek immunodiffusion test."},{"type":"flow","title":"Diphtheria toxin from phage to tissue damage","steps":["Phage carrying tox gene lysogenizes C. diphtheriae","Bacteria colonize pharynx and secrete toxin; they stay local","B fragment binds the HB-EGF precursor receptor; toxin is endocytosed","Endosomal acid moves the A fragment into the cytosol","A fragment ADP-ribosylates EF-2; protein synthesis stops; cells die","Absorbed toxin reaches the myocardium and peripheral nerves"],"caption":"Elongation factor 2 (EF-2) moves the ribosome along mRNA; a single A fragment can halt translation in a cell."},{"type":"p","text":"Local cell death produces a pseudomembrane of necrotic epithelium, fibrin, neutrophils, and bacteria on the tonsils, pharynx, or larynx. It is gray, adherent, and bleeds when scraped, and it can obstruct the airway. Edema and cervical lymphadenopathy give the bull neck. Circulating toxin causes myocarditis with conduction block, typically in the first or second week, and a demyelinating neuropathy that often begins with palatal paralysis and cranial nerve palsies."},{"type":"h","text":"Bordetella pertussis"},{"type":"p","text":"B. pertussis is a small, strictly aerobic gram-negative coccobacillus grown on Bordet-Gengou or Regan-Lowe agar. It attaches to ciliated respiratory epithelium with filamentous hemagglutinin and pertactin but does not invade. Pertussis toxin ADP-ribosylates the alpha subunit of Gi. The modified Gi can no longer be activated by its receptors, so it cannot inhibit adenylyl cyclase, and cAMP rises. Because chemokine receptors signal through Gi, lymphocytes fail to leave the blood, producing marked lymphocytosis; phagocyte recruitment and killing are impaired, and in infants increased insulin release can cause hypoglycemia. A second toxin, adenylate cyclase toxin, enters phagocytes and makes cAMP itself, while tracheal cytotoxin, a peptidoglycan fragment, kills ciliated cells and cripples mucociliary clearance."},{"type":"table","title":"Clinical stages of pertussis","cols":["Stage","Duration (about)","Findings and mechanism"],"rows":[["Catarrhal","1 to 2 weeks","Rhinorrhea, mild cough, low fever; highest bacterial load, most contagious"],["Paroxysmal","2 to 6 weeks","Bursts of cough, then an inspiratory whoop through a narrowed glottis; post-tussive vomiting; infants may have apnea instead"],["Convalescent","Weeks to months","Cough fades slowly as ciliated epithelium regenerates"]],"caption":"The cough outlasts the bacteria because toxin-damaged epithelium takes weeks to recover."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: four toxins, two targets","text":"Pertussis toxin and cholera toxin both raise cAMP, so why do they differ? Cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates Gs alpha and blocks its GTPase, so Gs stays bound to GTP and keeps stimulating adenylyl cyclase: the accelerator is stuck down. Pertussis toxin ADP-ribosylates Gi alpha so receptors cannot activate it, and the inhibitory signal is lost: the brake is cut. Both end with high cAMP, but in different cells with different results (intestinal chloride secretion versus lymphocytosis and phagocyte dysfunction). Diphtheria toxin and Pseudomonas exotoxin A use the same ADP-ribosylation reaction on a different target, EF-2, so their result is not altered signaling but halted protein synthesis and cell death."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-13-01-C04","obj":"PU-13.4","title":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis is a slender, nonmotile, obligately aerobic rod that divides only about once every 15 to 20 hours, so culture on Lowenstein-Jensen medium takes several weeks. Its defining feature is a thick, waxy cell wall in which long-chain mycolic acids are linked to arabinogalactan and peptidoglycan. This lipid-rich wall stains poorly with the Gram stain, resists drying and detergents, and slows the entry of nutrients and many antibiotics."},{"type":"p","text":"In the Ziehl-Neelsen stain, heated carbolfuchsin penetrates the waxy wall, and acid alcohol then fails to remove it, so the bacilli stay red against a blue counterstain: they are acid-fast. Fluorescent auramine-rhodamine staining relies on the same wall lipids."},{"type":"table","title":"Cell wall components and what they do","cols":["Component","Action","Consequence"],"rows":[["Mycolic acids","Form a waxy, hydrophobic outer layer","Acid-fast staining; resistance to drying, disinfectants, and many drugs"],["Cord factor (trehalose dimycolate)","Activates macrophages to release TNF-alpha; toxic to host cell mitochondria; inhibits neutrophil migration","Granuloma formation; virulent strains grow in serpentine cords in culture"],["Sulfatides","Sulfated glycolipids that inhibit phagosome and lysosome fusion","Bacilli survive inside macrophages"],["Lipoarabinomannan","Arrests phagosome maturation and dampens macrophage activation","Adds to intracellular survival"]],"caption":"The wall lipids explain the staining, the intracellular survival, and much of the inflammation."},{"type":"h","text":"Transmission and intracellular survival"},{"type":"p","text":"A person with active pulmonary disease, especially with cavities, expels respiratory droplets when coughing or talking. As they dry they become droplet nuclei about 1 to 5 um across, which stay suspended in room air for hours and are small enough to pass the mucociliary escalator and reach the alveoli. A few bacilli are enough to start infection. Larger droplets are trapped in the upper airway and cleared, which is why crowded, poorly ventilated spaces matter more than brief contact."},{"type":"p","text":"Alveolar macrophages ingest the bacilli, but the organism arrests phagosome maturation: the phagosome excludes the proton pump, stays near neutral pH, and does not fuse with lysosomes. The bacilli multiply inside the macrophage and spread through lymphatics and blood before adaptive immunity develops. After about 3 weeks, Th1 cells release IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages to complete phagolysosome fusion, produce nitric oxide, and kill the bacilli. TNF-alpha recruits cells and holds the granuloma together."},{"type":"flow","title":"Primary tuberculosis infection","steps":["Droplet nuclei carry bacilli to alveoli","Macrophages ingest bacilli; phagolysosome fusion is blocked","Bacilli multiply and spread to hilar nodes","Dendritic cells and macrophages release IL-12; Th1 cells develop","IFN-gamma activates macrophages to kill bacilli","Caseating granulomas form: Ghon focus plus hilar nodes (Ghon complex)","Most lesions heal, fibrose, and calcify, leaving latent infection"],"caption":"The same Th1 response makes the tuberculin skin test positive, a type IV hypersensitivity reaction."},{"type":"p","text":"The primary (Ghon) focus is a small subpleural lesion in the lower part of an upper lobe or the upper part of a lower lobe, the well-ventilated zones where inhaled particles land. Reactivation occurs when cell-mediated immunity weakens, as in HIV infection, malnutrition, older age, or glucocorticoid use, and it favors the apical posterior segments of the upper lobes. Their high ventilation-perfusion ratio gives the highest alveolar PO2, which suits an obligate aerobe. Caseous necrosis liquefies, drains into a bronchus, and leaves a cavity rich in bacilli; erosion of vessels causes hemoptysis. Fever, night sweats, and weight loss reflect TNF-alpha and IL-1."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with rheumatoid arthritis and a positive tuberculin test years ago starts a drug that neutralizes TNF-alpha. What might happen in the lungs, and why?","explain":"TNF-alpha maintains the granulomas that contain latent bacilli; without it they break down and latent infection reactivates, often as disseminated disease.","check":"Compare with the paragraphs on intracellular survival and the primary infection flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-13-01-C05","obj":"PU-13.5","title":"Influenza, respiratory syncytial virus, and coronaviruses","blocks":[{"type":"table","title":"Three respiratory viruses compared","cols":["Feature","Influenza","Respiratory syncytial virus (RSV)","Coronaviruses"],"rows":[["Family","Orthomyxoviridae","Pneumoviridae (formerly a paramyxovirus)","Coronaviridae"],["Genome","Segmented (8 segments in A and B) negative-sense ssRNA","Nonsegmented negative-sense ssRNA","Nonsegmented positive-sense ssRNA, about 30 kb"],["Attachment","Hemagglutinin binds sialic acid","G glycoprotein (no hemagglutinin or neuraminidase activity)","Spike; SARS-CoV-2 binds ACE2"],["Fusion","Hemagglutinin, at low endosomal pH","F protein, at the cell surface; forms syncytia","Spike, after cleavage by host proteases"],["Variation","Drift (A and B); shift (A only)","Incomplete immunity; reinfection common","New strains emerge from animal reservoirs"],["Typical disease","Abrupt fever, myalgia, cough; secondary bacterial pneumonia","Infant bronchiolitis; severe disease in older adults","Common colds; SARS, MERS, COVID-19 pneumonia"]],"caption":"All three are enveloped. Negative-sense viruses must carry their own RNA-dependent RNA polymerase; a positive-sense genome is translated directly."},{"type":"h","text":"Influenza"},{"type":"p","text":"Hemagglutinin binds sialic acid on respiratory epithelium; after endocytosis, endosomal acid changes its shape so it fuses the envelope with the endosome membrane. Hemagglutinin must first be cleaved by host proteases, which helps restrict infection to the respiratory tract. Unusually for an RNA virus, influenza replicates in the nucleus, where its polymerase takes 5' caps from host pre-mRNA to prime viral mRNA. Neuraminidase cleaves sialic acid to release new virions. Antibody to hemagglutinin neutralizes infection."},{"type":"p","text":"The viral polymerase has no proofreading, so point mutations accumulate in the hemagglutinin and neuraminidase genes. This antigenic drift allows partial escape from existing antibody and causes yearly epidemics of influenza A and B. Influenza A also infects birds and pigs, and when two strains infect one cell their segments can reassort. Antigenic shift introduces a hemagglutinin (or neuraminidase) subtype against which the population has little immunity, and pandemics follow."},{"type":"flow","title":"Antigenic shift in influenza A","steps":["Human and animal influenza A strains circulate separately","Both strains infect one host cell, often in a pig","Segments are packaged in new combinations (reassortment)","A virion carries a novel hemagglutinin subtype and human-adapted genes","Little population immunity: rapid worldwide spread (pandemic)"],"caption":"Shift requires a segmented genome and animal reservoirs."},{"type":"p","text":"Influenza kills ciliated tracheobronchial epithelium and strips away mucociliary clearance, so upper airway bacteria (S. aureus, S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae) can cause secondary pneumonia, often after initial improvement."},{"type":"h","text":"RSV"},{"type":"p","text":"RSV is the most common cause of bronchiolitis and viral pneumonia in infants. Its F protein, displayed on infected cells, fuses them with their neighbors to form syncytia. Necrotic epithelium, mucus, and edema plug the bronchioles. Because resistance varies inversely with the fourth power of the radius, slight narrowing of an infant's small bronchioles sharply raises resistance, causing wheezing, air trapping, and hypoxemia."},{"type":"h","text":"Coronaviruses"},{"type":"p","text":"Coronaviruses have among the largest RNA virus genomes and, unlike most RNA viruses, a proofreading exonuclease. The genome is translated directly into polyproteins that viral proteases cleave into the replication complex. Seasonal coronaviruses cause common colds. SARS-CoV-2 spike binds ACE2, and host proteases (furin and TMPRSS2) cleave spike to allow fusion; MERS coronavirus instead uses DPP4. ACE2 is present on nasal epithelium, type II pneumocytes, endothelium, and enterocytes. Severe COVID-19 causes diffuse alveolar damage with hyaline membranes, endothelial injury, and microvascular thrombosis. Loss of smell reflects infection of supporting cells of the olfactory epithelium."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: drift or shift?","text":"Outbreak 1: a winter influenza A epidemic in which most adults have some antibody, but this year's hemagglutinin differs from last year's by a few amino acids. Outbreak 2: a new hemagglutinin subtype appears in humans after a pig strain exchanges segments with a human strain, and illness spreads worldwide in all ages. Outbreak 1 is drift: point mutations from an error-prone polymerase allow partial escape, so prior immunity still blunts severity. Outbreak 2 is shift: reassortment brings in an antigen no one has met. Ask whether the change is gradual or abrupt and whether it needs exchanged segments. Only influenza A, with animal reservoirs, shifts; influenza B only drifts."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-13-01-C06","obj":"PU-13.6","title":"Dimorphic fungi","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The endemic dimorphic fungi grow in soil as molds at environmental temperatures and convert to a tissue form at body temperature, 37 °C (98.6 °F). People inhale conidia from disturbed soil, so geography and exposure history are the main clues. The tissue form is how the pathologist identifies each fungus."},{"type":"table","title":"Endemic dimorphic fungi","cols":["Fungus","Geography and source","Tissue form at 37 °C (98.6 °F)","Spread and clues"],"rows":[["Histoplasma capsulatum","Ohio and Mississippi River valleys; soil enriched by bird and bat droppings (caves, chicken coops, old buildings)","Small oval yeasts, about 2 to 4 um, inside macrophages","Calcified granulomas that mimic tuberculosis; dissemination with hepatosplenomegaly, oral ulcers, pancytopenia in AIDS"],["Blastomyces dermatitidis","Great Lakes and Ohio and Mississippi River basins; moist soil and decaying wood near waterways","Large yeasts, about 8 to 15 um, with a thick refractile wall and a single broad-based bud","Pneumonia; spread to skin (verrucous lesions), bone, and prostate; mixed suppurative and granulomatous inflammation"],["Coccidioides immitis and posadasii","Southwestern United States (Arizona, California's San Joaquin Valley) and northern Mexico; desert soil dust","Spherules, about 20 to 60 um or larger, filled with endospores (not a yeast)","Erythema nodosum and arthralgias; spread to skin, bone, and meninges"]],"caption":"All three are acquired by inhalation and are not spread person to person; most infections are mild or silent."},{"type":"p","text":"Defense against all three is cell-mediated. Alveolar macrophages ingest the inhaled conidia or yeasts, and Th1 cells release IFN-gamma that activates macrophages and organizes granulomas, much as in tuberculosis. The usual result is a self-limited pneumonia or silent infection that heals with calcified granulomas. Disseminated disease occurs mainly when T cell immunity is impaired, as in advanced HIV infection, transplantation, or glucocorticoid use."},{"type":"p","text":"Each fungus has a feature that shapes its disease. Histoplasma yeasts survive and multiply within macrophage phagosomes, so they travel with macrophages through the reticuloendothelial system to the liver, spleen, lymph nodes, and bone marrow. Blastomyces spreads mainly through the blood, most often to skin and bone. Coccidioides spherules are too large for macrophages to ingest; when a spherule ruptures it releases many endospores, each of which can form a new spherule and attracts neutrophils."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Coccidioides in soil and tissue","steps":["Mold in desert soil produces barrel-shaped arthroconidia","Wind and dust carry arthroconidia into the lungs","Each arthroconidium enlarges into a spherule at body temperature","Endospores develop inside the spherule","Spherule ruptures; each endospore forms a new spherule"],"caption":"Endospores amplify infection within tissue; arthroconidia form only in the environmental mold phase."},{"type":"p","text":"Erythema nodosum in coccidioidomycosis, and in some cases of histoplasmosis, is an immune reaction to fungal antigen rather than skin infection, and it marks a vigorous cell-mediated response. Coccidioides disseminates more often in pregnancy (especially the third trimester), in immunosuppression, and in people of Filipino or African ancestry."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-13-01-C07","obj":"PU-13.7","title":"Aspergillus and Pneumocystis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Aspergillus and Pneumocystis are opportunists: healthy people inhale them often without disease, and serious infection appears when a specific defense is missing. Knowing which defense controls each organism predicts which patient will be infected."},{"type":"table","title":"Opportunistic lung fungi compared","cols":["Feature","Aspergillus (mainly A. fumigatus)","Pneumocystis jirovecii"],"rows":[["Source","Conidia in soil, compost, decaying plants, and construction dust","Airborne spread between people; most children are exposed early"],["Morphology","Septate hyphae of uniform width with acute-angle (about 45 degrees) branching","Round or cup-shaped cysts on methenamine silver stain; also trophic forms"],["Culture","Grows readily","Cannot be grown in routine culture"],["Main defense","Macrophages kill conidia; neutrophils kill hyphae","CD4 T cells direct macrophage clearance"],["Host defect","Neutropenia (especially prolonged), high-dose glucocorticoids, chronic granulomatous disease, transplantation","HIV with CD4 count below 200 cells/mm3; glucocorticoids, chemotherapy, transplantation"],["Lung pathology","Hyphae invade vessels, causing thrombosis and hemorrhagic infarcts","Foamy, frothy intra-alveolar exudate; diffuse bilateral interstitial infiltrates"],["Other forms","Fungus ball (aspergilloma) in an old cavity; allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis in asthma and cystic fibrosis","Pneumothorax; rare extrapulmonary disease"]],"caption":"The host defect predicts the organism: a missing neutrophil suggests Aspergillus, a missing CD4 T cell suggests Pneumocystis."},{"type":"h","text":"Aspergillus"},{"type":"p","text":"Aspergillus conidia are about 2 to 3 um across and reach the alveoli. Alveolar macrophages normally kill them, and any conidia that germinate are killed as hyphae by neutrophils, which use NADPH oxidase products and granule contents. When neutrophils are absent or dysfunctional, as in prolonged neutropenia, chronic granulomatous disease (phagocytes lacking NADPH oxidase), or high-dose glucocorticoid use, hyphae grow freely and invade blood vessels. The resulting thrombosis and infarction cause nodules surrounded by hemorrhage, hemoptysis, and pleuritic pain, and the fungus can spread to the brain."},{"type":"p","text":"In tissue, Aspergillus hyphae are narrow, regularly septate, and branch at acute angles. Mucorales (such as Rhizopus and Mucor), which also invade vessels, have broad, ribbon-like hyphae with few or no septa that branch at wide angles, often near 90 degrees, and are linked to diabetic ketoacidosis. In people with normal immunity, Aspergillus can instead colonize an old tuberculous cavity as a fungus ball or, in asthma and cystic fibrosis, provoke an IgE-mediated and immune complex reaction (allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis)."},{"type":"h","text":"Pneumocystis"},{"type":"p","text":"Pneumocystis jirovecii, formerly called P. carinii (a name now kept for the rat species), is a fungus by ribosomal RNA and genome analysis, although its membrane contains cholesterol rather than ergosterol. CD4 T cells normally recruit and activate the macrophages that clear it. When the CD4 count falls below about 200 cells/mm3, organisms multiply while attached to type I pneumocytes, and alveoli fill with a foamy exudate of organisms, surfactant, and protein. This thickens the gas exchange barrier and fills alveoli, causing dry cough, dyspnea, and hypoxemia with a widened alveolar-arterial (A-a) oxygen gradient that worsens with exertion. Imaging shows bilateral interstitial or ground-glass infiltrates, and serum lactate dehydrogenase rises with lung cell injury. Silver stain of induced sputum or bronchoalveolar lavage fluid reveals the cysts."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two patients have fungal pneumonia: one is receiving chemotherapy with an absolute neutrophil count near zero, the other has untreated HIV and a CD4 count of 60 cells/mm3. Which organism fits each, and what would a biopsy or lavage show?","explain":"Neutropenia removes the hyphae-killing cell, so Aspergillus with septate, acute-angle hyphae invading vessels fits the first. Loss of CD4 T cells fits Pneumocystis, with silver-stained cysts in foamy alveolar exudate.","check":"Compare with the comparison table and the Aspergillus and Pneumocystis paragraphs."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Every pathogen in this chapter defeats a particular layer of lung defense, and the host who gets it is the host missing that layer. Capsules defeat complement and phagocytosis, so asplenia and antibody or complement defects favor S. pneumoniae and H. influenzae type b. Loss of mucociliary clearance from influenza, smoking, cystic fibrosis, or an endotracheal tube opens the lower airway to S. aureus, Pseudomonas, and other bacteria. Intracellular survivors (M. tuberculosis, Legionella, Histoplasma) require Th1 cells, IFN-gamma, and TNF-alpha, so they emerge with HIV, glucocorticoids, or TNF blockade, while neutropenia favors Aspergillus and CD4 depletion favors Pneumocystis. Biochemistry explains toxins (ADP-ribosylation of EF-2 or Gi) and culture needs (heme, NAD+, iron, cysteine), and pathology shows the host response as consolidation, granulomas, syncytia, or infarction."},{"type":"p","text":"These associations are probabilities, not rules. A patient with HIV can still get pneumococcal pneumonia, and a healthy traveler can develop histoplasmosis after a heavy exposure. Stains and colony traits support identification, but molecular tests and antigen assays now often confirm it, and some classic tests (such as the Legionella urine antigen) detect only part of a species. Board questions reward linking organism biology to host defect; real diagnosis integrates exposure, host, imaging, and laboratory evidence."}],"summary":["S. pneumoniae is a gram-positive lancet-shaped diplococcus, alpha-hemolytic, optochin sensitive, and bile soluble; its capsule resists complement and phagocytosis, so asplenia predisposes to sepsis.","Lobar pneumonia progresses through congestion, red hepatization, gray hepatization, and resolution, which explain rusty sputum, consolidation signs, and shunt hypoxemia.","H. influenzae needs factors X (hemin) and V (NAD+); Klebsiella has a thick capsule and follows aspiration in alcohol use; Pseudomonas is oxidase positive and colonizes cystic fibrosis airways and ventilator tubing; Legionella grows on charcoal yeast extract with iron and cysteine and comes from contaminated water.","Diphtheria toxin is phage-encoded and ADP-ribosylates EF-2, stopping protein synthesis, producing a pseudomembrane, myocarditis, and neuropathy.","Pertussis toxin ADP-ribosylates Gi so it cannot inhibit adenylyl cyclase; cAMP rises, causing lymphocytosis and phagocyte dysfunction, while ciliary damage drives the paroxysmal cough.","M. tuberculosis is acid-fast because of mycolic acids, spreads by droplet nuclei, survives in macrophages by blocking phagolysosome fusion, and is controlled by Th1 cells, IFN-gamma, and TNF-alpha within granulomas.","Influenza hemagglutinin binds sialic acid and neuraminidase releases virions; drift is point mutation, shift is segment reassortment in influenza A only.","RSV F protein forms syncytia and causes infant bronchiolitis; SARS-CoV-2 is an enveloped positive-sense RNA virus whose spike binds ACE2.","Histoplasma (small yeasts in macrophages), Blastomyces (broad-based budding yeast), and Coccidioides (spherules with endospores) are identified by geography and tissue form.","Aspergillus (septate acute-angle hyphae) invades vessels when neutrophils fail; Pneumocystis (silver-stained cysts) fills alveoli when CD4 counts fall below 200 cells/mm3."],"sources":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Bacteriology sections on streptococci, corynebacteria, Haemophilus and Bordetella, Pseudomonas, Legionella, and mycobacteria"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Virology sections on orthomyxoviruses, paramyxoviruses and pneumoviruses, and coronaviruses; Mycology section on systemic and opportunistic mycoses"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lung, pulmonary infections"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Immunity to Microbes"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Infectious Diseases"}]},"PU-14-A":{"unit":"PU-14-A","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-01","title":"Restrictive and Interstitial Lung Diseases","subject":"Respiratory System (RESP)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers diseases that keep the lungs from expanding fully: surfactant failure in the newborn, diffuse alveolar damage in ARDS, scarring in idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis and related interstitial pneumonias, mineral dust and occupational exposures, sarcoidosis, drug-induced fibrosis, and restriction caused by the chest wall or muscles rather than the lung. Board questions give an exposure, a histologic pattern, a chest film, or a set of pulmonary function and blood gas values and ask you to name the disease and explain the finding. If you can say where the problem sits (alveolar lining, interstitium, or chest bellows) and how that changes compliance, DLCO, and the A-a gradient, you can reason through most of them.","prerequisites":["Surfactant, surface tension, and the law of Laplace (PU-4)","Lung volumes, compliance, spirometry, and flow-volume loops","Alveolar gas equation, A-a gradient, and the five causes of hypoxemia (PU-7)","Diffusion, Fick law, and DLCO","Granulomatous inflammation and wound repair by fibroblasts (general pathology)"],"connections":[{"to":"Fetal circulation and lung development (PU-1, CV-1)","how":"Surfactant matures late in gestation and the ductus arteriosus closes in response to oxygen, so prematurity links neonatal respiratory distress to a patent ductus."},{"to":"Gas exchange (PU-7)","how":"Restrictive diseases are applied gas exchange: shunt in ARDS, diffusion limitation in fibrosis, and hypoventilation in chest wall and neuromuscular disease."},{"to":"Pulmonary vascular disease (PU-14-E)","how":"Chronic hypoxic vasoconstriction and loss of the capillary bed in fibrosis raise pulmonary artery pressure and lead to cor pulmonale."},{"to":"Immunology and calcium homeostasis (IM, EN)","how":"Sarcoidosis and berylliosis are Th1 granulomatous diseases, and granuloma macrophages make calcitriol outside PTH control."},{"to":"Pleural and neoplastic disease (PU-14-D)","how":"Asbestos causes pleural plaques, bronchogenic carcinoma, and mesothelioma as well as asbestosis."}],"terms":[{"term":"Restrictive lung disease","def":"A disorder that reduces total lung capacity (TLC below about 80% of predicted) by stiffening the lung or limiting the chest wall or muscles, with a normal or high FEV1/FVC ratio."},{"term":"Surfactant","def":"A phospholipid and protein mixture, chiefly dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine, secreted by type II pneumocytes that lowers alveolar surface tension."},{"term":"Hyaline membrane","def":"A pink, glassy layer of plasma proteins, fibrin, and necrotic epithelial debris lining alveolar ducts and alveoli after diffuse injury."},{"term":"Diffuse alveolar damage","def":"The histologic pattern of ARDS: injury to alveolar epithelium and capillary endothelium with protein-rich edema and hyaline membranes."},{"term":"Usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP)","def":"The histologic pattern of idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis: patchy subpleural and basal fibrosis of different ages with fibroblastic foci and honeycombing."},{"term":"Fibroblastic focus","def":"A small cluster of actively proliferating fibroblasts and myofibroblasts beneath injured epithelium, marking ongoing fibrosis."},{"term":"Honeycombing","def":"End-stage remodeling in which fibrosis destroys alveoli and leaves clustered, thick-walled cystic spaces lined by bronchiolar epithelium."},{"term":"Pneumoconiosis","def":"Lung disease caused by inhaled inorganic dust, such as coal, silica, asbestos, or beryllium, and the lung's reaction to it."},{"term":"Ferruginous body","def":"An asbestos fiber coated with iron-containing protein, golden brown and beaded, that marks asbestos exposure."},{"term":"Noncaseating granuloma","def":"A compact collection of epithelioid macrophages and multinucleated giant cells without central necrosis, typical of sarcoidosis and berylliosis."},{"term":"DLCO","def":"Diffusing capacity of the lung for carbon monoxide, a measure of how readily gas crosses the alveolar-capillary membrane, reflecting surface area, membrane thickness, and capillary blood volume."},{"term":"A-a gradient","def":"Alveolar PO2 calculated from the alveolar gas equation minus measured arterial PO2; about 5 to 15 mmHg in young adults on room air."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-01-C01","obj":"PU-14-A.1","title":"Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Surfactant is made by type II pneumocytes, which begin producing it late in the second trimester (often quoted as about 20 to 24 weeks of gestation); amounts usually become adequate at about 34 to 36 weeks. By the law of Laplace, the pressure tending to collapse an alveolus equals 2T/r, where T is surface tension and r is radius. Surfactant lowers T most in small alveoli, so alveoli of different sizes stay open together at end expiration. Without it, small alveoli collapse with every breath and the newborn must generate a large negative pressure to reopen them."},{"type":"p","text":"Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome (NRDS, hyaline membrane disease) is surfactant deficiency. Prematurity is the main cause. Maternal diabetes delays maturation because fetal hyperinsulinemia, a response to maternal hyperglycemia, opposes the glucocorticoid stimulation of surfactant synthesis. Cesarean delivery without labor removes the labor-associated surge in fetal cortisol and catecholamines that speeds surfactant release and lung fluid clearance. Male sex also raises risk."},{"type":"flow","title":"Surfactant deficiency to neonatal respiratory distress","steps":["Immature type II pneumocytes make too little surfactant","High surface tension collapses alveoli at end expiration","Widespread atelectasis lowers compliance and work of breathing rises","Perfused but collapsed alveoli act as shunt; hypoxemia","Repeated collapse injures epithelium; plasma proteins leak","Fibrin and dead cells form hyaline membranes"],"caption":"Atelectasis is the primary lesion; hyaline membranes are the consequence of the resulting epithelial and capillary injury."},{"type":"p","text":"Collapsed alveoli that remain perfused behave as an intrapulmonary right-to-left shunt, so hypoxemia responds poorly to added oxygen. Within minutes to hours of birth the infant shows tachypnea, expiratory grunting (closing the glottis to hold air in the lungs), nasal flaring, intercostal and subcostal retractions, and cyanosis. The chest film shows low lung volumes with a diffuse fine granular or ground-glass pattern and air bronchograms, because air-filled bronchi stand out against airless alveoli."},{"type":"h","text":"Predictable complications"},{"type":"p","text":"The ductus arteriosus normally closes because arterial PO2 rises and prostaglandin E2 falls after birth. In NRDS, hypoxemia and acidosis keep the ductus open, and the premature ductus is also less responsive to oxygen, so a patent ductus arteriosus is common. As pulmonary resistance falls, blood shunts left to right through it and floods the stiff lungs."},{"type":"p","text":"The supplemental oxygen and positive-pressure ventilation needed to support these infants cause their own injury. Bronchopulmonary dysplasia is chronic lung disease of the premature infant, with arrested alveolar and vascular development (fewer, larger alveoli) from oxygen free radicals, volume and pressure injury, and inflammation. Retinopathy of prematurity follows a two-step mechanism: high arterial PO2 suppresses vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF) and halts normal retinal vessel growth, then the avascular retina becomes hypoxic once oxygen is lowered, VEGF surges, and disorganized new vessels grow that can bleed, scar, and detach the retina."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does raising inspired oxygen improve PaO2 only a little in a newborn with severe NRDS?","explain":"The main cause of hypoxemia is shunt through perfused alveoli that are collapsed; blood passing them never contacts the added oxygen, and saturated blood from open alveoli cannot carry extra oxygen to compensate.","check":"The paragraph on shunt and the flow figure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-01-C02","obj":"PU-14-A.2","title":"Acute respiratory distress syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) is noncardiogenic pulmonary edema caused by diffuse injury to the alveolar-capillary barrier. The barrier can be injured from the airway side (pneumonia, aspiration of gastric contents, inhalation injury, near drowning) or from the blood side (sepsis, which with pneumonia is among the most common causes, severe trauma with shock or multiple transfusions, and acute pancreatitis, in which circulating enzymes and cytokines injure capillaries)."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of ARDS","steps":["Direct or systemic insult activates alveolar macrophages and endothelium","IL-1, TNF, and IL-8 recruit and activate neutrophils","Neutrophil proteases and oxygen radicals damage endothelium and type I pneumocytes","Protein-rich fluid floods the interstitium and alveoli","Edema fluid inactivates surfactant; alveoli collapse","Hyaline membranes line alveolar ducts","Shunt and stiff lungs cause refractory hypoxemia"],"caption":"Neutrophils are the main effector cells; the leak is caused by barrier injury, not by high capillary pressure."},{"type":"p","text":"The histologic pattern is diffuse alveolar damage. In the first week (exudative phase) the alveolar walls are edematous and hyaline membranes line the alveolar ducts. Over the next one to three weeks (proliferative or organizing phase) type II pneumocytes proliferate to reline the alveoli and fibroblasts organize the exudate; some patients progress to interstitial fibrosis. Because the fluid is rich in protein from barrier failure, pulmonary capillary wedge pressure is usually normal, which separates ARDS from cardiogenic edema, in which high left atrial pressure (wedge pressure above about 18 mmHg) pushes a protein-poor transudate across an intact barrier."},{"type":"p","text":"Flooded and collapsed alveoli remain perfused, so the dominant gas exchange defect is shunt, and hypoxemia responds poorly to oxygen. Compliance falls because fewer alveoli are open to receive each breath, and the chest film shows bilateral patchy or diffuse opacities. Inactivated surfactant adds atelectasis, and the stiff lungs force rapid, shallow breathing. Severity is graded by the ratio of arterial PO2 to the inspired oxygen fraction (PaO2/FiO2), normally above about 400 to 500."},{"type":"table","title":"Consensus (Berlin) criteria for ARDS","cols":["Feature","Requirement"],"rows":[["Timing","Within 1 week of a known insult or new or worsening respiratory symptoms"],["Imaging","Bilateral opacities not fully explained by effusion, collapse, or nodules"],["Origin of edema","Not fully explained by heart failure or fluid overload"],["Oxygenation (with PEEP or CPAP of at least 5 cm H2O)","Mild: PaO2/FiO2 over 200 to 300; moderate: over 100 to 200; severe: 100 or less"]],"caption":"A PaO2/FiO2 ratio of 300 or less defines the oxygenation threshold; the older term acute lung injury has been retired."},{"type":"example","title":"Grading oxygenation in ARDS","text":"A patient with septic shock has bilateral infiltrates and a normal echocardiogram. On FiO2 0.60 with PEEP of 10 cm H2O, PaO2 is 72 mmHg. PaO2/FiO2 = 72 / 0.60 = 120, which is moderate ARDS (over 100 to 200). Compare a healthy person on room air: 95 / 0.21 is about 450. The ratio corrects for the oxygen being given, which is why a PaO2 that looks acceptable can still mean severe gas exchange failure. Raising FiO2 to 1.0 raises PaO2 relatively little here because the defect is shunt."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-01-C03","obj":"PU-14-A.3","title":"Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis and the usual interstitial pneumonia pattern","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF) is a progressive fibrosing disease of unknown cause in older adults, usually over 60, more often men and smokers or former smokers. The current model is repeated microscopic injury to alveolar epithelium followed by abnormal repair in a susceptible lung. Susceptibility includes a common promoter variant of the mucin gene MUC5B and mutations that shorten telomeres (telomerase genes TERT and TERC), which impair renewal of type II pneumocytes."},{"type":"flow","title":"Aberrant repair in IPF","steps":["Repeated injury to alveolar epithelium (smoking, reflux, environmental)","Type II pneumocytes fail to restore the epithelium","Injured epithelium releases TGF-beta","Fibroblasts become myofibroblasts in fibroblastic foci","Excess collagen stiffens and distorts the interstitium","Alveoli are destroyed and honeycomb cysts form"],"caption":"Fibrosis follows failed epithelial repair rather than a prominent inflammatory phase, so inflammation is mild in UIP."},{"type":"p","text":"Transforming growth factor beta (TGF-beta) is the central profibrotic signal: it drives fibroblast proliferation, conversion to contractile myofibroblasts, and collagen deposition while inhibiting matrix breakdown. The histologic pattern is usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP). Fibrosis is patchy and worst in the subpleural and basal lung. Its defining feature is temporal heterogeneity: dense old collagen, active fibroblastic foci, and normal lung lie side by side, because injury occurs at different times in different places. End-stage scarring creates honeycombing, seen on CT as clustered subpleural cysts at the bases."},{"type":"p","text":"Patients have slowly worsening exertional dyspnea and a dry cough, fine inspiratory crackles at both bases (often called velcro crackles, from stiff alveoli popping open), and digital clubbing. Complications include pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale from hypoxic vasoconstriction and loss of the capillary bed, acute exacerbations with superimposed diffuse alveolar damage, and a higher risk of lung cancer. The course is relentless, with median survival often quoted at about 3 to 5 years from diagnosis."},{"type":"table","title":"Pulmonary function in IPF and why each value changes","cols":["Measure","Change","Mechanism"],"rows":[["TLC, FVC, residual volume","Reduced","Increased elastic recoil limits expansion"],["FEV1","Reduced, in proportion to FVC","Smaller lungs exhale a smaller volume"],["FEV1/FVC","Normal or high (often above 0.8)","Recoil and radial traction hold airways open"],["Lung compliance","Reduced","Collagen stiffens the alveolar walls"],["DLCO","Reduced","Thick membrane and lost capillary surface"],["A-a gradient","Widened, worse with exercise","Diffusion limit plus V/Q mismatch"],["PaCO2","Normal or low","Hypoxemia and J receptors drive rapid shallow breathing"]],"caption":"The flow-volume loop is narrow and shifted toward low volumes, with flow that is high for the volume."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why is FEV1/FVC normal or even raised in IPF even though FEV1 is low?","explain":"Both FEV1 and FVC fall because the lungs are small, but stiff lungs recoil strongly and scar tissue pulls airways open, so a normal or larger fraction of the reduced volume leaves in the first second.","check":"The pulmonary function table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-01-C04","obj":"PU-14-A.4","title":"Pneumoconioses: coal, silica, asbestos, and beryllium","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Particles about 1 to 5 micrometers across reach the respiratory bronchioles and alveoli, where alveolar macrophages engulf them. Disease depends on the dose retained and on what the particle does to the macrophage. Activated macrophages release cytokines (IL-1, TNF), oxidants, and fibrogenic growth factors (TGF-beta, PDGF), so the macrophage is the link between dust and fibrosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Coal dust is relatively inert. Anthracosis, carbon pigment in macrophages along lymphatics, is harmless and common in city dwellers. Simple coal workers pneumoconiosis forms coal macules and small nodules, mainly in the upper lobes, and causes little dysfunction. In a minority, nodules coalesce into progressive massive fibrosis, large scarred masses that impair function. Coal workers with rheumatoid arthritis may develop Caplan syndrome, large rheumatoid nodules in the lung."},{"type":"p","text":"Crystalline silica (sandblasting, quarrying, mining, foundries, stone cutting) is the most fibrogenic common dust. Engulfed silica damages phagolysosome membranes and activates the NLRP3 inflammasome, releasing IL-1 beta and killing the macrophage; the freed particle is taken up again and the cycle continues. Concentric, whorled hyalinized collagen nodules form in the upper lobes and hilar nodes, where calcification at the rim gives eggshell calcification on the chest film. Damaged macrophages kill mycobacteria poorly, so silicosis raises tuberculosis risk, and silica is a recognized lung carcinogen."},{"type":"p","text":"Asbestos (shipbuilding, insulation, roofing, brake linings, demolition) is fibrous. Long, thin amphibole fibers resist clearance and reach the lower lobes and pleura. Macrophages cannot fully engulf them and keep releasing oxidants and cytokines, so fibrosis begins around respiratory bronchioles in the lower lobes and spreads; iron and protein coat the fibers to form ferruginous bodies. Asbestos also causes calcified parietal pleural plaques (the most common lesion and a marker of exposure), bronchogenic carcinoma (the most common asbestos-related cancer, risk multiplied by smoking), and malignant mesothelioma, decades after exposure."},{"type":"p","text":"Beryllium (aerospace, nuclear, and electronics industries) acts differently: it is presented as an antigen, and in genetically susceptible people (certain HLA-DP alleles) it drives a Th1, type IV hypersensitivity reaction with noncaseating granulomas that resemble sarcoidosis. Because disease depends on sensitization, it can follow small exposures and is confirmed by a beryllium lymphocyte proliferation test."},{"type":"table","title":"Comparing the pneumoconioses","cols":["Dust","Typical exposure","Lung zone","Hallmark pathology","Key associations"],"rows":[["Coal","Coal mining","Upper lobes","Coal macules; progressive massive fibrosis","Caplan syndrome with rheumatoid arthritis"],["Silica","Sandblasting, quarrying, foundries","Upper lobes, hilar nodes","Whorled collagen nodules; eggshell calcified hilar nodes","Tuberculosis; lung cancer"],["Asbestos","Shipyards, insulation, roofing, plumbing","Lower lobes, pleura","Interstitial fibrosis; ferruginous bodies","Pleural plaques; bronchogenic carcinoma; mesothelioma"],["Beryllium","Aerospace, electronics, nuclear","Variable","Noncaseating granulomas","Type IV sensitization; mimics sarcoidosis"]],"caption":"Coal and silica favor the upper lobes; asbestos favors the lower lobes and pleura; beryllium causes a granulomatous immune disease rather than a dust-burden fibrosis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-01-C05","obj":"PU-14-A.5","title":"Sarcoidosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Sarcoidosis is a multisystem disease of noncaseating granulomas caused by an exaggerated CD4 Th1 response to an unidentified antigen in a genetically susceptible host. Helper T cells release IL-2 and interferon gamma, which activate macrophages into epithelioid cells and giant cells, and TNF sustains the granulomas. The intrathoracic lymph nodes or lungs are involved in about 90% of patients. Granulomas may contain asteroid bodies or laminated Schaumann bodies, but these are not specific, and sarcoidosis remains a diagnosis of exclusion: infection with mycobacteria or fungi, which usually causes caseating granulomas, and berylliosis must be ruled out."},{"type":"p","text":"In the United States it is most common in Black women, with onset usually between 20 and 40 years, and Black patients tend to have more severe, chronic disease. The classic finding is bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy on chest film, with or without reticulonodular interstitial disease in the upper and middle zones. Many patients have no symptoms and are found on a routine film; others have cough, dyspnea, fatigue, and fever. Lung involvement gives a restrictive pattern with reduced DLCO, though granulomas around airways can add obstruction."},{"type":"p","text":"Two laboratory findings come directly from granuloma macrophages. They produce angiotensin-converting enzyme, so serum ACE is often elevated, roughly in proportion to granuloma burden; it is neither sensitive nor specific enough to diagnose the disease. They also express 1-alpha-hydroxylase, which in the kidney is tightly controlled by PTH, phosphate, and calcitriol itself, but in macrophages is driven by interferon gamma and escapes that feedback. Other findings include polyclonal hypergammaglobulinemia and cutaneous anergy, because circulating T cells are sequestered in granulomas."},{"type":"flow","title":"Granuloma calcitriol and hypercalcemia","steps":["Interferon gamma activates granuloma macrophages","Macrophage 1-alpha-hydroxylase converts 25-hydroxyvitamin D to calcitriol","PTH and calcium do not suppress this enzyme","Calcitriol raises intestinal calcium absorption and bone resorption","Hypercalciuria, then hypercalcemia; kidney stones and nephrocalcinosis","Parathyroid glands sense high calcium; PTH falls"],"caption":"High calcium with a low PTH and high calcitriol points to an extrarenal source of calcitriol, as in sarcoidosis and some lymphomas."},{"type":"p","text":"Extrapulmonary disease follows the location of granulomas: anterior uveitis and other eye disease, erythema nodosum and lupus pernio (violaceous plaques on the nose and cheeks), parotid enlargement, facial nerve palsy, and cardiac sarcoidosis, which causes heart block, ventricular arrhythmias, and sudden death. Lofgren syndrome is an acute presentation with erythema nodosum, bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, arthritis (often periarticular ankle inflammation), and fever. It usually resolves on its own and carries an excellent prognosis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-01-C06","obj":"PU-14-A.6","title":"Intrinsic versus extrapulmonary restriction","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Restriction means total lung capacity is reduced; the FEV1/FVC ratio is normal or high because the airways are not narrowed. The defect can lie in the lung itself or in the bellows that expands it. Intrinsic (parenchymal) restriction comes from scar, inflammation, or fluid in the alveolar walls and spaces: IPF, pneumoconioses, sarcoidosis, ARDS, and drug-induced fibrosis. Extrapulmonary restriction leaves the lung normal but stops it from inflating: obesity, kyphoscoliosis, large pleural effusions or pleural thickening, and weakness of the respiratory muscles (amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, myasthenia gravis, Guillain-Barre syndrome, high cervical cord injury, diaphragm paralysis)."},{"type":"p","text":"DLCO and the A-a gradient separate the two groups. In intrinsic disease the membrane is thick and capillaries are lost, so DLCO falls and the A-a gradient widens. In extrapulmonary disease the membrane is normal, so DLCO is usually normal, and when DLCO is corrected for the smaller alveolar volume (KCO) it is often high. Hypoxemia in these patients comes mainly from hypoventilation: PaCO2 rises and PAO2 falls by the same mechanism, so PaO2 falls while the A-a gradient stays normal. Basal atelectasis in obesity or weakness can widen it modestly."},{"type":"table","title":"Intrinsic versus extrapulmonary restriction","cols":["Finding","Intrinsic (lung tissue)","Extrapulmonary (chest wall, muscle)"],"rows":[["TLC","Reduced","Reduced"],["FEV1/FVC","Normal or high","Normal or high"],["Lung compliance","Reduced","Normal (chest wall compliance reduced in obesity and kyphoscoliosis)"],["DLCO","Reduced","Usually normal; KCO often high"],["PaCO2","Normal or low until late","Often high (hypoventilation)"],["A-a gradient","Widened","Usually normal"],["Residual volume","Reduced","Often normal or high in muscle weakness (weak expiration)"]],"caption":"A low TLC with a low DLCO points to the lung tissue; a low TLC with a normal DLCO and high PaCO2 points to the chest bellows."},{"type":"p","text":"Obesity lowers chest wall compliance and pushes the diaphragm up, reducing expiratory reserve volume and functional residual capacity first. Obesity hypoventilation syndrome adds blunted ventilatory drive, with daytime PaCO2 above 45 mmHg in a person with a BMI of 30 or more. Weakness of the diaphragm causes a further fall in vital capacity when the patient lies supine, because the abdominal contents push the weak diaphragm upward."},{"type":"example","title":"Using the A-a gradient to locate restriction","text":"Both patients breathe room air at sea level (PIO2 about 150 mmHg). Patient 1 has Guillain-Barre syndrome: PaCO2 60, PaO2 66 mmHg. PAO2 = 150 minus 60/0.8 = 75, so the A-a gradient is 75 minus 66 = 9 mmHg, normal. Hypoventilation explains the hypoxemia. Patient 2 has IPF: PaCO2 32, PaO2 60 mmHg. PAO2 = 150 minus 32/0.8 = 110, so the gradient is 110 minus 60 = 50 mmHg, widened. The lung itself is failing to transfer oxygen, which fits a thick membrane with a low DLCO."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with kyphoscoliosis and one with sarcoidosis both have a TLC at 65% of predicted. Which test best separates them, and what result do you expect in each?","explain":"DLCO: it is usually normal in kyphoscoliosis, where the alveolar membrane is healthy, and reduced in parenchymal sarcoidosis, where granulomas and fibrosis thicken it and destroy capillaries.","check":"The comparison table and the paragraph on DLCO and the A-a gradient."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-01-C07","obj":"PU-14-A.7","title":"Desquamative, nonspecific, and organizing interstitial pneumonias","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The idiopathic interstitial pneumonias are classified by histologic pattern because the pattern predicts behavior. Three patterns must be separated from UIP (section C03), and each is more reversible because its dominant lesion is cellular or young rather than dense, established scar."},{"type":"p","text":"Desquamative interstitial pneumonia (DIP) occurs almost entirely in current or former smokers, usually in their 30s to 50s. The name is a historical mistake: the cells filling the alveoli were thought to be shed epithelial cells but are macrophages containing fine golden-brown smoking pigment. The change is uniform, with only mild interstitial thickening and fibrosis. It is related to respiratory bronchiolitis-associated interstitial lung disease, which shows the same pigmented macrophages confined to the bronchioles. Both usually improve after smoking stops."},{"type":"p","text":"Nonspecific interstitial pneumonia (NSIP) shows uniform interstitial inflammation, fibrosis, or both, all of the same age, without the patchy mix of old scar and fibroblastic foci that defines UIP. Uniformity implies a single, simultaneous injury, and NSIP is often the lung pattern of connective tissue disease (systemic sclerosis, polymyositis and dermatomyositis, rheumatoid arthritis), hypersensitivity pneumonitis, or drug toxicity. Patients are usually middle-aged, more often women, and the prognosis is better than in UIP, especially for the cellular form."},{"type":"p","text":"Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia (COP), formerly bronchiolitis obliterans organizing pneumonia, shows polypoid plugs of loose, pale fibroblastic and myxoid tissue (Masson bodies) filling bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli, while the lung architecture is preserved. These plugs are organizing exudate, young tissue that can be resorbed. Patients have a subacute illness over weeks with cough, dyspnea, fever, and malaise that resembles pneumonia unresponsive to antibiotics, and the film shows patchy, often migrating consolidations. The same organizing pattern occurs secondarily after infection, drugs, radiation, or connective tissue disease; it is called cryptogenic only when no cause is found."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing the interstitial pneumonia patterns","cols":["Pattern","Typical patient","Key histology","Course"],"rows":[["UIP (IPF)","Over 60, men, smokers","Patchy subpleural and basal fibrosis of different ages; fibroblastic foci; honeycombing","Progressive"],["NSIP","Middle-aged; connective tissue disease","Uniform interstitial inflammation and fibrosis of the same age","Better than UIP"],["DIP","Smokers, 30s to 50s","Pigmented macrophages filling alveoli; uniform, mild fibrosis","Improves after smoking stops"],["COP","Adults, subacute illness","Polypoid fibroblast plugs (Masson bodies) in airspaces; architecture kept","Often resolves"]],"caption":"Temporal heterogeneity with fibroblastic foci means UIP; uniform change means NSIP or DIP; plugs within airspaces mean organizing pneumonia."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-01-C08","obj":"PU-14-A.8","title":"Byssinosis, bagassosis, and silo-filler disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Organic dusts and toxic gases injure different levels of the respiratory tract, and the exposure history and timing point to the disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Byssinosis occurs in workers who process raw cotton, flax, or hemp. Contaminating bacterial endotoxin and other agents in the dust trigger airway inflammation and bronchoconstriction. The classic feature is chest tightness and dyspnea on the first day back at work after a weekend or holiday (Monday chest tightness), easing as the week goes on, a pattern attributed to tolerance with continued exposure. It is mainly an airway disease, and years of exposure can produce fixed airflow obstruction rather than fibrosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Bagassosis is a hypersensitivity pneumonitis (extrinsic allergic alveolitis) from moldy bagasse, the fiber left after sugarcane is crushed, contaminated with thermophilic actinomycetes. Inhaled antigen meets preformed antibody and sensitized T cells, causing type III immune complex and type IV cell-mediated injury centered on bronchioles and alveoli. Acute episodes bring fever, chills, cough, and dyspnea about 4 to 8 hours after exposure; histology shows poorly formed noncaseating granulomas and interstitial lymphocytes. It shares this mechanism with farmer's lung from moldy hay."},{"type":"p","text":"Silo-filler disease follows entry into a recently filled silo, where fermenting silage releases nitrogen dioxide, a heavy, poorly water-soluble gas. Because it dissolves poorly, it passes the upper airway and reaches the bronchioles and alveoli, where it forms nitric acid and oxidant radicals. Heavy exposure causes acute pulmonary edema within hours; survivors can develop bronchiolitis obliterans weeks later, with fibrous obliteration of small airways and progressive obstruction."},{"type":"table","title":"Occupational exposures beyond mineral dust","cols":["Disease","Exposure","Mechanism","Clue"],"rows":[["Byssinosis","Cotton, flax, hemp dust","Endotoxin and dust provoke bronchoconstriction","Chest tightness on the first workday of the week"],["Bagassosis","Moldy sugarcane fiber","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis (type III and IV)","Fever and dyspnea hours after exposure; poorly formed granulomas"],["Silo-filler disease","Nitrogen dioxide in a newly filled silo","Oxidant and acid injury to distal airways","Acute pulmonary edema; delayed bronchiolitis obliterans"]],"caption":"Organic antigen, endotoxin, and toxic gas each injure a different level of the airway."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-01-C09","obj":"PU-14-A.9","title":"Drug-induced pulmonary fibrosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Several drugs injure the lung parenchyma, and their effects must be separated from IPF because the cause is identifiable and the injury may stop progressing once exposure ends. The mechanisms are direct oxidant or cytotoxic injury to alveolar epithelium and endothelium, and immune-mediated hypersensitivity. Histology is varied and often not UIP: NSIP, organizing pneumonia, diffuse alveolar damage, or hypersensitivity patterns, which itself suggests a drug rather than IPF."},{"type":"table","title":"Drugs that cause interstitial lung disease","cols":["Drug","Mechanism of lung injury","Distinguishing features"],"rows":[["Bleomycin","Generates oxygen free radicals that break DNA; lung and skin have little bleomycin hydrolase to inactivate it","Dose related; worse with older age, renal impairment, high inspired oxygen, and chest radiation"],["Amiodarone","Iodinated, lipophilic drug accumulates in lung; direct toxicity and phospholipidosis","Foamy macrophages; related to cumulative dose; high-density opacities on CT from iodine"],["Busulfan","Alkylating cytotoxic injury to type II pneumocytes","Atypical type II pneumocytes; can appear months to years later"],["Methotrexate","Mainly hypersensitivity reaction","Subacute cough, fever, dyspnea; poorly formed granulomas; sometimes eosinophilia; not clearly dose related"],["Nitrofurantoin","Acute hypersensitivity or chronic fibrosis","Acute febrile reaction with eosinophilia, or slow fibrosis with long-term use"]],"caption":"A cytotoxic drug or antiarrhythmic in the history and a non-UIP pattern point away from IPF."},{"type":"p","text":"Bleomycin is the best example of mechanism. It forms a complex with iron and oxygen that produces free radicals and cleaves DNA. Most tissues contain bleomycin hydrolase, which inactivates the drug, but lung and skin contain little, so these are the sites of toxicity. Damaged endothelium and type I pneumocytes trigger the same TGF-beta driven fibrosis seen in IPF. High inspired oxygen is thought to supply more substrate for radical formation, which would explain why oxygen exposure worsens the injury."},{"type":"p","text":"Clues that point to a drug rather than IPF include the timeline (onset after a drug is started or a cumulative dose is reached), younger age, fever or peripheral eosinophilia, the histologic pattern, and stabilization when the drug is stopped. Radiation pneumonitis, which follows chest irradiation within weeks to months, causes a similar oxidant injury confined to the radiation field."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Each restrictive disease begins at a different structural level, and the level predicts the physiology. Surfactant failure in NRDS and barrier failure in ARDS fill or collapse perfused alveoli, so shunt dominates and oxygen helps little. Fibrosis in IPF, asbestosis, silicosis, and drug injury thickens the membrane, so DLCO falls and hypoxemia worsens with exercise as transit time shortens. Chest wall and muscle disease leave the membrane intact, so the problem is hypoventilation with a normal A-a gradient. Immunology adds the granulomatous diseases: sarcoidosis, berylliosis, and hypersensitivity pneumonitis share Th1-driven macrophage activation, and the same activated macrophages explain the ACE and calcitriol findings in sarcoidosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology and biochemistry unite the fibrosing diseases through one final pathway, TGF-beta driven fibroblast activation after epithelial injury, whether the trigger is unknown (IPF), mineral (silica, asbestos), oxidant (bleomycin, oxygen, nitrogen dioxide), or diffuse alveolar damage that fails to resolve. The pattern of the scar (patchy and of mixed age, uniform, or confined to airspaces) is the clue to cause and course. These distinctions have limits: patterns overlap, a single biopsy may miss patchy disease, and exposure history often decides between conditions that look alike under the microscope."}],"summary":["Restriction lowers TLC with a normal or high FEV1/FVC; a low DLCO places the problem in the lung tissue, a normal DLCO with high PaCO2 places it in the chest wall or muscles.","NRDS is surfactant deficiency (prematurity, maternal diabetes, cesarean without labor) causing atelectasis, shunt, and hyaline membranes; hypoxemia keeps the ductus open, and oxygen therapy contributes to bronchopulmonary dysplasia and retinopathy of prematurity.","ARDS is neutrophil-mediated injury to the alveolar-capillary barrier after sepsis, pneumonia, aspiration, trauma, or pancreatitis, with protein-rich edema, hyaline membranes, bilateral opacities, PaO2/FiO2 of 300 or less, and normal left heart pressures.","IPF is failed epithelial repair with TGF-beta driven fibrosis; UIP is patchy subpleural and basal scar of different ages with fibroblastic foci and honeycombing, causing clubbing, basal crackles, and low DLCO.","Coal and silica favor the upper lobes; silica kills macrophages, calcifies hilar nodes like eggshells, and raises tuberculosis risk; asbestos favors the lower lobes and pleura with ferruginous bodies, plaques, carcinoma, and mesothelioma.","Berylliosis and sarcoidosis both form noncaseating granulomas from Th1 responses; sarcoidosis gives bilateral hilar nodes, high ACE, and hypercalcemia from unregulated macrophage 1-alpha-hydroxylase with suppressed PTH.","Lofgren syndrome (erythema nodosum, hilar lymphadenopathy, arthritis, fever) is acute sarcoidosis with a good prognosis.","DIP is smokers with pigmented macrophages in alveoli; NSIP is uniform inflammation and fibrosis linked to connective tissue disease; COP is reversible fibroblast plugs (Masson bodies) in airspaces.","Byssinosis is cotton dust bronchoconstriction worst on the first workday; bagassosis is hypersensitivity pneumonitis from moldy sugarcane; silo-filler disease is nitrogen dioxide edema followed by bronchiolitis obliterans.","Bleomycin, amiodarone, busulfan, methotrexate, and nitrofurantoin cause drug-induced lung disease that is separated from IPF by history, timing, and a usually non-UIP pattern."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lung: acute lung injury and ARDS, chronic diffuse interstitial (restrictive) diseases, pneumoconioses, sarcoidosis, drug- and radiation-induced lung disease"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of Infancy and Childhood: neonatal respiratory distress syndrome"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pulmonary Ventilation (surfactant and surface tension); Respiratory Insufficiency: Pathophysiology, Diagnosis, Oxygen Therapy"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Mechanics of ventilation and gas exchange in the lungs"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Pulmonary Disease: restrictive lung diseases and acute respiratory distress syndrome"},{"book":"Abbas Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity reactions and granulomatous inflammation"}]},"PU-14-B":{"unit":"PU-14-B","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-02","title":"Obstructive Pulmonary Diseases","subject":"Respiratory System (RESP)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the four classic obstructive diseases: asthma, bronchiectasis, chronic bronchitis, and emphysema. In each, air has trouble leaving the lung, so the FEV1 falls more than the FVC and air is trapped behind narrowed or collapsing airways. The diseases differ in where the lesion lies and what drives it: reversible Th2 inflammation in asthma, destroyed bronchial walls in bronchiectasis, mucus hypersecretion and small airway disease in chronic bronchitis, and loss of alveolar walls in emphysema. Board questions give a sputum finding, a histologic feature, a spirometry pattern, a DLCO, or a blood gas and ask you to name the disease and explain the mechanism, so this chapter teaches you to reason from the lesion to the test result.","prerequisites":["Lung volumes, spirometry, and the flow-volume loop (PU-6)","Airway resistance, elastic recoil, and dynamic airway compression (PU-5)","Ventilation-perfusion matching, the alveolar gas equation, and DLCO (PU-7)","Type I hypersensitivity and Th2 cytokines (IM-15-C)","Interpretation of arterial blood gases and acid-base compensation"],"connections":[{"to":"Immune responses in pulmonary disease (PU-12)","how":"Th2 cytokines, IgE, mast cells, eosinophils, and neutrophil elastase are the effector mechanisms behind asthma and emphysema."},{"to":"Congenital pulmonary diseases (PU-14-G)","how":"Cystic fibrosis and primary ciliary dyskinesia are leading causes of bronchiectasis because both prevent mucus clearance."},{"to":"Diseases of amyloids (IM-15-E)","how":"Chronic suppurative bronchiectasis keeps serum amyloid A high and can cause systemic AA amyloidosis."},{"to":"Hepatic diseases and disorders (GI-13-C)","how":"In alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, misfolded protein retained in hepatocytes injures the liver while the low serum level exposes the lung to elastase."},{"to":"Pulmonary vascular disease (PU-14-E)","how":"Chronic alveolar hypoxia in COPD constricts and remodels pulmonary arterioles, causing pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale."}],"terms":[{"term":"Obstructive pattern","def":"A reduced FEV1/FVC ratio caused by increased airway resistance or lost elastic recoil, usually with a normal or increased total lung capacity."},{"term":"Airway hyperresponsiveness","def":"Exaggerated bronchoconstriction in response to stimuli that barely affect normal airways."},{"term":"Airway remodeling","def":"Structural change of chronically inflamed airways, including goblet cell hyperplasia, subepithelial fibrosis, gland enlargement, and smooth muscle hypertrophy."},{"term":"Curschmann spirals","def":"Whorled mucus plugs containing shed epithelial cells, found in the sputum of asthmatic patients."},{"term":"Charcot-Leyden crystals","def":"Slender bipyramidal crystals formed from the eosinophil protein galectin-10, marking eosinophilic inflammation."},{"term":"Pulsus paradoxus","def":"An exaggerated inspiratory fall in systolic blood pressure, greater than 10 mmHg."},{"term":"Reid index","def":"The thickness of the submucosal gland layer divided by the thickness of the bronchial wall between the epithelium and the cartilage."},{"term":"Acinus","def":"The gas-exchanging unit distal to a terminal bronchiole: respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, alveolar sacs, and alveoli."},{"term":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin","def":"A serine protease inhibitor made by hepatocytes that neutralizes neutrophil elastase in the lung."},{"term":"DLCO","def":"The diffusing capacity of the lung for carbon monoxide, which reflects the alveolar capillary surface area and the capillary blood volume available for gas transfer."},{"term":"Equal pressure point","def":"The point along an airway during forced expiration where pressure inside the airway equals pleural pressure; airways downstream of it can be compressed."},{"term":"Cor pulmonale","def":"Right ventricular hypertrophy, dilation, or failure caused by pulmonary hypertension that results from disease of the lungs or their vessels."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-02-C01","obj":"PU-14-B.1","title":"Asthma pathogenesis and airway pathology","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Asthma is chronic airway inflammation that produces airway hyperresponsiveness and recurrent episodes of obstruction that reverse, fully or largely, either spontaneously or with a bronchodilator. Three processes narrow the lumen together: smooth muscle contraction, mucosal edema, and mucus plugging. Because airway resistance varies inversely with the fourth power of the radius, a modest loss of caliber produces a large rise in resistance."},{"type":"p","text":"Atopic (allergic) asthma is the most common form. It usually begins in childhood, often with eczema or allergic rhinitis in the patient or family, and is a type I hypersensitivity. Dendritic cells carry inhaled allergen to lymph nodes and present it to naive CD4 T cells. Epithelial alarmins (thymic stromal lymphopoietin, IL-25, IL-33) and IL-4 steer these cells toward the Th2 lineage. IL-4 and IL-13 switch B cells to IgE, which binds high-affinity Fc epsilon receptors on mast cells; IL-5 drives eosinophil production, recruitment, and survival; and IL-13 stimulates goblet cell mucus production and hyperresponsiveness."},{"type":"flow","title":"Atopic asthma: early and late phase","steps":["Inhaled allergen cross-links IgE on sensitized mast cells","Mast cells release histamine, cysteinyl leukotrienes, and PGD2","Early phase within minutes: bronchospasm, edema, mucus","Mediators and Th2 cytokines recruit eosinophils","Late phase hours later: eosinophil proteins injure epithelium","Repeated injury and repair remodel the airway"],"caption":"The early phase is mast cell mediator release; the late phase is leukocyte inflammation that sustains hyperresponsiveness."},{"type":"p","text":"Nonatopic asthma shows no evidence of allergen sensitization, and serum IgE is often normal. The airways are still hyperresponsive, and attacks are set off by stimuli that do not act through IgE. These triggers also provoke attacks in atopic patients."},{"type":"table","title":"Nonatopic triggers and their mechanisms","cols":["Trigger","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Viral respiratory infection (rhinovirus, RSV)","Epithelial injury and inflammation increase hyperresponsiveness; the most common trigger of exacerbations"],["Exercise and cold, dry air","Heat and water loss make airway lining fluid hyperosmolar, so mast cells release mediators; bronchospasm typically peaks minutes after exercise ends"],["Aspirin and other COX-1 inhibitors","Blocking COX removes bronchoprotective PGE2 and diverts arachidonic acid to 5-lipoxygenase, overproducing cysteinyl leukotrienes; accompanied by chronic rhinosinusitis with nasal polyps"],["Smoke, pollutants, occupational agents","Direct epithelial irritation and reflex bronchoconstriction; some occupational agents also cause sensitization"]],"caption":"Different triggers converge on the same hyperresponsive, inflamed airway."},{"type":"p","text":"Repeated attacks leave a recognizable pathology. In fatal status asthmaticus the lungs are overinflated and the bronchi are occluded by thick, tenacious mucus plugs. The cysteinyl leukotrienes (C4, D4, and E4), released in both atopic and aspirin-exacerbated disease, are potent bronchoconstrictors that also increase vascular permeability and mucus secretion. Remodeling explains why long-standing asthma can develop a partly fixed component of obstruction."},{"type":"table","title":"Sputum and histologic findings in asthma","cols":["Finding","What it is and why it forms"],"rows":[["Curschmann spirals","Whorled mucus plugs containing shed epithelial cells, from hypersecretion and epithelial sloughing"],["Charcot-Leyden crystals","Crystals of galectin-10 released by eosinophils"],["Eosinophils in sputum and airway wall","IL-5 driven recruitment; eosinophil major basic protein damages epithelium"],["Goblet cell hyperplasia and submucosal gland enlargement","IL-13 driven mucus overproduction that forms plugs"],["Thickened subepithelial basement membrane","Collagen laid down beneath the true basement membrane by activated myofibroblasts"],["Smooth muscle hypertrophy and hyperplasia","More contractile mass, so each stimulus narrows the airway further"]],"caption":"Spirals and crystals are seen in sputum; the remaining features are seen in the airway wall."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-02-C02","obj":"PU-14-B.2","title":"Acute asthma: physical findings, spirometry, and the blood gas","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"During an attack, resistance rises most in expiration, when falling lung volume and rising pleural pressure narrow the airways further. Expiration lengthens, air forced through narrowed bronchi makes their walls oscillate and produces wheezes, and air trapped behind closing airways hyperinflates the chest, giving a hyperresonant percussion note. Breathing at high lung volume flattens and shortens the diaphragm, so the sternocleidomastoid and scalene muscles are recruited and the patient becomes tachypneic and uses accessory muscles."},{"type":"p","text":"In a severe attack, forceful inspiration makes pleural pressure strongly negative. Venous return to the right ventricle increases and the septum bulges toward the left ventricle, blood pools in the expanded pulmonary vessels, and the negative pressure around the left ventricle raises its afterload. Left ventricular stroke volume and systolic pressure therefore fall with each inspiration, and a fall greater than 10 mmHg is pulsus paradoxus. Inability to speak in full sentences and a quiet chest, where so little air moves that wheezing fades, also signal severe obstruction."},{"type":"p","text":"Spirometry shows an obstructive pattern: FEV1/FVC, FEV1, and peak expiratory flow fall. The defining feature is reversibility, conventionally a rise in FEV1 of at least 12% and 200 mL after an inhaled bronchodilator given as a diagnostic test. Between attacks spirometry may be normal, and hyperresponsiveness can then be shown by bronchoprovocation with methacholine. DLCO is normal or slightly increased because the alveolar membrane is intact."},{"type":"flow","title":"Evolution of the blood gas in acute asthma","steps":["Bronchospasm creates low V/Q units; PaO2 falls","Hypoxemia and airway receptors drive hyperventilation","Well ventilated units unload CO2: respiratory alkalosis","Air trapping raises the work of breathing","Respiratory muscles fatigue; alveolar ventilation falls","PaCO2 returns to normal, then rises: respiratory acidosis"],"caption":"A normal PaCO2 in a breathless, tachypneic asthmatic marks the turn toward respiratory failure."},{"type":"p","text":"Why does PaCO2 fall while PaO2 stays low? Hyperventilation of the better ventilated units removes extra CO2, because the CO2 content curve is nearly linear in the physiologic range. Those units cannot add extra O2, because their hemoglobin is already almost fully saturated, so they cannot offset the desaturated blood from low V/Q units. PaCO2 is inversely proportional to alveolar ventilation, so once fatigue reduces ventilation, PaCO2 climbs back through the normal range of 35 to 45 mmHg."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading serial gases in an asthma attack","text":"A 22-year-old woman arrives wheezing. First gas: pH 7.48, PaCO2 30 mmHg, PaO2 65 mmHg, an acute respiratory alkalosis with hypoxemia, which is expected because she is hyperventilating. Three hours later she is still wheezing, with a respiratory rate of 32, accessory muscle use, and fainter breath sounds. Second gas: pH 7.40, PaCO2 40 mmHg, PaO2 58 mmHg. The numbers look normal, but her drive to breathe is maximal, so a PaCO2 of 40 means alveolar ventilation is no longer keeping pace. Together with the worsening hypoxemia and quieter chest, this indicates muscle fatigue and impending respiratory failure, and the next gas would show a rising PaCO2 with acidemia."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-02-C03","obj":"PU-14-B.3","title":"Bronchiectasis: causes, pathology, and complications","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Bronchiectasis is permanent dilation of bronchi and bronchioles caused by destruction of the smooth muscle and elastic tissue in their walls. Two ingredients are required: obstruction or impaired clearance of secretions, and chronic necrotizing infection. Either can come first, and each sustains the other, which is why the disease progresses once established."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The vicious cycle of bronchiectasis","steps":["Obstruction or impaired clearance retains secretions","Bacteria colonize the pooled mucus","Neutrophils release elastase and other proteases","Muscle and elastic tissue of the wall are destroyed","Weakened bronchi dilate permanently","Dilated bronchi pool even more secretions"],"caption":"Infection and poor clearance feed each other, so dilation becomes irreversible."},{"type":"table","title":"Major causes of bronchiectasis","cols":["Cause","Mechanism","Distribution clue"],"rows":[["Cystic fibrosis","Autosomal recessive CFTR defect dehydrates airway surface liquid; thick mucus is colonized by Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Staphylococcus aureus","Diffuse, upper lobe predominant"],["Primary ciliary dyskinesia","Autosomal recessive axoneme defects, most often of the dynein arms, immobilize respiratory cilia, sperm flagella, and the embryonic nodal cilia that set left-right asymmetry; with situs inversus it is Kartagener syndrome","Diffuse, often middle and lower lobes; chronic sinusitis and otitis"],["Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis","IgE and IgG mediated reaction to Aspergillus fumigatus colonizing the airways of patients with asthma or cystic fibrosis; eosinophilia, very high IgE, mucus plugs","Central (proximal) bronchi, upper lobes"],["Tumor or foreign body","Localized obstruction traps secretions and infection beyond it","One segment or lobe"],["Severe necrotizing infection and immune deficiency","Necrotizing pneumonia (for example pertussis, measles, tuberculosis) or hypogammaglobulinemia allows repeated infection","Follows the infection; post-tuberculous disease favors the upper lobes"]],"caption":"Every cause either blocks an airway, defeats mucociliary clearance, or weakens host defense."},{"type":"p","text":"Grossly, the lower lobes are usually affected bilaterally, most severely in the more vertical airways. The bronchi may dilate to several times normal size and can be traced almost to the pleura. Histology shows intense acute and chronic inflammation with ulceration and desquamation of the mucosa, sometimes squamous metaplasia, and peribronchial fibrosis in long-standing disease. On CT the dilated bronchus is wider than its companion artery (signet ring sign) and its walls fail to taper (tram tracks)."},{"type":"p","text":"The manifestations follow from pooled, infected secretions. Patients have a daily cough with copious, often foul-smelling purulent sputum, worse with changes in position. Hemoptysis occurs because chronic inflammation enlarges the bronchial arteries, which carry systemic pressure, and erosion of these vessels can bleed heavily. Digital clubbing accompanies chronic suppuration, although its mechanism is not fully established. Persistent inflammation keeps IL-6 driven hepatic production of serum amyloid A high, and its fragments deposit as AA amyloid, often first appearing as proteinuria. Other complications include lung abscess, metastatic brain abscess, and, with extensive disease, pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale. Spirometry usually shows obstruction."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 30-year-old man has chronic sinusitis, bronchiectasis, dextrocardia, and infertility with immotile sperm. Which structure is defective, and why are the heart and the sperm affected?","explain":"The dynein arms of the microtubule axoneme. Without them, respiratory cilia cannot clear mucus, and sperm flagella cannot beat. Motile cilia at the embryonic node normally create a leftward flow that sets left-right asymmetry, so without it organ position is left to chance and about half of patients have situs inversus. Infertility in cystic fibrosis has a different cause: congenital absence of the vas deferens.","check":"The causes table and the cycle figure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-02-C04","obj":"PU-14-B.4","title":"Chronic bronchitis and the blue bloater","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Chronic bronchitis is defined clinically: a productive cough on most days for at least 3 months in each of 2 consecutive years, after other causes are excluded. Cigarette smoking is the dominant cause, with air pollution and occupational dusts contributing. Most smokers with COPD have both chronic bronchitis and emphysema in varying proportions."},{"type":"p","text":"In the large cartilaginous bronchi, irritants cause hypertrophy and hyperplasia of the submucosal mucous glands and an increase in goblet cells. The Reid index quantifies gland enlargement: the thickness of the gland layer divided by the thickness of the wall from the epithelium to the cartilage. It is normally below about 0.4 and exceeds 0.5 in chronic bronchitis. Smoke also impairs ciliary beating and causes squamous metaplasia, so mucus is overproduced and poorly cleared, which produces the chronic cough. Infection does not start the disease but maintains it and causes acute exacerbations."},{"type":"p","text":"Hypersecretion in large airways does not by itself limit airflow. Obstruction comes from small airway disease (chronic bronchiolitis) in airways less than 2 mm in diameter: goblet cell metaplasia, mucus plugging, inflammation, and peribronchiolar fibrosis narrow the lumen."},{"type":"p","text":"Narrowed and plugged small airways leave many alveoli poorly ventilated but still perfused. Blood leaving these low V/Q units is poorly oxygenated, so hypoxemia appears early. As obstruction and the work of breathing increase, and in patients whose ventilatory drive is blunted, alveolar ventilation falls and PaCO2 rises, producing a chronic respiratory acidosis that the kidneys compensate by retaining bicarbonate. Cyanosis appears when deoxygenated hemoglobin in capillary blood exceeds about 5 g/dL, and hypoxemia stimulates renal erythropoietin, so secondary polycythemia deepens the blue color and raises blood viscosity. Right heart failure produces the edema behind the name blue bloater."},{"type":"flow","title":"From low V/Q units to cor pulmonale","steps":["Plugged small airways lower alveolar PO2","Hypoxic vasoconstriction of pulmonary arterioles","Medial hypertrophy and fixed pulmonary hypertension","Right ventricular pressure overload and hypertrophy","Right ventricular failure: cor pulmonale","Raised jugular venous pressure, hepatomegaly, peripheral edema"],"caption":"Acidosis strengthens hypoxic vasoconstriction, and polycythemia adds viscosity, so both worsen the pressure load."},{"type":"stop","predict":"If alveolar PO2 in a patient with chronic bronchitis rose toward normal, what would happen to pulmonary arterial pressure early in the disease compared with after many years?","explain":"Early, pressure would fall substantially because hypoxic vasoconstriction is a reversible smooth muscle response. After years of hypoxia, medial hypertrophy and remodeling of the arterioles are structural, so pressure falls only partly.","check":"The cor pulmonale flow figure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-02-C05","obj":"PU-14-B.5","title":"Emphysema: patterns, alpha-1 antitrypsin, and hyperinflation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Emphysema is permanent enlargement of the airspaces distal to the terminal bronchiole with destruction of their walls and without obvious fibrosis. It results mainly from an imbalance between proteases and antiproteases, compounded by oxidant injury. When elastin is destroyed faster than it is repaired, alveolar walls and the capillaries within them are lost."},{"type":"flow","title":"Protease and antiprotease imbalance in smokers","steps":["Smoke activates alveolar macrophages and recruits neutrophils","Neutrophil elastase and macrophage metalloproteinases digest elastin","Oxidants inactivate alpha-1 antitrypsin","Alveolar walls and their capillaries are destroyed","Elastic recoil and airway tethering are lost","Small airways collapse in expiration: air trapping"],"caption":"Smoking adds protease and removes antiprotease at the same time."},{"type":"p","text":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin is a serine protease inhibitor made by hepatocytes, encoded by SERPINA1 on chromosome 14, and its alleles are expressed codominantly. The normal allele is PiM. In the PiZZ genotype serum levels are about 10 to 15% of normal, leaving the entire acinus exposed to elastase. The Z protein also misfolds and polymerizes in the hepatocyte endoplasmic reticulum, forming PAS-positive, diastase-resistant globules that can cause neonatal hepatitis and cirrhosis."},{"type":"table","title":"Patterns of emphysema","cols":["Pattern","Part of acinus destroyed","Location","Association"],"rows":[["Centriacinar (centrilobular)","Respiratory bronchioles in the center; distal alveoli spared","Upper lobes, apical segments","Cigarette smoking; the most common pattern"],["Panacinar (panlobular)","Entire acinus, from respiratory bronchiole to alveoli","Lower lobes and bases","Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency"],["Paraseptal (distal acinar)","Distal acinus next to pleura and septa","Subpleural, upper lobes, often near scars","Bullae that rupture: spontaneous pneumothorax in young adults"]],"caption":"Smoke injures the center of the acinus first; deficiency of the inhibitor leaves the whole acinus unprotected."},{"type":"p","text":"Loss of elastic recoil is the central physiologic defect. Recoil normally drives passive expiration, and the alveolar walls attached around small airways hold them open by radial traction. With both lost, the equal pressure point moves toward the alveoli during expiration, small airways collapse, and air is trapped. The lungs hyperinflate: the anteroposterior chest diameter increases (barrel chest), the diaphragms flatten, percussion is hyperresonant, and breath sounds are distant. Pursed-lip breathing raises pressure inside the airways during expiration, which moves the equal pressure point toward the mouth into cartilage-supported airways and slows exhalation, so less collapse occurs. Patients increase their ventilation to keep blood gases near normal, at a high work of breathing, so they are dyspneic, thin, and pink, the classic pink puffer."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 38-year-old nonsmoker has progressive dyspnea, hyperlucency at both lung bases, and elevated liver enzymes. Predict the emphysema pattern and explain why the liver is involved.","explain":"Panacinar emphysema from alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency (PiZZ). Too little inhibitor reaches the lung, so elastase destroys the whole acinus, most in the lower lobes. The liver is injured not by a lack of protein but by misfolded Z protein that polymerizes and accumulates in hepatocytes.","check":"The patterns table and the alpha-1 antitrypsin paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-02-C06","obj":"PU-14-B.6","title":"Testing in COPD: spirometry, lung volumes, DLCO, and blood gases","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) is persistent airflow limitation, usually from a mixture of small airway disease and emphysema. Spirometry establishes it: a post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC below 0.70. FEV1 falls more than FVC because narrowed or collapsing airways limit flow throughout forced expiration, and, unlike asthma, the obstruction does not reverse substantially after a bronchodilator. The expiratory limb of the flow-volume loop is scooped (concave). Severity is graded by FEV1 as a percentage of predicted (GOLD 1 to 4: at least 80%, 50 to 79%, 30 to 49%, below 30%)."},{"type":"p","text":"Air trapping raises residual volume, functional residual capacity, and the RV/TLC ratio. Total lung capacity rises mainly from the emphysema component, because lost elastic recoil increases compliance and lets the lung expand further for the same inspiratory effort; with predominant chronic bronchitis TLC is normal or only mildly increased. A reduced TLC defines restriction, so a normal or increased TLC alongside a low FEV1/FVC excludes a restrictive process."},{"type":"p","text":"DLCO depends on alveolar capillary surface area and capillary blood volume. Emphysema destroys alveolar walls together with their capillaries, so DLCO falls. In chronic bronchitis the membrane is intact, so DLCO is normal; in asthma it is normal or increased."},{"type":"p","text":"The blood gases follow V/Q logic. In emphysema ventilation and perfusion are lost together, so matching is relatively preserved, and increased respiratory drive keeps PaO2 near normal and PaCO2 normal or low until late disease, although desaturation with exercise appears earlier because of the reduced diffusing surface. In chronic bronchitis, perfused alveoli behind plugged airways form low V/Q units from the start, giving early hypoxemia and hypercapnia, polycythemia, and cor pulmonale. Most patients have features of both."},{"type":"table","title":"Testing in emphysema, chronic bronchitis, and asthma","cols":["Measure","Predominant emphysema","Predominant chronic bronchitis","Asthma (attack)"],"rows":[["FEV1/FVC","Below 0.70, little reversal","Below 0.70, little reversal","Reduced, reversible"],["TLC","Increased","Normal or mildly increased","Normal or increased"],["RV and FRC","Increased","Increased","Increased"],["Lung compliance","Increased","Near normal","Near normal"],["DLCO","Decreased","Normal","Normal or increased"],["PaO2","Near normal until late","Low early","Often low"],["PaCO2","Normal or low until late","High early","Low early; rising when tiring"]],"caption":"DLCO and the blood gas separate the two COPD phenotypes; reversibility separates asthma."},{"type":"example","title":"Interpreting pulmonary function tests in a smoker","text":"A 64-year-old former smoker has FEV1/FVC 0.55 after bronchodilator, FEV1 45% of predicted, TLC 125%, RV 180%, DLCO 40%, PaO2 76 mmHg, and PaCO2 38 mmHg. Step 1: a ratio below 0.70 that persists after bronchodilator means fixed obstruction, so this is COPD rather than asthma. Step 2: FEV1 of 45% places it in GOLD grade 3. Step 3: high TLC and RV show hyperinflation and air trapping, excluding restriction. Step 4: the low DLCO localizes the lesion to lost alveolar capillary surface. Step 5: near normal gases fit matched loss of ventilation and perfusion. The pattern is emphysema-predominant COPD. If instead DLCO were normal and PaCO2 52 mmHg with a high bicarbonate, the pattern would point to predominant chronic bronchitis."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and physiology explain where each disease acts and how it is measured. The airway tree narrows from cartilaginous bronchi to bronchioles less than 2 mm across that depend on surrounding alveolar walls to stay open, so disease of the large airways (gland hyperplasia, bronchiectasis) causes cough and sputum, while disease of the small airways and acini (bronchiolitis, emphysema) causes most of the airflow limitation. Spirometry, lung volumes, DLCO, and the blood gas each test a different part of this system, and reading them together, rather than any one alone, distinguishes the diseases."},{"type":"p","text":"Immunology, biochemistry, and pathology supply the mechanisms. Asthma is Th2 and IgE driven inflammation; bronchiectasis and emphysema are protease injury from neutrophils, with alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency linking a hepatocyte protein-folding defect to lung destruction; and chronic suppuration links to systemic AA amyloidosis. The clinical phenotypes are teaching models: most smokers have both emphysema and chronic bronchitis, some asthmatics develop fixed obstruction through remodeling, and bronchiectasis can coexist with COPD, so the test pattern describes the dominant process rather than a pure disease."}],"summary":["Asthma is Th2 inflammation: IL-4 and IL-13 drive IgE, IL-5 drives eosinophils, and IL-13 drives mucus; mast cells cause the early phase and eosinophils the late phase.","Nonatopic triggers (viral infection, exercise and cold air, aspirin, irritants) act on hyperresponsive airways without IgE; aspirin shifts arachidonic acid to leukotrienes.","Curschmann spirals and Charcot-Leyden crystals appear in asthmatic sputum; remodeling adds goblet cells, a thick subepithelial basement membrane, and smooth muscle hypertrophy.","Acute asthma causes wheeze, prolonged expiration, hyperinflation, pulsus paradoxus above 10 mmHg when severe, and obstruction that reverses with a bronchodilator.","Early asthma gases show hypoxemia with low PaCO2; a normal or rising PaCO2 in a tachypneic patient means fatigue and impending respiratory failure.","Bronchiectasis is permanent dilation from poor clearance or obstruction plus necrotizing infection, causing copious purulent sputum, hemoptysis, clubbing, and AA amyloidosis.","Chronic bronchitis is productive cough for 3 months in 2 consecutive years with a Reid index above 0.5; low V/Q units cause early hypoxemia, hypercapnia, and cor pulmonale.","Centriacinar emphysema is upper lobe and smoking related, panacinar is lower lobe and alpha-1 antitrypsin related, and paraseptal causes bullae and spontaneous pneumothorax.","Lost elastic recoil and airway tethering cause expiratory airway collapse, air trapping, barrel chest, and pursed-lip breathing.","COPD shows post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC below 0.70 with increased RV and TLC; DLCO is low in emphysema and normal in chronic bronchitis."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lung, obstructive lung diseases"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Respiratory insufficiency, pathophysiology and diagnosis"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Mechanics of ventilation, airway resistance and dynamic compression"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Pulmonary Disease, asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease"},{"book":"Abbas Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Allergy, immediate hypersensitivity and allergic asthma"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Fibrous proteins, elastin and alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency"}]},"PU-14-C":{"unit":"PU-14-C","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-03","title":"Diseases of the Upper Respiratory Tract","subject":"Respiratory System (RESP)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers infection and inflammation of the conducting airway from the nose to the large bronchi: acute bronchitis, epiglottitis, laryngitis, croup, bacterial tracheitis, rhinitis, sinusitis, pharyngitis, and tonsillitis. Most of these conditions are common and self-limited, so board questions focus on telling them apart and on predicting the few dangerous outcomes. The anatomy does most of the work: where the swelling sits explains the voice, the cough, the stridor, and the radiograph, and the venous and bony relations of the sinuses and tonsils explain how infection spreads. The microbiology and immunology explain the rest, from why rhinovirus stays in the nose to why a sore throat can damage a heart valve weeks later.","prerequisites":["Anatomy of the nose, paranasal sinuses, pharynx, and larynx (PU-3)","Airway resistance and the effect of airway radius on flow (PU-5)","Major respiratory pathogens: streptococci, Haemophilus, and respiratory viruses (PU-13)","Type I, II, and III hypersensitivity (IM-15-C)","Cranial nerves III to VI and the venous drainage of the face (NE-8)"],"connections":[{"to":"Infectious diseases of the pulmonary system (PU-14-H)","how":"Pertussis, pneumonia, and influenza continue the same infections into the lower airway, where alveolar filling adds consolidation."},{"to":"Obstructive pulmonary diseases (PU-14-B)","how":"Chronic bronchitis is defined by a persistent productive cough from sustained mucus hypersecretion, often with airflow obstruction, which acute bronchitis lacks."},{"to":"Diseases of hypersensitivity (IM-15-C)","how":"Allergic rhinitis is a type I reaction, rheumatic fever a type II reaction, and poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis a type III reaction."},{"to":"Valvular heart diseases (CV-18-D)","how":"Repeated rheumatic carditis after streptococcal pharyngitis is the classic cause of mitral stenosis."},{"to":"Glomerular diseases (UR-12-A)","how":"Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis is the prototype nephritic syndrome caused by immune complex deposition."}],"terms":[{"term":"Stridor","def":"A harsh, high-pitched sound made by turbulent airflow through a narrowed large airway, inspiratory when the narrowing is above the thoracic inlet."},{"term":"Supraglottis","def":"The part of the larynx above the vocal folds, including the epiglottis, aryepiglottic folds, and false cords."},{"term":"Subglottis","def":"The part of the larynx below the vocal folds, surrounded by the complete ring of the cricoid cartilage."},{"term":"Croup","def":"Viral laryngotracheobronchitis of young children, causing a barking cough and inspiratory stridor."},{"term":"Ostiomeatal complex","def":"The drainage pathway in the middle meatus through which the maxillary, frontal, and anterior ethmoid sinuses empty into the nose."},{"term":"Mucociliary clearance","def":"Movement of the mucus layer by beating cilia of the respiratory epithelium, which carries trapped particles and microbes out of the airways and sinuses."},{"term":"M protein","def":"The main surface virulence protein of Streptococcus pyogenes, which blocks complement opsonization and phagocytosis and defines the strain type."},{"term":"Molecular mimicry","def":"Cross-reaction of antibodies or T cells raised against a microbial antigen with a structurally similar host protein."},{"term":"Heterophile antibody","def":"An IgM antibody produced during Epstein-Barr virus infection that agglutinates sheep or horse red cells, detected by the Monospot test."},{"term":"Atypical lymphocyte","def":"A large reactive CD8 T cell with abundant cytoplasm seen in the blood in infectious mononucleosis."},{"term":"Trismus","def":"Restricted mouth opening caused by spasm or inflammation of the muscles of mastication."},{"term":"Angioinvasion","def":"Invasion of blood vessel walls by an organism, causing thrombosis and infarction of the tissue the vessel supplies."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-03-C01","obj":"PU-14-C.1","title":"Acute bronchitis and how it differs from pneumonia and chronic bronchitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Acute bronchitis is inflammation of the trachea and large bronchi that produces cough, with or without sputum, lasting up to about 3 weeks. About 90% or more of cases are viral: influenza, parainfluenza, respiratory syncytial virus, coronaviruses, adenovirus, and rhinovirus. A small minority are bacterial, mainly Bordetella pertussis, Mycoplasma pneumoniae, and Chlamydia pneumoniae (pertussis is taught in PU-14-H). Smoking and air pollution increase the risk and prolong the cough."},{"type":"p","text":"The viruses infect the ciliated columnar cells of the pseudostratified epithelium. Infected cells lose their cilia and slough, so mucociliary clearance fails while goblet cells and submucosal glands increase secretion. The denuded, inflamed mucosa exposes and sensitizes vagal irritant receptors and C fibers, so minor stimuli trigger cough. Regrowth of the epithelium from basal cells takes weeks, which is why the cough outlasts the fever and why transient airway hyperresponsiveness with mild wheeze is common. Sputum may turn yellow or green; the color comes from neutrophil myeloperoxidase, a green heme enzyme, and does not by itself mean bacterial infection."},{"type":"p","text":"The key distinction from pneumonia is location. Acute bronchitis stays in the conducting airways, so the alveoli remain filled with air. There is no consolidation on chest radiograph, no focal dullness, bronchial breath sounds, or egophony, and gas exchange is preserved. Pneumonia fills alveoli with exudate, which adds high fever, tachycardia, tachypnea, focal crackles, a new infiltrate, and often hypoxemia from right-to-left shunting through the filled units."},{"type":"p","text":"Chronic bronchitis is a separate disease, not a long episode of acute bronchitis. It is defined clinically as a productive cough for at least 3 months in each of 2 consecutive years, results from sustained irritant exposure (mostly tobacco smoke), and shows submucosal gland hyperplasia (Reid index above 0.5), goblet cell metaplasia, and, when small airways are involved, progressive airflow obstruction (PU-14-B). A viral bronchitis in someone with COPD is an acute exacerbation of that chronic disease."},{"type":"table","title":"Acute bronchitis, pneumonia, and chronic bronchitis","cols":["Feature","Acute bronchitis","Pneumonia","Chronic bronchitis"],"rows":[["Site","Trachea and large bronchi","Alveoli (with bronchioles)","Large and small airways"],["Usual cause","Respiratory viruses","Bacteria or viruses","Tobacco smoke, irritants"],["Time course","Cough up to about 3 weeks","Days, acute illness","At least 3 months in 2 consecutive years"],["Exam","Cough, scattered rhonchi or wheeze","Focal crackles, dullness, bronchial breath sounds","Chronic sputum, rhonchi, prolonged expiration"],["Chest radiograph","Clear","Consolidation or infiltrate","No consolidation; may show hyperinflation"],["Gas exchange","Preserved","Hypoxemia from shunt","Hypoxemia from low V/Q units in advanced disease"]],"caption":"The chest radiograph and focal exam findings separate acute bronchitis from pneumonia; the time course and chronic irritant exposure separate it from chronic bronchitis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-03-C02","obj":"PU-14-C.2","title":"Epiglottitis and the risk of sudden airway obstruction","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Epiglottitis (supraglottitis) is a bacterial cellulitis of the epiglottis, aryepiglottic folds, and arytenoid regions. The epiglottis is elastic cartilage covered by mucosa, and on its lingual surface and the aryepiglottic folds the submucosa is loose areolar tissue, so inflammatory edema accumulates quickly and the structure can swell to several times its normal size within hours."},{"type":"p","text":"The classic cause is Haemophilus influenzae type b (Hib) in an unvaccinated child about 2 to 7 years old. Hib is a gram-negative coccobacillus that needs factors X (hemin) and V (NAD) to grow. Its polyribosylribitol phosphate (PRP) capsule resists phagocytosis and complement-mediated killing, allowing bacteremia and invasive disease. Young children respond poorly to polysaccharide antigens, which are T cell independent, so they are especially vulnerable. Conjugate vaccines link PRP to a protein carrier, recruiting helper T cells and producing protective antibody in infancy, which is why epiglottitis is now rare in vaccinated children. Current cases occur more often in adults and may involve Streptococcus pyogenes, Streptococcus pneumoniae, Staphylococcus aureus, or nontypeable H. influenzae."},{"type":"p","text":"Each classic sign follows from supraglottic swelling. Swallowing over an inflamed epiglottis is very painful, so saliva pools and the child drools and refuses to swallow (dysphagia, odynophagia). The voice is muffled rather than hoarse, because the vocal folds themselves are spared. The child sits upright, leaning forward on the hands with the neck extended and chin forward (tripod posture), which pulls the tongue and epiglottis forward and opens the laryngeal inlet. Onset is sudden with high fever, often 39 to 40 °C (102.2 to 104 °F), and a toxic appearance; the barking cough of croup is usually absent. A lateral neck radiograph shows the thumb sign, a rounded, enlarged epiglottis."},{"type":"flow","title":"Epiglottitis to airway obstruction","steps":["Hib colonizing the nasopharynx invades supraglottic tissue","Cellulitis of loose supraglottic submucosa","Rapid edema enlarges epiglottis and aryepiglottic folds","Drooling, muffled voice, tripod posture","Swollen epiglottis falls over the laryngeal inlet","Sudden complete obstruction and respiratory arrest"],"caption":"Obstruction can happen within hours of onset; stridor is a late sign that the inlet is already critically narrowed."},{"type":"p","text":"The enlarged epiglottis is held clear of the laryngeal inlet mainly by posture. Lying supine lets it fall back over the glottis, and crying or pharyngeal stimulation can provoke laryngospasm, so a child who looked stable can obstruct abruptly."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a child with epiglottitis have a muffled voice without a barking cough, while a child with croup is hoarse and barks?","explain":"Epiglottitis swells tissue above the vocal folds and spares them, so voice is muffled by the mass in the pharynx; croup inflames the vocal folds and subglottis, so they vibrate abnormally and the narrowed subglottis produces the bark.","check":"Compare the epiglottitis signs paragraph with the croup paragraph in the next section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-03-C03","obj":"PU-14-C.3","title":"Acute laryngitis, croup, and bacterial tracheitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Acute laryngitis is inflammation of the laryngeal mucosa, usually from respiratory viruses (rhinovirus, parainfluenza, influenza, adenovirus) or vocal strain, less often from irritants or acid reflux. Edema in the superficial lamina propria of the vocal folds (Reinke space) adds mass and stiffness, so the folds vibrate irregularly and the voice becomes hoarse or is lost. In the wide adult larynx this swelling seldom narrows the airway enough to cause stridor, and the patient is not toxic."},{"type":"p","text":"Croup is viral laryngotracheobronchitis of children about 6 months to 3 years old, mostly in autumn and winter. Parainfluenza virus, an enveloped negative-sense RNA paramyxovirus with hemagglutinin-neuraminidase and fusion proteins, causes most cases, type 1 most often; respiratory syncytial virus, influenza, and adenovirus cause the rest. After 1 to 2 days of coryza, the child develops a barking cough, hoarseness, and inspiratory stridor, usually worse at night and with agitation, with low-grade fever and a nontoxic appearance."},{"type":"p","text":"The subglottis is surrounded by the cricoid, the only complete cartilage ring in the airway, so edema there cannot expand outward and narrows the lumen inward. Because resistance to laminar flow rises with the fourth power of a falling radius (Poiseuille law), a millimeter of edema matters far more in a small child. The stridor is inspiratory because this segment is extrathoracic: during inspiration, pressure inside the airway falls below atmospheric pressure and the narrowed segment collapses further. A frontal neck radiograph shows the steeple sign, a tapered subglottic narrowing."},{"type":"flow","title":"Croup: virus to steeple sign","steps":["Parainfluenza virus infects larynx, trachea, and bronchi","Inflammation causes subglottic mucosal edema","Rigid cricoid ring forces edema to narrow the lumen","Small fall in radius sharply raises resistance","Inspiratory collapse of extrathoracic segment: stridor","Barking cough, hoarseness, steeple sign on frontal film"],"caption":"The cricoid ring and the child's small airway radius explain why croup obstructs and why the obstruction is heard on inspiration."},{"type":"p","text":"Bacterial tracheitis is a secondary bacterial infection of the subglottic trachea, usually after viral injury to the epithelium (often influenza or parainfluenza). Staphylococcus aureus, including MRSA, is the most common cause; Streptococcus pyogenes, Streptococcus pneumoniae, Moraxella catarrhalis, and nontypeable H. influenzae also occur. It begins like croup but the child becomes toxic with high fever, and thick purulent secretions and sloughed pseudomembranes fill the trachea and can obstruct it. A lateral film may show irregular, ragged tracheal margins."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of hoarseness and stridor in children","cols":["Feature","Croup","Epiglottitis","Bacterial tracheitis"],"rows":[["Cause","Parainfluenza virus","Hib (unvaccinated); now also streptococci, S. aureus","S. aureus after a viral infection"],["Usual age","6 months to 3 years","About 2 to 7 years; adults now","Usually older than in croup"],["Onset","Gradual after coryza","Sudden, hours","Croup-like, then sudden worsening"],["Site","Subglottis","Supraglottis","Subglottic trachea"],["Cough and voice","Barking cough, hoarse","No bark, muffled voice","Brassy cough, hoarse"],["Drooling and posture","Absent","Drooling, tripod posture","Usually absent"],["Toxicity","Nontoxic, low fever","Toxic, high fever","Toxic, high fever"],["Radiograph","Steeple sign (frontal)","Thumb sign (lateral)","Ragged tracheal margins"]],"caption":"Acute laryngitis differs from all three: hoarseness alone in a nontoxic patient, without stridor."},{"type":"example","title":"Why 1 mm of edema obstructs a child","text":"Take an infant subglottis about 4 mm across (radius 2 mm) and, for comparison, an airway twice as wide, 8 mm across (radius 4 mm); a real adult subglottis is wider still. Add 1 mm of circumferential edema. The infant radius falls from 2 to 1 mm: cross-sectional area falls by 75%, and laminar resistance, proportional to 1 divided by radius to the fourth power, rises 2 to the fourth, or 16-fold. The wider radius falls from 4 to 3 mm: area falls by about 44%, and resistance rises (4/3) to the fourth, about 3-fold. Flow through the narrowed segment also becomes turbulent, heard as stridor, which raises resistance further. The same viral swelling that makes an adult hoarse can make a toddler struggle to breathe."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-03-C04","obj":"PU-14-C.4","title":"Allergic, infectious, and vasomotor rhinitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The nasal mucosa warms, humidifies, and filters inspired air. The turbinates contain venous sinusoids (erectile capacitance vessels) whose filling sets nasal patency. Sympathetic alpha-adrenergic tone constricts them and opens the airway, while parasympathetic fibers from the pterygopalatine ganglion drive glandular secretion and vasodilation. Rhinitis is inflammation or dysfunction of this mucosa causing congestion, rhinorrhea, sneezing, or itch, and the three main forms differ in what drives it."},{"type":"p","text":"Allergic rhinitis is a type I hypersensitivity. At sensitization, dendritic cells present allergen to naive T cells, Th2 cells release IL-4 and IL-13, and B cells switch to IgE, which binds the high-affinity Fc epsilon receptor I on mast cells. On re-exposure, allergen cross-links bound IgE. The early phase follows within minutes: histamine stimulates sensory nerves (itch and sneezing) and makes venules leak (clear rhinorrhea and swollen turbinates), and leukotrienes and prostaglandin D2 add congestion. The late phase follows hours later as IL-5 recruits eosinophils and Th2 cells sustain the inflammation, causing persistent congestion. Turbinates look pale, boggy, and bluish from submucosal edema; nasal smears show eosinophils and serum IgE may be raised. Allergic shiners (dark infraorbital venous congestion) and a transverse nasal crease from repeated upward rubbing are common in children. Disease is seasonal with pollens or perennial with dust mites, animal dander, cockroach, and molds, and often accompanies asthma and atopic dermatitis."},{"type":"flow","title":"Allergic rhinitis: sensitization to late phase","steps":["Allergen presented to Th2 cells","IL-4 and IL-13 drive B cell switch to IgE","IgE binds Fc epsilon RI on nasal mast cells","Re-exposure cross-links IgE; mast cells degranulate","Minutes: histamine causes itch, sneezing, leak, rhinorrhea","Hours: IL-5 recruits eosinophils; congestion persists"],"caption":"The early phase is mast cell mediated; the late phase is eosinophil and Th2 mediated."},{"type":"p","text":"Infectious rhinitis is mostly the common cold, and rhinovirus causes the largest share (roughly 30 to 50%). Rhinovirus is a small, nonenveloped, positive-sense RNA picornavirus. It is acid labile, so it does not survive the stomach and is not an enteric pathogen, unlike the related acid-stable enteroviruses. It replicates best at about 33 to 35 °C (91.4 to 95 °F), the temperature of the nasal passages. The major group of serotypes binds ICAM-1 (CD54) on nasal epithelium, and well over 100 serotypes explain repeated colds. The virus causes little cell death; symptoms come from host mediators such as bradykinin and IL-8, so the mucosa is red and swollen with neutrophils, and watery discharge turns mucopurulent."},{"type":"p","text":"Vasomotor rhinitis is nonallergic and noninfectious. Temperature change, strong odors, alcohol, or spicy food trigger congestion and watery rhinorrhea, thought to reflect autonomic imbalance with relative parasympathetic dominance and hyperreactive sensory nerves. IgE, skin tests, and nasal eosinophils are normal."},{"type":"table","title":"Three forms of rhinitis","cols":["Feature","Allergic","Infectious (common cold)","Vasomotor"],"rows":[["Mechanism","Type I hypersensitivity, IgE","Rhinovirus, other viruses","Autonomic and sensory nerve imbalance"],["Mucosa","Pale, boggy, bluish","Red, swollen","Congested, variable"],["Cells in secretions","Eosinophils","Neutrophils","Few, no eosinophils"],["Itch and sneezing","Prominent","Sneezing early","Little itch"],["Fever and sore throat","Absent","Low fever, sore throat possible","Absent"],["Timing","Seasonal or perennial with exposure","About 1 week, self-limited","Episodic with irritant triggers"]],"caption":"Eosinophils and pale mucosa point to allergy; neutrophils and red mucosa point to infection."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-03-C05","obj":"PU-14-C.5","title":"Sinusitis: ostial obstruction, organisms, and complications","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The paranasal sinuses are air spaces lined by ciliated respiratory epithelium, and they stay healthy only while cilia sweep mucus out through narrow ostia. The maxillary, frontal, and anterior ethmoid sinuses drain through the ostiomeatal complex in the middle meatus, the posterior ethmoid cells into the superior meatus, and the sphenoid into the sphenoethmoidal recess. The maxillary ostium lies high on the medial wall, so drainage depends on cilia rather than gravity. Only the maxillary and ethmoid sinuses are well formed at birth, so ethmoid disease is prominent in young children."},{"type":"p","text":"Sinusitis begins when mucosal swelling closes an ostium. Most episodes follow a viral cold; allergic rhinitis, polyps, and septal deviation also obstruct drainage. Behind the blocked ostium, secretions accumulate with low oxygen tension and pH, which impairs ciliary beating and neutrophil killing. Most acute rhinosinusitis stays viral; only a small fraction of colds (often quoted as about 0.5 to 2%) become bacterial, when organisms that colonize the nasopharynx overgrow in the trapped fluid: Streptococcus pneumoniae, nontypeable Haemophilus influenzae, and Moraxella catarrhalis. Bacterial infection is suggested by symptoms lasting more than about 10 days without improvement, a severe onset with high fever and purulent discharge, or worsening after initial improvement. Maxillary molar roots lie just below the sinus floor, so dental infection causes sinusitis with oral anaerobes, and poor clearance in cystic fibrosis and primary ciliary dyskinesia causes chronic sinusitis (more than 12 weeks)."},{"type":"p","text":"Complications follow the anatomy. The lamina papyracea, a paper-thin plate of ethmoid bone, separates the ethmoid cells from the orbit, so ethmoid infection reaches the orbit and causes orbital cellulitis: proptosis, painful and limited eye movement; preseptal cellulitis, in front of the orbital septum, swells the eyelid but spares eye movement. The ophthalmic veins, classically described as valveless, connect the face, orbit, and sinuses to the cavernous sinus, so ethmoid or sphenoid infection can cause septic cavernous sinus thrombosis. Cranial nerves III, IV, V1, and V2 run in its lateral wall and CN VI runs within it beside the internal carotid artery, producing ophthalmoplegia (often lateral gaze first), ptosis, forehead and cheek numbness, and chemosis, often becoming bilateral. Frontal sinusitis can cause frontal bone osteomyelitis, epidural or subdural abscess, meningitis, and brain abscess."},{"type":"flow","title":"Sinusitis to orbital and cavernous sinus spread","steps":["Viral cold swells mucosa and blocks sinus ostia","Trapped, hypoxic secretions allow bacterial overgrowth","Ethmoid infection crosses the thin lamina papyracea","Orbital cellulitis: proptosis, painful limited eye movement","Spread through ophthalmic veins to the cavernous sinus","Thrombosis: palsies of CN III, IV, VI; V1 and V2 numbness"],"caption":"Each step follows a bony or venous relation of the ethmoid sinus."},{"type":"p","text":"Mucormycosis is the classic sinus infection of diabetic ketoacidosis. Rhizopus and Mucor species form broad, ribbon-like hyphae with few or no septa that branch at wide angles, near 90 degrees. Acidosis releases iron from transferrin, which these fungi need for growth, while hyperglycemia and acidosis impair neutrophil chemotaxis and killing; neutropenia, high-dose glucocorticoids, and deferoxamine (used by the fungus as a siderophore) are other risks. The hyphae invade arteries, causing thrombosis and infarction, so black necrotic eschar appears on the turbinates or palate. Infection spreads into the orbit and brain (rhinocerebral mucormycosis) and can thrombose the cavernous sinus and internal carotid artery."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient in diabetic ketoacidosis develops facial swelling, a black lesion on the palate, and loss of eye movement on one side. What organism and what mechanism explain each finding?","explain":"Mucorales (Rhizopus or Mucor) thrive on iron freed by acidosis and evade impaired neutrophils; angioinvasion infarcts the palate (black eschar) and spreads through the orbit to the cavernous sinus, paralyzing CN III, IV, and VI.","check":"Compare with the mucormycosis and complications paragraphs."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-03-C06","obj":"PU-14-C.6","title":"Streptococcal and viral pharyngitis and the poststreptococcal sequelae","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Most pharyngitis is viral. Streptococcus pyogenes (group A streptococcus) causes roughly 15 to 30% of cases in children, peaking at ages 5 to 15, and fewer in adults. It is a gram-positive coccus in chains, catalase negative and beta-hemolytic, producing complete lysis through streptolysin O (oxygen labile and immunogenic) and streptolysin S (oxygen stable). It is bacitracin sensitive and PYR positive, and its Lancefield group A cell wall carbohydrate is the antigen detected by rapid tests. M protein is the main virulence factor: it binds the complement regulator factor H and fibrinogen, which limits C3b deposition and phagocytosis. A hyaluronic acid capsule that resembles host tissue adds resistance, and pyrogenic exotoxins act as superantigens in scarlet fever."},{"type":"p","text":"The findings reflect where the organism grows. Group A streptococcus infects the tonsils and pharynx but not the larynx or bronchi, so the illness is a sudden sore throat with fever, tonsillar exudate rich in neutrophils, palatal petechiae, and tender anterior cervical nodes (the jugulodigastric node drains the tonsil), without cough, coryza, hoarseness, or conjunctivitis. Respiratory viruses infect epithelium throughout the airway, which is why cough, rhinorrhea, hoarseness, and conjunctivitis point to a viral cause. Specific viral clues include conjunctivitis with adenovirus (pharyngoconjunctival fever) and posterior pharyngeal vesicles with coxsackie A virus (herpangina). In scarlet fever, the exotoxins add a fine sandpaper rash, strawberry tongue, circumoral pallor, and later desquamation."},{"type":"table","title":"Streptococcal versus viral pharyngitis","cols":["Feature","Streptococcal","Viral"],"rows":[["Onset and fever","Sudden, fever usually present","Variable; fever often low"],["Tonsils","Exudate, palatal petechiae","Red; exudate possible (adenovirus, EBV)"],["Nodes","Tender anterior cervical","Mild or diffuse"],["Cough, coryza, hoarseness","Absent","Usually present"],["Other clues","Scarlatiniform rash","Conjunctivitis, oral vesicles or ulcers"],["Laboratory","Rapid antigen test or culture positive","Negative for group A streptococcus"]],"caption":"Fever, exudate, tender anterior nodes, and no cough together favor group A streptococcus; no single feature is diagnostic."},{"type":"p","text":"Suppurative complications spread locally: peritonsillar and retropharyngeal abscess, otitis media, and sinusitis. The nonsuppurative sequelae are immune mediated and appear after a latent period. Acute rheumatic fever follows pharyngitis, not skin infection, about 2 to 4 weeks later. Antibodies and T cells directed at M protein and the group A carbohydrate cross-react with cardiac myosin, valve proteins, and other host molecules (molecular mimicry, a type II reaction), producing carditis with Aschoff bodies, migratory polyarthritis, Sydenham chorea, erythema marginatum, and subcutaneous nodules. Repeated attacks scar the valves, the mitral valve most often. Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis follows pharyngeal or skin infection with nephritogenic strains; immune complexes deposit in glomeruli and activate complement (type III), lowering serum C3 and causing a nephritic syndrome."},{"type":"table","title":"The two nonsuppurative sequelae","cols":["Feature","Acute rheumatic fever","Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis"],"rows":[["Preceding infection","Pharyngitis only","Pharyngitis or skin (impetigo)"],["Latent period","About 2 to 4 weeks","About 1 to 3 weeks after throat; 3 to 6 weeks after skin"],["Hypersensitivity","Type II, molecular mimicry","Type III, immune complexes"],["Target","Heart, joints, brain, skin","Glomeruli (subepithelial humps)"],["Laboratory","Raised antistreptolysin O or anti-DNase B","Low C3, raised anti-DNase B or ASO"],["Key outcome","Chronic valve scarring, mitral stenosis","Hematuria, edema, hypertension; most children recover"]],"caption":"Rheumatic fever needs a throat infection; glomerulonephritis can follow throat or skin."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why can impetigo cause glomerulonephritis but not rheumatic fever?","explain":"Glomerulonephritis depends on immune complexes from nephritogenic strains, which can infect skin or throat; rheumatic fever follows only pharyngeal infection, for reasons not fully established.","check":"Compare the Preceding infection row of the sequelae table with the paragraph above it."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-03-C07","obj":"PU-14-C.7","title":"Tonsillitis, infectious mononucleosis, and peritonsillar abscess","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The palatine tonsils lie in the tonsillar fossa between the palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal arches and form part of the ring of pharyngeal lymphoid tissue (Waldeyer ring). Deep crypts lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium sample antigens for underlying lymphoid follicles. A fibrous capsule separates each tonsil from the superior constrictor muscle, and the loose tissue between them is the peritonsillar space. The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) supplies both the tonsil and the middle ear, so tonsillar pain is referred to the ear. Exudative tonsillitis is caused by group A streptococcus (C06) or viruses, most importantly Epstein-Barr virus."},{"type":"p","text":"Infectious mononucleosis is primary Epstein-Barr virus (EBV, human herpesvirus 4) infection in adolescents and young adults, spread in saliva. EBV, an enveloped double-stranded DNA herpesvirus, infects oropharyngeal epithelium and then B cells, binding CD21 (complement receptor 2) through glycoprotein gp350 and using HLA class II as a coreceptor. Infected B cells proliferate, and a large cytotoxic CD8 T cell response controls them. Those reactive T cells are the atypical lymphocytes (large cells whose abundant cytoplasm indents around red cells), often more than 10% of lymphocytes. Their expansion in lymph nodes, spleen, and liver produces generalized lymphadenopathy, most notably posterior cervical, splenomegaly in about half of patients, and mild hepatitis with raised transaminases. The stretched splenic capsule can rupture, usually within the first few weeks."},{"type":"p","text":"Polyclonal B cell activation produces heterophile IgM antibodies that agglutinate sheep or horse red cells (Monospot test). This test can be negative early in the illness and in young children, when EBV-specific antibodies (IgM to viral capsid antigen) confirm acute infection."},{"type":"p","text":"Peritonsillar abscess, the most common deep neck infection of adolescents and young adults, forms when tonsillitis spreads through the capsule into the peritonsillar space, usually on one side, and the pus is often polymicrobial: S. pyogenes, S. aureus, and oral anaerobes such as Fusobacterium and Prevotella. The mass pushes the tonsil medially and the soft palate downward and forward, so the uvula deviates to the opposite side. Inflammation spreads to the adjacent medial pterygoid, causing trismus, and the bulging immobile palate gives a muffled hot potato voice with drooling and severe one-sided odynophagia. Spread into the parapharyngeal space threatens the airway and the internal jugular vein (septic thrombophlebitis)."},{"type":"table","title":"Exudative sore throat: three patterns","cols":["Feature","Streptococcal tonsillitis","Infectious mononucleosis","Peritonsillar abscess"],"rows":[["Cause","Streptococcus pyogenes","EBV infecting B cells via CD21","Strep, S. aureus, anaerobes"],["Nodes","Tender anterior cervical","Posterior cervical and generalized","Anterior, same side"],["Other findings","No cough, palatal petechiae","Splenomegaly, fatigue, hepatitis","Uvula deviated away, trismus, hot potato voice"],["Blood","Neutrophilia","Lymphocytosis, atypical lymphocytes, heterophile antibody","Neutrophilia"],["Danger","Rheumatic fever, glomerulonephritis","Splenic rupture, airway swelling","Deep neck spread, airway compromise"]],"caption":"Both strep and EBV cause exudate; posterior nodes, splenomegaly, and atypical lymphocytes point to EBV, while unilateral bulging and trismus point to abscess."},{"type":"example","title":"Working through an exudative sore throat","text":"A 17-year-old has 6 days of sore throat, fever, and exhaustion. The tonsils are covered in exudate, posterior cervical nodes are enlarged, and the spleen tip is palpable. Exudate alone does not separate strep from EBV. The posterior nodes and splenomegaly point to EBV, because the virus drives B cell proliferation and a CD8 T cell response throughout lymphoid tissue, whereas group A streptococcus inflames only the tonsil and its draining anterior nodes. The blood film would show atypical lymphocytes, and a heterophile test would likely be positive. Later one-sided pain, trismus, and a uvula pushed to the left would instead indicate a right peritonsillar abscess."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy predicts most of this chapter. Where swelling sits relative to the vocal folds decides the voice (muffled above, hoarse at the folds) and the radiograph (thumb above, steeple below), and the extrathoracic position of the larynx and trachea explains why their obstruction is heard on inspiration. The thin lamina papyracea and the venous connections to the cavernous sinus explain orbital and intracranial spread from the sinuses, and the peritonsillar space and its neighbor, the medial pterygoid, explain trismus."},{"type":"p","text":"Microbiology and immunology explain the rest. Rhinovirus temperature preference and acid lability keep it in the nose; the Hib capsule and the response of young children to polysaccharide explain the old age pattern of epiglottitis and its decline after conjugate vaccination; and the three hypersensitivity types appear side by side, with IgE in allergic rhinitis, mimicry in rheumatic fever, and immune complexes in glomerulonephritis. The classic presentations are teaching models: vaccinated children now rarely have Hib, adult epiglottitis is more common than pediatric, and exam findings overlap, so the full pattern, not one sign, separates the conditions."}],"summary":["Acute bronchitis is a mostly viral inflammation of the trachea and large bronchi with cough up to about 3 weeks; no consolidation separates it from pneumonia, and the time course and chronic irritant exposure separate it from chronic bronchitis.","Epiglottitis is supraglottic cellulitis, classically Hib in unvaccinated children, with sudden fever, drooling, muffled voice, tripod posture, a thumb sign, and risk of sudden complete obstruction.","Croup is parainfluenza laryngotracheobronchitis of children 6 months to 3 years; edema inside the cricoid ring causes barking cough, inspiratory stridor, and the steeple sign.","Bacterial tracheitis, usually S. aureus after a viral infection, looks like croup in a toxic child with purulent tracheal secretions; acute laryngitis causes hoarseness without stridor.","Allergic rhinitis is type I hypersensitivity with pale boggy mucosa and eosinophils; rhinovirus, an acid-labile picornavirus binding ICAM-1 and preferring nasal temperature, is the main cause of colds; vasomotor rhinitis lacks IgE and eosinophils.","Sinusitis starts with ostial obstruction, is mostly viral, and when bacterial is caused by S. pneumoniae, nontypeable H. influenzae, and M. catarrhalis.","Ethmoid sinusitis reaches the orbit through the lamina papyracea and the cavernous sinus through the ophthalmic veins, affecting CN III, IV, V1, V2, and VI; mucormycosis in ketoacidosis causes black necrotic eschar by angioinvasion.","Group A streptococcal pharyngitis causes fever, exudate, tender anterior nodes, and no cough; M protein is the main virulence factor and the target of molecular mimicry in rheumatic fever after pharyngitis only.","Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis follows throat or skin infection through immune complex deposition with low C3.","Infectious mononucleosis (EBV via CD21 on B cells) causes exudative tonsillitis with posterior nodes, splenomegaly, atypical CD8 T cells, and heterophile antibodies; peritonsillar abscess causes uvular deviation away from the abscess, trismus, and a muffled voice."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Head and Neck, upper airways and larynx; The Lung, acute bronchitis"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Streptococci; Haemophilus; Picornaviruses; Paramyxoviruses; Herpesviruses; Mucormycosis"},{"book":"Abbas Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Allergy and immediate hypersensitivity; immune complex and antibody-mediated disease"},{"book":"Moore Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head, nose and paranasal sinuses, cavernous sinus, orbit; Neck, pharynx and larynx"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pulmonary ventilation, airway resistance"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Infectious Diseases"}]},"PU-14-D":{"unit":"PU-14-D","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-04","title":"Disorders of the Pleural Cavity and Lung Expansion","subject":"Respiratory System (RESP)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers what happens when the pleural space fills with lymph, blood, fluid, pus, or air, when a lung segment collapses, and when the mediastinum or chest wall is injured. The unifying idea is that the lung stays expanded only because a thin, sealed pleural space holds subatmospheric pressure between an inward-recoiling lung and an outward-springing chest wall. Board questions give a pleural fluid analysis, a tracheal position, a percussion note, or a trauma scenario and ask you to name the process. If you can explain what each substance does to pleural pressure and lung volume, and which way it moves the mediastinum, you can reason through most of these items.","prerequisites":["Anatomy of the pleurae, mediastinum, thoracic duct, and diaphragm (PU-4)","Lung and chest wall recoil, intrapleural pressure, and compliance (PU-6)","Starling forces across capillaries and lymphatic drainage","Shunt, V/Q mismatch, and the alveolar gas equation (PU-7)","Acute inflammation, neutrophil exudates, and organization by fibrosis"],"connections":[{"to":"Ventilation and lung mechanics (PU-6)","how":"Pneumothorax and flail chest are direct demonstrations of the opposing recoil of lung and chest wall that sets intrapleural pressure."},{"to":"Restrictive lung disease (PU-14-A)","how":"Asbestosis is the interstitial counterpart of asbestos pleural disease, and fibrothorax and diffuse pleural thickening produce extraparenchymal restriction."},{"to":"Pulmonary pathogens (PU-13)","how":"Pneumonia organisms cause parapneumonic effusion and empyema, and Bacillus anthracis spores cause hemorrhagic mediastinitis."},{"to":"Cardiovascular and renal systems","how":"Heart failure, cirrhosis, and nephrotic syndrome produce transudates by changing hydrostatic or oncotic pressure, and tension pneumothorax causes obstructive shock."},{"to":"Pulmonary neoplasms (PU-14-F)","how":"Lymphoma causes chylothorax, malignancy causes exudates and hemothorax, and asbestos drives bronchogenic carcinoma and mesothelioma."}],"terms":[{"term":"Intrapleural pressure","def":"The pressure in the thin pleural space, about -5 cm H2O at functional residual capacity, created by the lung recoiling inward and the chest wall springing outward."},{"term":"Transudate","def":"Low-protein pleural fluid formed when hydrostatic or oncotic pressure changes push fluid across intact capillaries and pleura."},{"term":"Exudate","def":"Protein-rich pleural fluid formed when inflamed, infected, or invaded pleura or lymphatic obstruction lets protein, cells, and LDH accumulate."},{"term":"Chyle","def":"Milky intestinal lymph rich in chylomicrons, triglycerides, and lymphocytes, carried to the venous system by the thoracic duct."},{"term":"Fibrothorax","def":"A thick fibrous peel over the pleura, formed by organization of retained blood or pus, that encases and restricts the lung."},{"term":"Atelectasis","def":"Incomplete expansion or collapse of previously aerated lung, classified as resorption, compression, or contraction."},{"term":"Tension pneumothorax","def":"Pneumothorax in which a one-way valve traps air so pleural pressure becomes positive, shifting the mediastinum and impairing venous return."},{"term":"Ferruginous body","def":"An asbestos fiber or other inhaled fiber coated by macrophages with iron-containing protein, seen as a golden brown beaded rod that stains with Prussian blue."},{"term":"Light criteria","def":"Three comparisons of pleural fluid with serum protein and LDH; meeting any one classifies the fluid as an exudate."},{"term":"Hamman sign","def":"A crunching or clicking sound synchronous with the heartbeat, heard over the precordium in pneumomediastinum."},{"term":"Paradoxical motion","def":"Inward movement of a flail chest wall segment during inspiration and outward movement during expiration, opposite to the rest of the chest."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-04-C01","obj":"PU-14-D.1","title":"Chylothorax: thoracic duct disruption and milky pleural fluid","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Intestinal lacteals absorb dietary long-chain fatty acids packaged as chylomicrons, and this lymph (chyle) drains into the cisterna chyli near the upper lumbar vertebrae. The thoracic duct carries it upward through the aortic hiatus at about T12, ascends in the posterior mediastinum to the right of the midline between the aorta and the azygos vein, crosses to the left at about T4 to T6, and empties at the junction of the left subclavian and internal jugular veins. It returns about 1.5 to 2.5 L of chyle per day, more after a fatty meal."},{"type":"flow","title":"Path of chyle and the side of a chylothorax","steps":["Lacteals load chylomicrons into intestinal lymph","Cisterna chyli collects lymph near the upper lumbar spine","Duct enters thorax through aortic hiatus at T12","Ascends right of midline between aorta and azygos","Crosses left at about T4 to T6","Drains at left subclavian and internal jugular junction"],"caption":"Injury below the crossing usually produces a right chylothorax; injury above it usually produces a left one."},{"type":"p","text":"Chylothorax is chyle in the pleural space after the duct or its tributaries are torn or blocked. Traumatic causes include penetrating and blunt chest injury and, most often, thoracic surgery (esophagectomy, cardiac and aortic surgery) along the duct's course. Nontraumatic causes are dominated by malignancy, and lymphoma is the leading malignant cause because enlarged mediastinal nodes compress or invade the duct and raise pressure in the lymphatics behind the block. Less common causes include lymphangioleiomyomatosis, congenital lymphatic malformations, and central venous thrombosis that raises pressure at the duct's outlet."},{"type":"p","text":"The fluid looks milky because suspended chylomicrons scatter light. Its defining laboratory finding is a pleural triglyceride concentration above 110 mg/dL, and chylomicrons on lipoprotein analysis confirm it. A triglyceride level below 50 mg/dL makes chylothorax unlikely, and values between need lipoprotein testing. The fluid stays milky after centrifugation (cells in empyema settle and leave a clear supernatant), is lymphocyte predominant because chyle carries lymphocytes, and is usually odorless. A fasting patient may produce clear chyle, so a nonmilky fluid does not exclude the diagnosis."},{"type":"table","title":"Telling chylothorax from look-alike milky fluids","cols":["Feature","Chylothorax","Pseudochylothorax (cholesterol effusion)","Empyema"],"rows":[["Mechanism","Chyle leaks from injured or blocked thoracic duct","Cholesterol accumulates in a long-standing effusion with thick pleura (tuberculosis, rheumatoid arthritis)","Bacterial infection with neutrophils in the pleural space"],["Triglycerides","Above 110 mg/dL","Usually low","Low"],["Cholesterol","Low","High (often above 200 mg/dL), cholesterol crystals","Variable"],["Chylomicrons","Present","Absent","Absent"],["After centrifugation","Stays milky","Stays turbid","Supernatant clears"],["Predominant cell","Lymphocytes","Variable","Neutrophils"]],"caption":"Triglycerides and chylomicrons identify chyle; cholesterol crystals identify a pseudochylothorax; settling cells identify pus."},{"type":"p","text":"Because chyle carries fat, protein, fat-soluble vitamins, and lymphocytes, a persistent leak drains all of them. Prolonged chylothorax therefore produces malnutrition, hypoalbuminemia, lymphopenia, and impaired cell-mediated immunity, in addition to the restrictive effects of a large effusion. Chyle is relatively bacteriostatic, so infection of the collection itself is uncommon."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-04-C02","obj":"PU-14-D.2","title":"Hemothorax: bleeding into the pleural space and its consequences","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hemothorax is blood in the pleural space. Bloody-looking fluid needs only a small amount of blood (a red blood cell count of about 5,000 to 10,000 per microliter already looks pink), so hemothorax is defined by a pleural fluid hematocrit at least half (50%) of the patient's blood hematocrit. A serosanguineous malignant effusion usually has a fluid hematocrit far below that level."},{"type":"p","text":"Trauma is the most common cause. Penetrating wounds and rib fractures tear intercostal vessels and the internal thoracic artery, which carry systemic arterial pressure, and these bleed far more briskly than lung parenchyma, which is supplied by the low-pressure pulmonary circulation. Nontraumatic causes include rupture of an aortic dissection or aneurysm (typically into the left pleural space), pleural or metastatic malignancy (including mesothelioma), ruptured pulmonary arteriovenous malformations, catamenial hemothorax from thoracic endometriosis, coagulopathy or anticoagulation, and iatrogenic injury from procedures."},{"type":"p","text":"Each hemithorax can hold a large share of the circulating volume, often stated as 30% to 40%, so bleeding can be concealed there. A large hemothorax causes two problems at once. First, loss of intravascular volume lowers preload and produces hypovolemic shock: tachycardia, hypotension, cool skin, and flat neck veins. Second, the space-occupying blood compresses the lung, causing compression atelectasis, shunt, and hypoxemia, and a very large collection pushes the mediastinum toward the opposite side. Examination shows dullness to percussion and reduced breath sounds and fremitus on the affected side."},{"type":"flow","title":"From hemothorax to fibrothorax","steps":["Torn vessel bleeds into the pleural space","Volume loss lowers preload and causes hypovolemic shock","Collection compresses lung, causing atelectasis and hypoxemia","Movement partly defibrinates blood, but retained clot persists","Fibroblasts organize clot into a fibrous pleural peel","Peel traps the lung: fibrothorax with restriction"],"caption":"Early danger is volume loss; late danger is organization of retained clot into a restrictive peel."},{"type":"p","text":"Over days, blood that is not evacuated begins to organize. Motion of the heart, lungs, and diaphragm partly defibrinates pleural blood, which is why some of it stays liquid, but retained clot is invaded by fibroblasts and capillaries, as in organization anywhere. The result is fibrothorax, a thick collagenous peel over the visceral and parietal pleura that prevents the lung from expanding. It produces a restrictive pattern with reduced vital capacity and total lung capacity and a small, immobile hemithorax with rib crowding. Retained clot is also a good culture medium, so secondary empyema is another late complication."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A trauma patient has hypotension and absent breath sounds on the left. Percussion is dull and the neck veins are flat. Is this a hemothorax or a tension pneumothorax?","explain":"Dullness and flat neck veins point to hemothorax with hypovolemia. Tension pneumothorax gives hyperresonance and distended neck veins because venous return is obstructed, not because volume is lost.","check":"Compare the shock mechanism here with the tension pneumothorax discussion in C05."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-04-C03","obj":"PU-14-D.3","title":"Atelectasis: resorption versus compression collapse","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Atelectasis is incomplete expansion or collapse of previously aerated lung. It is classified by mechanism as resorption (obstructive), compression, or contraction (cicatrization). The first two are reversible once the cause is removed, and the direction of mediastinal shift distinguishes them."},{"type":"p","text":"Resorption atelectasis follows complete obstruction of an airway by a mucus plug (postoperative patients, asthma, bronchiectasis), an endobronchial tumor, or an aspirated foreign body. Gas in the trapped alveoli is still exposed to perfusing capillaries, and mixed venous blood has a total gas pressure below atmospheric. Venous PO2 is about 40 mmHg, far below the alveolar value of about 100 mmHg, while venous PCO2 is only about 6 mmHg higher than alveolar PCO2, so the sum of venous gas pressures is about 700 mmHg against an alveolar sum of 760 mmHg. Oxygen leaves the alveolus first, then nitrogen slowly follows down its own gradient, and the segment shrinks until it is airless. When the trapped gas is pure oxygen, as during breathing of 100% oxygen, there is no nitrogen to slow the process and collapse occurs within minutes."},{"type":"flow","title":"Mechanism of resorption atelectasis","steps":["Plug, tumor, or foreign body completely blocks a bronchus","Distal alveolar gas can no longer be replenished","Capillary blood absorbs oxygen, then nitrogen, from trapped gas","Alveoli collapse and the lobe loses volume","Lost volume pulls trachea and mediastinum toward the collapse"],"caption":"Subatmospheric total gas pressure in venous blood drives absorption of trapped alveolar gas."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the collapsed lobe occupies less space, the hemithorax loses volume: the trachea and mediastinum move toward the collapse, the ipsilateral hemidiaphragm rises, and the rib spaces narrow. Compression atelectasis is the opposite situation. Fluid (effusion, hemothorax), air (pneumothorax), or a mass fills the pleural space, the lung is passively squeezed, and a large collection pushes the mediastinum away from the lesion. Contraction atelectasis occurs when local or generalized fibrosis prevents full expansion; like resorption it pulls structures toward the lesion, but it is not reversible."},{"type":"table","title":"Types of atelectasis","cols":["Type","Cause","Mediastinal shift","Reversible"],"rows":[["Resorption (obstructive)","Complete airway obstruction: mucus plug, tumor, foreign body","Toward the collapse","Yes, if obstruction is relieved"],["Compression","Pleural fluid, air, or mass pressing on lung","Away from a large collection","Yes, if the collection is removed"],["Contraction (cicatrization)","Pulmonary or pleural fibrosis","Toward the lesion","No"]],"caption":"Volume loss pulls structures toward the lesion; added volume in the pleural space pushes them away."},{"type":"p","text":"Collapsed alveoli remain perfused, so atelectasis creates intrapulmonary shunt. Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction diverts some blood away and limits the fall in PaO2, and the hypoxemia responds poorly to supplemental oxygen because shunted blood never meets alveolar gas. Over the collapsed region, percussion is dull and breath sounds and fremitus are reduced when the bronchus is occluded. Collapsed lung beyond an obstruction clears secretions poorly and is prone to infection, which is why a persistent lobar collapse in an adult smoker raises concern for an obstructing carcinoma."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A dense opacity fills the left hemithorax. In patient A the trachea is pulled to the left; in patient B it is pushed to the right. What process explains each?","explain":"Patient A has lost volume, as in complete left lung collapse from an obstructed main bronchus. Patient B has added volume in the pleural space, as in a massive effusion that pushes the mediastinum away.","check":"Compare with the atelectasis types table and the effusion findings in C06."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-04-C04","obj":"PU-14-D.4","title":"Asbestos-related pleural disease and cancer risk","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Asbestos is a group of fibrous silicates. Serpentine chrysotile fibers are curly and flexible and tend to impact in larger airways, where mucociliary clearance and dissolution remove many of them. Amphibole fibers (crocidolite, amosite) are straight, stiff, and thin, so they penetrate deep into the lung, cross into the interstitium, and migrate to the pleura. Amphiboles are more pathogenic, especially for mesothelioma. Fibers too long for a macrophage to engulf cause frustrated phagocytosis: the macrophage keeps producing reactive oxygen species and releases cytokines and growth factors (including TGF-beta and PDGF) that recruit fibroblasts and damage DNA."},{"type":"p","text":"Pleural plaques are the most common lesion of asbestos exposure. They are well-circumscribed plates of dense, hyalinized collagen on the parietal pleura, typically over the posterolateral chest wall and the domes of the diaphragm, and they often calcify. They usually lack asbestos bodies, cause no symptoms, and mark exposure rather than disease; they do not become mesothelioma. Benign asbestos pleural effusion is an exudate, often bloody, that can appear within one to two decades of exposure, earlier than other asbestos lesions. Diffuse pleural fibrosis involves the visceral pleura, often follows recurrent effusion, blunts the costophrenic angle, and can restrict ventilation; folding of thickened pleura into the lung produces rounded atelectasis. Asbestosis itself, interstitial fibrosis that begins in the lower lobes, is covered in PU-14-A."},{"type":"p","text":"Asbestos bodies, a type of ferruginous body, form when macrophages coat a fiber with iron-containing protein (ferritin and hemosiderin). In tissue they are golden brown, fusiform or beaded rods with a thin translucent core, and they stain blue with Prussian blue. They prove exposure, but small numbers occur in many urban adults without disease, so the diagnosis of asbestosis requires fibrosis as well."},{"type":"table","title":"Asbestos lesions of the pleura and lung","cols":["Lesion","Site and appearance","What it means"],"rows":[["Pleural plaques","Parietal pleura, posterolateral wall and diaphragm; often calcified","Most common lesion; marker of exposure; benign"],["Benign pleural effusion","Exudate, often bloody","Often the earliest manifestation"],["Diffuse pleural fibrosis","Visceral pleura; blunted costophrenic angle","Can restrict ventilation; rounded atelectasis"],["Asbestosis","Lower lobe interstitial fibrosis","Restrictive lung disease (PU-14-A)"],["Bronchogenic carcinoma","Any histologic type","Most frequent asbestos-related cancer; risk multiplied by smoking"],["Malignant mesothelioma","Diffuse pleural tumor encasing the lung","Long latency; risk not increased by smoking"]],"caption":"Plaques signal exposure; carcinoma and mesothelioma are the lethal outcomes."},{"type":"p","text":"Bronchogenic carcinoma is the most frequent malignancy in asbestos-exposed people. Asbestos alone raises the risk about fivefold, and the combination with smoking is multiplicative rather than additive, with risk often quoted as about 50 times that of unexposed nonsmokers. One proposed reason is that fibers adsorb and concentrate carcinogens from smoke and deliver them to epithelial cells while sustaining oxidant injury. Malignant mesothelioma, by contrast, is strongly linked to asbestos (especially amphiboles) with a latency of roughly 25 to 45 years, and smoking does not raise its risk. Mesothelioma encases the lung in a thick rind and typically produces a hemorrhagic effusion; its pathology is taught in PU-14-F."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-04-C05","obj":"PU-14-D.5","title":"Pneumothorax: spontaneous, secondary, and tension","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The lung recoils inward and the chest wall springs outward, and the sealed pleural space between them holds a pressure of about -5 cm H2O at functional residual capacity. If air enters the space from a ruptured alveolus or through the chest wall, pleural pressure rises toward atmospheric, the lung collapses toward the hilum under its own elastic recoil, and the chest wall on that side springs slightly outward. Air between lung and chest wall percusses as hyperresonant and blocks transmission of breath sounds and vocal vibration, so the classic findings are hyperresonance, reduced or absent breath sounds, and reduced tactile fremitus on the affected side."},{"type":"p","text":"Primary spontaneous pneumothorax occurs without known lung disease, typically in tall, thin young men, and smoking increases the risk substantially. It results from rupture of small subpleural blebs at the lung apex. Pleural pressure is more negative at the apex than at the base because of gravity and the weight of the lung, so apical alveoli are more distended, and this stress is greater in a tall chest. Secondary spontaneous pneumothorax complicates underlying lung disease: COPD (ruptured emphysematous bullae, the most common cause), cystic fibrosis, Pneumocystis pneumonia, lymphangioleiomyomatosis, and necrotizing infections. Because reserve is already reduced, a secondary pneumothorax causes more hypoxemia for its size. Traumatic and iatrogenic pneumothorax (central line placement, lung biopsy, mechanical ventilation) form a third group."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The one-way valve in tension pneumothorax","steps":["Inspiration opens a flap and draws air into the pleural space","Expiration closes the flap and traps the air","Pleural pressure becomes positive and rises with each breath","Lung collapses and mediastinum shifts to the opposite side","Venae cavae kink and compress; venous return falls","Hypoxemia drives harder breathing, pulling in more air"],"caption":"Each breath adds air that cannot leave, so pressure climbs until cardiac output fails."},{"type":"p","text":"Tension pneumothorax develops when a tissue flap acts as a one-way valve. Positive and rising intrapleural pressure collapses the ipsilateral lung completely, pushes the trachea and mediastinum toward the opposite side, compresses the contralateral lung, and impairs filling of the right heart by compressing and kinking the venae cavae and raising intrathoracic pressure. The result is obstructive shock: tachycardia, hypotension, and distended neck veins (blood backs up because it cannot enter the chest), with severe hypoxemia. Positive pressure ventilation and trauma make tension physiology more likely because air is forced into the space with each breath."},{"type":"table","title":"Comparing the three main forms of pneumothorax","cols":["Feature","Primary spontaneous","Secondary spontaneous","Tension"],"rows":[["Typical patient","Tall, thin young man, often a smoker","Older patient with COPD, cystic fibrosis, or other lung disease","Trauma or positive pressure ventilation; any pneumothorax can progress"],["Mechanism","Rupture of apical subpleural bleb","Rupture of bulla or diseased lung","One-way valve traps air under positive pressure"],["Trachea","Usually midline","Usually midline","Deviated away from the affected side"],["Hemodynamics","Stable","Usually stable, but hypoxemia more severe","Hypotension, tachycardia, distended neck veins"],["Percussion and breath sounds","Hyperresonant, reduced","Hyperresonant, reduced","Hyperresonant, absent"]],"caption":"Tracheal deviation and hemodynamic collapse distinguish tension from simple pneumothorax."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-04-C06","obj":"PU-14-D.6","title":"Pleural effusion: Starling forces and Light criteria","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Normally each pleural space contains only about 0.1 to 0.3 mL/kg of fluid. It filters mainly from systemic capillaries of the parietal pleura, under Starling forces, and leaves through stomata and lymphatics of the parietal pleura, which can increase their drainage many times when filtration rises. An effusion forms when filtration exceeds lymphatic capacity, either because pressures change across an intact membrane or because the membrane or its lymphatics are damaged."},{"type":"p","text":"A transudate forms when hydrostatic pressure rises or oncotic pressure falls while the capillaries and pleura are intact, so protein, cells, and LDH are largely held back. Heart failure is the most common cause of any pleural effusion: raised pulmonary venous pressure drives interstitial edema that crosses the visceral pleura, and the effusions are usually bilateral or right-sided. Cirrhosis causes hepatic hydrothorax, typically on the right, as ascites passes through small diaphragmatic defects under the negative pleural pressure. Nephrotic syndrome lowers plasma oncotic pressure through urinary albumin loss. An exudate forms when inflammation, infection, or tumor makes the pleural capillaries leaky or blocks lymphatic drainage, so protein and LDH (released from inflammatory and tumor cells) accumulate."},{"type":"table","title":"Light criteria (exudate if any one is met)","cols":["Criterion","Exudate threshold","Why it works"],"rows":[["Pleural protein / serum protein","Above 0.5","A leaky membrane lets plasma protein into the fluid"],["Pleural LDH / serum LDH","Above 0.6","Inflammatory and tumor cells release LDH locally"],["Pleural LDH","Above two thirds of the upper limit of normal for serum LDH","Absolute marker of local cell injury and turnover"]],"caption":"A transudate meets none of the three; the criteria are very sensitive for exudates but can misclassify some transudates."},{"type":"table","title":"Common causes by category","cols":["Transudate","Exudate"],"rows":[["Heart failure (most common overall)","Parapneumonic effusion and empyema (most common exudate)"],["Cirrhosis with hepatic hydrothorax","Malignancy: lung and breast carcinoma, lymphoma, mesothelioma"],["Nephrotic syndrome and other hypoalbuminemia","Tuberculosis (lymphocyte predominant)"],["Peritoneal dialysis","Pulmonary embolism (usually exudate)"],["Atelectasis (more negative pleural pressure)","Pancreatitis (left-sided, high amylase), esophageal rupture"],["Hypothyroidism (variable)","Rheumatoid arthritis and SLE; chylothorax and hemothorax"]],"caption":"Pressure problems produce transudates; pleural disease produces exudates."},{"type":"p","text":"Light criteria identify nearly all exudates, but about one quarter of transudates are misclassified as exudates, most often in heart failure after diuresis concentrates protein and LDH in the remaining fluid. A serum minus pleural albumin gradient above 1.2 g/dL supports a transudate in that setting. Over any large effusion, percussion is stony dull, fremitus and breath sounds are reduced, egophony may be heard at the upper border where lung is compressed, and a massive effusion pushes the mediastinum away. On an upright posteroanterior radiograph about 200 mL is needed to blunt the costophrenic angle, and a lateral view shows smaller volumes."},{"type":"example","title":"Applying Light criteria","text":"Pleural protein is 3.0 g/dL with serum protein 7.0 g/dL; pleural LDH is 110 U/L with serum LDH 200 U/L and a serum upper limit of 220 U/L. Protein ratio: 3.0 / 7.0 = 0.43, below 0.5, so not exudative. LDH ratio: 110 / 200 = 0.55, below 0.6, so not exudative. Two thirds of 220 is about 147 U/L, and 110 is below it, so not exudative. No criterion is met, so the fluid is a transudate, consistent with heart failure, cirrhosis, or nephrotic syndrome. If pleural LDH had been 160 U/L, the third criterion alone would make it an exudate, because meeting any single criterion is enough."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-04-C07","obj":"PU-14-D.7","title":"Empyema, mediastinitis, pneumomediastinum, and costochondritis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Empyema is pus in the pleural space, most often a progression of a parapneumonic effusion. Bacteria from adjacent pneumonia (Streptococcus pneumoniae, Staphylococcus aureus, the Streptococcus anginosus group, and oral anaerobes after aspiration) invade the effusion, and neutrophils follow. Both neutrophils and bacteria metabolize glucose anaerobically to lactate and produce CO2, so the fluid becomes acidic (pH below about 7.2) with low glucose (below about 60 mg/dL) and very high LDH from cell lysis. Untreated, the process moves from a free-flowing exudate to fibrin deposition and loculation, then to organization into a fibrous peel that traps the lung, the same end point as a retained hemothorax. Patients have persistent fever and pleuritic pain after an apparently resolving pneumonia."},{"type":"flow","title":"Evolution of a parapneumonic effusion to empyema","steps":["Pneumonia inflames the visceral pleura","Leaky capillaries produce a sterile free-flowing exudate","Bacteria invade and neutrophils pour in","Glycolysis lowers pleural glucose and pH","Fibrin forms loculations and frank pus","Fibroblasts lay down a restrictive pleural peel"],"caption":"Low pH, low glucose, and high LDH reflect intense local metabolism by neutrophils and bacteria."},{"type":"p","text":"The mediastinum is a continuous connective tissue compartment, so infection or air that enters it spreads widely. Acute mediastinitis has three classic sources. Esophageal perforation, most often iatrogenic during endoscopy and also from forceful vomiting (Boerhaave syndrome, usually a tear of the distal left posterolateral wall), releases oral flora and gastric contents. Deep sternal wound infection follows median sternotomy. Descending infection from odontogenic or retropharyngeal abscesses travels down the cervical fascial planes. Inhalational anthrax is a special case: alveolar macrophages carry Bacillus anthracis spores to mediastinal lymph nodes, where they germinate and release edema toxin and lethal toxin, producing hemorrhagic necrotizing lymphadenitis and mediastinitis with a widened mediastinum and hemorrhagic pleural effusions, often without a true pneumonia."},{"type":"p","text":"Pneumomediastinum is air in the mediastinum. Most cases begin with alveolar rupture during high intrathoracic pressure (asthma, forceful Valsalva, vomiting, inhaled drug use). Air tracks along the bronchovascular sheaths to the hilum and into the mediastinum (the Macklin effect), then continues into the neck and chest wall, producing subcutaneous emphysema that crackles under the fingers (crepitus). The Hamman sign is a crunching sound synchronous with the heartbeat as the heart moves against air-filled tissue. Esophageal or tracheobronchial rupture causes a more dangerous pneumomediastinum because it is often accompanied by mediastinitis."},{"type":"p","text":"Costochondritis is inflammation of the costochondral or costosternal junctions, usually involving several of the second to fifth ribs, without visible swelling (Tietze syndrome is the variant with localized swelling, usually at a single upper junction). The pain arises from the chest wall itself, so pressing on the affected junction reproduces it exactly. It remains a diagnosis of exclusion, because reproducible tenderness does not by itself exclude cardiac or pulmonary causes of chest pain."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing features","cols":["Condition","Typical setting","Key finding"],"rows":[["Empyema","Pneumonia that fails to resolve","Pus; low pH and glucose; high LDH; organisms on Gram stain"],["Acute mediastinitis","Esophageal perforation, sternotomy, inhalational anthrax","Fever, chest pain, widened mediastinum"],["Pneumomediastinum","Asthma, Valsalva, vomiting, esophageal rupture","Subcutaneous crepitus; Hamman crunch"],["Costochondritis","Adult of any age, no systemic illness","Pain reproduced by pressing the costal cartilages"]],"caption":"Each condition is identified by its setting plus one characteristic finding."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-04-C08","obj":"PU-14-D.8","title":"Chest trauma: flail chest, contusion, open pneumothorax, and diaphragmatic rupture","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Flail chest occurs when several adjacent ribs (commonly defined as three or more) are each fractured in two or more places, leaving a segment of chest wall that is no longer attached to the rest of the cage. The segment follows pleural pressure instead of the chest wall: it is sucked inward during inspiration as pleural pressure falls and bulges outward during expiration. This paradoxical motion reduces effective tidal volume, and pain causes splinting that promotes atelectasis. The force needed to create a flail segment almost always bruises the lung beneath, and that contusion is usually the main cause of hypoxemia."},{"type":"p","text":"Pulmonary contusion is hemorrhage and edema in the alveoli and interstitium from direct impact. Blood and edema fluid flood alveoli that remain perfused, creating intrapulmonary shunt and reduced compliance. Because edema and inflammation continue to accumulate, hypoxemia and radiographic opacities typically worsen over the first 24 to 48 hours, and the initial radiograph can underestimate the injury. Opacities are patchy and do not respect lobar boundaries, unlike lobar pneumonia, and they usually resolve within about a week unless pneumonia or ARDS supervenes."},{"type":"p","text":"Open pneumothorax results from a chest wall defect that communicates with the pleural space. When the opening is larger than about two thirds of the tracheal diameter, it offers less resistance than the airway, so air moves preferentially through the wound with each inspiration (a sucking chest wound). The ipsilateral lung collapses and ventilation falls sharply. If tissue around the wound forms a flap valve, the open pneumothorax can convert to tension physiology."},{"type":"p","text":"Blunt diaphragmatic rupture is diagnosed more often on the left because the liver buffers the right hemidiaphragm. A sudden rise in abdominal pressure tears the muscle, and the abdomen-to-chest pressure gradient draws stomach, colon, or spleen into the left hemithorax, giving bowel sounds in the chest, a gas-filled viscus or coiled nasogastric tube above the diaphragm, and lung compression. Herniation can be delayed for months or years. Because the diaphragm rises to about the fourth or fifth intercostal space in expiration, penetrating lower chest wounds can also injure it."},{"type":"table","title":"Chest trauma findings by mechanism","cols":["Injury","Mechanism","Signature finding","Main threat"],"rows":[["Flail chest","Segment detached by double fractures of adjacent ribs","Paradoxical inward motion on inspiration","Underlying contusion and hypoventilation"],["Pulmonary contusion","Alveolar hemorrhage and edema","Hypoxemia worsening over 24 to 48 hours; nonlobar opacity","Shunt; pneumonia or ARDS"],["Open pneumothorax","Chest wall defect admits air","Air sucked through the wound; lung collapse","Hypoventilation; conversion to tension"],["Diaphragmatic rupture","Blunt abdominal pressure surge or penetrating wound","Bowel or stomach in left hemithorax","Lung compression; strangulated viscus"]],"caption":"Each injury breaks one part of the rigid wall, sealed pleura, or airtight diaphragm."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a patient with flail chest often become more hypoxemic the day after injury even though chest wall motion is unchanged?","explain":"The underlying pulmonary contusion progresses as hemorrhage and edema fill more alveoli, increasing shunt over the first one to two days.","check":"Compare with the description of pulmonary contusion in this section."},{"type":"example","title":"Two causes of shock after chest trauma","text":"A driver hit the steering wheel and has hypotension, tachycardia, and absent breath sounds on the right. Absent breath sounds on one side mean air or fluid between lung and chest wall. Percussion separates them: hyperresonance means air, dullness means fluid. Neck veins then reveal the shock mechanism. Distended neck veins with the trachea pushed left indicate tension pneumothorax, where positive pleural pressure blocks venous return (obstructive shock). Flat neck veins with dullness indicate massive hemothorax, where volume is lost (hypovolemic shock). Cardiac tamponade also gives distended neck veins and hypotension, but breath sounds are present and heart sounds are muffled."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy explains where these disorders arise and which side they affect. The thoracic duct crosses from right to left in the mid thorax, so the level of injury predicts the side of a chylothorax; intercostal and internal thoracic arteries carry systemic pressure, so they produce the dangerous hemothoraces; the liver shields the right hemidiaphragm; and the continuous mediastinal connective tissue lets air and infection spread from the alveoli or esophagus into the neck. Physiology unifies the bedside findings. Every condition either adds volume to the pleural space (fluid, blood, chyle, pus, air under tension), which pushes the mediastinum away, or removes volume from the lung (resorption or contraction atelectasis), which pulls it toward the lesion. Percussion then separates air from fluid, and neck veins separate obstructed venous return from lost volume."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology and microbiology supply the fluid chemistry and the late outcomes. Starling forces decide transudate versus exudate; neutrophil and bacterial glycolysis explain the low glucose and pH of empyema; and organization of retained blood or pus by fibroblasts produces fibrothorax, the same repair process seen in any unresolved acute inflammation. Asbestos links occupational exposure to frustrated phagocytosis, fibrosis, and two cancers with different relationships to smoking. The limit of these rules is that bedside signs can be subtle in small collections or partial collapse, and that laboratory cutoffs such as Light criteria are probabilistic, so findings must be read together."}],"summary":["The lung stays expanded because the sealed pleural space holds subatmospheric pressure between inward lung recoil and outward chest wall recoil.","Chylothorax is milky, lymphocyte-predominant fluid with triglycerides above 110 mg/dL and chylomicrons, from thoracic duct trauma, surgery, or lymphoma.","Hemothorax requires a pleural fluid hematocrit at least half the blood hematocrit; it causes hypovolemic shock early and fibrothorax from organized clot later.","Resorption atelectasis follows complete airway obstruction because venous blood has a subatmospheric total gas pressure; volume loss pulls the mediastinum toward the collapse, while compression by fluid or air pushes it away.","Calcified parietal pleural plaques are the most common asbestos lesion and mark exposure; asbestos multiplies smoking-related lung carcinoma risk, while mesothelioma risk is independent of smoking.","Pneumothorax gives hyperresonance with reduced or absent breath sounds; tension pneumothorax adds tracheal deviation away, hypotension, and distended neck veins from obstructed venous return.","An effusion is an exudate if any Light criterion is met: protein ratio above 0.5, LDH ratio above 0.6, or pleural LDH above two thirds of the serum upper limit.","Heart failure, cirrhosis, and nephrotic syndrome cause transudates; pneumonia, malignancy, tuberculosis, and pulmonary embolism cause exudates.","Empyema has low pH, low glucose, and high LDH; pneumomediastinum gives crepitus and the Hamman crunch; costochondritis pain is reproduced by palpation.","Flail segments move paradoxically, contusion hypoxemia worsens over 24 to 48 hours, sucking wounds collapse the lung, and blunt diaphragmatic rupture usually puts bowel in the left chest."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lung: atelectasis, pneumoconioses (asbestos-related diseases), and lesions of the pleura"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pulmonary Circulation, Pulmonary Edema, Pleural Fluid; Pulmonary Ventilation"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Mechanics of ventilation, intrapleural pressure and lung recoil"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax: pleurae, mediastinum, thoracic duct, and diaphragm"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Pulmonary Disease: pleural effusion and pneumothorax"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg Medical Microbiology","where":"Spore-forming gram-positive bacilli: Bacillus anthracis"}]},"PU-14-E":{"unit":"PU-14-E","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-05","title":"Pulmonary Vascular Disease","subject":"Respiratory System (RESP)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers disorders of the pulmonary circulation: fluid leaking into the lung (pulmonary edema), material lodging in the pulmonary arteries (thrombotic, fat, air, and amniotic fluid emboli), death of lung tissue after embolism (pulmonary infarction), sustained high pressure in the pulmonary circuit (pulmonary hypertension), and abnormal arteriovenous connections that bypass the alveoli. Board questions usually give a setting and a blood gas, a wedge pressure, or an autopsy finding and ask you to name the mechanism. If you can apply Starling forces, explain why embolism causes hypoxemia with a low PaCO2, and reason from the dual blood supply and the right ventricle's tolerance of afterload, you can work through most items.","prerequisites":["Starling forces and lymphatic drainage of the interstitium","Pulmonary and bronchial circulations and hypoxic vasoconstriction (PU-5)","Ventilation-perfusion matching, dead space, shunt, and the A-a gradient (PU-7)","Coagulation cascade, fibrinolysis, and Virchow triad (hematology)","Right and left ventricular function and the Frank-Starling relationship (cardiovascular)"],"connections":[{"to":"Pulmonary blood flow (PU-5)","how":"The low-pressure, high-compliance pulmonary bed and its dual supply explain why the right ventricle fails under sudden afterload and why most emboli do not infarct."},{"to":"Restrictive lung disease and ARDS (PU-14-A)","how":"ARDS is the prototype of noncardiogenic edema from diffuse alveolar capillary injury."},{"to":"Heart failure (cardiovascular)","how":"Left heart failure raises pulmonary venous pressure, causing cardiogenic edema, group 2 pulmonary hypertension, and the poor bronchial flow that permits infarction."},{"to":"Hemostasis and thrombosis (hematology)","how":"Inherited and acquired hypercoagulable states, D-dimer, and DIC link pulmonary embolism and amniotic fluid embolism to the coagulation system."},{"to":"Cerebrovascular disease (neurology)","how":"Right-to-left shunts through a patent foramen ovale or a pulmonary arteriovenous malformation let venous clots and bacteria reach the brain."}],"terms":[{"term":"Starling forces","def":"The hydrostatic and oncotic pressures inside and outside a capillary that, with wall permeability, set the rate of fluid filtration."},{"term":"Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure","def":"Pressure measured by a balloon-tipped catheter wedged in a small pulmonary artery branch, estimating left atrial pressure (normal about 6 to 12 mmHg)."},{"term":"Heart failure cells","def":"Alveolar macrophages filled with hemosiderin from phagocytosed red cells, a sign of chronic pulmonary venous congestion."},{"term":"Virchow triad","def":"The three conditions that favor thrombosis: venous stasis or turbulent flow, endothelial injury, and hypercoagulability."},{"term":"Dead space","def":"Ventilated lung that receives no perfusion, so it takes no part in gas exchange (ventilation-perfusion ratio of infinity)."},{"term":"A-a gradient","def":"The difference between calculated alveolar PO2 and measured arterial PO2; it widens with V/Q mismatch, shunt, or diffusion limitation."},{"term":"D-dimer","def":"A degradation product of cross-linked fibrin released when plasmin breaks down a clot; sensitive for thrombosis but not specific."},{"term":"Saddle embolus","def":"A large thromboembolus lodged across the bifurcation of the main pulmonary artery."},{"term":"Paradoxical embolism","def":"Passage of a venous embolus into the systemic arteries through a right-to-left shunt."},{"term":"Cor pulmonale","def":"Right ventricular hypertrophy, dilation, or failure caused by pulmonary hypertension from disease of the lung or its vessels."},{"term":"Plexiform lesion","def":"A tuft of small, slit-like vascular channels within a dilated segment of a small pulmonary artery, typical of severe pulmonary arterial hypertension."},{"term":"Right-to-left shunt","def":"Blood that passes from the venous to the arterial side without meeting alveolar gas, causing hypoxemia that 100% oxygen corrects poorly."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-05-C01","obj":"PU-14-E.1","title":"Pulmonary edema: Starling forces, cardiogenic and noncardiogenic","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Fluid movement across any capillary follows the Starling relationship: filtration is proportional to the filtration coefficient times the hydrostatic gradient (capillary minus interstitial pressure) minus the reflection coefficient times the oncotic gradient (plasma minus interstitial oncotic pressure). In the lung, capillary hydrostatic pressure is low, about 7 to 10 mmHg, and interstitial pressure is slightly negative, while plasma oncotic pressure is about 25 to 28 mmHg. The result is a small continuous outward leak that lymphatics return to the circulation, so alveoli stay dry."},{"type":"p","text":"Three safety factors protect the lung: a large gap between capillary and plasma oncotic pressure, lymph flow that can rise several fold, and washout of interstitial protein as filtration rises, which lowers interstitial oncotic pressure. Edema appears only when these are overwhelmed. Fluid collects first in the loose interstitium around bronchioles and vessels, then breaks through into the alveoli. Interstitial fluid stiffens the lung and stimulates juxtacapillary (J) receptors, causing rapid shallow breathing and dyspnea; alveolar fluid adds crackles, frothy sputum, and hypoxemia from low V/Q units and shunt."},{"type":"p","text":"Cardiogenic (hydrostatic) edema follows a rise in left atrial and pulmonary venous pressure from left ventricular failure, mitral stenosis, or volume overload. The wedge pressure is above 18 mmHg and the fluid is a low-protein transudate because the capillary wall is intact. Lying flat returns blood from the legs to the central circulation and raises pulmonary capillary pressure further, which explains orthopnea and paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea. Red cells leak into alveoli from engorged capillaries, and macrophages convert their hemoglobin iron into hemosiderin, producing heart failure cells; long-standing congestion with fibrosis gives brown induration of the lung."},{"type":"flow","title":"From left heart failure to alveolar flooding","steps":["Left ventricle fails or mitral valve narrows","Left atrial and pulmonary venous pressure rise","Capillary hydrostatic pressure exceeds plasma oncotic pressure","Filtration outpaces lymph flow; interstitial edema","Fluid breaks into alveoli; low V/Q and shunt","Dyspnea, crackles, hypoxemia; wedge above 18 mmHg"],"caption":"Wedge pressure estimates left atrial pressure, so it identifies the hydrostatic cause."},{"type":"p","text":"Noncardiogenic edema occurs with a normal wedge pressure (18 mmHg or less). Injury to the alveolar capillary endothelium or epithelium raises permeability and lowers the reflection coefficient, so protein-rich fluid floods alveoli even at normal pressure; this is acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS, PU-14-A). Low plasma oncotic pressure (nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis, malnutrition) narrows the safety margin, so modest pressure increases cause edema. Lymphatic obstruction, classically tumor spreading through lymphatics (lymphangitic carcinomatosis), removes the drainage that normally keeps pace with filtration."},{"type":"table","title":"Cardiogenic versus noncardiogenic edema","cols":["Feature","Cardiogenic","Noncardiogenic"],"rows":[["Starling term changed","Capillary hydrostatic pressure up","Permeability up, oncotic pressure down, or lymph flow down"],["Wedge pressure","Above 18 mmHg","18 mmHg or less"],["Edema fluid protein","Low (transudate)","High when capillaries are injured"],["Typical causes","Left ventricular failure, mitral stenosis, volume overload","ARDS (sepsis, aspiration, trauma, pancreatitis), nephrotic syndrome, lymphangitic tumor"],["Supporting findings","Heart failure cells, cardiomegaly, high BNP","Hyaline membranes in ARDS; normal heart size"]],"caption":"Wedge pressure separates the groups; fluid protein reflects whether the capillary wall is intact."},{"type":"example","title":"Applying Starling forces to the lung","text":"Using approximate textbook values, outward forces are capillary pressure 7 mmHg, interstitial pressure of -8 mmHg (which pulls fluid outward, adding 8), and interstitial oncotic pressure 14 mmHg, totaling 29 mmHg. The inward force is plasma oncotic pressure, 28 mmHg. Net filtration pressure is about +1 mmHg, which lymphatics easily remove. If left atrial pressure rises so that capillary pressure reaches 30 mmHg, outward force becomes about 52 mmHg and net pressure about +24 mmHg, beyond lymphatic capacity, so edema forms. Acutely, capillary pressure must exceed roughly the plasma oncotic pressure before edema appears; if plasma oncotic pressure is halved, edema appears at a much lower capillary pressure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-05-C02","obj":"PU-14-E.2","title":"Pulmonary thromboembolism","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"About 95% of pulmonary emboli arise from deep vein thrombosis (DVT) of the legs, mostly from the proximal deep veins (popliteal, femoral, and iliac); calf thrombi embolize less often unless they extend upward. The thrombus breaks free, passes through the right heart, and lodges in a pulmonary artery sized to its diameter. Venous thrombosis follows Virchow triad: stasis (immobility, surgery, long travel, heart failure), endothelial injury (trauma, surgery, prior thrombosis), and hypercoagulability (cancer, pregnancy and the postpartum period, estrogen-containing contraceptives, factor V Leiden, prothrombin G20210A, antiphospholipid antibodies)."},{"type":"p","text":"Most emboli are small and clinically silent. A significant embolus stops perfusion to ventilated alveoli, creating dead space. Blood is diverted to the remaining lung, where overperfusion creates low V/Q units; platelet mediators (serotonin, thromboxane A2) cause local vasoconstriction and bronchoconstriction, surfactant loss over the next day causes atelectasis, and falling cardiac output lowers mixed venous PO2. Together these widen the A-a gradient and cause hypoxemia. Hypoxemia and stimulation of J and irritant receptors drive hyperventilation; because CO2 is easy to eliminate through the remaining lung, PaCO2 falls and the blood gas shows respiratory alkalosis. A normal or rising PaCO2 suggests massive embolism or fatigue."},{"type":"flow","title":"From embolus to right heart strain","steps":["Leg DVT fragment lodges in pulmonary arteries","Obstruction plus serotonin and thromboxane vasoconstriction","Pulmonary vascular resistance rises abruptly","Thin right ventricle dilates; septum bows left","Left ventricular filling and cardiac output fall","Hypotension, RV ischemia, possible sudden death"],"caption":"Acute cor pulmonale: the right ventricle cannot hypertrophy in minutes, so it dilates and fails."},{"type":"p","text":"The right ventricle normally works against a low-resistance bed and cannot generate a high pressure acutely. When about 60% or more of the bed is obstructed, as with a saddle embolus across the main pulmonary artery bifurcation, the right ventricle dilates, the septum shifts into the left ventricle, and left ventricular filling falls (ventricular interdependence). Right ventricular wall tension rises while systemic pressure, and with it right coronary perfusion, falls, so the ventricle becomes ischemic. Death can occur within minutes, often as pulseless electrical activity."},{"type":"table","title":"Findings in pulmonary embolism and their mechanisms","cols":["Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Sudden dyspnea, tachypnea","Hypoxemia and J receptor and irritant receptor stimulation"],["Pleuritic chest pain","Peripheral infarction or hemorrhage irritating the parietal pleura"],["Tachycardia","Sympathetic response to hypoxemia and falling stroke volume"],["Hypoxemia, low PaCO2, respiratory alkalosis","V/Q mismatch with hyperventilation"],["Widened A-a gradient","Low V/Q units, atelectasis, low mixed venous PO2"],["Raised D-dimer","Plasmin degrading cross-linked fibrin; also high in surgery, cancer, pregnancy"],["Right heart strain: raised JVP, loud P2, sinus tachycardia, right axis, RBBB, T inversion V1 to V4, raised troponin and BNP","Acute pressure overload and RV ischemia"]],"caption":"D-dimer is sensitive but not specific, so a normal value argues against embolism when suspicion is low."},{"type":"example","title":"The blood gas in pulmonary embolism","text":"A patient breathing room air at sea level has PaO2 65 mmHg and PaCO2 28 mmHg. Alveolar PO2 equals FiO2 times (760 minus 47) minus PaCO2 divided by 0.8: 0.21 times 713 is about 150, and 28 divided by 0.8 is 35, so PAO2 is about 115 mmHg. The A-a gradient is 115 minus 65, about 50 mmHg, far above the normal of about 5 to 15 mmHg in a young adult. Hypoventilation would give a high PaCO2 and a normal gradient; here the low PaCO2 shows hyperventilation, and the wide gradient shows that oxygen transfer itself is impaired by V/Q mismatch."},{"type":"stop","predict":"An embolus creates dead space, which by itself should raise PaCO2. Why is PaCO2 usually low in pulmonary embolism?","explain":"Hypoxemia and lung receptor stimulation increase minute ventilation far beyond what is needed to clear the extra dead space. CO2 diffuses easily and the CO2 dissociation curve is nearly linear, so overventilated units lower PaCO2, while they cannot add much O2 to blood leaving low V/Q units.","check":"The gas exchange paragraph and the worked example."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-05-C03","obj":"PU-14-E.3","title":"Fat, air, and amniotic fluid embolism","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Nonthrombotic emboli are recognized by their setting and by the extra injury each material causes beyond simple obstruction. They differ from thromboembolism in timing, in the organs involved, and in the blood findings."},{"type":"p","text":"Fat embolism follows fractures of long bones (especially the femur) or the pelvis, and less often orthopedic surgery, liposuction, or extensive burns. Marrow fat globules enter torn marrow veins; they are found in the lungs of most patients with severe skeletal injury, but the fat embolism syndrome develops in only a small minority. Symptoms begin about 24 to 72 hours after injury because the damage is partly biochemical: lipase releases free fatty acids from the globules, and these injure endothelium in the lung and brain, while platelets and neutrophils adhere to the fat. The result is respiratory insufficiency with hypoxemia, neurologic changes (confusion, irritability, delirium, coma), anemia, thrombocytopenia, and a petechial rash over the upper trunk, axillae, and conjunctivae. Deformable globules squeeze through pulmonary capillaries, or through a patent foramen ovale, to reach the brain and skin. Fat in vessels is shown with fat stains such as oil red O on frozen sections."},{"type":"p","text":"Air embolism requires more than about 100 mL of air to cause clinical effects. Venous air enters during insertion, disconnection, or removal of a central venous catheter, especially with the patient upright and venous pressure low, or during neck, chest, and obstetric procedures. Air collects as froth in the right ventricle and pulmonary arteries and blocks outflow. Decompression sickness is a special case: under high pressure, more nitrogen dissolves in blood and tissue (Henry law), and when a diver ascends too fast, it comes out of solution as bubbles. These cause joint and muscle pain (the bends), dyspnea and chest pain with pulmonary edema and hemorrhage (the chokes), and neurologic deficits; repeated exposure causes ischemic necrosis of bone, typically the femoral and humeral heads. Bubbles crossing a right-to-left shunt become arterial emboli and cause stroke."},{"type":"p","text":"Amniotic fluid embolism is rare but has a high mortality. During labor or just after delivery, amniotic fluid enters the maternal circulation through tears in the uterine veins or placental membranes. Fetal squamous cells, lanugo hair, vernix fat, and mucin lodge in maternal pulmonary vessels, and the fluid triggers an anaphylactoid reaction with complement activation and intense pulmonary vasoconstriction, causing acute right heart failure, then left ventricular dysfunction and diffuse alveolar damage. Its tissue factor activates coagulation and causes disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC). The mother develops sudden dyspnea, cyanosis, hypotensive shock, and seizures, followed by bleeding. At autopsy, squamous cells in maternal pulmonary arterioles confirm the diagnosis."},{"type":"table","title":"Comparing emboli to the lung","cols":["Type","Setting","Onset","Distinguishing findings","Extra mechanism"],"rows":[["Thromboembolism","DVT with Virchow triad","Sudden","Pleuritic pain, respiratory alkalosis, high D-dimer","Platelet vasoconstrictors"],["Fat","Long bone or pelvic fracture","24 to 72 hours","Hypoxemia, confusion, upper body petechiae, low platelets","Free fatty acid endothelial injury"],["Air (venous)","Central line, neck or chest procedure","Sudden","Dyspnea, hypotension, possible stroke","Froth blocking RV outflow"],["Decompression","Rapid ascent from depth","Minutes to hours","Joint pain, chokes, neurologic signs; later bone necrosis","Nitrogen bubbles in blood and tissue"],["Amniotic fluid","Labor or just after delivery","Sudden","Collapse, seizures, then bleeding from DIC","Anaphylactoid reaction, tissue factor"]],"caption":"Setting and timing identify the embolus; the extra mechanism explains the findings beyond obstruction."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-05-C04","obj":"PU-14-E.4","title":"Pulmonary infarction","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Only about 10% of pulmonary emboli cause infarction because lung tissue has a dual blood supply. The pulmonary arteries carry deoxygenated blood for gas exchange, while the bronchial arteries, arising from the thoracic aorta or an upper intercostal artery, nourish the airways, vessels, and connective tissue at systemic pressure. Alveoli also receive oxygen directly from inspired air. When a pulmonary artery branch is blocked, bronchial blood flows through anastomoses into the territory and keeps it alive, often with hemorrhage into alveoli but no necrosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Infarction occurs when an embolus blocks a medium or small peripheral artery and the bronchial circulation cannot compensate. The classic setting is left heart failure, in which low cardiac output reduces bronchial inflow and high pulmonary venous pressure impedes drainage; shock, chronic lung disease, and older age add risk. Large central emboli tend to cause right heart strain or sudden death rather than infarction, because the patient dies before necrosis develops or the distal tissue still receives bronchial flow."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why an embolus infarcts the lung","steps":["Embolus blocks a medium or small peripheral artery","Bronchial flow is poor, as in left heart failure","Distal tissue becomes ischemic and dies","Bronchial blood leaks into the dead alveoli","Wedge-shaped red infarct based on the pleura","Fibrinous pleuritis and alveolar hemorrhage"],"caption":"Dual supply prevents infarction until bronchial flow fails."},{"type":"p","text":"About three quarters of infarcts affect the lower lobes, which receive the most blood flow, and they are often multiple. Each is wedge shaped because the occluded artery's territory fans out from the hilum toward the periphery, so the base lies on the pleural surface and the apex points to the hilum. The infarct is hemorrhagic (red) because the loose, spongy lung lets bronchial blood pour into the dead tissue, unlike the pale infarcts of solid organs with a single end-arterial supply such as the heart, kidney, and spleen. Over about 48 hours the red cells lyse, the infarct turns red brown as hemosiderin forms, and it is eventually replaced by a contracted fibrous scar. An infected embolus produces a septic infarct that can form an abscess."},{"type":"p","text":"The clinical features follow the anatomy. The visceral pleura lacks pain fibers, but the parietal pleura is innervated by the intercostal and phrenic nerves, so fibrinous exudate over a pleural-based infarct causes sharp pain on breathing and a pleural friction rub. Blood in the alveoli is coughed up as hemoptysis, and a small, often bloody, exudative pleural effusion and low-grade fever may follow. Imaging may show a pleural-based wedge-shaped opacity."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a patient with left heart failure develop a red, wedge-shaped infarct after a small embolus, when a healthy young adult with the same embolus does not?","explain":"In the healthy lung, bronchial arteries perfuse the blocked territory, so tissue survives with at most hemorrhage. In heart failure, low output and high venous pressure reduce bronchial flow, so the tissue dies, while the remaining bronchial blood still leaks into the necrotic, spongy tissue and makes it hemorrhagic.","check":"The dual supply paragraph and the infarction flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-05-C05","obj":"PU-14-E.5","title":"Pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The normal pulmonary circulation is a low-pressure, low-resistance bed (mean pulmonary artery pressure about 15 mmHg) whose thin, compliant vessels recruit and distend as flow rises. Pulmonary hypertension is a resting mean pulmonary artery pressure above about 20 mmHg (25 mmHg in older sources). Pressure equals flow times resistance plus left atrial pressure, so it rises when resistance increases (narrowed, obliterated, or constricted vessels) or when left atrial pressure is transmitted backward. A normal wedge pressure (15 mmHg or less) indicates a precapillary cause; a raised wedge pressure indicates a postcapillary cause from the left heart."},{"type":"table","title":"Clinical classification of pulmonary hypertension","cols":["Group","Mechanism","Examples"],"rows":[["1. Pulmonary arterial hypertension","Proliferative remodeling of small pulmonary arteries","Idiopathic; heritable (BMPR2); drugs (anorectic agents, methamphetamine); systemic sclerosis; HIV; portal hypertension; left-to-right shunts"],["2. Left heart disease","Raised left atrial pressure transmitted backward","Left ventricular failure, mitral stenosis or regurgitation (most common cause overall)"],["3. Lung disease or hypoxia","Hypoxic vasoconstriction and loss of vascular bed","COPD, interstitial lung disease, sleep apnea, high altitude"],["4. Chronic thromboembolic","Organized emboli obstructing arteries","Recurrent or unresolved pulmonary emboli"],["5. Unclear or multifactorial","Mixed","Sarcoidosis, myeloproliferative and some metabolic disorders"]],"caption":"Groups 1, 3, and 4 are precapillary; group 2 is postcapillary."},{"type":"p","text":"Pulmonary arterial hypertension is a disease of the vessel wall itself. Endothelial dysfunction shifts the balance away from vasodilators and antiproliferative mediators (nitric oxide, prostacyclin) toward vasoconstrictors and mitogens (endothelin-1, thromboxane A2). BMPR2 encodes a receptor of the TGF-beta superfamily that normally signals through SMAD proteins to restrain smooth muscle proliferation and promote apoptosis. Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations are inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern and account for most heritable cases and some apparently sporadic ones, but penetrance is low, so a second hit such as an environmental or genetic modifier is thought to be needed. Idiopathic disease is most common in young women."},{"type":"flow","title":"From BMPR2 loss to cor pulmonale","steps":["BMPR2 loss weakens SMAD antiproliferative signaling","Smooth muscle and endothelial cells proliferate","Medial hypertrophy, intimal fibrosis, plexiform lesions","Pulmonary vascular resistance rises","Right ventricle hypertrophies against higher afterload","RV dilates and fails: cor pulmonale"],"caption":"Remodeling, not just constriction, makes the resistance fixed."},{"type":"p","text":"In all groups the pulmonary arteries remodel. Muscular arteries show medial hypertrophy and arterioles become muscularized; the intima develops fibrosis, concentric and laminated (onion skin) in severe disease. Plexiform lesions are characteristic of advanced group 1 disease and long-standing congenital shunts. The large elastic pulmonary arteries develop atherosclerosis, which does not occur at normal pulmonary pressures."},{"type":"p","text":"The right ventricle first hypertrophies concentrically, then dilates, and tricuspid regurgitation follows. Cor pulmonale is this right ventricular disease caused by lung or pulmonary vascular disease, excluding left heart and congenital causes. Exertional dyspnea and fatigue reflect a cardiac output that cannot rise; exertional chest pain and syncope reflect right ventricular ischemia and a fixed output. Signs include a loud pulmonic component of S2, a right ventricular heave, and, with failure, raised jugular venous pressure, hepatomegaly, ascites, and peripheral edema. The ECG shows right axis deviation and right ventricular hypertrophy."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-05-C06","obj":"PU-14-E.6","title":"Pulmonary arteriovenous malformations and hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A pulmonary arteriovenous malformation (AVM) is a direct connection between a pulmonary artery and a pulmonary vein through a thin-walled sac with no capillary bed. Most occur in hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia (HHT, Osler-Weber-Rendu syndrome), an autosomal dominant disorder of TGF-beta superfamily signaling in endothelium. Mutations in ENG (endoglin) or ACVRL1 (ALK1) account for most cases, and SMAD4 mutations cause a combined juvenile polyposis and HHT syndrome. Abnormal vessel maturation produces fragile, dilated vessels that connect arteries directly to veins."},{"type":"table","title":"Vascular lesions in HHT","cols":["Site","Lesion","Consequence"],"rows":[["Nasal mucosa","Telangiectasias","Recurrent epistaxis, often the first sign"],["Skin, lips, tongue, fingertips","Telangiectasias","Visible red spots that blanch"],["Gastrointestinal tract","Telangiectasias","Chronic bleeding and iron deficiency anemia"],["Lung","AVMs, mostly lower lobes","Right-to-left shunt, paradoxical embolism, hemoptysis"],["Brain and liver","AVMs","Hemorrhage; high-output heart failure from large liver shunts"]],"caption":"Small lesions bleed; large lesions shunt."},{"type":"p","text":"Blood crossing a pulmonary AVM never meets alveolar gas, so it is a true right-to-left shunt. Mixing shunted blood with oxygenated blood lowers PaO2 and widens the A-a gradient, and because shunted blood never sees the enriched alveolar gas, breathing 100% oxygen raises PaO2 only slightly. PaCO2 stays normal or low, since chemoreceptors increase ventilation and the steep CO2 curve lets normal units compensate. Chronic hypoxemia causes cyanosis, digital clubbing, and secondary polycythemia, and a bruit may be heard over the lesion. Because most AVMs are in the lower lobes, which receive more flow when upright, dyspnea and desaturation can worsen on sitting or standing (platypnea and orthodeoxia)."},{"type":"p","text":"The pulmonary capillary bed normally filters small venous clots, air, and bacteria. An AVM bypasses this filter, so venous thrombi reach the systemic arteries and cause paradoxical embolic stroke or transient ischemic attack, and bacteria from the mouth or skin seed the brain, causing cerebral abscess. The thin sac can rupture, producing hemoptysis or hemothorax. On a bubble contrast echocardiogram, bubbles appear in the left atrium more than three beats after the right, which places the shunt in the lung, whereas a patent foramen ovale lets bubbles cross within about three beats."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with a pulmonary AVM and a patient with pneumonia both have PaO2 55 mmHg on room air. Which will show the smaller rise in PaO2 on 100% oxygen, and why?","explain":"The AVM patient. Pneumonia causes partly low V/Q units, whose alveolar PO2 rises with enriched gas. AVM blood bypasses alveoli entirely, so added oxygen reaches only the normal units, whose blood is already nearly saturated and can carry little extra oxygen.","check":"The shunt paragraph and the A-a gradient in C02."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and physiology set the rules. The lung's dual circulation, its low-pressure arteries, and the right ventricle's thin wall explain why emboli usually cause hemorrhage rather than infarction, why sudden obstruction kills through right heart failure, and why chronic pressure overload produces hypertrophy and cor pulmonale. Starling forces and lymphatic drainage explain edema, while V/Q mismatch, dead space, and shunt explain the blood gases: low PaO2 with low PaCO2 in embolism, and hypoxemia that oxygen barely corrects in AVMs."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology links these to other systems. Hematology supplies Virchow triad, D-dimer, and DIC; cardiology supplies left heart failure as the cause of cardiogenic edema, group 2 hypertension, and permissive conditions for infarction; genetics supplies BMPR2, ENG, and ACVRL1, all in TGF-beta signaling; and neurology receives paradoxical emboli and abscesses. The same lab or imaging finding can have several causes: a high D-dimer, a raised wedge pressure, or a wide A-a gradient must be read with the setting, not alone."}],"summary":["Pulmonary edema forms when Starling filtration outpaces lymph flow; cardiogenic edema has a wedge pressure above 18 mmHg and low-protein fluid, noncardiogenic edema a normal wedge pressure.","Chronic pulmonary venous congestion leaves hemosiderin-laden macrophages (heart failure cells).","About 95% of pulmonary emboli come from leg DVT, favored by stasis, endothelial injury, and hypercoagulability.","Embolism causes dead space and low V/Q units, giving hypoxemia, a wide A-a gradient, and hyperventilation with respiratory alkalosis.","A saddle embolus suddenly raises right ventricular afterload, causing acute dilation, septal shift, low output, and possible sudden death.","Fat embolism appears 24 to 72 hours after long bone fracture with hypoxemia, confusion, and petechiae; air embolism follows central lines or rapid ascent; amniotic fluid embolism causes collapse and DIC at delivery.","Pulmonary infarcts are wedge-shaped, red, pleura-based, and mostly lower lobe, occurring when bronchial flow is poor; they cause pleuritic pain and hemoptysis.","Pulmonary hypertension (mean pressure above about 20 mmHg) is grouped as arterial (BMPR2), left heart, lung or hypoxia, chronic thromboembolic, or multifactorial; vessels show medial hypertrophy, intimal fibrosis, and plexiform lesions.","HHT is autosomal dominant; pulmonary AVMs are true shunts that cause oxygen-resistant hypoxemia, paradoxical stroke, and brain abscess."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lung: pulmonary diseases of vascular origin"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Hemodynamic Disorders, Thromboembolic Disease, and Shock: edema, embolism, and infarction"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pulmonary Circulation, Pulmonary Edema, Pleural Fluid"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Pulmonary circulation and ventilation-perfusion matching"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Pulmonary Disease: pulmonary edema and pulmonary embolism"}]},"PU-14-F":{"unit":"PU-14-F","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-06","title":"Neoplasms of the Pulmonary System and Upper Respiratory Tract","subject":"Respiratory System (RESP)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers tumors and tumor-like masses from the nose to the pleura, together with esophageal cancer, which lies directly behind the trachea and shares its late complications. For each lesion it explains the cell of origin, the cause, and how location and secretory behavior produce the findings. Board questions usually give a location, a smoking history, a histologic or marker finding, or a paraneoplastic laboratory pattern and ask for the tumor type, or describe a hoarse voice, a drooping eyelid, or a swollen face and ask which structure the tumor has invaded. If you can trace each finding back to the cell type and its anatomic neighbors, most of these questions resolve.","prerequisites":["Histology of the respiratory tract epithelium from nasal cavity to alveolus, including neuroendocrine cells and type II pneumocytes","Metaplasia, dysplasia, carcinoma in situ, and invasion (general neoplasia)","Tumor suppressor genes TP53 and RB1 and the oncogenic proteins E6 and E7 of HPV","Anatomy of the mediastinum, thoracic inlet, larynx, and nasopharynx, including the phrenic and recurrent laryngeal nerves","Endocrine control of calcium (PTH), cortisol (ACTH), and water balance (ADH)"],"connections":[{"to":"Disorders of the pleural cavity and lung expansion (PU-14-D)","how":"Malignant pleural effusion, atelectasis from bronchial obstruction, and asbestos-related pleural plaques are the pleural and expansion consequences of the tumors in this chapter."},{"to":"Diseases of the upper respiratory tract (PU-14-C)","how":"Chronic rhinosinusitis, otitis media, and laryngeal inflammation must be separated from polyps, nasopharyngeal carcinoma, and laryngeal tumors that mimic them."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal neoplasms (GI-13-K)","how":"Esophageal carcinoma and the gut carcinoid are treated with the digestive tract; this chapter explains their thoracic and airway effects and why bronchial carcinoids differ."},{"to":"Diseases of endocrine hyperfunction (EN-12-A)","how":"Ectopic ACTH, ADH, and PTHrP from lung cancers are major causes of Cushing syndrome, SIADH, and hypercalcemia of malignancy."},{"to":"Neurological diseases (NE-20)","how":"Lambert-Eaton syndrome, Horner syndrome, and brachial plexus deficits connect lung tumors to the neuromuscular junction and the peripheral and autonomic nervous systems."}],"terms":[{"term":"Lepidic growth","def":"Growth of tumor cells along the surface of intact alveolar walls without destroying the alveolar architecture."},{"term":"Neuroendocrine tumor","def":"A tumor of cells that contain dense-core secretory granules and express markers such as chromogranin and synaptophysin."},{"term":"Nuclear molding","def":"Indentation of one nucleus by its neighbor, seen when cells with scant cytoplasm are packed tightly, as in small cell carcinoma."},{"term":"Keratin pearl","def":"A concentric whorl of keratinized squamous cells within a tumor, a sign of squamous differentiation."},{"term":"Intercellular bridges","def":"Desmosomal junctions between squamous cells, visible by light microscopy as fine spines crossing the space between cells."},{"term":"Paraneoplastic syndrome","def":"Signs and symptoms produced at a distance by tumor-derived hormones, cytokines, or autoantibodies rather than by local invasion or metastasis."},{"term":"Barrett esophagus","def":"Replacement of the distal esophageal squamous epithelium by intestinal-type columnar epithelium with goblet cells, a response to chronic reflux."},{"term":"Superior sulcus (Pancoast) tumor","def":"A carcinoma of the lung apex that invades the thoracic inlet structures, including the lower brachial plexus and cervical sympathetic chain."},{"term":"Solitary pulmonary nodule","def":"A single round opacity of 3 cm or less completely surrounded by lung tissue."},{"term":"Field cancerization","def":"Widespread carcinogen-induced genetic damage across an exposed mucosal surface, which favors multiple precursor lesions and separate primary tumors."},{"term":"Koilocyte","def":"An HPV-infected squamous cell with a wrinkled nucleus and a clear perinuclear halo."},{"term":"Hamartoma (pulmonary)","def":"The most common benign lung tumor, composed mainly of mature cartilage and fat, now known to be a clonal neoplasm."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-06-C01","obj":"PU-14-F.1","title":"Adenocarcinoma and adenocarcinoma in situ","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In the lung, the cell of origin largely decides where a tumor sits, what it looks like, and how it behaves. Adenocarcinoma is a malignant tumor with glandular differentiation that arises from the peripheral bronchiolar and alveolar epithelium, including club cells and type II pneumocytes. It accounts for about 40% or more of lung cancers, making it the most common type overall, and it is the most common type in women and in people who have never smoked. Even so, most adenocarcinomas occur in smokers."},{"type":"p","text":"Because it arises in the periphery, adenocarcinoma usually presents as a peripheral nodule or mass rather than as a central airway lesion and causes cough and hemoptysis later than central tumors do. It also reaches the pleura readily, a common source of malignant effusion. Histologically the tumor forms glands, papillae, or solid sheets, and the cells produce mucin, which stains with mucicarmine or periodic acid-Schiff. Most express TTF-1 (thyroid transcription factor 1) and napsin A, which help confirm a lung primary."},{"type":"p","text":"The molecular profile differs with smoking history. Tumors in never smokers more often carry activating mutations of EGFR (epidermal growth factor receptor), especially in East Asian women, or rearrangements of ALK, whereas tumors in smokers more often carry KRAS mutations. Each keeps growth signaling switched on without growth factor stimulation."},{"type":"h","text":"Adenocarcinoma in situ"},{"type":"p","text":"Peripheral adenocarcinoma is thought to develop stepwise, as shown below. Adenocarcinoma in situ (AIS), previously included under the term bronchioloalveolar carcinoma, is a lesion of 3 cm or less in which atypical cells grow purely along intact alveolar walls, the pattern called lepidic growth. The alveolar framework is preserved and there is no stromal, vascular, or pleural invasion, so the lesion cannot metastasize and its prognosis is excellent."},{"type":"p","text":"Because AIS lines alveoli without filling them, the alveoli still contain air, and on CT the lesion appears as a hazy ground-glass opacity; as invasion and stromal fibrosis develop, a solid component appears. Mucinous tumors can spread through the airspaces and produce consolidation that resembles a pneumonia that does not resolve."},{"type":"flow","title":"Stepwise progression of peripheral adenocarcinoma","steps":["Atypical adenomatous hyperplasia: atypical alveolar lining cells, 5 mm or less","Adenocarcinoma in situ: pure lepidic growth, 3 cm or less","Minimally invasive adenocarcinoma: invasive focus 5 mm or less","Invasive adenocarcinoma: stromal, vascular, or pleural invasion"],"caption":"Lepidic growth preserves alveolar walls and air; invasion destroys them and allows metastasis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-06-C02","obj":"PU-14-F.2","title":"Bronchial carcinoid","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Neuroendocrine cells are scattered through the bronchial epithelium and contain dense-core granules that store amines and peptides. Tumors of these cells form a spectrum graded by mitotic rate and necrosis: typical carcinoid (low grade), atypical carcinoid (intermediate grade), and the high-grade large cell neuroendocrine carcinoma and small cell carcinoma. Bronchial carcinoid sits at the low-grade end. It accounts for only a few percent of lung tumors, tends to affect younger adults than other lung cancers, and has no established link to smoking."},{"type":"p","text":"Microscopically, typical carcinoid consists of nests, cords, or ribbons of uniform cells with round nuclei, finely granular chromatin, few mitoses, and no necrosis. The cells are positive for chromogranin and synaptophysin, reflecting their secretory granules, and electron microscopy shows dense-core neurosecretory granules. Atypical carcinoid has more mitoses or foci of necrosis and metastasizes more often."},{"type":"p","text":"Most arise in main or lobar bronchi as a fleshy, polypoid mass covered by intact mucosa, and symptoms come from the mass rather than from hormones. Partial obstruction causes cough, localized wheeze, and recurrent pneumonia in the same segment, complete obstruction causes collapse (atelectasis) of the lung beyond it, and the vascular surface causes hemoptysis. Typical carcinoids grow slowly and seldom metastasize."},{"type":"p","text":"Carcinoid syndrome is the systemic effect of secreted serotonin, kinins, and other vasoactive products: episodic flushing, diarrhea, and bronchospasm. It is rare with bronchial carcinoids because most secrete little. Venous drainage also matters. Products of a gut carcinoid enter the portal vein and are inactivated in the liver, so the syndrome there usually requires liver metastases. Blood from a lung tumor returns largely through the pulmonary veins to the left heart without passing the liver, so a large secreting bronchial carcinoid can cause the syndrome without liver involvement, and rare valve fibrosis then favors the left side. Urinary 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid (5-HIAA), the main serotonin metabolite, rises when the syndrome is present."},{"type":"table","title":"Two neuroendocrine lung tumors","cols":["Feature","Typical bronchial carcinoid","Small cell carcinoma"],"rows":[["Grade","Low","High"],["Smoking","No established link","Nearly all patients smoke"],["Cells","Uniform, moderate cytoplasm, nests and ribbons","Small, scant cytoplasm, nuclear molding"],["Mitoses and necrosis","Few mitoses, no necrosis","Numerous mitoses, extensive necrosis"],["Markers","Chromogranin, synaptophysin","Chromogranin, synaptophysin, CD56"],["Behavior","Slow growth, rare metastasis","Early widespread metastasis"],["Typical presentation","Endobronchial polyp with obstruction or hemoptysis","Central mass with bulky nodes, paraneoplastic syndromes"]],"caption":"Both share neuroendocrine markers, so grade, cell appearance, and behavior separate them."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-06-C03","obj":"PU-14-F.3","title":"Esophageal carcinoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The esophagus runs through the posterior mediastinum directly behind the trachea and left main bronchus, so its cancers have respiratory complications. The esophagus is lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium, and its two main cancers reflect two kinds of injury. Squamous cell carcinoma arises from the native squamous lining; adenocarcinoma arises from columnar epithelium that has replaced the squamous lining in the distal esophagus. Squamous cell carcinoma is more common worldwide, whereas adenocarcinoma is now the more common type in the United States and other Western countries."},{"type":"p","text":"Squamous cell carcinoma develops through squamous dysplasia after long chemical and thermal injury, mostly in the upper and middle thirds. Tobacco and alcohol act together: alcohol dissolves tobacco carcinogens and its metabolite acetaldehyde is mutagenic. Very hot beverages cause repeated thermal injury, and dietary nitrosamines alkylate DNA. In achalasia, failure of the lower esophageal sphincter to relax leaves food stagnant in a dilated esophagus, keeping the mucosa chronically irritated; caustic (lye) strictures carry a similar risk."},{"type":"p","text":"Adenocarcinoma follows chronic gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD). Repeated exposure to acid and bile causes the distal squamous lining to be replaced by intestinal-type columnar epithelium with goblet cells, Barrett esophagus. The metaplasia is an adaptation, but the new cells can accumulate mutations such as TP53 and progress through dysplasia to invasive carcinoma, so these tumors arise in the distal third and near the gastroesophageal junction. Obesity increases risk by promoting reflux, and white men are affected most."},{"type":"p","text":"Both types cause the same mechanical symptoms. A growing mass narrows the lumen, so dysphagia is progressive, first for solids and later for liquids, and weight loss follows reduced intake and tumor catabolism. Most of the esophagus is covered by adventitia rather than serosa, so there is no barrier to local spread, and its rich submucosal lymphatics allow early nodal spread. A tumor that erodes into the trachea or left main bronchus creates a tracheoesophageal fistula: swallowed food and liquid enter the airway, causing cough with swallowing and recurrent aspiration pneumonia. Invasion of the left recurrent laryngeal nerve adds hoarseness."},{"type":"table","title":"Squamous cell carcinoma versus adenocarcinoma of the esophagus","cols":["Feature","Squamous cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma"],"rows":[["Usual site","Upper and middle third","Distal third, gastroesophageal junction"],["Cell of origin","Native squamous epithelium","Columnar metaplastic epithelium"],["Precursor","Squamous dysplasia","Barrett esophagus with dysplasia"],["Main risks","Smoking, alcohol, very hot drinks, achalasia, nitrosamines, caustic stricture","GERD, Barrett esophagus, obesity"],["Epidemiology","Most common type worldwide","Most common type in Western countries"]],"caption":"Both present with progressive dysphagia, weight loss, and possible tracheoesophageal fistula."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-06-C04","obj":"PU-14-F.4","title":"Laryngeal carcinoma, vocal fold nodules, and papillomatosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The larynx has a supraglottis (epiglottis and false vocal folds), glottis (true vocal folds), and subglottis. About 95% of laryngeal cancers are squamous cell carcinomas. They arise from the squamous epithelium of the true folds, or from squamous metaplasia elsewhere, through hyperplasia, dysplasia, and carcinoma in situ. Smoking is the dominant cause and alcohol multiplies its effect, so most patients are older men who smoke."},{"type":"p","text":"Glottic tumors are the most common and produce hoarseness early. Voice requires two pliable folds that meet in the midline and vibrate, and even a small tumor stiffens the mucosa and adds mass, so the folds vibrate irregularly and close incompletely, and a persistent voice change draws attention while the tumor is small. The glottis also has sparse lymphatics, so nodal metastasis comes late. Supraglottic tumors cause few early voice changes, drain into a rich lymphatic network, and often present with neck node metastases, sore throat, or pain referred to the ear. Advanced tumors fix the vocal fold and obstruct the airway, causing stridor."},{"type":"p","text":"Vocal fold nodules (singer's nodules) are small, usually bilateral and symmetric swellings at the junction of the anterior and middle thirds of the true folds, the point of greatest impact during vibration. They form from repeated collision of the folds in people who overuse the voice, such as singers, teachers, and children who shout, and they consist of edematous or fibrous connective tissue covered by squamous epithelium. They are reactive, not neoplastic, and they do not become malignant."},{"type":"p","text":"Squamous papillomas are benign tumors of the true folds caused by low-risk HPV types 6 and 11. In adults they are usually single. In children they are typically multiple and recur after removal, called juvenile (recurrent respiratory) papillomatosis, and are thought to follow transmission of the virus from the mother during birth. Each papilloma is a finger-like projection of squamous epithelium around a fibrovascular core, often with koilocytes. They cause hoarseness and can spread down the airway. Malignant change is rare because the E6 and E7 proteins of types 6 and 11 bind p53 and Rb only weakly."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of hoarseness at the true vocal fold","cols":["Lesion","Cause","Usual patient","Laterality and course"],"rows":[["Glottic squamous cell carcinoma","Smoking, alcohol","Older adult smoker","Usually one side; invasive, late nodal spread"],["Vocal fold nodules","Voice overuse","Singers, teachers, children who shout","Bilateral; benign, reactive"],["Squamous papilloma","HPV 6 and 11","Child (multiple, recurrent) or adult (single)","Benign; recurrence common in children"]],"caption":"Hoarseness arises early at the glottis because any mass alters vocal fold vibration and closure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-06-C05","obj":"PU-14-F.5","title":"Malignant mesothelioma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Malignant mesothelioma is a cancer of the mesothelial cells that line the pleura (most often), the peritoneum, and the pericardium. Most cases follow occupational or environmental asbestos exposure, with a latency of several decades, usually about 25 to 45 years. Amphibole fibers such as crocidolite are long, thin, and straight; they reach the pleura and persist, and they are more carcinogenic than the curly serpentine fiber chrysotile. Persistent fibers drive chronic inflammation, reactive oxygen species, and DNA damage in mesothelial cells. Loss of the tumor suppressor BAP1 is common, and germline BAP1 mutations explain some familial cases."},{"type":"p","text":"Smoking multiplies the risk of lung carcinoma in asbestos-exposed workers, but it does not increase the risk of mesothelioma. Even in an asbestos-exposed smoker, lung carcinoma remains more common than mesothelioma. Pleural plaques, dense and often calcified collagen plaques on the parietal pleura, are the most common asbestos lesion; they mark exposure but are not precursors of mesothelioma. Ferruginous bodies, asbestos fibers coated with iron-containing protein, may be found in the lung."},{"type":"p","text":"The tumor begins as small pleural nodules that spread and fuse into a thick sheet encasing the lung like a rind and obliterating the pleural space. Patients present with chest pain from involvement of the sensitive parietal pleura, dyspnea, and a recurrent pleural effusion that is often hemorrhagic, because tumor on the pleural surface bleeds and keeps producing fluid. Histologic patterns are epithelioid (most common), sarcomatoid, and biphasic."},{"type":"h","text":"Mesothelioma or metastatic adenocarcinoma"},{"type":"p","text":"Metastatic adenocarcinoma, from lung, breast, ovary, or stomach, is much more common in the pleura than mesothelioma, and both can produce bloody effusions and gland-like or papillary patterns. Cytokeratin does not separate them, because mesothelial cells are epithelial and express it too. The decision rests on lineage markers and on the shape of the cells' microvilli, as the table shows."},{"type":"table","title":"Mesothelioma versus metastatic adenocarcinoma in the pleura","cols":["Finding","Mesothelioma","Metastatic adenocarcinoma"],"rows":[["Cytokeratin","Positive","Positive"],["Calretinin, WT1, D2-40","Positive","Usually negative"],["CEA, MOC-31, BerEP4 (TTF-1 if lung primary)","Negative","Positive"],["Intracellular mucin","Absent","Often present"],["Microvilli on electron microscopy","Long and slender","Short and stubby"],["Gross pattern","Rind encasing the lung","Discrete nodules or effusion"],["Main cause","Asbestos; no smoking link","Primary carcinoma elsewhere"]],"caption":"Mesothelial markers and microvillus shape identify the cell of origin."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: identifying a pleural tumor","text":"A 68-year-old retired shipyard worker who never smoked has dyspnea and a bloody right pleural effusion that returns after drainage; CT shows a thick pleural rind around the lung. Biopsy shows malignant cells forming tubules. Step 1: cytokeratin is positive, which proves only epithelial lineage, and both candidates are epithelial. Step 2: calretinin and WT1 are positive while CEA and TTF-1 are negative, which points to mesothelial cells. Step 3: electron microscopy shows long slender microvilli, a mesothelial feature, not the short stubby microvilli of glandular cells. Step 4: decades-old asbestos exposure fits, and the lack of smoking is irrelevant because smoking does not raise mesothelioma risk. Diagnosis: malignant mesothelioma. Had CEA and TTF-1 been positive with calretinin negative, the answer would be metastatic lung adenocarcinoma."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-06-C06","obj":"PU-14-F.6","title":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma arises most often in the lateral recess of the nasopharynx, just behind the opening of the eustachian (auditory) tube. Its cause combines Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) infection, genetic susceptibility, and environment. Incidence is far higher in southern China, Southeast Asia, and parts of Africa (especially North Africa) than in most Western countries. In high-risk regions, eating salt-cured fish, which contains nitrosamines, from early childhood is a recognized cofactor, and certain HLA types raise susceptibility. In parts of Africa the disease also affects children and adolescents."},{"type":"p","text":"EBV infects nasopharyngeal epithelial cells in a latent form. Nearly every tumor cell carries the viral genome, and the viral DNA is clonal, showing that infection came before transformation. The latent protein LMP1 (latent membrane protein 1) behaves like a permanently activated CD40 receptor and switches on NF-kappaB, promoting proliferation and survival. Tumor cells contain EBV-encoded small RNAs (EBER), which can be detected by in situ hybridization."},{"type":"p","text":"The tumors are keratinizing squamous cell carcinomas or nonkeratinizing carcinomas, the latter subdivided into differentiated and undifferentiated forms. Nonkeratinizing tumors, especially the undifferentiated form, dominate in high-incidence regions and are the most consistently EBV positive. The undifferentiated form shows large epithelial cells with vesicular nuclei and prominent nucleoli, with indistinct cell borders, mixed with many reactive lymphocytes, a pattern once called lymphoepithelioma. Cytokeratin staining identifies the malignant cells as epithelial and separates the tumor from lymphoma."},{"type":"p","text":"The primary tumor is often small and silent in this hidden space, so the most common presenting sign is a painless, firm, enlarging upper neck mass, because the nasopharynx drains through a rich lymphatic network to the retropharyngeal and deep cervical nodes. A tumor at the eustachian tube opening blocks ventilation of the middle ear; the mucosa absorbs the trapped gas, pressure falls, and a serous transudate fills the middle ear, causing one-sided conductive hearing loss and fullness. One-sided serous otitis media in an adult should therefore raise suspicion. Larger tumors cause nasal obstruction and bloody nasal discharge, and invasion of the skull base can produce cranial nerve palsies."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-06-C07","obj":"PU-14-F.7","title":"Nasal polyps and vocal fold polyps","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Nasal and vocal fold polyps are inflammatory or reactive projections of mucosa, not neoplasms, but they are the most common upper airway masses and must be separated from tumors."},{"type":"p","text":"Nasal polyps are smooth, pale, grape-like, edematous outgrowths of the sinonasal mucosa, usually bilateral and arising mainly from the ethmoid sinuses and middle meatus. They consist of loose, edematous stroma covered by respiratory epithelium with a thickened basement membrane and an inflammatory infiltrate that in most adults is rich in eosinophils. They develop in chronic rhinosinusitis, often with allergy or asthma, especially with type 2 inflammation, in which IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13 recruit eosinophils and increase vascular permeability, so fluid collects in the lamina propria and the mucosa sags into the nasal cavity."},{"type":"p","text":"Aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease combines asthma, chronic rhinosinusitis with nasal polyps, and respiratory reactions to aspirin and other COX-1 inhibitors. These patients overproduce cysteinyl leukotrienes and depend on prostaglandin E2 to restrain their mast cells and leukotriene synthesis, so blocking COX-1 sets off the sequence below."},{"type":"flow","title":"Aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease","steps":["Aspirin or another NSAID inhibits COX-1","Prostaglandin E2 falls, removing its brake on mast cells","Arachidonic acid shifts to the 5-lipoxygenase pathway","Cysteinyl leukotrienes and mast cell mediators surge","Bronchospasm, nasal congestion, and rhinorrhea follow within hours"],"caption":"The reaction is not IgE mediated; it results from altered arachidonic acid metabolism."},{"type":"p","text":"Nasal polyps are unusual in children, and their presence should prompt evaluation for cystic fibrosis. Defective CFTR chloride transport makes sinus mucus thick and poorly cleared, leading to chronic infection and neutrophil-rich inflammation. A single, one-sided nasal mass, especially with bleeding, does not fit an inflammatory polyp and raises concern for a tumor."},{"type":"p","text":"Polyps obstruct the nasal airway, causing congestion and mouth breathing, and block sinus openings, predisposing to recurrent sinusitis. Anosmia (loss of smell) results mainly because polyps prevent odorant-carrying air from reaching the olfactory epithelium in the roof of the nasal cavity, with inflammation of the olfactory mucosa adding a sensory component."},{"type":"p","text":"Vocal fold polyps form on the free edge of a true vocal fold, usually on one side, after vocal strain such as shouting or chronic irritation from smoking. Shearing forces injure small vessels in the superficial lamina propria, and the edema or hemorrhage organizes into a soft polyp that keeps the folds from closing evenly and disturbs their vibration, causing hoarseness or a breathy voice. In heavy smokers, diffuse bilateral swelling of the superficial lamina propria (polypoid degeneration) lowers the pitch of the voice."},{"type":"table","title":"Benign upper airway masses","cols":["Lesion","Site","Usual laterality","Associations","Main effect"],"rows":[["Nasal polyp","Ethmoid sinuses, middle meatus","Bilateral","Chronic rhinosinusitis, atopy, asthma, aspirin-exacerbated disease, cystic fibrosis in a child","Obstruction, anosmia"],["Vocal fold polyp","Free edge of one true fold","One-sided","Voice strain, smoking","Hoarseness"],["Vocal fold nodule","Junction of anterior and middle thirds of true folds","Bilateral","Chronic voice overuse","Hoarseness"]],"caption":"A one-sided nasal mass or a nasal polyp in a child is atypical and needs further explanation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-06-C08","obj":"PU-14-F.8","title":"Small cell carcinoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Small cell carcinoma is a high-grade neuroendocrine carcinoma that makes up about 15% of lung cancers. It is the type most strongly tied to smoking, since nearly all patients are current or former heavy smokers. It usually arises centrally in the major bronchi and spreads in the bronchial wall and peribronchial tissue, so it often appears as a large hilar and mediastinal mass with bulky nodes rather than an endobronchial polyp."},{"type":"p","text":"The cells are small, usually less than about three resting lymphocytes in diameter, round, oval, or spindle shaped, with scant cytoplasm, finely granular salt-and-pepper chromatin, and absent or inconspicuous nucleoli. With so little cytoplasm, neighboring nuclei press into one another, producing nuclear molding. Mitoses are numerous, necrosis is extensive, and the fragile cells are easily crushed in biopsies. As neuroendocrine cells they contain dense-core granules and express chromogranin, synaptophysin, and CD56, which separates them from lymphocytes and from poorly differentiated squamous carcinoma."},{"type":"p","text":"Nearly all small cell carcinomas have lost function of both TP53 and RB1. Without these tumor suppressors the cells lose the G1 checkpoint and the normal response to DNA damage, so they divide rapidly while tolerating genomic injury. The result is a very high growth fraction and early spread through lymphatics and blood vessels. Most patients already have metastases at diagnosis, commonly to the liver, bone, brain, and adrenal glands, so the disease behaves as systemic rather than local."},{"type":"p","text":"Neuroendocrine differentiation also explains the paraneoplastic syndromes. The cells keep the machinery to make and secrete peptide hormones, so they can release ACTH, causing Cushing syndrome, or ADH, causing the syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion (SIADH) with hyponatremia. Their neuronal antigens can provoke autoantibodies that cross-react with the nervous system, the basis of Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome and paraneoplastic encephalomyelitis. The mechanisms are explained in PU-14-F.10."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Small cell carcinoma and typical carcinoid both arise from bronchial neuroendocrine cells and both stain for chromogranin. Why is one usually metastatic at diagnosis while the other rarely spreads?","explain":"Lineage determines markers and secretory ability, but grade determines behavior. Small cell carcinoma has lost TP53 and RB1, divides rapidly, and invades vessels early; typical carcinoid keeps these controls, grows slowly, and stays local for years.","check":"The loss of TP53 and RB1 in this section and the comparison table in PU-14-F.2."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-06-C09","obj":"PU-14-F.9","title":"Non-small cell carcinomas: squamous cell and large cell","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Non-small cell lung carcinoma groups adenocarcinoma, squamous cell carcinoma, and large cell carcinoma, together about 85% of lung cancers. They are grouped because they grow and spread more slowly than small cell carcinoma, so a tumor can still be confined to the chest when found. Adenocarcinoma is covered in PU-14-F.1; this section completes the comparison."},{"type":"p","text":"Squamous cell carcinoma makes up about 25% to 30% of lung cancers and is strongly linked to smoking. It arises centrally in main or lobar bronchi, where smoke particles deposit heavily. Chronic irritation converts the pseudostratified ciliated epithelium into squamous epithelium (squamous metaplasia), an adaptation that sacrifices mucociliary clearance; continued exposure then produces dysplasia, carcinoma in situ, and invasive carcinoma. The tumor shows squamous features: keratin pearls, individual cells with dense pink keratinized cytoplasm, and intercellular bridges, the desmosomes between cells seen by light microscopy. It expresses p40, p63, and cytokeratin 5/6."},{"type":"p","text":"Central location explains early airway symptoms: cough, hemoptysis, and obstruction with atelectasis or postobstructive pneumonia, and the tumor sheds cells that can be found in sputum. Large squamous tumors outgrow their blood supply; the necrotic center liquefies and drains into a bronchus, leaving a cavity with an air-fluid level. Squamous cell carcinoma is also the lung cancer most often associated with PTHrP secretion and hypercalcemia."},{"type":"p","text":"Large cell carcinoma is an undifferentiated malignant epithelial tumor that shows no glandular, squamous, or neuroendocrine differentiation by light microscopy and immunostaining, so it is a diagnosis of exclusion. It consists of sheets of large cells with moderate to abundant cytoplasm, large vesicular nuclei, and prominent nucleoli. It tends to arise peripherally, grows rapidly, and metastasizes early, giving it a poor prognosis. Improved immunostaining has reclassified many former large cell tumors as poorly differentiated adenocarcinoma or squamous carcinoma, so it is now a small fraction of cases."},{"type":"table","title":"Lung carcinoma types compared","cols":["Type","Share (about)","Location","Smoking link","Histology and markers","Characteristic association"],"rows":[["Adenocarcinoma","40% or more","Peripheral","Present, but most common type in never smokers","Glands, mucin; TTF-1, napsin A; lepidic in AIS","Clubbing and hypertrophic osteoarthropathy; EGFR, ALK, KRAS"],["Squamous cell","25% to 30%","Central","Strong","Keratin pearls, intercellular bridges; p40, p63","PTHrP hypercalcemia, cavitation"],["Large cell","Small fraction today","Peripheral","Present","Undifferentiated large cells, prominent nucleoli","Rapid growth, poor prognosis"],["Small cell","About 15%","Central","Strongest","Small cells, nuclear molding; chromogranin, synaptophysin","ACTH, ADH, Lambert-Eaton; early metastasis"],["Carcinoid","Few percent","Central, endobronchial","None established","Uniform nests; chromogranin, synaptophysin","Obstruction; rare carcinoid syndrome"]],"caption":"The two central tumors, squamous cell and small cell, have the strongest smoking link; peripheral tumors are adenocarcinoma and large cell."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-06-C10","obj":"PU-14-F.10","title":"Paraneoplastic syndromes of lung cancer","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Paraneoplastic syndromes act at a distance through hormones, cytokines, or autoantibodies. They may precede discovery of the tumor, and each points to a likely cell type."},{"type":"p","text":"Squamous cell carcinoma secretes parathyroid hormone related peptide (PTHrP), which binds the same receptor as PTH. In bone it increases osteoblast expression of RANKL, which activates osteoclasts and releases calcium; in the kidney it increases calcium reabsorption and phosphate excretion. Serum calcium rises and phosphate falls, while native PTH is suppressed by the high calcium. Hypercalcemia causes polyuria, constipation, confusion, and a short QT interval."},{"type":"p","text":"Ectopic ACTH from small cell carcinoma drives cortisol production; because levels are high and the course is rapid, patients often show hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis, hypertension, hyperglycemia, proximal weakness, and hyperpigmentation (ACTH stimulates melanocortin receptors on melanocytes) before a full cushingoid appearance develops. Ectopic ADH acts on V2 receptors to insert aquaporin-2 channels in the collecting duct, so water is retained despite low plasma osmolality. Serum sodium falls below about 135 mEq/L (reference about 135 to 145), urine is inappropriately concentrated with sodium usually above about 40 mEq/L, and the patient is euvolemic; severe hyponatremia swells brain cells, causing confusion and seizures."},{"type":"p","text":"Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome follows the sequence below, causing proximal weakness, reduced reflexes, and dry mouth. With sustained contraction, calcium accumulates in the terminal and release increases, so strength briefly improves; high-frequency nerve stimulation gives an increasing muscle response."},{"type":"flow","title":"Small cell carcinoma to Lambert-Eaton syndrome","steps":["Tumor cells express P/Q-type voltage-gated calcium channels","Immune response produces antibodies against these channels","Antibodies also bind channels on motor nerve terminals","Less calcium enters with each nerve action potential","Less acetylcholine is released; proximal weakness and hyporeflexia"],"caption":"Autoantibodies raised against tumor antigens cross-react with the presynaptic membrane."},{"type":"p","text":"Hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy (clubbing, periosteal new bone on distal long bones, and arthralgia) is most associated with adenocarcinoma. A leading explanation is that megakaryocytes or platelet clumps escape lung filtration, lodge in fingertip vessels, and release platelet-derived growth factor and vascular endothelial growth factor."},{"type":"table","title":"Paraneoplastic syndromes matched to lung cancer type","cols":["Syndrome","Usual tumor","Mediator","Key findings"],"rows":[["Hypercalcemia of malignancy","Squamous cell","PTHrP","High calcium, low phosphate, suppressed PTH"],["Ectopic Cushing syndrome","Small cell (also carcinoid)","ACTH","Hypokalemic alkalosis, hypertension, hyperpigmentation, high cortisol and ACTH"],["SIADH","Small cell","ADH","Euvolemic hyponatremia, concentrated urine, urine sodium high"],["Lambert-Eaton syndrome","Small cell","Antibodies to presynaptic calcium channels","Proximal weakness improving with effort, hyporeflexia, dry mouth"],["Hypertrophic osteoarthropathy","Especially adenocarcinoma","Proposed platelet-derived growth factors","Clubbing, periosteal new bone, arthralgia"]],"caption":"Squamous cell secretes PTHrP; small cell secretes peptide hormones and triggers neural autoantibodies."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: two confused smokers","text":"Two heavy smokers with central lung masses are confused. Patient A: calcium 13.2 mg/dL (reference about 8.5 to 10.5), low phosphate, low PTH. Patient B: sodium 118 mEq/L, low plasma osmolality, urine osmolality above plasma, urine sodium 60 mEq/L, normal volume status. Step 1: in A, high calcium with suppressed PTH means the parathyroids are not the source, and low phosphate fits a PTH-like hormone acting on the kidney: PTHrP from squamous cell carcinoma. Step 2: in B, the kidney concentrates urine while plasma is dilute, so ADH is acting without an osmotic stimulus; with euvolemia this is SIADH from small cell carcinoma. Both tumors are central, so the laboratory pattern, not location, separates them."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with a hilar mass has proximal leg weakness. Would you expect strength to improve or worsen during several seconds of maximal effort?","explain":"Improve. In Lambert-Eaton syndrome the defect is reduced calcium entry into the nerve terminal; repeated firing lets calcium accumulate and increases acetylcholine release. In myasthenia gravis the defect is postsynaptic, so weakness worsens with use.","check":"The Lambert-Eaton flow and the paragraph on Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-06-C11","obj":"PU-14-F.11","title":"Local effects of lung cancer spread","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Local signs of a lung tumor reveal its position. The superior sulcus is the groove for the subclavian vessels at the lung apex. A superior sulcus (Pancoast) tumor, usually a non-small cell carcinoma, grows out of the apex into the thoracic inlet, where it meets the lower trunk of the brachial plexus (C8 and T1 roots), the cervical sympathetic chain and stellate ganglion, the subclavian vessels, and the upper ribs."},{"type":"p","text":"Invasion of C8 and T1 causes shoulder pain radiating along the medial arm, forearm, and ulnar side of the hand, with weakness and wasting of the intrinsic hand muscles. Invasion of the sympathetic chain interrupts the pathway shown below and produces Horner syndrome on the same side: ptosis from loss of sympathetic tone to the superior tarsal muscle, miosis from unopposed parasympathetic constriction of the pupil, and anhidrosis of the face. Rib destruction adds severe local pain."},{"type":"flow","title":"Sympathetic pathway to the eye interrupted at the lung apex","steps":["Hypothalamic neurons descend to the ciliospinal center (C8 to T2)","Preganglionic fibers exit mainly at T1 and cross the lung apex","Fibers ascend in the sympathetic chain to the superior cervical ganglion","Postganglionic fibers follow the carotid arteries to the eye and face","Apical tumor cuts the chain: ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis"],"caption":"The preganglionic fibers pass directly over the pleural dome, so apical tumors interrupt them."},{"type":"p","text":"The superior vena cava is a thin-walled, low-pressure vein in the right side of the mediastinum, easily compressed or invaded by right-sided central tumors and nodes, most often small cell carcinoma, and also by lymphoma. Venous return from the head, neck, and arms backs up, causing a swollen, plethoric face, distended neck and chest wall collateral veins, arm edema, headache, and dyspnea."},{"type":"p","text":"The left recurrent laryngeal nerve loops beneath the aortic arch beside the ligamentum arteriosum and then ascends in the groove between trachea and esophagus, so a left hilar tumor or enlarged nodes beneath the aortic arch can paralyze it. The right nerve hooks around the subclavian artery at the root of the neck and is involved less often. Loss of the nerve paralyzes all intrinsic laryngeal muscles except the cricothyroid, so the fold on that side cannot close fully and the voice becomes hoarse and breathy. The phrenic nerve (C3 to C5) descends anterior to the lung root on each side; invasion paralyzes the hemidiaphragm, which is elevated on imaging and moves paradoxically upward during a sniff."},{"type":"table","title":"Structures invaded by lung cancer and the resulting signs","cols":["Structure","Where the tumor is","Result"],"rows":[["Cervical sympathetic chain, stellate ganglion","Apex (superior sulcus)","Horner syndrome on the same side"],["Brachial plexus lower trunk (C8, T1)","Apex (superior sulcus)","Ulnar-side arm pain, intrinsic hand weakness"],["Superior vena cava","Right central or mediastinal","Facial plethora and swelling, distended neck veins, arm edema"],["Left recurrent laryngeal nerve","Left hilum, below the aortic arch","Hoarseness from vocal fold paralysis"],["Phrenic nerve","Anterior to the lung root","Elevated, paralyzed hemidiaphragm"],["Esophagus","Mediastinal nodes or tumor","Dysphagia"],["Pericardium","Direct or lymphatic spread","Pericardial effusion, possible tamponade"]],"caption":"Local signs map the tumor onto thoracic anatomy."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A smoker with a right apical mass has a small right pupil and a drooping right eyelid. Will the right side of the forehead sweat normally?","explain":"No. The tumor interrupts preganglionic fibers before they reach the superior cervical ganglion, so the postganglionic fibers to the facial sweat glands, which travel with the external carotid branches, lose their input and the face is dry on that side.","check":"The sympathetic pathway flow and the description of Horner syndrome."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-06-C12","obj":"PU-14-F.12","title":"Metastases to the lung and pulmonary hamartoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The lung is among the most common sites of metastasis, and metastatic tumors in the lung outnumber primary lung cancers. The reason is circulatory: the lungs receive the entire systemic venous return through the pulmonary arteries, and their capillary bed acts as a filter for tumor cells released from almost any organ drained by the systemic veins. Tumors also reach the lung through lymphatics."},{"type":"p","text":"Blood-borne metastases appear as multiple, round, well-circumscribed nodules of varying size, often in both lungs and more numerous in the periphery and lower zones, where blood flow is greatest. Very large round deposits are called cannonball metastases. Common primaries include carcinomas of the breast, colon, and kidney, and sarcomas. A different pattern, lymphangitic spread, fills the lymphatics with tumor and produces thickened interstitial lines rather than round nodules."},{"type":"p","text":"A solitary pulmonary nodule is a single round opacity of 3 cm or less surrounded by lung; a larger lesion is called a mass. Causes include primary lung cancer, a single metastasis, infectious granulomas from tuberculosis or endemic fungi, and benign tumors such as hamartoma. Small size, smooth margins, no growth over about 2 years, and benign calcification patterns (central, diffuse, laminated, or popcorn) favor a benign cause. Larger size, spiculated margins, upper lobe location, growth, smoking, and older age favor malignancy."},{"type":"p","text":"Pulmonary hamartoma is the most common benign lung tumor. Its name implies a disorganized overgrowth of native tissue, but it is a true benign neoplasm with clonal chromosomal rearrangements, often involving the HMGA2 gene. It is usually a peripheral, well-circumscribed, round nodule, most often a few centimeters or less, found incidentally in an adult. Microscopically it contains lobules of mature hyaline cartilage mixed with fat, fibrous tissue, and clefts lined by respiratory epithelium. The cartilage calcifies in clumps, producing popcorn calcification on imaging, and fat within the nodule on CT is nearly diagnostic."},{"type":"table","title":"Evaluating lung nodules","cols":["Feature","Metastases","Pulmonary hamartoma","Primary lung carcinoma"],"rows":[["Number","Usually multiple","Single","Usually single"],["Margins","Smooth, well circumscribed","Smooth, well circumscribed","Often irregular or spiculated"],["Distribution","Both lungs, periphery and bases","Peripheral","Often upper lobe"],["Calcification","Usually none (some exceptions)","Popcorn","Usually none or eccentric"],["Fat on CT","No","Yes","No"],["Clue in history","Known cancer elsewhere","Incidental, stable","Smoking, older age, growth"]],"caption":"Number, margins, calcification pattern, and fat content separate the main causes."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-06-C13","obj":"PU-14-F.13","title":"HPV-related and tobacco-related squamous cell carcinoma of the head and neck","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Squamous cell carcinoma is the dominant cancer of the oral cavity and pharynx, but it has two distinct causes that produce two different diseases. The classic form follows tobacco, smoked or chewed (including betel quid), and alcohol, which act synergistically. Carcinogens such as polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons and nitrosamines cause mutations, most often in TP53, across the whole exposed mucosa. This field cancerization explains why these patients often have precursor lesions, leukoplakia (a white patch) and the higher-risk erythroplakia (a red patch), and why second primary tumors of the upper aerodigestive tract and lung are common. Typical sites are the lateral tongue, floor of the mouth, larynx, and hypopharynx, mainly in older men."},{"type":"p","text":"HPV-related carcinoma arises in the oropharynx, mainly the palatine tonsils and the base of the tongue (lingual tonsil). These sites have deep crypts lined by reticulated epithelium, a thin, discontinuous lining infiltrated by lymphoid cells that is thought to give the virus access to basal cells. HPV 16 causes the large majority of cases. Infection is acquired sexually, including through oral sex, and patients tend to be younger and are often nonsmokers."},{"type":"flow","title":"How high-risk HPV transforms tonsillar epithelium","steps":["HPV 16 infects basal cells of the tonsillar crypts","Viral DNA integrates; E6 and E7 are expressed continuously","E6 targets p53 for degradation","E7 binds Rb, releasing E2F to drive the cell cycle","p16 accumulates; cells proliferate with unrepaired DNA damage"],"caption":"p16 overexpression is the routine marker of an HPV-driven oropharyngeal carcinoma."},{"type":"p","text":"Because E7 disables Rb, the normal feedback that limits p16 is lost and p16 accumulates, so strong p16 staining serves as a surrogate marker for HPV-driven tumors. Because the virus inactivates p53 and Rb at the protein level, these tumors usually lack TP53 mutations, unlike tobacco-related tumors."},{"type":"p","text":"The two forms present differently. HPV-positive tumors often have a small primary with early, enlarged, frequently cystic neck node metastases, so a painless neck mass is a common first sign, and they carry a better prognosis than tobacco-related tumors. Tobacco-related tumors more often present as a visible ulcer or mass with pain and are more likely to be accompanied by a second primary."},{"type":"table","title":"HPV-related versus tobacco-related head and neck squamous cell carcinoma","cols":["Feature","HPV-related","Tobacco and alcohol related"],"rows":[["Main site","Tonsil and base of tongue (oropharynx)","Oral cavity, larynx, hypopharynx, and pharynx"],["Cause","HPV 16, sexually acquired","Tobacco and alcohol, synergistic"],["Typical patient","Younger, often nonsmoker","Older, heavy smoker and drinker"],["Molecular","E6 and E7; p16 positive; TP53 usually intact","TP53 mutations; p16 usually negative"],["Precursor","Not usually visible","Leukoplakia, erythroplakia"],["Presentation","Small primary, cystic neck node","Ulcer or mass, pain, second primaries"],["Prognosis","Better","Worse"]],"caption":"The same histologic type arises by two pathways with different sites, patients, and markers."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and histology explain where each tumor arises and what it does next. Central bronchi carry the heaviest smoke exposure and contain the squamous metaplasia and neuroendocrine cells that give rise to squamous and small cell carcinoma, while peripheral alveolar epithelium gives rise to adenocarcinoma. The neighbors of each site predict the signs: the sympathetic chain and brachial plexus at the apex, the superior vena cava and recurrent laryngeal nerve in the mediastinum, the eustachian tube beside the nasopharynx, and the trachea beside the esophagus. Microbiology and immunology add two virus-driven pathways, EBV in the nasopharynx and HPV in the tonsils and larynx, where HPV 16 oncoproteins disable p53 and Rb strongly and those of types 6 and 11 only weakly, and paraneoplastic autoimmunity in which tumor-directed antibodies attack nerve terminals."},{"type":"p","text":"Biochemistry and physiology explain the systemic findings: PTHrP mimics PTH, ectopic ADH concentrates urine against a dilute plasma, and loss of presynaptic calcium entry reduces acetylcholine release. These rules have limits. Markers support rather than prove a diagnosis and must be read as a panel, many tumors are mixed or poorly differentiated, percentages for each lung cancer type shift with smoking patterns and classification changes, and an adenocarcinoma in a smoker or a squamous carcinoma in a nonsmoker remains entirely possible."}],"summary":["Adenocarcinoma is the most common lung cancer overall and in never smokers and women; it is peripheral, gland forming, mucin producing, and TTF-1 positive.","Adenocarcinoma in situ shows pure lepidic growth along intact alveolar walls, 3 cm or less, with no stromal, vascular, or pleural invasion.","Squamous cell and small cell carcinomas are central and strongly smoking related; adenocarcinoma and large cell carcinoma are peripheral.","Squamous cell carcinoma shows keratin pearls and intercellular bridges, may cavitate, and secretes PTHrP; small cell carcinoma shows nuclear molding, expresses chromogranin and synaptophysin, loses TP53 and RB1, and metastasizes early.","Small cell carcinoma secretes ACTH and ADH and triggers Lambert-Eaton antibodies against presynaptic P/Q-type calcium channels; hypertrophic osteoarthropathy is most associated with adenocarcinoma.","Bronchial carcinoid is a low-grade neuroendocrine endobronchial tumor without a smoking link that obstructs bronchi and rarely causes carcinoid syndrome.","Pancoast tumors cause Horner syndrome and C8 to T1 deficits; central tumors cause superior vena cava syndrome, recurrent laryngeal hoarseness, and phrenic paralysis.","Mesothelioma follows asbestos exposure by decades, is not related to smoking, encases the lung, and is calretinin positive with long slender microvilli.","Metastases outnumber primary lung cancers and form multiple round nodules; hamartoma is a single nodule of cartilage and fat with popcorn calcification.","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is EBV related and presents with neck nodes or one-sided serous otitis media; HPV 16 causes p16-positive tonsil and base of tongue carcinoma, while tobacco and alcohol cause TP53-mutated oral and laryngeal carcinoma."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lung, sections on lung tumors and pleural tumors"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Head and Neck, sections on the nose, nasopharynx, larynx, and oral cavity"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Gastrointestinal Tract, section on esophageal tumors"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax, sections on the pleura, phrenic and recurrent laryngeal nerves, and superior vena cava; Neck, section on the larynx and cervical sympathetic trunk"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Neoplasia and Pulmonary Disease sections on lung cancer and paraneoplastic syndromes"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Sections on Epstein-Barr virus and human papillomaviruses"}]},"PU-14-G":{"unit":"PU-14-G","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-07","title":"Congenital Pulmonary Diseases","subject":"Respiratory System (RESP)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers lung and airway disease present from birth: one single-gene disorder, cystic fibrosis, and four errors of foregut and diaphragm development (tracheoesophageal fistula, congenital diaphragmatic hernia, pulmonary sequestration, and bronchogenic cyst). Board questions describe a newborn who chokes with feeds, a baby in distress with a sunken abdomen, a child with salty skin and greasy stools, or a teenager with repeated pneumonia in one lower lobe, and ask you to name the lesion and explain its findings. The unifying skill is tracing every sign back to one transport defect or one wrong turn in embryology.","prerequisites":["Foregut development, the lung bud, and tracheoesophageal septation (PU-1)","Stages of lung development and causes of pulmonary hypoplasia (PU-1)","CFTR genetics, F508del processing, and carrier risk (PU-10)","Epithelial ion transport: Na+/K+-ATPase, NKCC1, ENaC, and transepithelial potential","Mucociliary clearance and the periciliary liquid layer (PU-4)"],"connections":[{"to":"Gastrointestinal system (exocrine pancreas and small bowel)","how":"The same CFTR defect that dehydrates airway mucus plugs pancreatic ducts and thickens meconium, causing steatorrhea and meconium ileus."},{"to":"Microbiology","how":"Staphylococcus aureus and mucoid Pseudomonas aeruginosa biofilms exploit retained CF mucus, and infected sequestrations and cysts show why drainage matters more than the organism."},{"to":"Reproductive system","how":"Congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens explains male infertility in CF with normal sperm production in the testes."},{"to":"Cardiovascular system","how":"Pulmonary hypoplasia in diaphragmatic hernia causes persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn, and cardiac defects are a core part of the VACTERL association."},{"to":"Integumentary physiology (eccrine sweat glands)","how":"The sweat duct is the clearest tissue in which to see CFTR absorbing rather than secreting chloride, which is the basis of the sweat test."}],"terms":[{"term":"CFTR","def":"Cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator, an ATP-gated apical anion channel for chloride and bicarbonate that is opened after protein kinase A phosphorylation."},{"term":"ENaC","def":"The amiloride-sensitive epithelial sodium channel in the apical membrane of airway, sweat duct, and renal collecting duct cells."},{"term":"Airway surface liquid","def":"The thin fluid on the airway surface, made of a periciliary layer in which cilia beat and an overlying mucus layer."},{"term":"Meconium ileus","def":"Obstruction of the distal ileum in a newborn by abnormally thick, dehydrated meconium."},{"term":"Congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens","def":"Absence of both vasa deferentia from birth, causing obstructive azoospermia with normal spermatogenesis."},{"term":"Esophageal atresia","def":"Failure of the esophagus to form a continuous tube, leaving a blind upper pouch."},{"term":"Tracheoesophageal fistula","def":"An abnormal open channel between the trachea and the esophagus."},{"term":"VACTERL association","def":"Nonrandom co-occurrence of vertebral, anal, cardiac, tracheoesophageal, renal, and limb anomalies, usually diagnosed when at least three are present."},{"term":"Pleuroperitoneal membrane","def":"The fold that grows from the posterolateral body wall to close each pleuroperitoneal canal and form part of the diaphragm."},{"term":"Pulmonary hypoplasia","def":"A lung with too few airway generations, alveoli, and arteries for the size of the infant."},{"term":"Pulmonary sequestration","def":"A mass of nonfunctioning lung tissue with no normal bronchial connection, supplied by a systemic artery."},{"term":"Bronchogenic cyst","def":"A closed, fluid-filled cyst with a bronchus-like wall, formed from abnormal budding of the foregut, usually in the mediastinum."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-07-C01","obj":"PU-14-G.1","title":"Cystic fibrosis: the clinical picture across organs and ages","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Cystic fibrosis (CF) is an autosomal recessive loss of CFTR function, most often from the F508del variant, which misfolds and is degraded before reaching the cell surface (PU-10). Every organ problem follows one rule: an epithelium that should secrete chloride, bicarbonate, and water cannot, so its secretions become thick, acidic, and protein rich, and they block the lumen they drain into. The problems appear in a predictable order with age."},{"type":"h","text":"Newborn and infant"},{"type":"p","text":"Meconium ileus is often the first sign. Dehydrated, sticky meconium plugs the distal ileum, causing abdominal distension, bilious vomiting, and failure to pass meconium in the first days. It occurs in about 15 to 20% of affected newborns, and most infants with meconium ileus (about 80 to 90%) have CF. The pancreas is damaged before birth: without fluid and bicarbonate to flush them, ducts fill with inspissated enzyme secretions, acini are destroyed, and the gland is replaced by fibrosis and fat. About 85% of patients have exocrine pancreatic insufficiency, so fat and protein are poorly digested. The result is steatorrhea (bulky, greasy, foul stools), failure to thrive despite a good appetite, and deficiency of the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K. Infants who lose salt in sweat during hot weather can develop hyponatremic, hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis."},{"type":"h","text":"Lungs and sinuses"},{"type":"p","text":"The airways are normal at birth. Dehydrated mucus is not cleared, bacteria persist, and neutrophils flood in; their elastase and released DNA thicken the mucus further and digest the bronchial wall. Early colonizers are Staphylococcus aureus and Haemophilus influenzae. Pseudomonas aeruginosa follows and converts to a mucoid form that secretes alginate, a polysaccharide that builds a biofilm resistant to phagocytes and antibiotics. The repeated cycle of obstruction, infection, and inflammation produces bronchiectasis, permanent dilation of bronchi, predominantly in the upper lobes, with chronic productive cough, digital clubbing, hemoptysis, and eventually hypoxemia, cor pulmonale, and respiratory failure, the usual cause of death. In the upper airway the same dehydration causes chronic sinusitis and nasal polyps; polyps in a child are unusual enough that CF should be considered."},{"type":"table","title":"Cystic fibrosis by organ","cols":["Organ","Mechanism","Finding"],"rows":[["Small intestine","Dehydrated meconium; later thick intestinal contents","Meconium ileus in newborns; distal intestinal obstruction syndrome in older patients"],["Exocrine pancreas","Ducts plugged, acini destroyed","Steatorrhea, failure to thrive, vitamin A, D, E, K deficiency"],["Endocrine pancreas","Fibrosis gradually destroys islets","CF-related diabetes, mostly from adolescence"],["Liver and biliary tree","Thick bile in small ducts","Prolonged neonatal jaundice, focal biliary cirrhosis, gallstones"],["Lower airway","Retained mucus, infection, neutrophil injury","S. aureus then mucoid P. aeruginosa; bronchiectasis, clubbing"],["Sinuses","Dehydrated sinus mucus","Chronic sinusitis, nasal polyps"],["Male reproductive tract","Wolffian duct derivatives obstructed and lost in fetal life","Congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens, azoospermia"],["Female reproductive tract","Thick cervical mucus","Reduced fertility"],["Sweat gland","Failed duct chloride reabsorption","Salty skin; sweat chloride 60 mmol/L or higher"]],"caption":"Every row except the sweat gland reflects too little fluid secretion; the sweat gland reflects too little absorption (C02)."},{"type":"h","text":"Reproduction and diagnosis"},{"type":"p","text":"About 95% of men with CF are infertile from congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens: the duct is thought to be obstructed by thick secretions in fetal life and then to degenerate, so the testes make sperm normally but cannot deliver them (obstructive azoospermia with low ejaculate volume). Some men with milder CFTR variants have only this finding."},{"type":"p","text":"Most cases are now suspected from a raised immunoreactive trypsinogen on newborn screening, a marker of pancreatic duct injury, or from the clinical picture above. Diagnosis requires evidence of CFTR dysfunction, classically a sweat chloride of 60 mmol/L or higher on a properly collected pilocarpine-stimulated sample. Values of 30 to 59 mmol/L are intermediate and need CFTR genotyping, and values below 30 mmol/L make CF unlikely. In a child with a positive screen or typical features, two disease-causing CFTR variants also establish it."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-07-C02","obj":"PU-14-G.2","title":"Why sweat is salty while the airway surface dries out","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"CFTR is a passive channel: chloride moves through it down its electrochemical gradient, so the tissue, not the channel, sets the direction. Airway cells load chloride above equilibrium and release it into the lumen; the sweat duct takes chloride from a salty lumen into the cell. Losing the channel stops secretion in one tissue and absorption in the other."},{"type":"h","text":"The sweat duct: failed absorption"},{"type":"p","text":"An eccrine sweat gland has two parts. The secretory coil produces a primary fluid that is nearly isotonic with plasma, with sodium and chloride about 140 and 105 mmol/L. As this fluid flows up the duct, the duct cells reabsorb sodium through apical ENaC and chloride through apical CFTR, and the Na+/K+-ATPase on the basolateral side moves sodium on into the interstitium. The duct is nearly impermeable to water, so salt is recovered while water stays behind, and normal sweat reaching the skin is hypotonic, with chloride usually below 30 mmol/L."},{"type":"p","text":"Without CFTR, the duct has almost no route for chloride to leave the lumen. Sodium cannot be absorbed alone for long: as positive charge leaves, the lumen becomes strongly negative and opposes further sodium entry, so sodium absorption falls too. Both ions stay in the sweat, and sweat chloride rises toward the primary-fluid value, usually above 60 mmol/L. Hence salty-tasting skin and salt depletion in hot weather. Secretion by the coil is largely intact because cholinergic sweating can use other chloride channels, so the volume of sweat is roughly normal."},{"type":"flow","title":"Sweat duct in cystic fibrosis","steps":["Coil secretes near-isotonic NaCl fluid","Duct lacks apical CFTR chloride path","Chloride stays in the lumen","Lumen becomes very negative; sodium absorption falls","Water-tight duct cannot concentrate further","Salty sweat: chloride 60 mmol/L or higher"],"caption":"Loss of absorption leaves near-plasma salt concentrations in sweat."},{"type":"h","text":"The airway: failed secretion with excess sodium absorption"},{"type":"p","text":"Chloride enters the cell across the basolateral membrane through the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter (NKCC1), accumulates above equilibrium, and exits through apical CFTR; sodium follows paracellularly and water follows osmotically, adding liquid. At the same time ENaC absorbs sodium, chloride follows, and water is drawn out. CFTR normally restrains ENaC, so the two processes balance and keep the periciliary layer about 7 micrometers deep, the height of an extended cilium."},{"type":"p","text":"In CF the secretory arm is lost, and in the classic model ENaC is released from restraint, so sodium absorption increases. Salt and water are pulled out of the lumen, the periciliary layer collapses, and mucus presses onto the cilia, which can no longer beat it forward. Lost bicarbonate secretion also acidifies the liquid, impairing mucin expansion and antimicrobial peptides. Unlike sweat, airway liquid in CF is not saltier: there is simply less of it. The more negative nasal potential difference in CF reflects the increased sodium absorption."},{"type":"table","title":"One channel, opposite jobs","cols":["Feature","Sweat duct","Airway epithelium"],"rows":[["Normal CFTR role","Absorbs chloride from lumen","Secretes chloride into lumen"],["ENaC in CF","Sodium absorption falls with chloride","Sodium absorption increases"],["Water permeability","Very low","High"],["Result of CFTR loss","Salty sweat of normal volume","Too little surface liquid; thick mucus"],["Measured abnormality","Sweat chloride 60 mmol/L or higher","More negative nasal potential difference"]],"caption":"The chloride gradient in each tissue decides whether CFTR loss blocks absorption or secretion."},{"type":"stop","predict":"If CFTR loss reduces chloride movement in both tissues, why does the sweat test show too much chloride while the airway shows too little liquid?","explain":"The water-tight sweat duct normally removes salt from a fixed volume, so failure leaves the concentration high. The airway normally adds chloride and water, so failure, plus unchecked ENaC absorption, lowers liquid volume while composition changes little.","check":"The two transport paragraphs and the comparison table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-07-C03","obj":"PU-14-G.3","title":"Tracheoesophageal fistula and esophageal atresia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In week 4 the respiratory diverticulum (lung bud) grows from the ventral wall of the foregut. Tracheoesophageal ridges then grow in from each side and fuse into the tracheoesophageal septum, dividing the foregut into the trachea in front and the esophagus behind (PU-1). If the septum deviates posteriorly or the ridges fail to fuse, the esophagus loses its continuity and an opening between airway and gut persists. Atresia and fistula therefore usually occur together, in about 1 in 3,000 to 4,500 births."},{"type":"p","text":"The most common form, about 85% of cases, is esophageal atresia with a distal fistula: the upper esophagus ends in a blind pouch, and the lower esophagus opens into the trachea near the carina."},{"type":"flow","title":"From blind pouch and distal fistula to the findings","steps":["Fetus cannot swallow amniotic fluid into the gut","Polyhydramnios","Newborn cannot clear saliva: drooling, frothing","Feeds overflow the pouch into the larynx","Choking, coughing, and cyanosis with feeds","Air enters the stomach through the fistula","Gastric bubble; acid refluxes into the trachea"],"caption":"The blind pouch explains the feeding signs; the distal fistula explains gastric air and chemical pneumonitis."},{"type":"p","text":"Polyhydramnios develops because the fetus normally swallows amniotic fluid and absorbs it in the intestine, while fetal urine keeps adding fluid. After birth, saliva and the first feed fill the small upper pouch and spill into the larynx. Cyanosis comes from aspiration and laryngospasm, and from a stomach distended by air pumped through the fistula with each cry, which pushes up the diaphragm. The bedside clue is a nasogastric tube that stops at about 10 cm; a radiograph shows it coiled in the upper pouch, and gas in the stomach and bowel proves that a distal fistula connects the airway to the gut. Acid gastric content refluxing up the fistula causes chemical pneumonitis."},{"type":"table","title":"Main variants","cols":["Anatomy","About","Distinguishing findings"],"rows":[["Atresia with distal fistula","85%","Tube coils in pouch; gas-filled stomach"],["Isolated atresia, no fistula","8%","Marked polyhydramnios; gasless abdomen"],["H-type fistula, no atresia","4%","Feeds pass; cough with feeds and recurrent pneumonia, diagnosed late"],["Proximal fistula, or fistulas to both segments","Rare","Immediate aspiration of feeds from the upper segment"]],"caption":"Frequencies vary between series; the distal fistula type is consistently the most common."},{"type":"p","text":"About half of affected infants have another anomaly, so finding one should start a search. The VACTERL association groups vertebral defects, anal atresia, cardiac defects (often ventricular septal defect), tracheoesophageal fistula, esophageal atresia, renal anomalies, and limb defects, typically of the radial ray. It is an association, not a syndrome with a single cause, and is usually diagnosed when at least three components are present. Many infants also have tracheomalacia, a soft trachea at the fistula site that collapses on expiration."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading the newborn radiograph","text":"A newborn of a pregnancy complicated by polyhydramnios drools and coughs with the first feed. A tube stops at 10 cm. Step 1: the tube cannot reach the stomach, so the esophagus is interrupted; the radiograph shows it coiled in the upper chest. Step 2: look below the diaphragm. A gas-filled stomach means air reached it, and the only route left is a fistula from the trachea to the lower esophagus, the common type. Step 3: if the abdomen were gasless, the lower segment has no connection to the airway, pointing to isolated atresia, which also explains the more severe polyhydramnios. Step 4: examine the spine, anus, heart, kidneys, and radii for VACTERL."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-07-C04","obj":"PU-14-G.4","title":"Congenital diaphragmatic hernia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The diaphragm is assembled from four sources: the septum transversum (central tendon), the two pleuroperitoneal membranes, the dorsal mesentery of the esophagus (crura), and muscle from the body wall at the periphery. The pleuroperitoneal membranes grow from the posterolateral body wall and close the pleuroperitoneal canals, the last openings between the thoracic and abdominal cavities, around week 7. The left canal is larger and closes later than the right."},{"type":"p","text":"Congenital diaphragmatic hernia occurs in about 1 in 2,000 to 4,000 births. In most cases a pleuroperitoneal membrane fails to close its canal, leaving a posterolateral (Bochdalek) defect; about 85% are on the left, because the left canal closes later and the liver protects the right side. The timing explains the damage. The midgut lies in the umbilical cord (physiological herniation) until about week 10, and when it returns to the abdomen it can pass through the open canal into the chest. This happens during the pseudoglandular stage, when the bronchial tree is branching."},{"type":"flow","title":"From an open canal to pulmonary hypoplasia","steps":["Pleuroperitoneal membrane fails to close the canal","Returning midgut enters the left hemithorax","Left lung compressed; mediastinum shifts right","Fewer airway branches, alveoli, and arteries in both lungs","Muscular small arteries keep pulmonary resistance high","Hypoxemia and right-to-left shunting after birth"],"caption":"The lethal problem is the small lung and its vascular bed, not the hernia itself."},{"type":"p","text":"Compression during branching leaves both lungs hypoplastic, the ipsilateral lung most. The pulmonary arteries are few in number and muscularized abnormally far into the periphery, so pulmonary vascular resistance fails to fall at birth. Blood then shunts right to left through the ductus arteriosus and foramen ovale, producing persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn and profound hypoxemia, often with higher saturation in the right hand than the feet (PU-1)."},{"type":"p","text":"At birth the infant has severe respiratory distress and cyanosis. The abdomen is scaphoid (sunken) because its contents are in the chest, the chest may look barrel shaped, breath sounds are absent on the left, bowel sounds may be heard in the chest, and heart sounds are displaced to the right by mediastinal shift. A radiograph shows gas-filled bowel loops in the left hemithorax and the mediastinum pushed right. Prenatal ultrasound often detects the lesion first."},{"type":"table","title":"Diaphragmatic defects compared","cols":["Defect","Site and origin","Typical presentation"],"rows":[["Bochdalek hernia","Posterolateral, usually left; failed pleuroperitoneal membrane","Newborn respiratory distress, scaphoid abdomen, pulmonary hypoplasia"],["Morgagni hernia","Anterior retrosternal, usually right; defect beside the sternum","Small, often found later or incidentally"],["Eventration","Thin, poorly muscularized diaphragm that is intact","Elevated hemidiaphragm, often mild"]],"caption":"Board questions about a newborn in distress with a sunken abdomen are almost always describing the Bochdalek type."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does an infant with a left-sided hernia often have hypoplasia of the right lung as well?","explain":"Herniated bowel pushes the mediastinum toward the right, compressing the right lung during the same branching period, so both lungs develop fewer airways and arteries.","check":"The flow figure and the hypoplasia paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-07-C05","obj":"PU-14-G.5","title":"Pulmonary sequestration versus bronchogenic cyst","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Both lesions arise from abnormal budding of the foregut, and both tend to become infected, but they differ in what they connect to. The two questions to ask of any congenital lung mass are where its blood comes from and whether it drains into an airway."},{"type":"h","text":"Pulmonary sequestration"},{"type":"p","text":"A sequestration is a mass of lung tissue that does not communicate with the normal tracheobronchial tree and receives its arterial blood from a systemic artery, usually from the descending thoracic or upper abdominal aorta, instead of from the pulmonary artery. It is thought to come from an accessory lung bud arising from the foregut below the normal one. Because it takes no part in ventilation, it performs no gas exchange. It most often lies in a lower lobe, usually the left posterior basal segment. Two forms are recognized. An intralobar sequestration lies within the visceral pleura of a normal lobe and usually drains to the pulmonary veins; it is the more common form and often presents in later childhood or adulthood. An extralobar sequestration has its own pleural covering, usually drains to systemic veins such as the azygos system, presents in infancy, and is more often associated with other anomalies, including diaphragmatic hernia."},{"type":"p","text":"Without a bronchus, secretions cannot be cleared. Bacteria reach the tissue through collateral channels or small acquired airway connections, and the stagnant tissue becomes infected again and again, so the classic presentation is recurrent pneumonia in the same lower lobe segment. Imaging that shows an artery running from the aorta into the mass establishes the diagnosis. A large systemic supply can rarely divert enough aortic flow to cause high-output heart failure."},{"type":"h","text":"Bronchogenic cyst"},{"type":"p","text":"A bronchogenic cyst forms when a small bud separates abnormally from the developing trachea or bronchial tree and grows into a closed sac. Its wall resembles a bronchus, with ciliated pseudostratified columnar epithelium, cartilage plates, smooth muscle, and seromucous glands, and it fills with mucus or clear fluid. Most lie in the middle mediastinum near the carina or beside the trachea, and fewer lie within the lung. Its blood supply is normal. Many are found incidentally as a round mediastinal mass; others compress the trachea, a bronchus, or the esophagus, causing wheeze, stridor, cough, or dysphagia, or they become infected, especially if they gain a connection to an airway, which then shows an air-fluid level."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing the two lesions","cols":["Feature","Pulmonary sequestration","Bronchogenic cyst"],"rows":[["Origin","Accessory lung bud from the foregut","Abnormal budding of the trachea or bronchial tree"],["Usual site","Lower lobe, usually left posterior basal","Middle mediastinum near the carina"],["Content","Solid nonfunctioning lung tissue","Fluid or mucus in a closed sac"],["Arterial supply","Systemic, from the aorta","Normal local vessels"],["Bronchial connection","None to the normal tree","Usually none"],["Typical problem","Recurrent pneumonia in one segment","Compression of airway or esophagus; infection"]],"caption":"A systemic artery from the aorta is the single most specific finding for sequestration."},{"type":"example","title":"Recurrent pneumonia in one place","text":"A 14-year-old has had three pneumonias, each in the left lower lobe, with full clearing between them. Step 1: recurrence in one site points to a local lesion, not an immune defect, which would cause infections in varied sites and organs. Step 2: a lower lobe posterior basal location suggests sequestration. Step 3: CT angiography shows an artery from the descending aorta entering the mass, confirming systemic supply. Step 4: the tissue lies within the normal pleura and drains to pulmonary veins, so it is intralobar, which fits presentation in adolescence. A bronchogenic cyst would instead appear as a round, fluid-density mass near the carina with normal vessels."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy, embryology, and physiology explain each lesion. The foregut gives rise to the trachea, lungs, and esophagus, so errors in septation produce tracheoesophageal fistula, and abnormal buds produce sequestration and bronchogenic cyst. Lung growth needs stretch and space during the pseudoglandular stage, so a diaphragmatic defect present then leaves hypoplastic lungs and a high-resistance pulmonary circulation. Cystic fibrosis is a transport disease: whether CFTR normally secretes or absorbs chloride in a tissue predicts whether its loss dries secretions (airway, pancreas, intestine, vas deferens) or leaves salt in a fluid (sweat)."},{"type":"p","text":"Microbiology and pathology tie the conditions together through impaired drainage. Mucus that cannot be cleared from CF airways, aspirated feeds in tracheoesophageal fistula, and secretions trapped in a sequestration all lead to recurrent infection, and the organism matters less than the obstruction. CF severity varies with genotype and environment, so not every patient shows every finding."}],"summary":["Cystic fibrosis is autosomal recessive CFTR loss; thick, dehydrated secretions block the intestine, pancreas, bile ducts, airways, sinuses, and vas deferens.","Meconium ileus, steatorrhea with A, D, E, K deficiency, S. aureus then mucoid P. aeruginosa, bronchiectasis, nasal polyps, and absent vas deferens form the CF picture.","Sweat chloride of 60 mmol/L or higher supports CF; 30 to 59 is intermediate and below 30 makes CF unlikely.","In the sweat duct CFTR absorbs chloride from a water-tight lumen, so its loss leaves salty sweat.","In the airway CFTR secretes chloride and restrains ENaC, so its loss reduces secretion, increases sodium absorption, and collapses the periciliary layer.","The common tracheoesophageal fistula is a blind upper pouch with a distal fistula: polyhydramnios, drooling, choking and cyanosis with feeds, a coiled tube, and gastric air.","A gasless abdomen means atresia without a distal fistula; an H-type fistula presents late with recurrent aspiration.","VACTERL groups vertebral, anal, cardiac, tracheoesophageal, esophageal, renal, and limb anomalies; one should prompt a search for the others.","Bochdalek hernia is a left posterolateral pleuroperitoneal defect; returning bowel compresses the branching lung, causing hypoplasia, pulmonary hypertension, and a scaphoid abdomen.","Sequestration has a systemic arterial supply from the aorta and no bronchial connection; a bronchogenic cyst is a closed, fluid-filled foregut bud near the carina."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of Infancy and Childhood, cystic fibrosis; The Lung, congenital anomalies"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Respiratory System; Body Cavities (diaphragm formation)"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax and abdomen, the diaphragm"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Transport of solutes and water; airway epithelium and sweat gland ion transport"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Cystic fibrosis"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Pseudomonads and the mucoid phenotype"}]},"PU-14-H":{"unit":"PU-14-H","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-08","title":"Infectious Diseases of the Lungs and Upper Respiratory Tract","subject":"Respiratory System (RESP)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers infections of the lung and airways: bacterial, atypical, fungal, mycobacterial, and viral pneumonias, lung abscess and aspiration, the toxin diseases diphtheria and pertussis, infant bronchiolitis, and zoonotic and bioterrorism pneumonias. Board questions usually give a host, an exposure, and an imaging or tissue pattern and ask for the organism, or give the organism and ask for the complication. Three questions organize most answers: where the organism lands and spreads (alveolar space, bronchiole, or interstitium), which defense controls it (antibody and neutrophils, or Th1 cells and macrophages), and whether a toxin acts far from the infection. The biology of each pathogen is taught in PU-13; this chapter applies it to disease.","prerequisites":["Bronchial tree, bronchopulmonary segments, and mediastinal lymph nodes (PU-4)","Alveolar structure, type I and II pneumocytes, and mucociliary clearance (PU-2)","Ventilation and perfusion matching, shunt, and diffusion limitation (PU-7)","Structure and virulence factors of the major respiratory pathogens (PU-13)","Th1 immunity, macrophage activation, and granuloma formation (PU-12, IM-6)"],"connections":[{"to":"Pulmonary pathogens (PU-13)","how":"The capsules, toxins, cell walls, and viral attachment proteins described there explain the patterns of disease in this chapter."},{"to":"Immune responses in pulmonary disease (PU-12)","how":"Failure of innate lung defenses explains bacterial pneumonia, and Th1 granulomas explain tuberculosis and the endemic mycoses."},{"to":"Restrictive pulmonary diseases (PU-14-A)","how":"Severe influenza, COVID-19, and chemical aspiration injure the alveolar wall and can progress to diffuse alveolar damage and ARDS."},{"to":"Disorders of the pleural cavity (PU-14-D)","how":"Parapneumonic effusion and empyema follow pneumonia and lung abscess, and anthrax causes hemorrhagic effusions and mediastinitis."},{"to":"Primary myocardial diseases (CV-18-E)","how":"Diphtheria toxin is a classic cause of toxic myocarditis with conduction block."}],"terms":[{"term":"Consolidation","def":"Replacement of alveolar air by exudate, so a region of lung becomes solid and airless."},{"term":"Hepatization","def":"The firm, liver-like texture of a consolidated lobe in lobar pneumonia."},{"term":"Bronchopneumonia","def":"Patchy consolidation centered on bronchioles and the alveoli around them, often in several lobes."},{"term":"Atypical pneumonia","def":"Pneumonia with inflammation mainly in the alveolar septa (interstitium) rather than the alveolar spaces, usually caused by organisms not seen on Gram stain."},{"term":"Pseudomembrane","def":"An adherent layer of fibrin, necrotic epithelium, leukocytes, and bacteria on a mucosal surface."},{"term":"Dimorphic fungus","def":"A fungus that grows as a mold at environmental temperature and as yeast or spherules at body temperature."},{"term":"Ghon complex","def":"The subpleural parenchymal focus of primary tuberculosis together with the involved hilar lymph nodes."},{"term":"Caseous necrosis","def":"Cheese-like, amorphous granular necrosis at the center of tuberculous and some fungal granulomas."},{"term":"Interferon-gamma release assay (IGRA)","def":"A blood test that measures interferon-gamma released by T cells exposed to Mycobacterium tuberculosis antigens absent from BCG."},{"term":"Diffuse alveolar damage","def":"Injury to alveolar epithelium and capillaries with edema and hyaline membranes; the tissue pattern of ARDS."},{"term":"Antigenic shift","def":"A major change in influenza A surface antigens caused by reassortment of gene segments between strains."},{"term":"Angioinvasion","def":"Invasion of blood vessel walls by an organism, causing thrombosis, infarction, and hemorrhage."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-08-C01","obj":"PU-14-H.1","title":"Lobar pneumonia versus bronchopneumonia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Pneumonia is infection of the lung parenchyma in which exudate fills alveoli. Its gross pattern depends on where the organism starts and how the exudate spreads. In lobar pneumonia, organisms deposited in distal airspaces provoke a protein-rich edema fluid that carries them through the pores of Kohn and small airways into neighboring alveoli, so a large part of a lobe, or the whole lobe, consolidates at once. Streptococcus pneumoniae causes most cases; its polysaccharide capsule resists phagocytosis until antibody and complement opsonize it. Klebsiella pneumoniae causes a similar pattern in people with alcohol use disorder, diabetes, or aspiration risk, often in an upper lobe; its thick capsule and the necrosis it causes produce mucoid, blood-tinged currant jelly sputum, and abscess is common."},{"type":"p","text":"Untreated lobar pneumonia passes through four stages that follow the course of acute inflammation. The pleura over the lobe is usually inflamed (fibrinous pleuritis), which causes pleuritic chest pain and can lead to effusion or empyema."},{"type":"flow","title":"Stages of lobar pneumonia","steps":["Congestion: engorged capillaries, edema fluid, many bacteria, few neutrophils","Red hepatization: neutrophils, red cells, and fibrin fill alveoli","Gray hepatization: red cells break down, fibrinosuppurative exudate persists","Resolution: enzymes digest exudate, macrophages clear debris, architecture restored"],"caption":"Classically about 1 to 2 days for congestion, then several days each for the hepatization stages; timing is approximate and shortened by treatment."},{"type":"p","text":"Consolidated lung is airless but solid, and solid tissue transmits sound and vibration better than aerated lung. That single fact explains the exam: dullness to percussion, increased tactile fremitus, bronchial breath sounds, egophony (spoken \"ee\" heard as \"ay\"), and whispered pectoriloquy, often with crackles. The filled alveoli are still perfused, so blood passes them without gas exchange; this right-to-left shunt causes hypoxemia that improves only partly with supplemental oxygen."},{"type":"p","text":"Bronchopneumonia begins in bronchioles and spreads to the alveoli around each one, producing scattered patchy foci of consolidation, often bilateral and basal. Typical causes are Staphylococcus aureus, Haemophilus influenzae, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, and other gram-negative rods; it is common in infants, older adults, and hospitalized or debilitated patients. Because the consolidated areas are small and separated by aerated lung, the classic consolidation signs are often absent, and crackles over several areas are the main finding."},{"type":"table","title":"Lobar pneumonia and bronchopneumonia","cols":["Feature","Lobar pneumonia","Bronchopneumonia"],"rows":[["Starting site","Alveolar spaces","Bronchioles"],["Distribution","Large part of one lobe, uniform","Patchy, often multilobar and bilateral"],["Typical organisms","S. pneumoniae, Klebsiella","S. aureus, H. influenzae, Pseudomonas, enteric gram-negative rods"],["Radiograph","Dense lobar opacity with air bronchograms","Patchy opacities around airways"],["Exam","Dullness, increased fremitus, bronchial breath sounds, egophony","Scattered crackles; consolidation signs often absent"],["Complications","Pleuritis, effusion, empyema, bacteremia; abscess with Klebsiella","Abscess and empyema, especially with S. aureus"]],"caption":"The pattern reflects anatomy of spread more than any single organism; pneumococcus can also cause bronchopneumonia."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with right lower lobe consolidation breathes 100% oxygen. Will the arterial PO2 rise to normal?","explain":"Only partly. Blood flowing through the consolidated lobe never meets alveolar gas, so added oxygen cannot reach it; the shunted blood keeps arterial PO2 low.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on why consolidated lung is perfused but not ventilated."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-08-C02","obj":"PU-14-H.2","title":"Atypical pneumonia: Mycoplasma, Chlamydia, Legionella, and viruses","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Atypical pneumonia is defined by its pattern rather than one organism. The inflammation sits mainly in the alveolar septa, which fill with lymphocytes, plasma cells, and macrophages, while the alveolar spaces stay relatively free of exudate. Patients have a gradual illness with low-grade fever, headache, myalgia, sore throat, and a dry or scantily productive cough. Because little exudate reaches the airspaces, sputum is scant, Gram stain shows no dominant organism, and focal consolidation signs are usually absent. The chest radiograph nevertheless shows patchy or reticular interstitial infiltrates, often in several areas, so the film looks worse than the patient (\"walking pneumonia\")."},{"type":"p","text":"Mycoplasma pneumoniae is the classic cause in school-age children and young adults in close quarters such as dormitories, barracks, and households. It has no cell wall: it lacks peptidoglycan and has sterols in its membrane, so it does not Gram stain and has no target for beta-lactam drugs. A terminal adhesin protein (P1) binds ciliated respiratory epithelium, where the organism damages cells and stops ciliary beating, causing a persistent cough. About half of patients form cold agglutinins, IgM antibodies against the I antigen on red cells that bind best below body temperature and fix complement, which can cause hemolysis. Other extrapulmonary effects include erythema multiforme and bullous myringitis."},{"type":"p","text":"Chlamydia pneumoniae (also called Chlamydophila pneumoniae) is an obligate intracellular bacterium that causes a mild, prolonged illness, often with pharyngitis and hoarseness. Respiratory viruses, including influenza, respiratory syncytial virus, adenovirus, parainfluenza, and coronaviruses, produce the same interstitial pattern."},{"type":"p","text":"Legionella pneumophila is a gram-negative rod that stains poorly with Gram stain and lives in warm water inside amoebae. People inhale aerosols from cooling towers, hot tubs, showers, and plumbing; it does not spread person to person. Inside alveolar macrophages it blocks phagolysosome fusion. Legionnaires disease often affects older smokers, people with chronic lung disease, and the immunosuppressed, and is more severe than other atypical pneumonias: high fever, confusion, diarrhea, hyponatremia, and sometimes abnormal liver tests. Its radiograph may show consolidation, so it is atypical in organism more than in pattern. Legionella needs buffered charcoal yeast extract agar with cysteine and iron, and a urinary antigen test detects serogroup 1."},{"type":"table","title":"Atypical pneumonia organisms","cols":["Organism","Key biology","Typical host and clues"],"rows":[["Mycoplasma pneumoniae","No cell wall; sterols in membrane; P1 adhesin","Young adults in close quarters; cold agglutinins; bullous myringitis"],["Chlamydia pneumoniae","Obligate intracellular; elementary and reticulate bodies","Mild prolonged illness with pharyngitis and hoarseness"],["Legionella pneumophila","Intracellular gram-negative rod; water and amoebae","Older smokers; high fever, diarrhea, confusion, hyponatremia"],["Respiratory viruses","Infect airway and alveolar epithelium","Seasonal outbreaks; influenza can add bacterial superinfection"]],"caption":"All produce mainly interstitial inflammation; Legionella is the one most likely to cause severe illness and consolidation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-08-C03","obj":"PU-14-H.3","title":"Diphtheria: local pseudomembrane and distant toxin injury","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Corynebacterium diphtheriae is a gram-positive, club-shaped rod that colonizes the pharynx, tonsils, larynx, or skin and does not usually invade deeper tissue. Disease is caused by diphtheria toxin, whose gene (tox) is carried by a lysogenic beta-prophage. Only strains carrying the phage are toxigenic, and the iron-dependent repressor DtxR blocks toxin production until iron becomes scarce. The toxin is an A-B toxin: the B portion binds the heparin-binding EGF-like growth factor precursor on host cells and the A fragment enters the cytoplasm and ADP-ribosylates elongation factor 2 (EF-2). Protein synthesis stops and the cell dies."},{"type":"flow","title":"From colonization to distant toxin injury","steps":["Toxigenic strain colonizes the pharynx or tonsils","Toxin kills epithelial cells; fibrin and leukocytes accumulate","Gray adherent pseudomembrane forms; bleeds when scraped","Local edema and cervical nodes produce bull neck","Absorbed toxin travels in blood","Myocardium: myocarditis, conduction block, arrhythmia","Nerves: palatal and cranial palsies, later demyelinating polyneuropathy"],"caption":"The local lesion and the systemic damage come from the same toxin acting at different sites."},{"type":"p","text":"Local cell death produces the pseudomembrane, a tough gray layer of fibrin, necrotic epithelium, leukocytes, red cells, and bacteria that is firmly attached to the underlying mucosa, so removing it leaves a bleeding surface. This adherence separates it from the easily wiped exudate of streptococcal or viral tonsillitis. The membrane can extend into the larynx and trachea and obstruct the airway, or detach and be aspirated. Marked cervical lymphadenopathy with soft tissue edema gives the swollen bull neck of severe disease. Fever is usually low grade."},{"type":"p","text":"Toxin absorbed into the blood damages organs far from the throat, chiefly the heart and nerves, and the size of the local lesion roughly predicts the amount of toxin absorbed. Myocarditis usually appears in the first or second week, causing ST and T wave changes, AV block or bundle branch block, arrhythmias, and heart failure; it is the main cause of death. Neuropathy is demyelinating and appears in sequence: palatal paralysis with nasal speech and regurgitation of fluids within the first weeks, then other cranial nerve palsies such as loss of accommodation, then a peripheral polyneuropathy weeks later. Because damage comes from protein synthesis arrest rather than tissue invasion, recovery is usually slow but complete in survivors."},{"type":"p","text":"The organism grows on Loeffler medium, where methylene blue shows metachromatic (volutin) granules, and on cystine-tellurite agar, where it reduces tellurite to form gray to black colonies. Since nontoxigenic strains exist, toxin production must be proved, classically by the Elek test, an immunodiffusion test in which toxin from the isolate and antitoxin form a precipitin line in the agar; PCR can detect the tox gene."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-08-C04","obj":"PU-14-H.4","title":"Endemic fungal pneumonia in healthy hosts","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Histoplasma capsulatum, Blastomyces dermatitidis, and Coccidioides immitis and posadasii are thermally dimorphic fungi. In soil they grow as molds that release airborne conidia; at about 37 °C (98.6 °F) in the lung they convert to yeasts or, for Coccidioides, spherules. Unlike Aspergillus or Pneumocystis, they infect people with normal immunity, and geography and exposure point to the organism."},{"type":"p","text":"The tissue forms resist killing by neutrophils, so control depends on cell-mediated immunity, the same mechanism that controls tuberculosis. Dendritic cells and macrophages present fungal antigen and secrete IL-12, Th1 cells release interferon-gamma that activates macrophages, and TNF-alpha organizes granulomas. Most healthy hosts have mild or no illness and heal with granulomas that may caseate, fibrose, and calcify. The result mimics tuberculosis: necrotizing granulomas, calcified lung nodules and hilar or mediastinal nodes, and sometimes chronic cavitary upper lobe disease, especially in people with emphysema. Histologic stains for fungi (methenamine silver, periodic acid-Schiff) and the shape of the organism make the distinction."},{"type":"table","title":"The three endemic mycoses","cols":["Feature","Histoplasmosis","Blastomycosis","Coccidioidomycosis"],"rows":[["Region (US)","Ohio and Mississippi River valleys","Ohio and Mississippi valleys, Great Lakes, southeast","Southwest deserts (California, Arizona), northern Mexico"],["Exposure","Bird and bat droppings, caves, old buildings","Moist soil, decaying wood near waterways","Dust from construction, earthquakes, dust storms"],["Tissue form","Small yeasts (about 2 to 4 um) inside macrophages","Large yeasts (about 8 to 15 um), thick wall, broad-based bud","Large spherules filled with endospores"],["Typical lung disease","Often silent; calcified nodes; mediastinal granulomas","Pneumonia resembling bacterial pneumonia or cancer","Valley fever: flu-like pneumonia, erythema nodosum, arthralgias"],["Main dissemination","Reticuloendothelial system (liver, spleen, marrow), adrenals, oral ulcers","Skin (verrucous or ulcerated lesions), bone, prostate","Skin, bone, meninges (basilar meningitis)"]],"caption":"All three form granulomas that can mimic tuberculosis on imaging and in tissue."},{"type":"p","text":"Dissemination occurs when cell-mediated immunity fails to contain the primary focus. Risk rises with HIV infection, organ transplantation, glucocorticoids, and TNF-alpha inhibitors, which weaken the Th1 and granuloma response. Coccidioides also disseminates more often in people of Filipino or African ancestry and in pregnancy, especially the third trimester. Disseminated disease reaches the skin, bones and joints, and meninges; Histoplasma favors macrophage-rich organs because it lives inside macrophages. Erythema nodosum in valley fever is a hypersensitivity reaction and signals a strong cell-mediated response, not spread of the organism."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-08-C05","obj":"PU-14-H.5","title":"Pneumocystis pneumonia versus the forms of aspergillosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Pneumocystis jirovecii is an atypical fungus that cannot be cultured routinely. It is acquired by inhalation, is common in healthy people without disease, and attaches to type I pneumocytes. Control requires CD4 T cells, which direct alveolar macrophages to clear it, so pneumonia occurs when the CD4 count falls below about 200 cells per microliter in HIV infection, and with high-dose glucocorticoids, transplantation, and some cancers. The alveoli fill with a foamy, eosinophilic exudate made of organisms and protein, and the alveolar septa thicken with interstitial inflammation."},{"type":"p","text":"Onset in HIV is subacute over weeks: fever, dry cough, and progressive dyspnea on exertion. The radiograph shows bilateral, diffuse, often perihilar ground-glass opacities, and cysts may form and rupture to cause pneumothorax. Serum lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) is elevated by lung injury, a sensitive but nonspecific clue. Diagnosis is by finding cysts with methenamine silver or immunofluorescent stain in induced sputum or bronchoalveolar lavage fluid; serum beta-D-glucan supports it."},{"type":"example","title":"Why oxygen saturation falls with exertion","text":"At rest, red cells spend about 0.75 s in a pulmonary capillary and normally reach equilibrium with alveolar gas in about the first third of that time. In Pneumocystis pneumonia, exudate and septal thickening slow oxygen diffusion, so equilibrium takes longer but can still be reached at rest, and resting saturation may be near normal. During exercise, cardiac output rises and transit time can fall to about 0.25 s. Blood now leaves the capillary before it is fully oxygenated, the A-a gradient widens, and saturation drops. A fall in saturation on walking with a normal resting value is therefore a sign of diffusion limitation."},{"type":"p","text":"Aspergillus fumigatus is a mold with septate hyphae that branch at acute angles. Its small conidia are inhaled by everyone; alveolar macrophages kill conidia and neutrophils kill hyphae. Disease depends on the host: an old cavity, absent neutrophils, or an allergic airway."},{"type":"table","title":"Pneumocystis pneumonia and the three main forms of aspergillosis","cols":["Disease","Host","Mechanism","Findings"],"rows":[["Pneumocystis pneumonia","CD4 below about 200 per microliter, steroids, transplant","Organisms and foamy exudate fill alveoli","Bilateral ground-glass opacities, exertional hypoxemia, high LDH"],["Aspergilloma","Preexisting cavity (old tuberculosis, sarcoidosis), normal immunity","Saprophytic fungal ball of hyphae; no tissue invasion","Mobile mass in cavity with air crescent; hemoptysis"],["Invasive (angioinvasive) aspergillosis","Prolonged neutropenia, high-dose steroids, transplant","Hyphae invade vessels, causing thrombosis and hemorrhagic infarction","Nodules with surrounding ground-glass halo, pleuritic pain, hemoptysis, spread to brain"],["Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis","Asthma or cystic fibrosis","IgE (type I) and IgG precipitin (type III) response to fungus growing in mucus","Wheeze, eosinophilia, very high IgE, mucus plugs, central bronchiectasis"]],"caption":"Pneumocystis follows T cell failure; invasive aspergillosis follows neutrophil failure; aspergilloma and allergic disease occur in hosts with normal immunity."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient receiving chemotherapy has a neutrophil count near zero for three weeks and develops fever, hemoptysis, and a lung nodule with a ground-glass halo. Is the CD4 count the key risk?","explain":"No. Neutrophils kill Aspergillus hyphae, so prolonged neutropenia allows angioinvasion; the halo is hemorrhage around an infarcted nodule. CD4 failure is the main risk for Pneumocystis.","check":"Compare with the table rows for invasive aspergillosis and Pneumocystis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-08-C06","obj":"PU-14-H.6","title":"Lung abscess after aspiration","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A lung abscess is a localized area of suppurative necrosis that forms a cavity in the lung. The most common route is aspiration of oropharyngeal secretions. Normally the cough and gag reflexes and closure of the glottis keep these secretions out of the lower airway. Aspiration becomes likely when consciousness is reduced (alcohol intoxication, seizures, general anesthesia, drug overdose) or swallowing is impaired (stroke, neuromuscular disease, esophageal disorders). Poor dental hygiene and periodontal disease raise the number of anaerobes in each aspirated drop, which is why abscess is uncommon in people without teeth."},{"type":"p","text":"The organisms are mixed oral anaerobes, mainly Peptostreptococcus, Prevotella and oral Bacteroides species, and Fusobacterium, often with microaerophilic streptococci. They cause liquefactive necrosis over one to two weeks. When the necrotic center drains into a bronchus, air enters the cavity and the radiograph shows a cavity with an air-fluid level. Anaerobic metabolism produces foul-smelling short-chain fatty acids and sulfur compounds, so the sputum and breath are putrid. The illness is usually subacute, with fever, cough, weight loss, and night sweats over weeks, and clubbing may develop if it persists. Complications include rupture into the pleural space (empyema), hemorrhage, and septic spread to the brain."},{"type":"p","text":"The site follows gravity and the anatomy of the bronchial tree. The right main bronchus is wider, shorter, and more vertical than the left, so aspirated material more often enters the right lung. In a person lying supine, it flows into the posterior segment of the upper lobe and the superior segment of the lower lobe; in an upright person, it falls to the basal segments of the lower lobe."},{"type":"p","text":"Not every cavity is anaerobic. Staphylococcus aureus causes necrotizing pneumonia with abscesses, often after influenza, and septic emboli from right-sided endocarditis in people who inject drugs produce multiple cavitating nodules. Klebsiella causes necrotizing upper lobe pneumonia in people with alcohol use disorder or diabetes. Other causes of a cavity to keep in mind are an abscess behind a bronchus obstructed by carcinoma (suspect in an older smoker), tuberculosis, endemic fungi, and a cavitating squamous cell carcinoma itself."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-08-C07","obj":"PU-14-H.7","title":"Pertussis: stages, lymphocytosis, and infant disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Bordetella pertussis is a small gram-negative coccobacillus that attaches to ciliated respiratory epithelium with filamentous hemagglutinin, pertactin, and fimbriae. It stays on the epithelial surface and causes disease through toxins. Pertussis toxin ADP-ribosylates the inhibitory G protein (Gi), so Gi can no longer restrain adenylate cyclase and cAMP rises in many cell types. Adenylate cyclase toxin enters phagocytes and makes cAMP directly, weakening their killing. Tracheal cytotoxin, a fragment of peptidoglycan, kills ciliated cells, so mucus and debris accumulate in the airways. Spread is by respiratory droplets, and the disease is highly contagious."},{"type":"table","title":"Clinical stages of pertussis","cols":["Stage","Approximate duration","Features","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Catarrhal","1 to 2 weeks","Rhinorrhea, mild cough, low-grade fever; most contagious; culture most often positive","Bacteria multiply on ciliated epithelium"],["Paroxysmal","2 to 6 weeks or longer","Bursts of rapid coughs on one breath, then an inspiratory whoop; posttussive vomiting, cyanosis, exhaustion; well between attacks","Ciliated cells lost; mucus pools and triggers cough; toxin effects peak"],["Convalescent","Weeks to months","Paroxysms gradually lessen; may return with later viral infections","Epithelium slowly regenerates"]],"caption":"The whoop is a forceful inspiration through a narrowed glottis after the coughing burst empties the lungs."},{"type":"p","text":"A marked absolute lymphocytosis is typical, often with a total white cell count well above normal. The mechanism is that lymphocytes leave the blood in response to chemokines, whose receptors signal through Gi. When pertussis toxin disables Gi, lymphocytes cannot migrate into lymph nodes and tissues, so they accumulate in the circulation. Very high counts in young infants are associated with pulmonary hypertension and a worse outcome."},{"type":"p","text":"Pertussis is most dangerous in infants younger than about 6 months, especially those too young to be fully immunized. They often do not whoop, because they cannot generate a forceful inspiration; instead they gag, turn cyanotic, or have apnea. Pneumonia, from Bordetella itself or secondary bacteria, is the most common cause of death, and hypoxia can cause seizures and encephalopathy. Adolescents and adults, whose vaccine immunity has waned, often have only a prolonged cough without a whoop and serve as a source for infants. The force of the paroxysms explains other complications: subconjunctival hemorrhage, rib fracture, pneumothorax, and hernia."},{"type":"p","text":"Diagnosis uses nasopharyngeal PCR or culture on Bordet-Gengou or Regan-Lowe medium, and culture is most likely to be positive early, before the typical cough is recognized. The fall in positivity later explains why a child in the paroxysmal stage may have a negative culture."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-08-C08","obj":"PU-14-H.8","title":"Respiratory syncytial virus in infants","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) is an enveloped, negative-sense, single-stranded RNA virus long grouped with the paramyxoviruses and now placed in the pneumovirus family. Its G protein attaches to respiratory epithelium and its F (fusion) protein fuses infected cells with neighbors, forming the multinucleated syncytia that give the virus its name; it has no hemagglutinin or neuraminidase. Nearly all children are infected by about age 2, and immunity is incomplete, so reinfection occurs throughout life, usually as a cold. In temperate climates, epidemics occur each winter, roughly from late fall to early spring."},{"type":"p","text":"RSV is the most common cause of bronchiolitis and of viral pneumonia in infants, with hospitalization peaking in the first months of life. Infection begins in the nose and spreads to the bronchiolar epithelium over a few days. Infected cells become necrotic and slough, lymphocytes infiltrate around the bronchioles, the submucosa swells, and mucus mixes with cell debris to form plugs."},{"type":"flow","title":"From bronchiolar injury to hypoxemia","steps":["RSV infects and kills bronchiolar epithelial cells","Edema, mucus, and debris narrow small bronchioles","Airway resistance rises steeply in tiny infant airways","Partial plugs trap air: hyperinflation and wheeze","Complete plugs absorb distal gas: patchy atelectasis","Ventilation and perfusion mismatch causes hypoxemia"],"caption":"Small airway radius, not alveolar filling, drives most of the disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Infant bronchioles have a very small radius, and resistance to laminar flow varies inversely with the fourth power of the radius, so a thin layer of edema and mucus raises resistance far more than it would in an adult. Air can pass a partial plug on inspiration, when airways widen, but is trapped on expiration, when they narrow; this produces hyperinflation and expiratory wheeze. The illness starts with rhinorrhea and cough and progresses over a few days to tachypnea, wheezing, fine crackles, nasal flaring, and intercostal retractions. Very young and premature infants may present with apnea before other signs."},{"type":"p","text":"Severe disease is most likely in infants born prematurely, who have small airways, little maternal antibody (most IgG crosses the placenta late in pregnancy), and often chronic lung disease of prematurity; in those with hemodynamically significant congenital heart disease, especially lesions with increased pulmonary blood flow or pulmonary hypertension, which leave little cardiopulmonary reserve; and in those with chronic lung disease, immunodeficiency, or neuromuscular weakness. RSV also causes serious lower respiratory infection in older adults and the immunocompromised. Diagnosis, when needed, is by antigen or PCR testing of nasopharyngeal secretions."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-08-C09","obj":"PU-14-H.9","title":"Primary tuberculosis, latent infection, and tuberculosis testing","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis spreads in droplet nuclei, particles about 1 to 5 um across that stay suspended in air and reach the alveoli. Primary tuberculosis is infection in a person never exposed before. Inhaled bacilli land where ventilation is greatest, typically in the lower part of an upper lobe or the upper part of a lower lobe, close to the pleura. Alveolar macrophages ingest them, but the organism blocks phagosome maturation and fusion with lysosomes, so it multiplies inside the cells and travels in lymphatics to hilar nodes and often briefly into the blood."},{"type":"flow","title":"Primary infection to latency","steps":["Droplet nuclei deliver bacilli to subpleural alveoli","Bacilli multiply in macrophages; spread to hilar nodes","About 3 weeks: IL-12 drives Th1 cells","Interferon-gamma activates macrophages to kill bacilli","Epithelioid granulomas with caseous centers form; TNF-alpha maintains them","Ghon focus and hilar node heal by fibrosis and calcification","Viable bacilli persist: latent infection"],"caption":"The same cell-mediated response that contains the bacilli produces the tuberculin reaction and caseous necrosis."},{"type":"p","text":"The parenchymal lesion, about 1 to 1.5 cm of gray-white consolidation with central caseation, is the Ghon focus; with the involved hilar nodes it forms the Ghon complex, which after fibrosis and calcification is called a Ranke complex. Granulomas contain epithelioid macrophages and Langhans giant cells around caseous necrosis. In most people this response contains the infection with few or no symptoms, leaving latent tuberculosis infection: viable but controlled bacilli, no symptoms, and no transmission. Without treatment, the lifetime risk of reactivation is about 5 to 10% in immunocompetent people and much higher with HIV. In young children and the immunosuppressed, primary infection may instead progress directly to pneumonia, miliary disease, or meningitis (progressive primary tuberculosis)."},{"type":"p","text":"The tuberculin skin test (Mantoux) injects purified protein derivative (PPD) intradermally; sensitized T cells recruit macrophages over 48 to 72 hours, producing induration, a type IV hypersensitivity reaction. Induration, not redness, is measured, and the cutoff depends on risk: about 5 mm in HIV infection, recent close contacts, and the immunosuppressed; about 10 mm in other higher-risk groups; and about 15 mm with no risk factors. Interferon-gamma release assays (IGRAs) instead use antigens absent from BCG."},{"type":"table","title":"Interpreting tuberculosis immune tests","cols":["Feature","Tuberculin skin test","Interferon-gamma release assay"],"rows":[["What it detects","Delayed-type skin reaction to PPD","T cell interferon-gamma response to ESAT-6 and CFP-10"],["Prior BCG","Can cause a false positive","No false positive"],["Nontuberculous mycobacteria","Can cause a false positive","Rarely, except a few species (for example M. kansasii, M. marinum)"],["False negatives","HIV, steroids, malnutrition, sarcoidosis, overwhelming tuberculosis, infection under about 8 weeks old","Same causes of weak T cell response"],["Latent versus active disease","Cannot distinguish","Cannot distinguish"]],"caption":"A positive test shows sensitization only; radiography and sputum testing decide whether disease is active."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading a tuberculin skin test","text":"A 30-year-old woman who moved from a country with high tuberculosis prevalence 2 years ago, and who received BCG in infancy, has 12 mm of induration at 72 hours on screening. Step 1: recent arrival from a high-prevalence country places her in the 10 mm group, so the test is positive. Step 2: BCG can cause a positive skin test, so the result may not mean infection. Step 3: an IGRA uses antigens absent from BCG; a positive IGRA supports true infection, while a negative one points to a BCG effect. Step 4: neither test shows activity; a chest radiograph and, if abnormal or symptomatic, sputum testing decide between latent and active tuberculosis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with advanced HIV and a CD4 count of 50 cells per microliter has active tuberculosis but 0 mm of induration on a tuberculin skin test. Why?","explain":"The skin reaction needs CD4 Th1 cells to recruit and activate macrophages. Without them there is no induration even with active infection, an example of anergy.","check":"Compare with the false negative row of the testing table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-08-C10","obj":"PU-14-H.10","title":"Secondary tuberculosis and miliary spread","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Secondary tuberculosis arises in a person already sensitized, usually by reactivation of latent bacilli when cell-mediated immunity weakens, and sometimes by reinfection. It favors the apical and posterior segments of the upper lobes. M. tuberculosis is a strict aerobe, and the apices have the highest ventilation to perfusion ratio in upright lungs, so alveolar PO2 there is highest; reduced lymphatic flow at the apices may also contribute."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the host is already sensitized, the response is rapid and destructive. Caseous necrosis enlarges and liquefies, and when it erodes into a bronchus the contents are coughed out, leaving a cavity. Cavities contain enormous numbers of bacilli in a well-oxygenated space, which makes these patients highly infectious and spreads infection along the airways to other parts of the lung. Erosion of vessels in the cavity wall causes hemoptysis, occasionally massive from a dilated pulmonary artery branch (Rasmussen aneurysm). Healing produces fibrosis that retracts the upper lobes."},{"type":"p","text":"The systemic symptoms come from cytokines, not from the bacillus directly. TNF-alpha and IL-1 from activated macrophages act on the hypothalamus to cause fever, often in the evening with drenching night sweats, and TNF-alpha (also called cachectin) promotes the loss of appetite, muscle, and fat that causes weight loss. Chronic productive cough, fatigue, and hemoptysis complete the picture. Sputum smear for acid-fast bacilli, nucleic acid amplification, and culture confirm the diagnosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Miliary tuberculosis results when bacilli enter the bloodstream in large numbers, from a primary or secondary focus, and seed many organs with lesions about 2 mm across that resemble millet seeds. Erosion into pulmonary arteries or lymphatics seeds the lungs, while spread into pulmonary veins and the systemic circulation seeds other organs. Extrapulmonary disease can also appear years after silent seeding during primary infection."},{"type":"table","title":"Major extrapulmonary sites of tuberculosis","cols":["Site","Disease and findings"],"rows":[["Meninges","Basilar meningitis with cranial nerve palsies, high protein, low glucose, lymphocytic cerebrospinal fluid"],["Vertebrae","Pott disease: thoracolumbar vertebral bodies and discs collapse anteriorly, causing kyphosis (gibbus) and sometimes psoas abscess or cord compression"],["Kidneys and urinary tract","Sterile pyuria (white cells with negative routine culture), hematuria"],["Adrenal glands","Bilateral destruction causing primary adrenal insufficiency"],["Lymph nodes","Cervical lymphadenitis (scrofula)"],["Liver, spleen, bone marrow","Miliary granulomas, cytopenias"]],"caption":"Extrapulmonary disease is more common with HIV and in young children."},{"type":"p","text":"Risk of reactivation and dissemination rises whenever Th1 immunity or granuloma maintenance fails: HIV (the strongest risk factor), TNF-alpha inhibitors (which dissolve granulomas), glucocorticoids, transplantation, diabetes mellitus, silicosis, chronic kidney disease, malnutrition, and older age. In advanced HIV, too few CD4 cells remain to form organized granulomas, so tuberculosis often looks like primary disease, with lower or middle lobe infiltrates, hilar nodes, few cavities, and more extrapulmonary and miliary disease."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-08-C11","obj":"PU-14-H.11","title":"Coronaviruses: common colds, SARS, MERS, and COVID-19","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Coronaviruses are enveloped, positive-sense, single-stranded RNA viruses with large genomes (about 30 kb) and club-shaped spike proteins that form a crown on electron microscopy. Four endemic human strains (229E, OC43, NL63, and HKU1) cause common colds, mostly in winter, and a minority of lower respiratory infections. Three strains that crossed from animals caused severe pneumonia: SARS-CoV (2002 to 2003), MERS-CoV (from 2012, Arabian Peninsula, linked to dromedary camels, binding dipeptidyl peptidase 4), and SARS-CoV-2, which causes COVID-19 (from late 2019)."},{"type":"p","text":"SARS-CoV-2 spike binds angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 (ACE2), expressed on nasal ciliated and goblet cells, type II pneumocytes, vascular endothelium, and intestinal epithelium. Before fusion the spike must be cleaved, at the cell surface by the host protease TMPRSS2 or inside endosomes by cathepsins. High ACE2 expression in the nose favors early upper airway replication and spread before or without symptoms. Loss of smell results mainly from infection of the supporting (sustentacular) cells of the olfactory epithelium, which express ACE2, rather than of olfactory neurons, which helps explain why smell usually returns."},{"type":"p","text":"Most infections cause a mild upper respiratory illness. In a minority, disease progresses in the second week to pneumonia with bilateral, peripheral, lower zone ground-glass opacities. The lung shows diffuse alveolar damage, the tissue pattern of ARDS: injured type I and II pneumocytes, protein-rich edema, and hyaline membranes, followed by an organizing phase. Endothelial injury, complement activation, and inflammatory cytokines such as IL-6 make the blood hypercoagulable; D-dimer rises, and microthrombi in alveolar capillaries and venous and arterial thrombosis elsewhere are common in severe cases. Lymphopenia is typical."},{"type":"p","text":"Older age is the strongest risk factor for severe disease and death. Others include obesity, diabetes mellitus, cardiovascular and chronic kidney disease, chronic lung disease, and immunosuppression, together with male sex. Children usually have mild illness, but a few develop a delayed multisystem inflammatory syndrome weeks after infection. SARS and MERS caused similar diffuse alveolar damage with higher case fatality, but transmission occurred mainly after symptoms began, often in hospitals, which made them easier to contain."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-08-C12","obj":"PU-14-H.12","title":"Hospital-acquired pneumonia and aspiration syndromes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hospital-acquired pneumonia develops 48 hours or more after admission and was not incubating at admission; ventilator-associated pneumonia develops 48 hours or more after endotracheal intubation. The organisms differ from community-acquired pneumonia because the host and the route differ. Within days of serious illness, the oropharynx becomes colonized by gram-negative rods, partly because epithelial cells lose surface fibronectin that normally favors gram-positive flora. Raising gastric pH with acid suppression allows gastric colonization as well. These secretions are then microaspirated."},{"type":"p","text":"An endotracheal tube bypasses the nose, glottis, and cough, carries organisms in a biofilm on its surface, and allows secretions pooled above the cuff to leak into the trachea. Sedation and lying flat add to aspiration. The usual organisms are Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Staphylococcus aureus including methicillin-resistant strains (MRSA), and enteric gram-negative rods such as Klebsiella, Escherichia coli, Enterobacter, and Serratia, with Acinetobacter in some units. Multidrug resistance and severe underlying illness give these pneumonias a high mortality. Pseudomonas also stands out in cystic fibrosis, bronchiectasis, and neutropenia."},{"type":"p","text":"Aspiration covers two different processes that boards ask you to separate. Chemical aspiration pneumonitis (Mendelson syndrome) is acute lung injury from inhaled acidic gastric contents, typically during anesthesia, drug overdose, or seizure in a person with a full stomach. Acid burns the airway and alveolar epithelium and the injury is initially sterile; dyspnea, wheeze, hypoxemia, and infiltrates in dependent segments appear within hours. It may resolve within a day or two or progress to ARDS. Bacterial aspiration pneumonia is infection by oropharyngeal bacteria in a person with dysphagia or reduced consciousness, often with poor dentition. It develops over days with fever, cough, and an infiltrate in a dependent segment and can progress to lung abscess and empyema (C06)."},{"type":"table","title":"Chemical pneumonitis and bacterial aspiration pneumonia","cols":["Feature","Chemical pneumonitis","Bacterial aspiration pneumonia"],"rows":[["Material aspirated","Acidic gastric contents","Oropharyngeal secretions with bacteria"],["Typical setting","Anesthesia, overdose, seizure with full stomach; often witnessed","Dysphagia, stroke, alcohol, poor dentition; often unwitnessed"],["Mechanism","Sterile acid injury and inflammation","Infection by oral anaerobes and streptococci; gram-negatives and S. aureus in hospital"],["Onset","Within hours","Over days"],["Course","May clear in 1 to 2 days or progress to ARDS","Persists; can form abscess or empyema"],["Fever and purulent sputum","Low-grade fever possible; no purulent sputum at first","Common"]],"caption":"Both favor dependent segments; timing and the presence of infection separate them, and pneumonitis can later become infected."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-08-C13","obj":"PU-14-H.13","title":"Influenza pneumonia, secondary bacterial pneumonia, and new strains","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Influenza viruses are enveloped orthomyxoviruses with a negative-sense RNA genome in eight separate segments. Hemagglutinin binds sialic acid on respiratory epithelium to start infection, and neuraminidase cleaves sialic acid to release new virions. The virus destroys ciliated columnar cells of the trachea and bronchi, causing necrotizing tracheobronchitis and loss of mucociliary clearance; regrowth of epithelium takes about two weeks or more."},{"type":"p","text":"Primary influenza pneumonia occurs when the virus reaches the alveoli. It causes interstitial inflammation and diffuse alveolar damage with hyaline membranes and can progress quickly to ARDS. Risk is highest in older adults, pregnancy, and people with heart or lung disease. Secondary bacterial pneumonia is more common and follows a typical sequence: the patient improves after the viral illness, then, often within the first two weeks, relapses with new fever, productive cough, and consolidation."},{"type":"flow","title":"Influenza opens the door to bacteria","steps":["Influenza kills ciliated airway epithelium","Mucociliary clearance fails; basement membrane exposed","Neuraminidase exposes receptors used by bacteria","Viral infection impairs macrophage and neutrophil killing","S. aureus or S. pneumoniae adhere and invade","Relapse with consolidation; necrotizing pneumonia with S. aureus"],"caption":"H. influenzae and Streptococcus pyogenes are less common causes of postinfluenza pneumonia."},{"type":"p","text":"Streptococcus pneumoniae remains the most common secondary pathogen, but Staphylococcus aureus is characteristic of postinfluenza pneumonia and is often necrotizing, with cavities, hemoptysis, and rapid deterioration; strains producing Panton-Valentine leukocidin, a toxin that lyses leukocytes, are especially destructive."},{"type":"p","text":"Influenza A infects wild waterfowl, the natural reservoir, as well as poultry, pigs, and humans, while influenza B is essentially human only. Antigenic drift is gradual point mutation in hemagglutinin and neuraminidase genes, causing yearly epidemics. Antigenic shift occurs when two influenza A strains infect the same cell and package each other's segments (reassortment), producing a new hemagglutinin or neuraminidase subtype to which the population has no immunity and which can cause a pandemic. Human-adapted strains prefer sialic acid in alpha-2,6 linkage, abundant in the upper airway; avian strains prefer alpha-2,3 linkage, found in birds and deeper in the human lung. Pigs carry both types, so they can be co-infected and act as mixing vessels. This receptor difference explains why avian strains such as H5N1 rarely infect humans and spread poorly between them, but cause severe lower respiratory disease when they do."},{"type":"table","title":"Antigenic drift and antigenic shift","cols":["Feature","Drift","Shift"],"rows":[["Genetic change","Point mutations in HA and NA genes","Reassortment of whole gene segments"],["Types affected","Influenza A and B","Influenza A only"],["Size of change","Minor; partial immunity persists","New HA or NA subtype; little or no immunity"],["Result","Seasonal epidemics","Possible pandemic"]],"caption":"Shift requires a segmented genome and an animal reservoir, which is why only influenza A shifts."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-14-08-C14","obj":"PU-14-H.14","title":"Zoonotic and bioterrorism pneumonias","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Several zoonotic organisms cause pneumonia when inhaled, and some are listed as potential bioterrorism agents because they are infectious by aerosol at low doses. The key to each is the exposure history, and the key to anthrax is that the lesion is in the mediastinum rather than the alveoli."},{"type":"p","text":"Bacillus anthracis is a large, gram-positive, spore-forming rod with a polypeptide capsule of poly-D-glutamate that resists phagocytosis. Inhalational anthrax follows exposure to spores from contaminated animal hides or wool (woolsorters disease) or a deliberate release. Inhaled spores are ingested by alveolar macrophages and carried to mediastinal lymph nodes, where they germinate. The bacteria release toxins that share a binding component, protective antigen: edema factor, an adenylate cyclase that raises cAMP and causes edema, and lethal factor, a zinc protease that cleaves MAP kinase kinases and kills macrophages. The result is hemorrhagic necrotizing lymphadenitis and mediastinitis, seen as a widened mediastinum, often with hemorrhagic pleural effusions, after a brief flu-like phase. Shock and hemorrhagic meningitis can follow. It does not spread from person to person."},{"type":"p","text":"Francisella tularensis, a small gram-negative coccobacillus that survives inside macrophages, causes tularemia; as few as about 10 to 50 organisms can cause infection. Humans are infected by handling rabbits and hares, by tick or deer fly bites, or by inhaling aerosols, for example when mowing over an infected carcass. Pneumonic tularemia causes fever, cough, and patchy infiltrates with hilar nodes and pleural effusion, and the organism is a hazard to laboratory workers."},{"type":"p","text":"Coxiella burnetii causes Q fever. It is an intracellular bacterium with a hardy, spore-like small form that survives drying for long periods. Infected cattle, sheep, and goats shed huge numbers in placentas and birth fluids, and people inhale contaminated dust, often without direct animal contact. Acute infection causes fever, headache, atypical pneumonia, and granulomatous hepatitis; chronic infection most often causes culture-negative endocarditis."},{"type":"p","text":"Burkholderia pseudomallei, a gram-negative rod in soil and surface water of Southeast Asia and northern Australia, causes melioidosis. Infection follows inhalation or skin inoculation, especially in the rainy season, and diabetes mellitus is the main risk factor. It causes acute pneumonia with sepsis, chronic cavitary upper lobe disease that mimics tuberculosis, and abscesses in the liver, spleen, and prostate, and it can reactivate years after exposure."},{"type":"table","title":"Zoonotic and bioterrorism pneumonias","cols":["Disease","Organism","Exposure","Distinctive lung finding"],"rows":[["Inhalational anthrax","Bacillus anthracis","Spores from hides, wool, or deliberate release","Widened mediastinum from hemorrhagic mediastinitis"],["Pneumonic tularemia","Francisella tularensis","Rabbits and hares, ticks, deer flies, aerosols","Patchy infiltrates, hilar nodes, pleural effusion"],["Q fever","Coxiella burnetii","Birth products of cattle, sheep, goats","Atypical pneumonia with hepatitis; later endocarditis"],["Melioidosis","Burkholderia pseudomallei","Soil and water in Southeast Asia, northern Australia","Sepsis with pneumonia, or upper lobe cavities resembling tuberculosis"]],"caption":"A travel, occupational, or animal history is usually the key clue."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"The organizing idea of this chapter is that host defense determines the disease. Encapsulated extracellular bacteria such as S. pneumoniae and Klebsiella are cleared by antibody, complement, and neutrophils, and they fill the alveoli with neutrophil exudate (lobar pneumonia, physiologically a shunt). Intracellular organisms that survive inside macrophages, including M. tuberculosis, the endemic fungi, and Legionella, require Th1 cells and interferon-gamma; mycobacteria and fungi form granulomas, and all are released by HIV, glucocorticoids, and TNF-alpha inhibitors. Aspergillus hyphae need neutrophils, Pneumocystis needs CD4 cells, and allergic hosts turn harmless colonization into type I and type III hypersensitivity. Mechanical defenses add the anatomy: loss of cilia (influenza, pertussis, Mycoplasma), loss of the cough and glottic barrier (aspiration, intubation), and small infant airways (RSV) explain who gets which infection."},{"type":"p","text":"Toxins explain disease far from the organism: diphtheria toxin stops protein synthesis in heart and nerves, pertussis toxin produces lymphocytosis by disabling Gi, and anthrax toxins act in the mediastinum. Physiology then explains the findings: consolidation causes shunt and loud transmitted sounds, alveolar wall thickening causes diffusion limitation seen on exertion, and bronchiolar plugs cause air trapping. These are general rules for reasoning; individual organisms can cross patterns, so the host, exposure, and timing should always be weighed together."}],"summary":["Lobar pneumonia (mainly S. pneumoniae, also Klebsiella) consolidates a lobe through congestion, red and gray hepatization, and resolution, with dullness, increased fremitus, bronchial breath sounds, egophony, and shunt hypoxemia; bronchopneumonia (S. aureus, H. influenzae, Pseudomonas) is patchy and centered on bronchioles.","Atypical pneumonia inflames the alveolar septa, so the film looks worse than the patient; Mycoplasma lacks a cell wall and causes cold agglutinins, and Legionella from water aerosols causes high fever, diarrhea, confusion, and hyponatremia.","Diphtheria toxin, encoded by a prophage, ADP-ribosylates EF-2, forming an adherent pseudomembrane locally and causing myocarditis with heart block and demyelinating cranial and peripheral neuropathy at a distance; the Elek test proves toxin production.","Histoplasma, Blastomyces, and Coccidioides infect healthy hosts by inhaled conidia, are controlled by Th1 granulomas that mimic tuberculosis, and disseminate to skin, bone, and meninges when cell-mediated immunity fails.","Pneumocystis pneumonia (CD4 below about 200) causes bilateral ground-glass opacities, high LDH, and exertional desaturation from diffusion limitation; Aspergillus causes aspergilloma in old cavities, angioinvasion in neutropenia, and ABPA in asthma and cystic fibrosis.","Aspiration of oral anaerobes in reduced consciousness or dysphagia causes lung abscess with an air-fluid level and putrid sputum in dependent right lung segments; hospital and ventilator pneumonias are caused by Pseudomonas, MRSA, and enteric gram-negative rods, and chemical pneumonitis is a sterile acid injury that appears within hours.","Pertussis toxin inactivates Gi, raising cAMP and causing lymphocytosis; the disease passes through catarrhal, paroxysmal, and convalescent stages, and young infants have apnea and pneumonia instead of a whoop.","RSV is the leading cause of infant bronchiolitis in winter, with bronchiolar plugging, wheeze, air trapping, and hypoxemia that are most severe in premature infants and those with heart or lung disease.","Primary tuberculosis forms a Ghon complex contained by Th1 caseating granulomas into latent infection; the skin test and IGRA detect sensitization only, and only the IGRA avoids BCG false positives. Reactivation favors the oxygen-rich apices with cavitation and cytokine-driven systemic symptoms, and miliary spread reaches meninges, kidneys, adrenals, and vertebrae.","SARS-CoV-2 enters through ACE2 with TMPRSS2 cleavage and causes anosmia, diffuse alveolar damage, and thrombosis; influenza causes secondary S. aureus and S. pneumoniae pneumonia and new pandemic strains by reassortment, and anthrax, tularemia, Q fever, and melioidosis are recognized by exposure history."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lung: pulmonary infections, including community-acquired, hospital-acquired, and aspiration pneumonia, lung abscess, tuberculosis, and fungal infections"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Corynebacteria; Bordetella; Mycobacteria; Mycoplasmas; Legionellae; Medical mycology; Orthomyxoviruses; Paramyxoviruses; Coronaviruses"},{"book":"Abbas Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Immunity to microbes: intracellular bacteria, fungi, and viruses; hypersensitivity disorders"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Physical principles of gas exchange and diffusion through the respiratory membrane; physiology of pneumonia and abnormal ventilation-perfusion ratios"},{"book":"Moore Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax: trachea, bronchi, and bronchopulmonary segments"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Infectious diseases: pneumonia"}]},"PU-2":{"unit":"PU-2","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-02","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-02-01","title":"Respiratory Histology","subject":"Respiratory System (RESP)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows the airway wall from the trachea to the alveolus and explains why its lining, cartilage, glands, and muscle change at each level. It covers the cells that clean the air, the cells that exchange gas and repair the alveolus, the ciliary machinery of mucociliary clearance, the blood-air barrier, and the squamous lining of the true vocal folds. Board questions use this histology to explain smoking injury, primary ciliary dyskinesia, air trapping in emphysema, low diffusing capacity in fibrosis and edema, and the predominance of squamous cell carcinoma in the larynx.","prerequisites":["Classification of epithelia by layering and cell shape, and the basement membrane","Microtubules, motor proteins (dynein), and ATP hydrolysis","Gross anatomy of the larynx, trachea, and bronchial tree (PU-3, PU-4)","Lung bud development and surfactant maturation (PU-1)","Metaplasia, dysplasia, and neoplasia as general pathology concepts"],"connections":[{"to":"Ventilation and airflow (PU-5, PU-6)","how":"Surfactant from type II cells and alveolar tethering of bronchioles determine compliance, airway resistance, and expiratory airway collapse."},{"to":"Gas exchange (PU-7)","how":"The thickness and area of the blood-air barrier enter directly into Fick's law and the diffusing capacity for carbon monoxide (DLCO)."},{"to":"Pulmonary genetics (PU-10)","how":"Dynein gene mutations cause primary ciliary dyskinesia, and CFTR mutations cause cystic fibrosis, two different routes to failed mucus clearance."},{"to":"Obstructive and restrictive diseases (PU-14-A, PU-14-B)","how":"Chronic bronchitis, emphysema, and interstitial fibrosis are each explained by injury to a specific layer or cell described here."},{"to":"Neoplasms of the respiratory tract (PU-14-F)","how":"Squamous metaplasia in smokers and the native squamous lining of the vocal folds explain where squamous cell carcinoma arises."}],"terms":[{"term":"Pseudostratified epithelium","def":"A single layer of cells that all touch the basement membrane but whose nuclei sit at different heights, so it looks layered."},{"term":"Goblet cell","def":"A mucus-secreting epithelial cell whose apical cytoplasm is filled with mucinogen granules."},{"term":"Club cell","def":"A nonciliated, dome-topped secretory cell of the bronchioles that makes protective proteins, metabolizes inhaled chemicals, and serves as a local progenitor (formerly called the Clara cell)."},{"term":"Type I pneumocyte","def":"A very thin squamous alveolar cell that covers most of the alveolar surface and forms the epithelial part of the blood-air barrier."},{"term":"Type II pneumocyte","def":"A cuboidal alveolar cell with lamellar bodies that secretes surfactant and divides to replace injured type I cells."},{"term":"Lamellar body","def":"A membrane-bound organelle in type II cells that stores surfactant phospholipid in concentric layers before secretion."},{"term":"Axoneme","def":"The microtubule core of a cilium, arranged as nine outer doublets around a central pair (9 plus 2)."},{"term":"Radial traction","def":"The outward pull that elastic alveolar walls exert on the bronchioles embedded among them, holding the bronchioles open."},{"term":"Squamous metaplasia","def":"Reversible replacement of ciliated columnar epithelium by stratified squamous epithelium in response to chronic irritation."},{"term":"Blood-air barrier","def":"The tissue gas crosses between alveolar air and capillary blood: type I cell, fused basement membranes, and capillary endothelium."},{"term":"DLCO","def":"Diffusing capacity of the lung for carbon monoxide, a measure of how readily gas crosses the blood-air barrier, which falls when the barrier thickens or its area is lost."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-02-01-C01","obj":"PU-2.1","title":"How the airway epithelium changes from trachea to alveolus","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The conducting airways warm, humidify, and clean inspired air, while the respiratory portion exchanges gas. The lining changes along the tree to match these jobs, and it does so gradually rather than at sharp boundaries. As a rule, the epithelium becomes shorter and simpler distally, goblet cells thin out, and ciliated cells give way to secretory club cells and finally to the flat cells of the alveolus."},{"type":"p","text":"The trachea and bronchi are lined by respiratory epithelium: pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells. Every cell touches the basement membrane, but basal cells (the stem cells of this lining) stay short, so the nuclei sit at several levels. Ciliated cells are the most numerous; goblet cells and the submucosal glands supply the mucus that traps particles. Scattered brush cells and neuroendocrine cells are also present."},{"type":"p","text":"In the bronchioles the epithelium becomes simple ciliated columnar, then simple cuboidal in the terminal bronchioles, the last purely conducting segment. Goblet cells fall away and are absent from terminal bronchioles, where club cells take over secretion. Respiratory bronchioles keep a low cuboidal lining with club cells and a few ciliated cells, but alveoli open from their walls, so gas exchange begins at this level. Alveolar ducts, sacs, and alveoli are lined by type I and type II pneumocytes."},{"type":"table","title":"Epithelium and wall along the lower airway","cols":["Segment","Epithelium","Goblet cells","Club cells","Function"],"rows":[["Trachea and bronchi","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar","Many","None","Conducting; mucus trapping and clearance"],["Larger bronchioles","Simple ciliated columnar","Few, decreasing","Present, increasing","Conducting"],["Terminal bronchioles","Simple cuboidal, ciliated and club cells","None","Many","Last conducting segment"],["Respiratory bronchioles","Low cuboidal, interrupted by alveoli","None","Present","Transitional; first gas exchange"],["Alveoli","Type I and type II pneumocytes","None","None","Gas exchange"]],"caption":"Moving distally, the lining shortens from pseudostratified to cuboidal to squamous, goblet cells disappear, and club cells peak in terminal bronchioles."},{"type":"flow","title":"Shape of the lining from trachea to alveolus","steps":["Trachea and bronchi: pseudostratified ciliated columnar with goblet cells","Bronchioles: simple ciliated columnar, goblet cells thinning","Terminal bronchioles: simple cuboidal with club cells, no goblet cells","Respiratory bronchioles: cuboidal lining broken by alveolar openings","Alveoli: type I and type II pneumocytes"],"caption":"The epithelium flattens as the airway changes from cleaning air to exchanging gas."},{"type":"p","text":"This gradient explains where disease appears. Goblet cell hyperplasia and gland enlargement in chronic bronchitis mainly affect the large airways, while centriacinar emphysema in smokers begins at the respiratory bronchiole, the junction of conducting and exchanging tissue. Because the alveolar lining is so thin, injury there leads directly to impaired diffusion and leak of fluid into airspaces."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-02-01-C02","obj":"PU-2.2","title":"Bronchi versus bronchioles: cartilage, glands, and what holds small airways open","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The trachea is held open by 16 to 20 C-shaped rings of hyaline cartilage whose open ends face posteriorly and are bridged by the trachealis smooth muscle. As the trachea divides, the rings give way to irregular cartilage plates in the bronchi. Bronchi also carry seromucous glands in the submucosa, abundant goblet cells, and spirally arranged bundles of smooth muscle in the lamina propria, inside the cartilage."},{"type":"p","text":"Bronchioles are defined histologically by the absence of cartilage and submucosal glands. They are usually about 1 mm or less in diameter, goblet cells are few and then absent, and the smooth muscle layer is relatively thick for the size of the tube. A practical rule for slides is that cartilage and glands occur together: where the cartilage plates end, the glands end too."},{"type":"table","title":"Bronchus versus bronchiole","cols":["Feature","Bronchus","Bronchiole"],"rows":[["Cartilage","Irregular hyaline plates","None"],["Submucosal glands","Present","None"],["Goblet cells","Many","Few, absent in terminal bronchioles"],["Epithelium","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar","Simple columnar to cuboidal"],["Smooth muscle","Spiral bundles in the lamina propria, inside the cartilage","Prominent relative to wall thickness"],["What sets caliber","Mostly fixed by cartilage","Muscle tone and radial traction"]],"caption":"Absence of cartilage and glands identifies a bronchiole and explains why its diameter can change."},{"type":"p","text":"Without cartilage, a bronchiole's diameter is set by two opposing influences. Smooth muscle tone narrows it; parasympathetic (vagal, muscarinic) activity and mediators such as histamine and leukotrienes increase tone, while beta-2 adrenergic stimulation relaxes it. Radial traction holds it open: bronchioles run through the lung parenchyma, and the elastic walls of the surrounding alveoli attach to their outer surface and pull outward. That pull increases as lung volume increases, which is why airway resistance falls at high lung volumes."},{"type":"flow","title":"Loss of radial traction in emphysema","steps":["Protease destruction of alveolar walls","Fewer elastic attachments pull on each bronchiole","Forced expiration raises pleural pressure around small airways","Unsupported bronchioles collapse early in expiration","Air is trapped distally; residual volume rises"],"caption":"Emphysema narrows small airways by removing their external support, not by thickening their walls."},{"type":"p","text":"This explains two classic patterns. In asthma, smooth muscle contraction narrows both bronchi and bronchioles, but bronchioles narrow most freely because no cartilage limits them. In emphysema, the bronchiole wall may be nearly normal, but loss of tethering lets it close during forced expiration, producing air trapping and an increased residual volume. Cartilage protects the bronchi from this expiratory collapse, so air trapping arises in the small airways."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a patient with emphysema have more trouble breathing out forcefully than breathing in?","explain":"During inspiration pleural pressure is negative and pulls bronchioles open, while during forced expiration positive pleural pressure squeezes bronchioles that have lost the alveolar attachments that normally hold them open.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on radial traction and the emphysema flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-02-01-C03","obj":"PU-2.3","title":"Type I and type II pneumocytes, club cells, and alveolar macrophages","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Type I pneumocytes are extremely thin squamous cells that cover about 95% of the alveolar surface, although they make up only about 40% of alveolar epithelial cells. Their cytoplasm spreads into sheets thin enough for gas to cross easily, and tight junctions between them stop fluid leaking from the interstitium into the airspace. They do not divide and are easily injured by toxins, oxidants, and infection."},{"type":"p","text":"Type II pneumocytes are cuboidal cells that bulge into the alveolus, often at the corners where alveolar walls meet. They are more numerous than type I cells but cover only about 5% of the surface. Their lamellar bodies store surfactant, a mixture of phospholipids (mainly dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine) and surfactant proteins A, B, C, and D. Surfactant lowers alveolar surface tension, which increases compliance and keeps small alveoli from collapsing. Proteins A and D also bind microbes and help macrophages engulf them. Type II cells are the progenitors of the alveolus: after injury they proliferate and differentiate into new type I cells, so type II cell hyperplasia on biopsy is a sign of alveolar damage and repair."},{"type":"p","text":"Club cells are nonciliated cells with dome-shaped apices that project into the lumen of bronchioles, most densely in terminal bronchioles. They secrete club cell secretory protein and other protective proteins into the fluid lining the small airways. Their abundant smooth endoplasmic reticulum contains cytochrome P450 enzymes that metabolize inhaled chemicals; this can detoxify a compound or convert it into a reactive product, so club cells are both protectors and targets. They also act as progenitors that renew themselves and ciliated cells after bronchiolar injury."},{"type":"p","text":"Alveolar macrophages lie on the alveolar surface and in the septa. They are derived from monocytes: most arise from fetal monocytes that seed the lung around birth and then renew locally, and blood monocytes add to them during inflammation. They phagocytose particles, microbes, and surplus surfactant, then move up the mucociliary escalator or enter lymphatics. Macrophages filled with carbon are called dust cells. In chronic left heart failure, red cells leak from congested alveolar capillaries, macrophages break down their hemoglobin, and the iron is stored as hemosiderin; these hemosiderin-laden macrophages, called heart failure cells, stain blue with Prussian blue and point to chronic pulmonary congestion."},{"type":"table","title":"Cells of the distal airway and alveolus","cols":["Cell","Location","Key structure","Main functions","Clinical link"],"rows":[["Type I pneumocyte","Alveolar wall","Thin squamous sheet, tight junctions","Gas exchange surface; fluid barrier","Necrosis in diffuse alveolar damage"],["Type II pneumocyte","Alveolar wall, corners","Cuboidal, lamellar bodies","Surfactant; regenerates type I cells","Surfactant deficiency in preterm infants; hyperplasia after injury"],["Club cell","Bronchioles, mostly terminal","Dome apex, smooth ER","Protective secretion; P450 metabolism; progenitor","Target of some inhaled toxins"],["Alveolar macrophage","Alveolar space and septa","Phagolysosomes, inclusions","Phagocytosis; clears particles and surfactant","Dust cells; heart failure cells"]],"caption":"Type I cells are built for diffusion; type II cells and club cells provide secretion and repair; macrophages provide clearance."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-02-01-C04","obj":"PU-2.4","title":"Mucociliary clearance, the ciliary axoneme, and how clearance fails","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Mucociliary clearance is the main defense of the conducting airways. The surface fluid has two layers: a thin, watery periciliary layer in which the cilia beat freely, and a gel mucus layer on top that traps inhaled particles and microbes. Each ciliated cell carries about 200 to 300 cilia that beat in coordinated waves; the tips catch the mucus during the forward stroke and carry the blanket upward toward the pharynx, where it is swallowed or coughed out. Clearance needs three things: working cilia, mucus of the right amount and viscosity, and enough periciliary fluid, whose depth depends on ion transport through channels such as CFTR."},{"type":"p","text":"Each motile cilium is built around an axoneme of nine outer microtubule doublets surrounding two central single microtubules, the 9 plus 2 pattern. Inner and outer dynein arms project from each doublet toward its neighbor. Dynein is an ATPase motor: it hydrolyzes ATP and walks along the adjacent doublet, so neighboring doublets try to slide past each other. Nexin links and radial spokes tie the doublets together and to the central pair, so that sliding cannot run freely and is converted into bending. The cilium grows from a basal body of nine microtubule triplets with no central pair."},{"type":"flow","title":"Mucociliary clearance","steps":["Inhaled particles stick to mucus from goblet cells and glands","Dynein arms hydrolyze ATP and slide adjacent outer doublets","Nexin links and radial spokes convert sliding into bending","Coordinated beats move the mucus blanket toward the pharynx","Mucus is swallowed or coughed out"],"caption":"Clearance fails if cilia stop, mucus becomes abnormal, or the periciliary fluid layer is lost."},{"type":"p","text":"Smoking damages clearance from both sides. Smoke slows ciliary beating, and chronic irritation stimulates goblet cell hyperplasia and enlargement of submucosal glands, so more mucus is made. With persistent injury, basal cells differentiate into stratified squamous epithelium instead of ciliated columnar cells. This squamous metaplasia is an adaptation: the new lining resists irritation but has no cilia and no goblet cells, so in metaplastic patches the escalator stops. Metaplasia is reversible if smoking stops, but under continued exposure it can progress to dysplasia and squamous cell carcinoma of the bronchus."},{"type":"flow","title":"Smoking and loss of airway clearance","steps":["Smoke chronically irritates the bronchial lining","Glands enlarge and goblet cells multiply: more mucus","Cilia slow and squamous metaplasia removes ciliated cells","Mucus is made faster than it is cleared and pools","Bacteria grow in retained mucus: recurrent bronchitis and pneumonia"],"caption":"Increased production plus reduced removal is the mechanism of chronic productive cough and infection in smokers."},{"type":"p","text":"Primary ciliary dyskinesia is an autosomal recessive defect of ciliary structure, most often absent or abnormal dynein arms. Mucus is normal, but cilia cannot move it, so patients have chronic sinusitis, otitis, and bronchiectasis from repeated infection. The same motor drives sperm flagella, so men are usually infertile, and motile cilia in the embryonic node set left-right asymmetry, so about half of patients have situs inversus. The triad of situs inversus, chronic sinusitis, and bronchiectasis is called Kartagener syndrome."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: two causes of retained mucus","text":"A 20-year-old has recurrent pneumonia, chronic sinusitis, and bronchiectasis. Step 1: ask whether the problem is the mucus or the cilia. In cystic fibrosis, defective CFTR reduces chloride and fluid secretion, the periciliary layer collapses, and thick mucus pins down cilia that are themselves normal; pancreatic insufficiency and a high sweat chloride follow from the same channel defect in other organs. Step 2: in primary ciliary dyskinesia, the mucus is normal but the axoneme lacks working dynein arms, so cilia are immotile or beat abnormally. Step 3: look for clues outside the lung. Dextrocardia or male infertility with immotile sperm points to ciliary dyskinesia; steatorrhea and salty sweat point to cystic fibrosis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-02-01-C05","obj":"PU-2.5","title":"The blood-air barrier and diffusion","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Oxygen moving from alveolar gas into blood crosses the blood-air barrier. On the thin side of each alveolar septum, the barrier has three tissue layers: the cytoplasm of a type I pneumocyte, the fused basement membranes of the alveolar epithelium and the capillary endothelium, and the capillary endothelial cell. Gas also passes through the thin surfactant-containing fluid film on the alveolar surface and, after the endothelium, through plasma and the red cell membrane. Over most of its area the barrier is about 0.2 to 0.6 micrometers thick, and its total area in an adult is about 70 square meters."},{"type":"flow","title":"Path of oxygen from alveolus to hemoglobin","steps":["Alveolar gas","Surface fluid film with surfactant","Type I pneumocyte cytoplasm","Fused basement membranes","Capillary endothelium","Plasma, red cell membrane, hemoglobin"],"caption":"Thickening at any step lengthens the diffusion path."},{"type":"p","text":"Each septum also has a thick side, where the basement membranes separate around an interstitium of fibroblasts, collagen, and elastic fibers. Alveolar walls themselves lack lymphatics; interstitial fluid moves toward lymphatics that begin around the smallest bronchioles and vessels. Excess filtered fluid collects first on the thick side, which protects gas exchange early, but fluid that exceeds lymphatic drainage widens the barrier and then floods alveoli."},{"type":"p","text":"Fick's law states that the rate of gas transfer across a sheet of tissue is proportional to its area, the partial pressure difference, and the diffusion constant of the gas, and inversely proportional to its thickness. Thickening the barrier therefore slows transfer. In interstitial fibrosis, repeated injury to type I cells is followed by type II cell hyperplasia, fibroblast proliferation, and collagen deposition in the septa. In interstitial edema, fluid widens the interstitium. Both lower DLCO. Emphysema also lowers DLCO, but by destroying surface area rather than by thickening the barrier."},{"type":"p","text":"Oxygen is affected more than carbon dioxide. CO2 is much more soluble and diffuses about 20 times faster across the barrier, so it still equilibrates when O2 transfer has become limited. At rest, red cells spend about 0.75 s in the alveolar capillary, while O2 normally equilibrates within about 0.25 s, leaving a large reserve. A thickened barrier uses up part of that reserve, so resting saturation may be near normal. During exercise, transit time can fall to about 0.25 s, equilibration is no longer complete, and saturation falls."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: why fibrosis shows first with exertion","text":"A patient with interstitial fibrosis has a barrier about twice normal thickness. Step 1: by Fick's law, doubling thickness at the same gradient halves the rate of O2 transfer, so equilibration takes roughly twice as long, about 0.5 s instead of 0.25 s. Step 2: at rest, transit time is about 0.75 s, so blood still nearly equilibrates and saturation is close to normal. Step 3: with exercise, transit falls toward 0.25 s, blood leaves before equilibrating, and the patient desaturates. Step 4: PaCO2 is normal or low, because CO2 diffuses easily and the patient hyperventilates. In practice, resting hypoxemia in fibrosis also reflects ventilation-perfusion mismatch."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a thickened blood-air barrier lower PaO2 with exercise but leave PaCO2 normal or low?","explain":"Exercise shortens capillary transit time below what slowed O2 diffusion needs, while CO2 diffuses about 20 times faster and the hypoxemia drives extra ventilation that removes CO2.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on transit time and the worked example in this section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-02-01-C06","obj":"PU-2.6","title":"Stratified squamous epithelium of the true vocal folds and oropharynx, and laryngeal squamous cell carcinoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Not every surface of the upper airway carries respiratory epithelium. Where friction or abrasion is constant, the lining is nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium: many cell layers, with the surface cells flattened and still nucleated. This covers the oropharynx and laryngopharynx, which carry swallowed food, the lingual surface and upper laryngeal surface of the epiglottis, and the true vocal folds, whose surfaces strike each other hundreds of times per second during speech. Stratified squamous epithelium withstands this wear but has no cilia, so clearance across it relies on swallowing and coughing."},{"type":"p","text":"The true vocal fold has, beneath its epithelium, a loose superficial lamina propria (Reinke space) that lets the mucosa ripple over the deeper vocal ligament and the vocalis muscle. The false vocal folds (vestibular folds) above them, the lower laryngeal surface of the epiglottis, and the larynx below the folds have respiratory epithelium. The nasopharynx is mainly respiratory epithelium, with some stratified squamous areas."},{"type":"table","title":"Linings of the upper airway","cols":["Site","Epithelium","Why"],"rows":[["Nasal cavity and nasopharynx","Mainly pseudostratified ciliated columnar","Warm, humidify, and clean air"],["Oropharynx and laryngopharynx","Nonkeratinized stratified squamous","Abrasion from swallowed food"],["Epiglottis, lingual and upper laryngeal surfaces","Nonkeratinized stratified squamous","Contact with food bolus"],["False vocal folds","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar","Conducting airway"],["True vocal folds","Nonkeratinized stratified squamous","Repeated contact and vibration"],["Larynx below the folds, trachea","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar","Mucociliary clearance"]],"caption":"Squamous linings mark sites of mechanical stress; respiratory epithelium marks conducting surfaces that need clearance."},{"type":"p","text":"About 95% of malignant laryngeal tumors are squamous cell carcinomas. They arise from the native squamous epithelium of the true folds, and from squamous metaplasia elsewhere in the larynx, through a sequence driven mainly by tobacco smoke, with alcohol acting synergistically. Histologically they show keratin pearls and intercellular bridges, the features of squamous differentiation."},{"type":"flow","title":"Progression to laryngeal squamous cell carcinoma","steps":["Chronic tobacco and alcohol exposure","Squamous hyperplasia and keratosis (white plaque, leukoplakia)","Dysplasia of increasing grade","Carcinoma in situ","Invasive squamous cell carcinoma"],"caption":"Each step reflects accumulating genetic damage in squamous epithelium; the early steps are potentially reversible."},{"type":"p","text":"Location shapes the presentation. Glottic tumors on the true folds are the most common and cause hoarseness early, because even a small lesion disrupts vibration; the true folds have few lymphatics, so nodal spread is late. Supraglottic tumors have a rich lymphatic supply and often present with nodal metastases. The oropharynx has the same squamous lining, but there, especially in the tonsils and base of tongue, high-risk human papillomavirus (mainly HPV-16) causes a large share of squamous carcinomas. In the larynx HPV-6 and HPV-11 instead cause benign squamous papillomas, not the usual carcinoma."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a small carcinoma of the true vocal fold usually present earlier and with fewer nodal metastases than a supraglottic tumor of the same size?","explain":"Any mass on the true fold disturbs vibration and causes hoarseness at once, and the true folds have sparse lymphatic drainage, while the supraglottis is clinically silent longer and drains through a rich lymphatic network.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on location and presentation."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Histology links the respiratory chapters. Embryology explains the pattern: the epithelium of the larynx, trachea, and lungs comes from endoderm of the foregut, while cartilage, muscle, and connective tissue come from surrounding mesoderm, and type II cells mature late in fetal life, so preterm infants lack surfactant. Physiology reads the same structures quantitatively: surfactant sets compliance, radial traction sets small airway resistance and the volume dependence of airflow, and barrier thickness and area set DLCO. Genetics and immunology add mechanisms of failure, such as dynein and CFTR mutations and the collectin function of surfactant proteins A and D."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology follows from cell biology. Each cell type has a characteristic injury: ciliated columnar cells undergo squamous metaplasia, goblet cells and glands enlarge in chronic bronchitis, type I cells die in diffuse alveolar damage while type II cells repair, alveolar walls are lost in emphysema, and septa thicken in fibrosis and edema. The limits are worth stating: transitions along the airway are gradual, cell proportions and dimensions vary between sources and species, and impaired gas exchange in most diseases reflects several mechanisms at once rather than barrier thickness alone."}],"summary":["The airway lining flattens distally: pseudostratified ciliated columnar with goblet cells in trachea and bronchi, simple cuboidal with club cells in terminal bronchioles, and type I and type II pneumocytes in alveoli.","Bronchi have cartilage plates, submucosal glands, and goblet cells; bronchioles have none of these and prominent smooth muscle.","Bronchioles stay open through the balance of muscle tone and radial traction from alveolar walls; loss of that traction in emphysema causes expiratory collapse and air trapping.","Type I pneumocytes cover about 95% of the alveolar surface for gas exchange but cannot divide; type II cells secrete surfactant and regenerate type I cells.","Club cells secrete protective proteins, metabolize inhaled chemicals with cytochrome P450, and act as bronchiolar progenitors; alveolar macrophages clear particles and become heart failure cells in chronic congestion.","Cilia have a 9 plus 2 axoneme; dynein ATPase slides doublets and nexin links convert sliding to bending, moving mucus toward the pharynx.","Smoking increases mucus while squamous metaplasia removes cilia, so mucus pools and infections recur; dynein defects cause primary ciliary dyskinesia with situs inversus in about half.","The blood-air barrier is type I cell, fused basement membranes, and endothelium; fibrosis or edema thickens it, lowers DLCO, and causes desaturation first with exercise.","True vocal folds and oropharynx have nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium, and about 95% of laryngeal cancers are squamous cell carcinomas linked to tobacco and alcohol."],"sources":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"The Respiratory System"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Principles of Gas Exchange; Diffusion of Oxygen and Carbon Dioxide Through the Respiratory Membrane"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Organization of the Respiratory System"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lung"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Head and Neck, tumors of the larynx"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Pulmonary Disease"}]},"PU-3":{"unit":"PU-3","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-03","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-03-01","title":"Upper Respiratory Tract: Nose, Sinuses, Pharynx, and Larynx","subject":"Respiratory System (RESP)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the anatomy of the upper airway: the nasal cavity and where each paranasal sinus drains, the blood supply that explains nosebleeds, the three levels of the pharynx with their lymphoid tissue and eustachian tube openings, and the cartilages, muscles, and nerves of the larynx. Board questions use this anatomy to explain sinusitis, epistaxis, otitis media from enlarged adenoids, hoarseness, stridor, and aspiration. If you know where each opening sits, which vessel supplies each region, and which nerve senses or moves each part of the larynx, you can reason from a symptom back to the structure involved.","prerequisites":["Bones of the skull and face: maxilla, ethmoid, sphenoid, frontal, palatine","Branches of the external and internal carotid arteries","Course of the vagus nerve and the divisions of the trigeminal nerve","Pharyngeal arch derivatives and aortic arch development","Respiratory epithelium and mucociliary clearance"],"connections":[{"to":"Lower airway and lung anatomy (PU-2)","how":"The larynx protects the trachea and bronchi, and failure of laryngeal sensation sends aspirated material into the dependent bronchopulmonary segments."},{"to":"Cardiovascular embryology and anatomy","how":"The left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks under the aortic arch, so an aortic aneurysm or an enlarged left atrium can cause hoarseness."},{"to":"Endocrine anatomy (thyroid)","how":"The external laryngeal nerve runs with the superior thyroid artery and the recurrent nerve near the inferior thyroid artery, which places both at risk in thyroid disease and surgery."},{"to":"Immunology (mucosal lymphoid tissue)","how":"Waldeyer ring is mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue that samples inhaled and swallowed antigens and enlarges with repeated childhood exposure."},{"to":"Head and neck pathology","how":"Sinusitis, orbital spread of infection, nasopharyngeal carcinoma, and angiofibroma are all explained by the anatomical relations in this chapter."}],"terms":[{"term":"Concha","def":"A curved shelf of bone covered by mucosa that projects from the lateral nasal wall and increases the surface for warming, humidifying, and filtering air."},{"term":"Meatus","def":"The air passage lying beneath and lateral to each concha, into which sinuses and the nasolacrimal duct open."},{"term":"Semilunar hiatus","def":"A curved groove in the middle meatus that receives the openings of the frontal, maxillary, and anterior ethmoid sinuses."},{"term":"Ostium","def":"The opening through which a paranasal sinus drains into the nasal cavity."},{"term":"Mucociliary clearance","def":"Removal of trapped particles and microbes by a mucus layer moved along by coordinated beating of cilia toward the sinus ostia and the pharynx."},{"term":"Kiesselbach area","def":"The anteroinferior part of the nasal septum where several arteries from both carotid systems anastomose; the source of most nosebleeds."},{"term":"Waldeyer ring","def":"The ring of lymphoid tissue (pharyngeal, tubal, palatine, and lingual tonsils) surrounding the entrances to the airway and digestive tract."},{"term":"Pharyngotympanic tube","def":"The auditory (eustachian) tube linking the middle ear to the nasopharynx, which equalizes middle ear pressure and drains middle ear secretions."},{"term":"Rima glottidis","def":"The opening between the vocal folds and the arytenoid cartilages, the narrowest part of the adult laryngeal airway."},{"term":"Abduction (vocal folds)","def":"Movement of the vocal folds away from the midline, which opens the glottis for breathing."},{"term":"Recurrent laryngeal nerve","def":"The vagal branch that loops back up to the larynx to supply all intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid and the mucosa below the vocal folds."},{"term":"Aspiration","def":"Entry of food, liquid, or secretions into the airway below the vocal folds."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-03-01-C01","obj":"PU-3.1","title":"Nasal conchae, meatuses, and sinus drainage","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The nasal cavity is divided by the septum into two chambers that open in front through the nares and behind through the choanae into the nasopharynx. Each lateral wall carries three conchae (turbinates). The superior and middle conchae are parts of the ethmoid bone, and the inferior concha is a separate bone. The space beneath and lateral to each concha is its meatus, and the narrow space above the superior concha is the sphenoethmoidal recess. The olfactory region covers the roof, the upper septum, and the superior concha; the rest is lined by respiratory epithelium, pseudostratified ciliated columnar cells with goblet cells over a richly vascular lamina propria."},{"type":"p","text":"The conchae turn the nasal airstream into thin, turbulent sheets that pass close to warm, moist mucosa. Inspired air is warmed and humidified, and particles are thrown against the mucus layer. Cilia sweep this mucus back toward the nasopharynx to be swallowed, and inside each sinus they beat toward its natural ostium."},{"type":"h","text":"Where each sinus opens"},{"type":"p","text":"The paranasal sinuses are air spaces in the frontal, maxillary, ethmoid, and sphenoid bones, lined by the same respiratory mucosa. Most sinus drainage clusters in the middle meatus. There, the ethmoidal bulla (a bulge formed by middle ethmoid cells) sits above a curved groove, the semilunar hiatus, which leads forward into a channel called the ethmoidal infundibulum. The frontal sinus drains through the frontonasal duct into the infundibulum or the front of the hiatus, the anterior ethmoid cells open into the infundibulum, and the maxillary sinus opens into the back of the hiatus. This shared drainage region is called the ostiomeatal complex. Swelling here can block the frontal, maxillary, and anterior ethmoid sinuses at once, which is why sinusitis often involves several sinuses on one side."},{"type":"table","title":"Drainage of the paranasal sinuses and nasolacrimal duct","cols":["Structure","Drains into","Clinically important relation"],"rows":[["Frontal sinus","Middle meatus (frontonasal duct to the infundibulum or semilunar hiatus)","Anterior cranial fossa behind its thin posterior wall"],["Maxillary sinus","Middle meatus (posterior semilunar hiatus)","Orbit above, upper molar roots below (C02)"],["Anterior ethmoid cells","Middle meatus (ethmoidal infundibulum)","Orbit across the lamina papyracea"],["Middle ethmoid cells","Middle meatus (on the ethmoidal bulla)","Orbit across the lamina papyracea"],["Posterior ethmoid cells","Superior meatus","Optic canal nearby"],["Sphenoid sinus","Sphenoethmoidal recess","Pituitary above, cavernous sinus and internal carotid artery laterally, optic nerve superolaterally"],["Nasolacrimal duct","Inferior meatus (anterior part)","Carries tears from the medial eye"]],"caption":"Only the posterior ethmoid cells, the sphenoid sinus, and the nasolacrimal duct use openings outside the middle meatus."},{"type":"p","text":"The nasolacrimal duct explains why crying produces a runny nose: tears drain from the medial corner of the eye into the inferior meatus. The relations in the table predict complications. The ethmoid cells are separated from the orbit by the paper-thin lamina papyracea and by valveless veins, so ethmoid sinusitis is the most common source of orbital cellulitis, especially in children. Sphenoid disease can reach the cavernous sinus and optic nerve, and the frontal sinus can spread infection to the frontal bone and anterior cranial fossa. Sinus pain follows trigeminal innervation: frontal and ethmoid sinuses through branches of the ophthalmic division (V1), the maxillary sinus through the maxillary division (V2)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient has swelling of the mucosa confined to the middle meatus. Which sinuses will fail to drain, and which will keep draining?","explain":"The frontal, maxillary, and anterior and middle ethmoid sinuses share the middle meatus and will be blocked; the posterior ethmoid cells (superior meatus) and the sphenoid sinus (sphenoethmoidal recess) open elsewhere.","check":"Compare with the drainage table and the paragraph on the ostiomeatal complex."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-03-01-C02","obj":"PU-3.2","title":"The maxillary sinus and odontogenic sinusitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The maxillary sinus is the largest paranasal sinus and occupies the body of the maxilla. It is shaped like a pyramid: its base is the lateral wall of the nasal cavity, its roof is the floor of the orbit (with the infraorbital nerve running in a canal within it), and its floor is the alveolar process of the maxilla. It is also the sinus most often infected, and its anatomy explains why."},{"type":"h","text":"Why drainage fails"},{"type":"p","text":"The maxillary ostium lies high on the medial wall, near the roof of the sinus. In an upright person, secretions pooled on the floor cannot run out by gravity; cilia must carry them up the walls and out through the ostium. Drainage therefore depends entirely on working cilia, normal mucus, and an open ostium. The ostium is also narrow and opens into the crowded middle meatus, so the mucosal swelling of a viral cold or allergic rhinitis easily closes it. Trapped secretions lose oxygen, ciliary beating slows, and bacteria that normally colonize the nasopharynx multiply in the retained fluid. In community-acquired disease these are mainly Streptococcus pneumoniae, nontypeable Haemophilus influenzae, and Moraxella catarrhalis, although most acute sinusitis is viral and resolves without bacterial infection."},{"type":"flow","title":"Acute maxillary sinusitis","steps":["Viral rhinitis or allergy swells middle meatus mucosa","Swelling closes the high maxillary ostium","Secretions pool on the sinus floor","Low oxygen slows ciliary clearance","Bacteria multiply in retained fluid","Facial pressure, purulent discharge, upper tooth pain"],"caption":"Obstruction of the ostium, not the presence of bacteria, starts the process."},{"type":"p","text":"Anything that impairs clearance raises the risk. In cystic fibrosis, defective CFTR chloride transport dehydrates mucus so cilia cannot move it. In primary ciliary dyskinesia, defective dynein arms leave cilia immotile or poorly coordinated, giving chronic sinusitis, bronchiectasis, and infertility, with situs inversus in about half of patients (Kartagener syndrome)."},{"type":"h","text":"The teeth and the sinus floor"},{"type":"p","text":"The roots of the maxillary molars, and often the second premolar, lie directly beneath the sinus floor. The bone between them may be very thin, and in some people the root tips project into the sinus covered only by mucosa. A periapical abscess can therefore spread upward into the sinus, and an extraction, a displaced root fragment, or a dental implant can open a communication between the mouth and the sinus. The result is odontogenic sinusitis, which is typically unilateral, often foul smelling, and caused by mixed oral flora including anaerobes rather than the usual respiratory pathogens."},{"type":"p","text":"Shared nerves link the two sites in both directions. The superior alveolar nerves, branches of V2, supply both the upper teeth and the sinus lining, so maxillary sinusitis is commonly felt as aching in the upper teeth, and a dental infection can be felt as sinus pressure. A unilateral sinusitis that does not behave like an ordinary viral or bacterial episode should prompt a look at the teeth on that side."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-03-01-C03","obj":"PU-3.3","title":"Kiesselbach area and the sources of epistaxis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The nasal septum receives blood from both the internal and external carotid systems. In the anteroinferior part of the septum, just inside the nostril, several of these arteries anastomose in a dense plexus called Kiesselbach area (also called Little area)."},{"type":"table","title":"Arteries that anastomose in Kiesselbach area","cols":["Artery","Parent vessel","Carotid system"],"rows":[["Sphenopalatine (septal branches)","Maxillary artery","External"],["Anterior ethmoidal","Ophthalmic artery","Internal"],["Greater palatine (through the incisive canal)","Descending palatine branch of the maxillary artery","External"],["Septal branch of the superior labial","Facial artery","External"]],"caption":"Because the anterior ethmoidal artery comes from the internal carotid, the area is fed from both carotid systems."},{"type":"p","text":"Kiesselbach area bleeds easily for three reasons. The vessels converge in thin mucosa tightly applied to septal cartilage, with little supporting tissue. The area lies directly in the inspired airstream, so dry, cold air cracks the surface and forms crusts. It is also within reach of a finger, and nose picking is the most common cause of anterior bleeding in children. Rhinitis, a deviated septum, and topical irritants add to these factors, while thrombocytopenia, von Willebrand disease, anticoagulants, and hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia (fragile dilated vessels from defects in endoglin or ALK1 signaling) make bleeding more frequent or prolonged."},{"type":"p","text":"About 90 percent of nosebleeds are anterior and arise here. They are usually mild, flow mainly out of the nostril, and can be seen on anterior inspection."},{"type":"h","text":"Posterior epistaxis"},{"type":"p","text":"Posterior bleeding arises from branches of the sphenopalatine artery on the posterior septum and lateral wall, deep in the cavity behind the reach of external pressure. These vessels are larger, so bleeding is heavier and often bilateral, and much of the blood runs into the nasopharynx and is swallowed. Swallowed blood can cause nausea, vomiting of blood, dark stools, and an elevated blood urea nitrogen from digested protein. Posterior bleeds occur more often in older adults, in whom atherosclerotic vessels contract poorly after injury, and are associated with hypertension and anticoagulant use. In an adolescent male with recurrent heavy epistaxis and nasal obstruction, consider juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma, a benign but highly vascular tumor near the sphenopalatine foramen."},{"type":"table","title":"Anterior versus posterior epistaxis","cols":["Feature","Anterior","Posterior"],"rows":[["Source","Kiesselbach area, anterior septum","Sphenopalatine artery branches, posterior cavity"],["Share of cases","About 90 percent","About 10 percent"],["Typical patient","Child or young adult","Older adult, often with vascular disease or on anticoagulants"],["Blood flow","Mainly out of the nostril","Down the pharynx, often both sides"],["Visible on anterior inspection","Usually","Usually not"]],"caption":"Vessel size and depth decide how heavy the bleed is and where blood goes."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-03-01-C04","obj":"PU-3.4","title":"Pharynx, Waldeyer ring, and the pharyngotympanic tube","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The pharynx is a fibromuscular tube, about 15 cm long, shared by air and food. It runs from the base of the skull to the lower border of the cricoid cartilage at about the level of C6, where it becomes the esophagus. The soft palate and the epiglottis divide it into three levels."},{"type":"table","title":"The three levels of the pharynx","cols":["Level","Boundaries","Lining","Key contents"],"rows":[["Nasopharynx","Base of skull to soft palate","Respiratory (ciliated) epithelium","Adenoids, pharyngotympanic tube openings, tubal tonsils"],["Oropharynx","Soft palate to upper border of epiglottis","Nonkeratinized stratified squamous","Palatine tonsils; lingual tonsil"],["Laryngopharynx","Upper border of the epiglottis to the lower border of the cricoid (about C6)","Nonkeratinized stratified squamous","Laryngeal inlet; piriform recesses on each side"]],"caption":"Squamous epithelium lines the levels that food passes through; the nasopharynx carries only air."},{"type":"p","text":"The piriform recesses beside the laryngeal inlet channel swallowed material around the larynx, which is why fish bones lodge there. The internal laryngeal nerve runs beneath their mucosa (C06)."},{"type":"h","text":"Waldeyer ring"},{"type":"p","text":"Lymphoid tissue surrounds the entrances to the airway and gut: the pharyngeal tonsil on the roof and posterior wall of the nasopharynx, the tubal tonsils around the tube openings, the palatine tonsils, and the lingual tonsil. Like other mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue, the tonsils have no afferent lymphatics; they sample antigens directly from the surface. The palatine tonsils have deep crypts lined by stratified squamous epithelium, while the pharyngeal tonsil is covered by respiratory epithelium thrown into folds. The tissue enlarges through early childhood with repeated antigen exposure and regresses in adolescence."},{"type":"h","text":"The pharyngotympanic tube"},{"type":"p","text":"The pharyngotympanic (eustachian) tube opens on the lateral wall of the nasopharynx, behind the inferior concha, beneath a cartilaginous ridge (torus tubarius). Its lateral third is bony and its medial two thirds cartilaginous. It is closed at rest and opened briefly during swallowing and yawning, mainly by tensor veli palatini, which ventilates the middle ear and lets it drain. In young children the tube is shorter, wider, and more horizontal, so nasopharyngeal secretions and pathogens reach the middle ear easily. In cleft palate the palatal muscles open the tube poorly, so effusions are common."},{"type":"p","text":"Enlarged adenoids cause predictable problems. Blocking the choanae forces mouth breathing, gives a hyponasal voice, and causes snoring and sometimes obstructive sleep apnea. Blocking or inflaming the tube openings stops middle ear ventilation, and the adenoids can act as a bacterial reservoir for acute otitis media. Because adenoids normally regress, a new unilateral middle ear effusion in an adult suggests a mass in the nasopharynx, such as nasopharyngeal carcinoma, an Epstein-Barr virus associated tumor that often arises in the pharyngeal recess beside the tube opening."},{"type":"flow","title":"Enlarged adenoids to otitis media with effusion","steps":["Adenoids enlarge and block the tube opening","Middle ear can no longer be ventilated","Mucosa absorbs trapped middle ear gas","Middle ear pressure becomes negative","Fluid is drawn into the middle ear","Damped eardrum movement: conductive hearing loss"],"caption":"Otitis media with effusion can follow failed ventilation alone, without infection."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: a child who snores and does not listen","text":"A 5-year-old snores loudly, sleeps with the mouth open, and has a hyponasal voice. Teachers report inattention, and the tympanic membranes are dull and retracted with reduced mobility. Hyponasal speech means something blocks the choanae, and in a child that is usually the pharyngeal tonsil. The same tissue sits beside the pharyngotympanic tube openings. With the tubes blocked, middle ear gas is absorbed, the eardrum is pulled inward (retracted), and fluid fills the middle ear. Fluid damps sound transmission, so a conductive hearing loss looks like inattention."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-03-01-C05","obj":"PU-3.5","title":"Laryngeal cartilages, intrinsic muscles, and recurrent laryngeal nerve injury","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The larynx guards the lower airway and produces voice. Its framework is three unpaired and three paired cartilages. The thyroid cartilage forms the laryngeal prominence; its inferior horns articulate with the cricoid at the cricothyroid joints. The cricoid, at about C6, is shaped like a signet ring and is the only complete cartilaginous ring of the airway. The epiglottis is elastic cartilage that folds over the inlet during swallowing. The paired arytenoids sit on the upper border of the cricoid lamina; each has a vocal process in front, where the vocal ligament attaches, and a muscular process laterally, where muscles pull. The corniculate and cuneiform cartilages are small nodules in the aryepiglottic folds."},{"type":"p","text":"The vocal folds run from the vocal processes to the back of the thyroid angle, covered by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium that withstands constant contact; above them lie the vestibular (false) folds. Every movement of the folds comes from the arytenoids rotating and sliding on the cricoid, or from the thyroid tilting on the cricoid."},{"type":"table","title":"Intrinsic muscles of the larynx","cols":["Muscle","Action on the arytenoids or thyroid","Effect on the vocal folds","Nerve"],"rows":[["Posterior cricoarytenoid","Pulls muscular process backward, rotating vocal processes outward","Abducts (opens the glottis); the only abductor","Recurrent laryngeal"],["Lateral cricoarytenoid","Pulls muscular process forward, rotating vocal processes inward","Adducts","Recurrent laryngeal"],["Transverse and oblique arytenoids","Draw the arytenoids together","Adduct, closing the posterior glottis","Recurrent laryngeal"],["Thyroarytenoid (with vocalis)","Draws arytenoids toward the thyroid","Shortens and relaxes the folds; vocalis fine-tunes tension","Recurrent laryngeal"],["Cricothyroid","Tilts thyroid cartilage forward on the cricoid","Lengthens and tenses the folds, raising pitch","External laryngeal"]],"caption":"One muscle opens the glottis, one tenses the folds, and the rest close or relax them."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the posterior cricoarytenoid is the only abductor, losing it leaves the fold unable to move outward. The recurrent laryngeal nerve supplies it and all the adductors, so recurrent nerve injury leaves the fold motionless near the midline (the paramedian position). With one side paralyzed, the folds cannot meet firmly, air escapes during phonation, and the voice becomes hoarse and breathy, with a weak cough. With both sides paralyzed, both folds sit near the midline: the voice may be fairly preserved, but the airway is narrowed, causing inspiratory stridor and possible airway obstruction."},{"type":"h","text":"Why the left nerve is vulnerable"},{"type":"p","text":"The recurrent nerves hook under arteries derived from the embryonic aortic arches. On the right, the distal sixth arch artery regresses, so the nerve moves up to hook under the right subclavian artery (fourth arch) at the root of the neck. On the left, the sixth arch persists as the ductus arteriosus, so the nerve hooks under the aortic arch beside the ligamentum arteriosum and has a long intrathoracic course. Both nerves then ascend in the tracheoesophageal groove, pass close to the inferior thyroid artery, and enter the larynx behind the cricothyroid joint."},{"type":"flow","title":"Course of the left recurrent laryngeal nerve","steps":["Left vagus crosses the aortic arch","Recurrent branch hooks under the arch beside the ligamentum arteriosum","Ascends in the left tracheoesophageal groove","Passes near the inferior thyroid artery behind the thyroid","Enters the larynx behind the cricothyroid joint","Supplies all intrinsic muscles except cricothyroid"],"caption":"Each point on this course can be compressed or injured."},{"type":"p","text":"Thoracic disease therefore affects the left nerve: aortic arch aneurysm, a left hilar or upper lobe lung tumor, mediastinal lymph nodes, and a markedly enlarged left atrium in mitral stenosis. Thyroid surgery or tumors can injure either nerve."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with long-standing mitral stenosis develops a hoarse voice. Which nerve is affected, and why the left rather than the right?","explain":"The left recurrent laryngeal nerve, which hooks under the aortic arch and runs through the mediastinum near the left atrium; the right nerve turns under the subclavian artery in the neck.","check":"Compare with the course of the left recurrent laryngeal nerve."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-03-01-C06","obj":"PU-3.6","title":"Sensory and motor innervation of the larynx and aspiration","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"All laryngeal nerves come from the vagus. The superior laryngeal nerve arises high in the neck and divides into internal and external branches; the recurrent laryngeal nerve arises lower. The vocal folds are the dividing line for sensation."},{"type":"table","title":"Laryngeal nerves and the result of injury","cols":["Nerve","Motor","Sensory","Result of injury"],"rows":[["Internal laryngeal (superior laryngeal)","None","Mucosa above the vocal folds, including the laryngeal inlet and piriform recesses","Loss of supraglottic sensation and protective reflexes; silent aspiration"],["External laryngeal (superior laryngeal)","Cricothyroid (and part of the inferior constrictor)","None","Cannot raise pitch; monotonous voice that tires easily"],["Recurrent laryngeal","All other intrinsic muscles","Mucosa below the vocal folds","Unilateral: hoarseness. Bilateral: stridor"]],"caption":"Sensation divides at the vocal folds; motor supply divides at the cricothyroid."},{"type":"p","text":"The internal laryngeal nerve pierces the thyrohyoid membrane with the superior laryngeal artery and runs under the mucosa of the piriform recess. The external laryngeal nerve descends on the inferior pharyngeal constrictor beside the superior thyroid artery, where it can be injured when that artery is tied during thyroid surgery. Its loss leaves the cricothyroid unable to tense the fold, so the voice is weak, monotonous, and cannot reach high notes, although the airway and the fold position remain normal."},{"type":"h","text":"Why supraglottic sensation protects the airway"},{"type":"p","text":"The mucosa above the folds is the airway's alarm system. When food or secretions touch it, internal laryngeal afferents carry the signal to the nucleus of the solitary tract, and motor fibers from the nucleus ambiguus travel in the recurrent laryngeal nerve to close the glottis (the laryngeal adductor reflex) and, together with the respiratory muscles, produce a cough. If supraglottic sensation is lost, material can spill across the inlet and through the glottis without triggering closure or coughing. This silent aspiration leads to aspiration pneumonia, typically in the dependent segments of the right lung, since the right main bronchus is wider, shorter, and more vertical."},{"type":"flow","title":"Protective reflex of the supraglottic larynx","steps":["Bolus or secretions touch supraglottic mucosa","Internal laryngeal afferents fire","Nucleus of the solitary tract in the medulla","Nucleus ambiguus activates recurrent laryngeal motor fibers","Glottis closes and cough expels the material"],"caption":"Cutting the afferent limb removes the reflex even when the motor nerves are intact."},{"type":"p","text":"The level of a lesion predicts the findings. A recurrent nerve lesion causes hoarseness with intact supraglottic sensation. A lesion of the vagus above the origin of the superior laryngeal nerve, as at the jugular foramen or in a lateral medullary infarct involving the nucleus ambiguus and the nucleus of the solitary tract, combines fold paralysis, loss of supraglottic sensation, and loss of cricothyroid tension, together with dysphagia and deviation of the uvula away from the lesion. Because such patients cannot close the glottis or sense the inlet, they are at greatest risk of aspiration."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: localizing a vagal lesion","text":"After a thyroidectomy, patient A has a breathy, hoarse voice but swallows without coughing or choking. Patient B has a normal speaking voice but cannot sing high notes. Patient C, after a brainstem stroke, is hoarse, coughs weakly, and develops right lower lobe pneumonia. Patient A has a recurrent laryngeal injury: an adductor-weak fold that cannot close firmly, but intact sensation above it. Patient B has lost the external laryngeal nerve and so cricothyroid tension alone. Patient C has a medullary lesion of the nucleus ambiguus and the nucleus of the solitary tract, above every laryngeal branch, so fold movement and protective reflexes fail together, and aspirated material falls into the right lower lobe."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology and anatomy explain the clinical patterns. The paranasal sinuses grow as outpouchings of the nasal cavity, so their ostia drain into the meatuses and their mucosa shares the defenses and diseases of the nose. The laryngeal cartilages and muscles derive from the fourth and sixth pharyngeal arches, which is why their nerves are vagal, and the remodeling of the aortic arches places the left recurrent nerve in the chest. Respiratory epithelium, mucociliary transport, and mucosal lymphoid tissue tie the upper airway to cell biology, genetics (CFTR, dynein), and immunology."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology follows obstruction, vascular anatomy, and nerve course. Obstruction of a small opening, whether a sinus ostium or a pharyngotympanic tube, turns a ventilated space into a stagnant one. Bleeding depends on vessel size and position more than on cause. Hoarseness, stridor, loss of pitch, and silent aspiration each localize a lesion along the vagus. The limits are that drainage patterns and sinus sizes vary between people, sinus and tube disease often overlap with allergy and infection, and imaging or endoscopy, not anatomy alone, confirms the site."}],"summary":["The frontal, maxillary, and anterior ethmoid sinuses drain to the middle meatus; posterior ethmoid cells to the superior meatus; the sphenoid to the sphenoethmoidal recess; the nasolacrimal duct to the inferior meatus.","Swelling of the ostiomeatal complex blocks several sinuses at once, and thin bony walls let ethmoid infection reach the orbit and sphenoid infection reach the cavernous sinus.","The maxillary sinus is most often infected because its ostium is high on the medial wall, so drainage depends entirely on cilia and an open ostium.","Maxillary molar roots lie under the sinus floor, so dental infection or extraction causes unilateral odontogenic sinusitis with mixed oral and anaerobic flora, and V2 refers pain between teeth and sinus.","About 90 percent of nosebleeds arise in Kiesselbach area, fed by sphenopalatine, anterior ethmoidal, greater palatine, and superior labial branches from both carotid systems.","Posterior epistaxis comes from sphenopalatine branches, is heavier, drains into the pharynx, and cannot be reached by external pressure.","Enlarged adenoids block the choanae (mouth breathing, snoring) and the pharyngotympanic tubes (negative middle ear pressure, effusion, conductive hearing loss).","The posterior cricoarytenoid is the only abductor; recurrent nerve injury leaves the fold paramedian, causing hoarseness if unilateral and stridor if bilateral.","The left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks under the aortic arch, so aneurysm, lung tumor, nodes, or an enlarged left atrium cause left-sided hoarseness.","Internal laryngeal loss removes supraglottic sensation and causes silent aspiration; external laryngeal loss removes cricothyroid tension and high pitch."],"sources":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head, nose and paranasal sinuses"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Neck, pharynx and larynx"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Head and neck, pharyngeal arches and their nerves; aortic arch development"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"The respiratory system, nasal cavity and larynx"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Head and Neck, nose, paranasal sinuses, and nasopharynx"}]},"PU-4":{"unit":"PU-4","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-04","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-04-01","title":"Thorax, Pleura, Lungs, and Mediastinum","subject":"Respiratory System (RESP)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter places the lungs, pleura, and mediastinal structures in the chest so that clinical findings can be traced to anatomy. It covers the surface projections of the lungs and pleura, the lobes and segments of each lung, the arrangement of structures at each hilum, the compartments of the mediastinum and the masses that arise in them, the innervation of the pleura, the openings and nerve supply of the diaphragm, and the course of the thoracic duct. Board questions use this anatomy to choose a needle site, predict where aspirated material settles, localize a mediastinal mass, explain shoulder pain, and recognize chylothorax.","prerequisites":["Ribs, intercostal muscles, and the layers of the thoracic wall","Components of a typical spinal nerve and the dermatome map","Development of the body cavities and serous membranes (PU-1)","Structure of the tracheobronchial tree from trachea to bronchioles (PU-2)","Lymphatic drainage and chylomicron formation in the small intestine"],"connections":[{"to":"Upper respiratory tract (PU-3)","how":"The left recurrent laryngeal nerve loops under the aortic arch beside the left hilum, so a hilar or aortopulmonary mass can cause hoarseness."},{"to":"Pulmonary blood flow and ventilation (PU-5, PU-6)","how":"The hilar vessels carry the pulmonary and bronchial circulations, and negative pleural pressure links the chest wall to lung expansion."},{"to":"Disorders of the pleural cavity (PU-14-D)","how":"Pleural effusion, pneumothorax, and chylothorax are read through the pleural borders, recesses, and thoracic duct described here."},{"to":"Thymus embryology and immunology (IM-1)","how":"Thymoma in the anterior mediastinum is linked to myasthenia gravis and other autoimmune disorders through faulty T cell selection."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal physiology","how":"The esophageal hiatus contributes to the antireflux barrier, and dietary fat absorbed as chylomicrons is what makes chyle milky."}],"terms":[{"term":"Costodiaphragmatic recess","def":"The lowest gutter of the pleural cavity, between costal and diaphragmatic pleura, which the lung does not fill in quiet breathing."},{"term":"Costal groove","def":"The groove on the inner lower border of each rib that shelters the intercostal vein, artery, and nerve."},{"term":"Bronchopulmonary segment","def":"The pyramidal portion of lung supplied by one segmental (tertiary) bronchus and segmental artery, surgically separable from its neighbors."},{"term":"Lingula","def":"The tongue-shaped inferior part of the left upper lobe, the counterpart of the right middle lobe."},{"term":"Hilum","def":"The depression on the medial surface of the lung where the root structures (bronchus, vessels, nerves, lymphatics) enter and leave."},{"term":"Mediastinum","def":"The central compartment of the thoracic cavity between the two pleural sacs, from the thoracic inlet to the diaphragm."},{"term":"Sternal angle","def":"The joint between manubrium and sternal body at the level of the second costal cartilage, marking the T4 to T5 plane."},{"term":"Parietal pleura","def":"The pleura lining the chest wall, diaphragm, and mediastinum, supplied by somatic nerves and sensitive to pain."},{"term":"Visceral pleura","def":"The pleura covering the lung surface, supplied by autonomic nerves and insensitive to sharp pain."},{"term":"Referred pain","def":"Pain perceived at a site distant from its source because both sites send afferents to the same spinal cord segments."},{"term":"Cisterna chyli","def":"The dilated sac at the start of the thoracic duct, near L1 to L2, that collects lymph from the abdomen, pelvis, and lower limbs."},{"term":"Chylothorax","def":"Accumulation of chyle, lymph rich in chylomicron triglyceride, in the pleural cavity after thoracic duct leak or obstruction."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-04-01-C01","obj":"PU-4.1","title":"Surface projections of the lungs and pleura and the thoracentesis approach","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each lung sits inside a pleural sac that is larger than the lung itself. The parietal pleura lines the chest wall, diaphragm, and mediastinum; the visceral pleura covers the lung; and between them lies the pleural cavity, a potential space holding only a few milliliters of fluid. During quiet breathing the lower edge of the lung stays well above the lower edge of the sac, so both borders must be known to place a needle or interpret an effusion."},{"type":"p","text":"At the apex, the cervical pleura and lung rise about 2 to 3 cm above the medial third of the clavicle into the root of the neck, which is why a stab wound or needle at the base of the neck can cause pneumothorax. Anteriorly the two pleural sacs meet behind the sternum, but the left diverges at the fourth costal cartilage around the heart. Inferiorly, the borders are best remembered at three vertical lines, with the pleura two ribs lower than the lung at each line."},{"type":"table","title":"Lower borders of the lung and pleura","cols":["Line","Lung (quiet breathing)","Parietal pleura"],"rows":[["Midclavicular","Rib 6","Rib 8"],["Midaxillary","Rib 8","Rib 10"],["Paravertebral","Rib 10","Rib 12"]],"caption":"The space between the two borders is the costodiaphragmatic recess, which the lung enters only on deep inspiration."},{"type":"p","text":"The costodiaphragmatic recess is the lowest part of the pleural cavity in an upright person, so free fluid collects there first and blunts the costophrenic angle on a radiograph. Because the posterior pleural border reaches rib 12 and even passes below it beside the vertebral column, a posterior approach to the kidney or a renal biopsy can open the pleural cavity."},{"type":"h","text":"The intercostal space and the needle path"},{"type":"p","text":"The main intercostal vein, artery, and nerve run in the costal groove on the inner lower border of each rib, in that order from above down, between the internal and innermost intercostal muscles. The nerve is lowest and least protected by the groove. Small collateral branches run along the upper border of the rib below. A thoracentesis needle is therefore passed just above the upper border of the lower rib, high enough to miss the collateral branches, rather than below the rib above, where the main bundle lies."},{"type":"flow","title":"Layers crossed by a thoracentesis needle","steps":["Skin and subcutaneous tissue","Serratus anterior or latissimus dorsi, depending on site","External, internal, and innermost intercostal muscles","Endothoracic fascia","Costal parietal pleura (pain-sensitive)","Pleural cavity and costodiaphragmatic recess"],"caption":"The needle crosses the space close to the upper border of the rib below, away from the costal groove of the rib above."},{"type":"p","text":"Moore describes entry through the ninth intercostal space in the midaxillary line during expiration, with the needle angled upward. Expiration raises the lung edge out of the recess, and the upward angle keeps the tip from passing through the diaphragm into the liver or spleen, which lie just beneath the floor of the recess."},{"type":"example","title":"Choosing a safe level","text":"An upright patient has a moderate right effusion. At the midaxillary line the lung edge lies at rib 8 and the pleura at rib 10, so the recess spans the eighth and ninth intercostal spaces. A needle in the ninth space during expiration enters fluid below the lung. Entering the tenth space would pass below the pleural reflection, through the diaphragm and into the liver. Within the ninth space, the needle hugs the top of rib 10: hugging the bottom of rib 9 would strike the intercostal vein, artery, and nerve in its costal groove."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-04-01-C02","obj":"PU-4.2","title":"Lobes and fissures of the two lungs and the anatomy of aspiration","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The two lungs differ because of their neighbors. The heart bulges to the left, so the left lung is narrower and has a cardiac notch on its anterior border; the right dome of the diaphragm sits higher over the liver, so the right lung is shorter but wider and heavier. Each lobe is divided into bronchopulmonary segments, each supplied by one segmental bronchus and accompanying artery, which lets disease, and resection, be localized to a segment."},{"type":"table","title":"Right versus left lung","cols":["Feature","Right lung","Left lung"],"rows":[["Lobes","Upper, middle, lower","Upper (with lingula), lower"],["Fissures","Oblique and horizontal","Oblique only"],["Segments","10","8 to 10 (some segments fused)"],["Anterior border","Straight","Cardiac notch, lingula below it"],["Main bronchus","Shorter (about 2.5 cm), wider, more vertical","Longer (about 5 cm), narrower, more horizontal"]],"caption":"The lingula is the left counterpart of the right middle lobe."},{"type":"p","text":"The oblique fissure runs from about the level of the T2 spinous process behind, around the side, to the sixth costal cartilage in front, roughly following rib 6. The horizontal fissure runs from the sternum along the fourth costal cartilage to meet the oblique fissure near the midaxillary line. This geometry explains the physical examination: the lower lobes lie mostly posterior and are heard from the back, the right middle lobe and lingula lie anterior and are heard from the front, and the upper lobes occupy the apex and upper anterior chest. On a frontal radiograph, consolidation of the right middle lobe erases the right heart border, the lingula erases the left heart border, and the lower lobes erase the hemidiaphragms, because air no longer separates tissues of similar density (the silhouette sign)."},{"type":"h","text":"Where aspirated material goes"},{"type":"p","text":"The right main bronchus is wider and leaves the trachea at a steeper angle, closer to the line of the trachea, so inhaled foreign bodies and aspirated secretions usually enter the right lung. Gravity then decides the segment. In an upright patient, material falls into the most dependent bronchi, the basal segments of the right lower lobe. In a supine patient the most dependent bronchi point backward: the superior segment of the right lower lobe and the posterior segment of the right upper lobe. Aspiration typically follows depressed consciousness or impaired swallowing, as with alcohol intoxication, seizures, anesthesia, or stroke."},{"type":"flow","title":"Aspiration in a supine patient","steps":["Cough and swallowing reflexes fail","Oral contents enter the larynx and trachea","Material follows the wider, steeper right main bronchus","Gravity carries it into backward-pointing bronchi","It lodges in the RLL superior or RUL posterior segment","Mouth flora cause pneumonia that can form an abscess"],"caption":"Position at the time of aspiration predicts the segment involved."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A man found unconscious on his back after heavy drinking develops a lung abscess. Which segment is most likely involved, and would the answer change if he had aspirated while sitting?","explain":"Supine, the dependent bronchi of the right lung point posteriorly, so the superior segment of the right lower lobe (or posterior segment of the right upper lobe) is most likely. Sitting, material falls straight down into the basal segments of the right lower lobe.","check":"The aspiration paragraph and flow above."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-04-01-C03","obj":"PU-4.3","title":"Structures at the hilum and their arrangement","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The root of the lung is the bundle of structures that joins the lung to the heart and trachea; the hilum is where that bundle enters the medial surface. Each root contains a main bronchus, one pulmonary artery, two pulmonary veins, bronchial arteries and veins, the pulmonary nerve plexus, and bronchopulmonary (hilar) lymph nodes. A sleeve of pleura surrounds the root, where visceral and parietal pleura are continuous, and hangs below it as the pulmonary ligament."},{"type":"p","text":"The pattern at each hilum follows how each pulmonary artery reaches its lung. The pulmonary trunk divides below the aortic arch. The left pulmonary artery is short and arches over the left main bronchus, so it lies superior to the bronchus. The right pulmonary artery is longer, passes under the arch and behind the ascending aorta and superior vena cava, and arrives in front of the right bronchial tree. Because the right upper lobe bronchus branches off before the artery reaches it, that bronchus lies above the artery and is called the eparterial bronchus; the rest of the right bronchial tree is hyparterial, and the right pulmonary artery lies anterior to the right main bronchus."},{"type":"table","title":"Arrangement of the hilar structures","cols":["Structure","Right hilum","Left hilum"],"rows":[["Pulmonary artery","Anterior to the main bronchus","Superior to the main bronchus (highest structure)"],["Main bronchus","Posterior; upper lobe (eparterial) bronchus is the highest structure","Posterior, below the artery"],["Pulmonary veins","Most anterior and most inferior","Most anterior and most inferior"],["Arching vessel above the root","Azygos vein","Aortic arch"]],"caption":"Right artery anterior, left artery superior. On both sides the bronchi are posterior and the veins anterior and inferior."},{"type":"p","text":"On both sides the two pulmonary veins are the most anterior and most inferior structures of the root, because they descend to enter the left atrium below the level of the pulmonary artery bifurcation. Bronchial arteries (usually two on the left from the thoracic aorta and one on the right, often from the third right posterior intercostal artery) run on the back of the bronchi. The phrenic nerve passes in front of each root and the vagus nerve behind it, which is how a hilar tumor can involve either nerve."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the left pulmonary artery climbs over the left bronchus, the left hilum normally sits slightly higher than the right on a frontal radiograph; reversal of this relation suggests volume loss or a mass. Enlarged hilar lymph nodes, as in sarcoidosis (often bilateral and symmetric), tuberculosis, lymphoma, or metastatic carcinoma, widen the hilar shadow. Central lung carcinomas, especially squamous cell and small cell types, arise near the hilum and can narrow a main bronchus to cause collapse of the lung beyond it. On the left, a mass near the hilum and aortic arch can stretch the left recurrent laryngeal nerve as it loops under the arch, producing hoarseness, and involvement of the phrenic nerve in front of the root paralyzes the hemidiaphragm."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-04-01-C04","obj":"PU-4.4","title":"Compartments of the mediastinum and the masses that arise in them","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The mediastinum is the central partition of the thorax between the pleural sacs, extending from the thoracic inlet to the diaphragm and from the sternum to the vertebral bodies. A horizontal plane from the sternal angle to the T4 to T5 intervertebral disc (the transverse thoracic plane) divides it into superior and inferior parts. The same plane marks the tracheal bifurcation, the beginning and end of the aortic arch, and the entry of the azygos vein into the superior vena cava, so it is a reliable landmark. The pericardium then divides the inferior mediastinum into anterior, middle, and posterior compartments."},{"type":"table","title":"Mediastinal compartments and their contents","cols":["Compartment","Boundaries","Main contents"],"rows":[["Superior","Thoracic inlet to the T4 to T5 plane","Thymus, brachiocephalic veins, upper SVC, aortic arch and branches, trachea, esophagus, thoracic duct, vagus, phrenic, and left recurrent laryngeal nerves"],["Anterior","Sternal body to pericardium","Thymic remnant (thymus in children), fat, lymph nodes, sternopericardial ligaments"],["Middle","The pericardial sac","Heart, pericardium, ascending aorta, pulmonary trunk, lower SVC, phrenic nerves, tracheobronchial lymph nodes"],["Posterior","Pericardium and diaphragm to T5 to T12 vertebral bodies","Esophagus, descending aorta, azygos and hemiazygos veins, thoracic duct, vagus nerves, lymph nodes; sympathetic trunks lie just lateral in the paravertebral gutters"]],"caption":"The superior mediastinum is layered from front to back: thymus, veins, arteries, trachea, esophagus, thoracic duct."},{"type":"p","text":"A mediastinal mass usually comes from a tissue that belongs in its compartment, so localization on a lateral radiograph or CT narrows the diagnosis considerably. Age matters as well: in children, neurogenic tumors and lymphoma predominate; in adults, thymoma, lymphoma, and benign cysts are common."},{"type":"table","title":"Typical masses by compartment","cols":["Compartment","Typical masses","Tissue of origin"],"rows":[["Anterior (and anterosuperior)","Thymoma, teratoma and other germ cell tumors, lymphoma, retrosternal thyroid","Thymus, midline germ cell rests, lymph nodes, thyroid extending from the neck"],["Middle","Lymph node enlargement, bronchogenic cyst, pericardial cyst","Nodes along the airways, foregut buds, pericardium"],["Posterior (paravertebral)","Neurogenic tumors (schwannoma, neurofibroma; neuroblastoma or ganglioneuroma in children), esophageal lesions, aortic aneurysm","Nerve roots, sympathetic ganglia, esophagus, aorta"]],"caption":"Anterior masses are often summarized as the four Ts: thymoma, teratoma, thyroid, and (terrible) lymphoma."},{"type":"p","text":"Thymoma is the classic anterior mass of adults. Many patients with thymoma have myasthenia gravis, and thymoma is also associated with pure red cell aplasia and hypogammaglobulinemia. The link is immunologic: neoplastic thymic epithelium selects T cells abnormally, so autoreactive T cells escape and support autoantibody production, for example against the acetylcholine receptor. Germ cell tumors arise from germ cells that lodged along the midline during their migration and occur mainly in young men; nonseminomatous types may raise serum alpha-fetoprotein or hCG. Mediastinal lymphoma includes nodular sclerosis Hodgkin lymphoma in young adults and T-lymphoblastic lymphoma in adolescents."},{"type":"p","text":"Symptoms follow what the mass compresses. An anterior or superior mass can obstruct the thin-walled superior vena cava (facial and arm swelling, distended neck veins) or compress the trachea, which may worsen when the patient lies supine. A middle mass can compress a bronchus or the left recurrent laryngeal nerve. A posterior neurogenic tumor can grow through an intervertebral foramen into a dumbbell shape and compress the spinal cord, or involve the sympathetic chain."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-04-01-C05","obj":"PU-4.5","title":"Innervation of the pleura and referred shoulder pain","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The two pleural layers come from different embryonic layers and inherit different nerves. The parietal pleura develops from somatic mesoderm of the body wall, so it is supplied by somatic sensory nerves that signal sharp, well-localized pain. The visceral pleura develops from splanchnic mesoderm with the lung, so it shares the lung's autonomic supply from the pulmonary plexus, which carries stretch and reflex information but not sharp localized pain. The two layers are separated by a thin film of fluid at a pressure of about -5 cm H2O at rest, which keeps the lung expanded against the chest wall."},{"type":"table","title":"Nerve supply of the pleura","cols":["Part","Nerve","Where pain is felt"],"rows":[["Costal parietal pleura","Intercostal nerves","Overlying chest wall"],["Peripheral diaphragmatic pleura","Lower intercostal and subcostal nerves","Lower chest wall and upper abdominal wall"],["Central diaphragmatic pleura","Phrenic nerve (C3 to C5)","Shoulder and base of the neck"],["Mediastinal parietal pleura","Phrenic nerve (C3 to C5)","Shoulder and base of the neck"],["Visceral pleura","Autonomic (pulmonary plexus)","No sharp pain"]],"caption":"Pain sensitivity follows somatic innervation, which only the parietal pleura has."},{"type":"p","text":"Pleuritic pain is sharp, localized, and worse with deep inspiration or coughing, because each breath slides and stretches the inflamed parietal layer; a pleural friction rub may be heard as the roughened surfaces move. A process confined to the lung and visceral pleura, such as an early pneumonia or a small central tumor, can therefore be painless. Pain appears once the process reaches the parietal pleura, for example when pneumonia extends to the surface or a pulmonary infarct after an embolus involves the pleura over it."},{"type":"h","text":"Why diaphragmatic irritation hurts in the shoulder"},{"type":"p","text":"The phrenic nerve arises from C3, C4, and C5. Its sensory fibers from the central diaphragmatic and mediastinal pleura, and from the pericardium, enter the same cord segments as the supraclavicular nerves (C3 and C4), which supply the skin over the shoulder and clavicle. The brain has learned to attribute activity in these segments to the skin, so it refers the pain there. The irritant can come from above the diaphragm (pleurisy, pericarditis) or from below it (blood, pus, or bile against the peritoneal surface of the central diaphragm), and the pain appears on the same side as the irritated half."},{"type":"flow","title":"Referred shoulder pain from the central diaphragm","steps":["Inflammation, blood, or pus irritates the central diaphragm","Phrenic sensory fibers carry the signal","They synapse in cord segments C3 to C5","Supraclavicular nerves from shoulder skin enter the same segments","Convergent dorsal horn neurons project to the cortex","Pain is perceived over the shoulder"],"caption":"Irritation of the peripheral diaphragm instead travels in lower intercostal nerves and is felt over the lower chest and upper abdomen."},{"type":"p","text":"The peripheral rim of the diaphragm and its pleura are supplied by the lower intercostal and subcostal nerves, which also supply the anterior abdominal wall. Basal pleurisy can therefore present as upper abdominal pain and guarding, and lower lobe pneumonia is a recognized mimic of an acute abdomen."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-04-01-C06","obj":"PU-4.6","title":"Openings of the diaphragm and the phrenic nerve","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The diaphragm is a dome of skeletal muscle inserting into a central tendon. Its muscle arises from the xiphoid, the lower six costal cartilages, and the lumbar vertebrae through two crura and the arcuate ligaments. Each structure that passes between thorax and abdomen crosses a different part of the diaphragm, and that part determines how the structure behaves when the muscle contracts."},{"type":"table","title":"Major openings of the diaphragm","cols":["Level","Opening and location","Structures transmitted","Effect of contraction"],"rows":[["T8","Caval opening, in the central tendon","Inferior vena cava, branches of the right phrenic nerve","Tendon is pulled taut, opening widens, venous return aided"],["T10","Esophageal hiatus, in the muscle of the right crus","Esophagus, anterior and posterior vagal trunks, esophageal branches of left gastric vessels","Crural fibers pinch the esophagus, adding to the antireflux barrier"],["T12","Aortic hiatus, behind the median arcuate ligament","Aorta, thoracic duct, azygos vein (often)","None; the aorta lies behind the diaphragm, not through it"]],"caption":"Smaller openings: the splanchnic nerves pierce the crura, the hemiazygos vein passes through the left crus, the sympathetic trunks pass behind the medial arcuate ligaments, and the left phrenic nerve pierces the left dome."},{"type":"p","text":"The vagal trunks follow the esophagus, so the anterior trunk (mainly from the left vagus) and posterior trunk (mainly from the right vagus) pass at T10. The thoracic duct and azygos vein run beside the aorta and pass with it at T12. Weakness or widening of the esophageal hiatus allows the stomach to herniate into the thorax (hiatal hernia), and the loss of crural support helps explain the reflux that often accompanies a sliding hernia. A posterolateral defect in the developing diaphragm, usually on the left, allows abdominal viscera into the chest and causes pulmonary hypoplasia."},{"type":"h","text":"The phrenic nerve"},{"type":"p","text":"The diaphragm develops largely from the septum transversum and pleuroperitoneal membranes, which form at the level of the cervical somites and receive their nerve supply there before descending with the heart and lungs. That is why a muscle at the level of the lower ribs is supplied from the neck. Each phrenic nerve (C3 to C5, mainly C4) runs down the front of the anterior scalene, enters the thorax between the subclavian artery and vein, and passes anterior to the lung root along the pericardium. It provides all motor supply to its half of the diaphragm and sensory supply to the central diaphragm, mediastinal pleura, and pericardium; the periphery of the diaphragm receives sensory fibers from the lower intercostal and subcostal nerves."},{"type":"p","text":"A spinal cord injury at or above C3 removes all diaphragmatic function, so spontaneous breathing stops, whereas an injury below C5 spares the diaphragm. A unilateral phrenic lesion, from surgery, trauma, or a tumor at the hilum or neck, leaves a raised hemidiaphragm on a radiograph. On fluoroscopy during a sharp sniff, the paralyzed side moves upward (paradoxical motion), because the flaccid muscle is sucked up by the negative intrathoracic pressure generated by the other side."},{"type":"stop","predict":"During a sniff test, a patient's left hemidiaphragm rises while the right descends. Which nerve is injured, and what would a chest radiograph show at rest?","explain":"The left phrenic nerve. The right diaphragm contracts and lowers intrathoracic pressure, which pulls the flaccid left dome upward; at rest the left hemidiaphragm sits abnormally high.","check":"The phrenic nerve paragraphs above."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-04-01-C07","obj":"PU-4.7","title":"Course of the thoracic duct and chylothorax","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The thoracic duct is the main lymphatic channel of the body. It drains lymph from the lower limbs, pelvis, abdomen, left thorax, left upper limb, and left side of the head and neck; the right lymphatic duct drains the remaining right upper quarter. Its intestinal lymph is chyle, because dietary long-chain fatty acids are reassembled in enterocytes into triglyceride-rich chylomicrons that are too large to enter blood capillaries and instead enter the lacteals. Flow is driven by valves, smooth muscle in the duct wall, and the pressure changes of breathing."},{"type":"flow","title":"Course of the thoracic duct","steps":["Lacteals and lumbar trunks drain to the cisterna chyli (about L1 to L2)","Duct enters the thorax through the aortic hiatus at T12","Ascends right of midline between the aorta and azygos vein","Lies behind the esophagus in the posterior mediastinum","Crosses to the left at about T5","Ascends left of the esophagus into the superior mediastinum and neck","Arches at about C7 and enters the left venous angle"],"caption":"The left venous angle is the junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the duct changes sides, the level of injury predicts the side of the leak: damage below about T5 usually produces a right chylothorax and damage above it a left one. The duct can be torn by surgery on the esophagus, aorta, or heart, by neck dissection near the left venous angle, or by penetrating or blunt trauma, and it can be obstructed or invaded by tumor, of which lymphoma is the most common nontraumatic cause. Raised venous pressure at the venous angle, as with central venous thrombosis, can also impede drainage."},{"type":"table","title":"Chylous effusion compared with similar-looking fluids","cols":["Feature","Chylothorax","Pseudochylothorax","Empyema"],"rows":[["Cause","Thoracic duct leak","Long-standing effusion with a thick pleura","Pleural infection"],["Triglyceride","Over about 110 mg/dL (1.24 mmol/L); chylomicrons present","Low; no chylomicrons","Not diagnostic"],["Cholesterol","Low (under about 200 mg/dL)","High; cholesterol crystals","Variable"],["After centrifugation","Stays milky","Stays turbid","Supernatant clears"],["Cells","Lymphocytes predominate","Variable","Neutrophils predominate"]],"caption":"A triglyceride under about 50 mg/dL makes chylothorax unlikely; in a fasting patient chyle may not look milky."},{"type":"p","text":"The milky color comes from light scattering by chylomicrons, so it fades when the patient is fasting or fed little fat. Because chyle carries dietary fat, fat-soluble vitamins, protein, and many T lymphocytes, a persistent leak causes malnutrition, lymphopenia, and impaired cellular immunity, while the accumulating fluid compresses the lung and causes dyspnea. The termination of the duct in the left neck also explains why abdominal cancers, especially gastric carcinoma, can metastasize to a left supraclavicular lymph node."},{"type":"example","title":"Predicting the side of a chylothorax","text":"After an esophagectomy involving the lower third of the esophagus, a patient develops a milky right pleural effusion with triglyceride of 400 mg/dL. The lower thoracic duct lies behind the esophagus and to the right of the aorta, below its crossing at about T5, so a leak there drains into the right pleural cavity. By contrast, a left neck dissection that injures the duct near the left venous angle leaks into the left neck or, if the leak tracks downward, the left pleural cavity. The side of the effusion therefore points to the level of injury."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology explains most of this anatomy. The pleura inherits somatic or autonomic nerves from the mesoderm it develops from, which is why only the parietal layer hurts; the diaphragm keeps its cervical nerve after descending, which is why its central part refers pain to the shoulder; and mediastinal masses reflect resident tissues, migrating germ cells, and foregut buds. Physiology and pathology build on the same map: negative pleural pressure holds the lung to the chest wall, gravity and bronchial geometry place aspiration pneumonia and abscess (usually from mouth anaerobes), and immunology links thymoma to autoimmune disease through defective T cell selection."},{"type":"p","text":"These relations have limits. Surface borders vary with body habitus, respiratory phase, and posture, so clinical procedures rely on imaging rather than rib counts alone, and the paravertebral borders are approximate. Compartment lists describe typical rather than exclusive origins: lymphoma can occupy any compartment, and a mass can cross boundaries. Biochemical cutoffs for chylous fluid are guides, interpreted with the appearance, the patient's diet, and the clinical setting."}],"summary":["At the midclavicular, midaxillary, and paravertebral lines, the lung reaches ribs 6, 8, and 10 and the pleura ribs 8, 10, and 12; the gap is the costodiaphragmatic recess, where fluid collects first.","The intercostal vein, artery, and nerve run in the costal groove under each rib, so a thoracentesis needle passes just above the rib below.","The right lung has three lobes and two fissures; the left has two lobes, one fissure, a lingula, and a cardiac notch.","The right main bronchus is wider and more vertical; aspiration goes to the right lower lobe basal segments when upright and to the superior segment of the right lower lobe when supine.","At the hilum the right pulmonary artery is anterior and the left superior to its bronchus; the veins are anterior and inferior on both sides.","The sternal angle plane (T4 to T5) divides the superior from the inferior mediastinum; anterior masses are thymoma, teratoma, lymphoma, and thyroid, and posterior masses are mainly neurogenic.","Parietal pleura is pain-sensitive through intercostal and phrenic nerves; visceral pleura has only autonomic supply.","Central diaphragmatic irritation refers pain to the shoulder because phrenic (C3 to C5) and supraclavicular (C3, C4) afferents share cord segments.","The IVC passes at T8 through the central tendon, the esophagus and vagal trunks at T10, and the aorta, thoracic duct, and azygos vein at T12; the phrenic nerve is the sole motor supply.","The thoracic duct runs from the cisterna chyli through the aortic hiatus, crosses left at about T5, and ends at the left venous angle; its injury causes a triglyceride-rich chylothorax."],"sources":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax: pleurae, lungs, and tracheobronchial tree"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax: mediastinum; Abdomen: diaphragm"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Body cavities and development of the diaphragm"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pulmonary Ventilation (pleural pressure)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lung (lesions of the pleura); Thymus (thymoma)"}]},"PU-5":{"unit":"PU-5","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-05","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-05-01","title":"Pulmonary Blood Flow and Airflow","subject":"Respiratory System (RESP)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how blood and air move through the lungs and what sets the resistance to each. It covers the two blood supplies of the lung, the unusual response of pulmonary vessels to hypoxia, the effect of gravity on regional perfusion and ventilation, the anatomy of the airway tree, the physics of airway resistance, and how lung volume changes pulmonary vascular resistance. Board questions use these ideas to explain why most pulmonary emboli do not infarct, how chronic hypoxia leads to cor pulmonale, why reactivation tuberculosis favors the apex, and why narrowing an airway slightly reduces airflow so much.","prerequisites":["Pressure, flow, and resistance in the circulation (flow equals pressure difference divided by resistance)","Right and left heart pressures and the pulmonary and systemic circuits","Airway histology from trachea to alveolus (PU-2)","Thoracic anatomy, hilum, and pleura (PU-4)","G protein signaling: Gq, IP3, and Ca2+; Gs, cAMP, and protein kinase A"],"connections":[{"to":"Ventilation and lung mechanics (PU-6)","how":"Lung volume, pleural pressure, and compliance determine both regional ventilation and the caliber of airways and extra-alveolar vessels."},{"to":"Gas exchange and hypoxemia (PU-7)","how":"West zones and hypoxic vasoconstriction set the regional ventilation-perfusion ratios that decide alveolar and arterial PO2."},{"to":"Cardiovascular hemodynamics","how":"Pulmonary vascular resistance is the afterload of the right ventricle, so lung disease that raises it causes right ventricular hypertrophy and failure."},{"to":"Pulmonary pathology (PU-14)","how":"Embolism, infarction, chronic obstructive disease, asthma, and tuberculosis are explained by the flow and resistance principles in this chapter."},{"to":"Neonatal transition","how":"The fall in pulmonary vascular resistance at the first breath reverses hypoxic vasoconstriction in the fetal lung and redirects blood through the lungs."}],"terms":[{"term":"Pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR)","def":"Mean pulmonary artery pressure minus left atrial pressure, divided by pulmonary blood flow; the afterload of the right ventricle."},{"term":"Bronchial circulation","def":"Systemic arterial supply from the thoracic aorta that nourishes the airway walls, connective tissue, and visceral pleura."},{"term":"Recruitment and distension","def":"Opening of previously closed pulmonary capillaries and widening of open ones, which lower PVR as flow or pressure rises."},{"term":"Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction","def":"Constriction of small pulmonary arteries in response to low alveolar PO2, diverting blood toward better ventilated alveoli."},{"term":"Cor pulmonale","def":"Right ventricular hypertrophy or dilation caused by pulmonary hypertension from disease of the lungs or pulmonary vessels, not from left heart disease."},{"term":"West zones","def":"Regions of the upright lung defined by the order of alveolar, pulmonary arterial, and pulmonary venous pressures."},{"term":"Ventilation-perfusion ratio (V/Q)","def":"Alveolar ventilation divided by capillary blood flow in a region; about 0.8 for the whole lung."},{"term":"Anatomic dead space","def":"Volume of the conducting airways, about 150 mL in an adult, which moves gas without exchanging it."},{"term":"Poiseuille law","def":"For laminar flow in a tube, resistance equals 8 times viscosity times length divided by pi times the radius to the fourth power."},{"term":"Radial traction","def":"Outward pull of surrounding lung tissue on airways and extra-alveolar vessels, which increases as lung volume increases."},{"term":"Functional residual capacity (FRC)","def":"Lung volume at the end of a quiet expiration, where lung recoil and chest wall recoil balance."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-05-01-C01","obj":"PU-5.1","title":"Pulmonary and bronchial circulations and why emboli rarely infarct","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The lung receives blood from two separate systems. The pulmonary circulation carries the entire right ventricular output through the alveolar capillaries for gas exchange. The bronchial circulation is a small systemic supply, about 1% to 2% of cardiac output, that nourishes the tissue of the lung itself."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the pulmonary circuit must accept the whole cardiac output, it is built for low resistance. Its arteries are short, wide, and thin walled with little smooth muscle, and its capillary network is vast. Pulmonary artery pressure is about 25/8 mmHg (mean about 15 mmHg) against a left atrial pressure of about 5 mmHg, so a pressure drop of only about 10 mmHg drives the full cardiac output. Pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR) is therefore about one tenth of systemic resistance. When cardiac output rises, closed capillaries open (recruitment) and open capillaries widen (distension), so PVR falls and pressure rises only a little. Pulmonary capillary pressure stays near 7 mmHg, well below plasma oncotic pressure, which keeps the alveoli dry."},{"type":"table","title":"Pulmonary versus bronchial circulation","cols":["Feature","Pulmonary","Bronchial"],"rows":[["Source","Right ventricle through the pulmonary trunk","Thoracic aorta; the right bronchial artery often arises from the third right posterior intercostal artery or a common trunk"],["Pressure","Low, mean about 15 mmHg","Systemic, mean about 90 to 95 mmHg"],["Blood carried","Mixed venous blood","Oxygenated arterial blood"],["Supplies","Alveolar capillaries for gas exchange","Bronchi and bronchioles, connective tissue, visceral pleura, lymph nodes"],["Venous return","Pulmonary veins to the left atrium","Hilar bronchial veins to the azygos and accessory hemiazygos veins; most deeper bronchial blood enters pulmonary veins"]],"caption":"Bronchial venous blood that drains into the pulmonary veins is a small normal shunt that keeps arterial PO2 slightly below alveolar PO2."},{"type":"p","text":"Lung tissue therefore has three sources of oxygen: pulmonary blood, bronchial blood, and the alveolar gas itself. When an embolus blocks a branch of the pulmonary artery, bronchial flow and alveolar oxygen keep the parenchyma alive, so only about 10% of emboli cause infarction. The blocked region becomes alveolar dead space, ventilated but not perfused, and the redirected flow overperfuses the remaining lung, which lowers PaO2."},{"type":"flow","title":"When a pulmonary embolus causes infarction","steps":["Embolus occludes a medium or small pulmonary artery","Bronchial flow already reduced, as in left heart failure","Parenchyma beyond the block becomes ischemic and necrotic","Bronchial blood leaks into the dead zone: hemorrhagic infarct","Wedge shape with its base on the pleura","Fibrinous pleuritis: pleuritic pain, friction rub, hemoptysis"],"caption":"Infarction needs both circulations to fail; the infarct is red because bronchial blood keeps entering it, and it later organizes into a scar."},{"type":"p","text":"Infarction is more likely when the bronchial circulation or cardiac output is already compromised, as in left heart failure, or with underlying lung disease. The infarct follows the conical territory of the occluded artery, so it is wedge shaped with its base at the pleura, often in the lower lobes, which receive the most blood flow. The bronchial arteries also explain why they, not the pulmonary arteries, are the usual source of massive hemoptysis: they carry systemic pressure and enlarge in chronic inflammatory disease such as bronchiectasis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-05-01-C02","obj":"PU-5.2","title":"Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction and cor pulmonale","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Small pulmonary arteries constrict when the PO2 of the alveoli around them falls, especially below about 70 mmHg. The main stimulus is alveolar PO2, which reaches the arteriolar smooth muscle by diffusion; mixed venous PO2 contributes less. Hypercapnia and acidosis strengthen the response. This is the reverse of the systemic response, and it makes sense once the purpose is clear: a hypoxic systemic tissue needs more blood to deliver oxygen, but a poorly ventilated alveolus cannot load oxygen into the blood passing it, so the lung sends that blood elsewhere."},{"type":"flow","title":"Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction","steps":["Low alveolar PO2 reaches pulmonary arteriolar smooth muscle","Oxygen-sensitive K+ channels close","Membrane depolarizes","L-type Ca2+ channels open and Ca2+ enters","Smooth muscle contracts, local resistance rises","Blood shifts to well ventilated alveoli; V/Q matching improves"],"caption":"Mitochondria are thought to act as the oxygen sensor that signals to these channels; the carotid body glomus cell uses a similar K+ channel mechanism."},{"type":"table","title":"Hypoxia in the pulmonary and systemic beds","cols":["Feature","Pulmonary arterioles","Systemic arterioles"],"rows":[["Response to hypoxia","Constriction","Dilation"],["Membrane effect","K+ channels close, depolarization, Ca2+ entry","KATP channels open, hyperpolarization, less Ca2+ entry"],["Other mediators","Endothelin release and reduced nitric oxide sustain it","Adenosine, nitric oxide, H+, CO2, lactate"],["Purpose","Match perfusion to ventilation","Match flow to tissue oxygen demand"]],"caption":"The same stimulus produces opposite effects because the two beds serve opposite goals."},{"type":"p","text":"Before birth this response keeps PVR high, because the lungs are fluid filled and hypoxic, so most right ventricular output crosses the ductus arteriosus. With the first breaths alveolar PO2 rises, the vessels dilate, and PVR falls sharply."},{"type":"p","text":"Regional hypoxia, such as one consolidated lobe, constricts only local vessels and protects PaO2. Global alveolar hypoxia, as in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), obstructive sleep apnea, obesity hypoventilation, or residence at high altitude, constricts the whole pulmonary bed at once. Pulmonary artery pressure rises. With time the arteries remodel: medial smooth muscle hypertrophies, muscle extends into normally nonmuscular arterioles, and the intima thickens, so the hypertension becomes partly fixed. Hypoxia also stimulates erythropoietin, and the resulting polycythemia raises blood viscosity and PVR further. In emphysema, destruction of alveolar walls also removes capillary bed."},{"type":"flow","title":"Chronic alveolar hypoxia to cor pulmonale","steps":["Chronic alveolar hypoxia: COPD, sleep apnea, high altitude","Constriction throughout the pulmonary arterial bed","Pulmonary hypertension; mean pressure above about 20 mmHg","Vascular remodeling: medial hypertrophy, muscularized arterioles","Higher right ventricular afterload: concentric RV hypertrophy","RV failure: raised JVP, hepatomegaly, peripheral edema"],"caption":"Cor pulmonale is right heart disease caused by lung or pulmonary vascular disease, not by left heart failure."},{"type":"p","text":"The findings follow from the mechanism. A loud pulmonic component of S2 reflects forceful closure of the pulmonic valve under high pressure. A left parasternal heave reflects the hypertrophied right ventricle. The ECG may show right axis deviation, a dominant R wave in V1, and tall P waves in lead II from right atrial enlargement. When the ventricle fails, raised venous pressure produces jugular distension, hepatic congestion, and dependent edema."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient has pneumonia confined to the right lower lobe. Predict how flow to that lobe changes and what would happen to PaO2 if that response were lost.","explain":"Alveolar hypoxia in the lobe constricts its arterioles, diverting blood to ventilated lung. Without the response, more blood would pass unventilated alveoli, acting as a shunt, and PaO2 would fall further.","check":"Compare with the first flow figure and the purpose row of the table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-05-01-C03","obj":"PU-5.3","title":"West zones and regional ventilation-perfusion","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In the upright lung, gravity adds hydrostatic pressure to blood in the pulmonary vessels, about 0.74 mmHg for every centimeter below the heart. The lung extends roughly 20 cm above to 10 cm below the pulmonary artery, so vascular pressure is about 15 mmHg lower at the apex and about 8 mmHg higher at the base than at heart level. Alveolar pressure, by contrast, is the same throughout the lung (atmospheric at rest between breaths). Because a low-pressure circuit has little pressure to spare, the order of alveolar (PA), arterial (Pa), and venous (Pv) pressures changes with height, and that order decides blood flow."},{"type":"table","title":"West zones of the upright lung","cols":["Zone","Pressure order","What controls flow","Where it occurs"],"rows":[["Zone 1","PA > Pa > Pv","No flow; capillaries collapsed; alveolar dead space","Apex only when Pa falls (hemorrhage, shock) or PA rises (positive pressure ventilation)"],["Zone 2","Pa > PA > Pv","Arterial minus alveolar pressure; venous pressure has no effect","Upper and middle lung; at the apex flow may occur only in systole"],["Zone 3","Pa > Pv > PA","Arterial minus venous pressure; vessels fully open","Lower lung, which receives the most flow"]],"caption":"Moving down the lung, alveolar pressure drops from first to last place in the order. Zone 1 is absent in a healthy upright person at rest."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: when the apex becomes zone 1","text":"Pulmonary artery pressure at heart level is 25/8 mmHg. The apex lies about 20 cm higher, which subtracts about 15 mmHg, so apical arterial pressure is about 10 mmHg in systole and about -7 mmHg in diastole. Alveolar pressure is 0. The capillaries open in systole (Pa > PA, zone 2) and collapse in diastole, so apical flow is intermittent. After a large hemorrhage, pulmonary artery pressure falls to about 15/4 mmHg; apical systolic pressure becomes about 0, never exceeding alveolar pressure, so the apex is zone 1 throughout the cycle. Ventilated but unperfused, it adds alveolar dead space. Raising alveolar pressure with positive pressure ventilation has the same effect."},{"type":"p","text":"Ventilation also increases toward the base. Pleural pressure is about -10 cmH2O at the apex and about -2.5 cmH2O at the base because the lung hangs in the chest, so apical alveoli are already more inflated at rest and sit on the flat, stiff upper part of the pressure-volume curve. Basal alveoli are smaller and sit on the steep part, so each breath expands them more. Perfusion, however, rises far more steeply down the lung than ventilation does. As a result V/Q falls from about 3.3 at the apex to about 0.6 at the base, averaging about 0.8."},{"type":"table","title":"Apex versus base in the upright lung","cols":["Feature","Apex","Base"],"rows":[["Ventilation per unit volume","Lower","Higher"],["Perfusion per unit volume","Much lower","Much higher"],["V/Q","About 3.3","About 0.6"],["Alveolar PO2","Highest, about 130 mmHg","Lowest, about 90 mmHg"],["Alveolar PCO2","Lowest, about 28 mmHg","Highest, about 42 mmHg"]],"caption":"A high V/Q region behaves like inspired air; a low V/Q region behaves like mixed venous blood."},{"type":"p","text":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis is an obligate aerobe, and reactivation (secondary) tuberculosis favors the apical and posterior segments of the upper lobes, where alveolar PO2 is highest. Primary infection instead seeds the lower part of the upper lobe or upper part of the lower lobe, where more inhaled air, and therefore more inhaled bacilli, arrives. During exercise, higher pulmonary artery pressure recruits apical capillaries and makes V/Q more uniform; lying supine reduces the vertical gradient."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Predict the effect of a sudden fall in pulmonary artery pressure on the apex of an upright patient receiving positive pressure ventilation.","explain":"Lower arterial pressure and higher alveolar pressure both push the apex toward PA > Pa, creating zone 1 with no flow and more dead space.","check":"Compare with the West zone table and the worked example."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-05-01-C04","obj":"PU-5.4","title":"Conducting and respiratory zones and where airway resistance lies","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The airway tree branches by repeated division, about 23 generations from trachea to alveolar sacs in the classic Weibel model. The first part, the conducting zone, moves, warms, humidifies, and filters air but exchanges no gas. The second part, the respiratory zone, has alveoli in its walls and exchanges gas with capillary blood."},{"type":"table","title":"Conducting and respiratory zones","cols":["Feature","Conducting zone","Respiratory zone"],"rows":[["Structures","Trachea, main, lobar, and segmental bronchi, small bronchi, bronchioles, terminal bronchioles","Respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, alveolar sacs and alveoli"],["Generations (approximate)","0 to 16","17 to 23"],["Gas exchange","None","Yes"],["Wall support","Cartilage in trachea and bronchi; bronchioles have none and rely on radial traction","Alveolar walls with elastic fibers; no cartilage"],["Gas movement","Bulk flow","Mostly diffusion, because total cross-section is enormous"],["Volume","Anatomic dead space, about 150 mL","Most of the lung volume, about 3 L at FRC"]],"caption":"The terminal bronchiole is the last purely conducting airway; the respiratory bronchiole is the first with alveoli."},{"type":"p","text":"The anatomic dead space matters because each breath first pushes in the 150 mL of used air left in the conducting airways. With a tidal volume of 500 mL, only about 350 mL of fresh air reaches the alveoli, so alveolar ventilation at 12 breaths per minute is about 4.2 L/min rather than 6 L/min. Rapid shallow breathing wastes a larger fraction of each breath on dead space. Anatomic dead space rises slightly with lung volume and neck extension, which stretch the airways. Added to the alveolar dead space of ventilated but unperfused alveoli (C03), it forms the physiologic dead space (PU-6)."},{"type":"p","text":"It seems that the narrowest airways should resist airflow most, because resistance of a single tube rises steeply as its radius falls (C05). But airways at each generation are arranged in parallel, and the combined resistance of n identical tubes in parallel equals one tube's resistance divided by n. The number of airways roughly doubles with each generation while each one narrows only modestly, so after the first several generations total cross-sectional area expands enormously. Total resistance therefore peaks in the medium-sized bronchi, around the segmental bronchi to about the seventh generation, and airways smaller than about 2 mm in diameter contribute less than 20% of the total. Flow in the trachea and large bronchi is also partly turbulent, which adds to resistance there."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why total resistance peaks in medium-sized bronchi","steps":["Trachea: one wide tube, low resistance","Medium bronchi: narrower tubes, still few in parallel","Total resistance reaches its maximum here","Bronchioles: tiny radius but thousands in parallel","Huge total cross-section, low combined resistance","Respiratory zone: flow so slow that diffusion takes over"],"caption":"Total resistance of a generation is the single-tube resistance divided by the number of tubes in parallel."},{"type":"p","text":"This has a practical consequence. Disease that begins in the small airways, as in early chronic bronchitis in smokers, can narrow many bronchioles while hardly changing total resistance, so routine spirometry stays normal until the disease is advanced. The small airways are therefore called the quiet zone of the lung."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-05-01-C05","obj":"PU-5.5","title":"Poiseuille law and what changes airway resistance","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Airflow follows the same relationship as blood flow: flow equals the pressure difference between mouth and alveoli divided by airway resistance. For laminar flow through a tube, Poiseuille law gives resistance as R = 8 x viscosity x length / (pi x r^4). Radius dominates because it is raised to the fourth power. Halving the radius raises resistance 2 x 2 x 2 x 2 = 16-fold, so at the same driving pressure flow falls to one sixteenth. Normal total airway resistance is low, about 1.5 to 2 cmH2O per L/s, so quiet breathing needs only about 1 to 2 cmH2O of pressure."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: a small change in radius","text":"Bronchoconstriction narrows the medium bronchi so their radius falls by 20%, to 0.8 of normal. Resistance changes by 1 / (0.8 x 0.8 x 0.8 x 0.8) = 1 / 0.41, about 2.4 times normal. If the patient keeps the same alveolar driving pressure, airflow falls to about 41% of normal. To restore normal flow, the respiratory muscles must generate about 2.4 times the pressure gradient. A modest change in airway caliber therefore produces a large rise in the work of breathing."},{"type":"p","text":"Poiseuille law strictly applies only to laminar flow. Whether flow is laminar or turbulent is predicted by the Reynolds number, which rises with gas density, velocity, and airway diameter and falls with viscosity. In the trachea and large bronchi, where velocity is high, flow is turbulent or transitional; there the pressure needed rises with the square of flow, and resistance depends on gas density rather than viscosity. In the small airways, where velocity is very low, flow is laminar and density matters little."},{"type":"table","title":"Factors that change airway resistance","cols":["Factor","Effect on resistance","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Vagal acetylcholine on M3 receptors","Increases","Gq, IP3, rise in intracellular Ca2+, smooth muscle contraction"],["Histamine, leukotrienes, irritants","Increases","Smooth muscle contraction, mucosal edema, mucus in the lumen"],["Beta-2 stimulation by circulating epinephrine","Decreases","Gs, cAMP, protein kinase A, smooth muscle relaxation"],["High lung volume","Decreases","Greater radial traction holds airways open"],["Low lung volume","Increases","Less traction; small airways may close near residual volume"],["Denser gas (diving at depth)","Increases","More turbulence and density-dependent resistance in large airways"],["Low-density gas (helium-oxygen)","Decreases","Less turbulence; effect is greatest where flow is turbulent"]],"caption":"Airway smooth muscle has little direct sympathetic innervation; its beta-2 receptors respond mainly to circulating epinephrine."},{"type":"p","text":"Lung volume acts mechanically. Bronchioles lack cartilage, so their caliber depends on the outward pull of the alveolar walls attached to them. As the lung inflates, that radial traction widens them and resistance falls; as volume falls toward residual volume, resistance rises steeply. People with asthma often breathe at a higher lung volume during an attack, which partly offsets narrowing by increasing traction. In emphysema, destruction of alveolar walls removes that traction, so the airways narrow and collapse during expiration."},{"type":"p","text":"Asthma combines several of these mechanisms: smooth muscle contraction, mucosal edema, and mucus plugging each reduce radius, and because resistance scales with the fourth power, their effects multiply. Wheezing is the sound of air forced at high velocity through narrowed, oscillating airways. Expiration is affected most, because intrathoracic airways are narrower when pleural pressure is less negative."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-05-01-C06","obj":"PU-5.6","title":"Pulmonary vascular resistance and lung volume","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Pulmonary vessels sit in two different mechanical settings, and lung inflation affects them in opposite ways. Alveolar vessels are the capillaries in the alveolar walls; they are surrounded by alveolar pressure and are stretched along their length and flattened as the alveoli expand. Extra-alveolar vessels are the arteries, arterioles, and veins in the interstitium; they are tethered to the surrounding lung, so radial traction pulls them open as the lung inflates and lets them narrow as it deflates."},{"type":"table","title":"Alveolar and extra-alveolar vessels","cols":["Feature","Alveolar vessels","Extra-alveolar vessels"],"rows":[["Location","Capillaries in alveolar walls","Arteries, arterioles, veins in the interstitium"],["Surrounding pressure","Alveolar pressure","Interstitial pressure, which follows pleural pressure"],["At high lung volume","Stretched and compressed: resistance rises","Pulled open: resistance falls"],["At low lung volume","Open and wider: resistance falls","Lose traction and narrow: resistance rises"]],"caption":"Total PVR is the sum of these two resistances in series."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the two components move in opposite directions, total PVR plotted against lung volume forms a U-shaped curve with its lowest point near functional residual capacity (FRC), the volume at which a person rests at the end of a quiet breath. Below FRC, narrowing of extra-alveolar vessels dominates. Their own smooth muscle tone and elastic recoil add to the narrowing, and collapsed alveoli become hypoxic, which adds active hypoxic vasoconstriction (C02). Above FRC, compression of alveolar capillaries dominates and resistance rises again toward total lung capacity."},{"type":"p","text":"These passive effects explain several clinical patterns of right ventricular strain. Lobar collapse (atelectasis) raises PVR through loss of traction and local hypoxia. Hyperinflation in severe COPD, and large tidal volumes or high end-expiratory pressure during mechanical ventilation, raise PVR by compressing alveolar capillaries and also convert more lung to zone 1 or zone 2 (C03). In each case right ventricular afterload rises."},{"type":"p","text":"Lung volume is one of several passive influences on PVR. Rising pulmonary artery or left atrial pressure lowers PVR by recruitment and distension (C01). Active influences act on vascular smooth muscle: nitric oxide and prostacyclin from the endothelium dilate, while hypoxia, endothelin-1, and thromboxane A2 constrict. Autonomic nerves have only a weak effect on the pulmonary vessels."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Predict how PVR changes when a patient breathes from FRC down to residual volume, and when the same patient inhales to total lung capacity.","explain":"Both raise PVR. Near residual volume extra-alveolar vessels narrow from lost traction; near total lung capacity alveolar capillaries are stretched and compressed.","check":"Compare with the alveolar and extra-alveolar vessel table."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and physiology meet in the lung's blood and air pathways. The bronchial arteries from the aorta explain why emboli rarely infarct and why massive hemoptysis is usually bronchial in origin. Branching geometry explains why total airway resistance peaks in the medium bronchi and why small airway disease is silent, while the absence of cartilage in bronchioles makes their caliber depend on lung volume, linking airway resistance to the same radial traction that controls extra-alveolar vessels. Gravity, alveolar pressure, and low vascular pressure create West zones and the apex to base gradient in V/Q, which in turn explains the apical location of reactivation tuberculosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology, biochemistry, and pathology connect through smooth muscle signaling. Oxygen-sensitive K+ channels and L-type Ca2+ channels drive hypoxic vasoconstriction, Gq and Gs pathways set bronchial tone, and the same hypoxic signal that protects PaO2 in a single lobe becomes harmful when the whole lung is hypoxic, producing pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale. West zone boundaries are not fixed lines but shift with posture, cardiac output, and ventilation, and regional values for V/Q and alveolar gas pressures are approximate averages."}],"summary":["The pulmonary circulation carries the whole cardiac output at low pressure (about 25/8, mean about 15 mmHg); PVR is about one tenth of systemic resistance and falls further with recruitment and distension.","The bronchial arteries from the aorta nourish airway walls and pleura, so most pulmonary emboli do not infarct; infarction needs compromised bronchial flow and is hemorrhagic and wedge shaped with a pleural base.","Low alveolar PO2 closes K+ channels in pulmonary arteriolar muscle, causing depolarization, Ca2+ entry, and constriction; systemic arterioles dilate in hypoxia.","Global chronic hypoxia constricts and remodels the whole pulmonary bed, causing pulmonary hypertension and right ventricular hypertrophy (cor pulmonale).","Zone 1: PA > Pa > Pv, no flow; zone 2: Pa > PA > Pv; zone 3: Pa > Pv > PA. Zone 1 appears only with low arterial or high alveolar pressure.","Ventilation and perfusion both rise toward the base, but perfusion rises more, so V/Q is about 3.3 at the apex and about 0.6 at the base; high apical PO2 favors reactivation tuberculosis.","The conducting zone (generations 0 to 16) is anatomic dead space of about 150 mL; the respiratory zone begins at the respiratory bronchioles.","Resistance varies with 1/r^4, so halving the radius raises it 16-fold; total resistance peaks in medium-sized bronchi because small airways are arranged in vast parallel networks.","Airway resistance rises with M3 stimulation, low lung volume, and denser gas, and falls with beta-2 stimulation, high lung volume, and low-density gas.","PVR is lowest near FRC; it rises at low volumes as extra-alveolar vessels narrow and at high volumes as alveolar capillaries are compressed."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pulmonary Circulation, Pulmonary Edema, Pleural Fluid"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pulmonary Ventilation, section on airway resistance and dead space"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Pulmonary blood flow and ventilation-perfusion matching; mechanics of airflow"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax, bronchial arteries and veins"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lung, pulmonary embolism, hemorrhage, and infarction; pulmonary hypertension"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Pulmonary Disease"}]},"PU-6":{"unit":"PU-6","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-06","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-06-01","title":"Ventilation: Lung Volumes, Mechanics, and the Control of Breathing","subject":"Respiratory System (RESP)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how air is moved into and out of the alveoli and how the brainstem decides how much to move. It covers lung volumes and how they are measured, minute and alveolar ventilation and dead space, the elastic forces of the lung and chest wall, spirometry and flow-volume loops, the respiratory centers and chemoreceptors, and the sleep-related breathing disorders. Board questions ask you to calculate alveolar ventilation, classify a spirometry pattern, predict the ventilatory response to a change in PaCO2 or PaO2, and trace nighttime hypoxemia to pulmonary hypertension and polycythemia. Each of these rests on a few relationships taught here.","prerequisites":["Gas laws: Boyle law and partial pressures (Dalton law)","Anatomy of the thoracic cage, diaphragm, pleura, and conducting airways (PU-4)","Airway resistance and the pressure gradients that drive airflow (PU-5)","The bicarbonate buffer system and the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation","Brainstem anatomy and the glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves"],"connections":[{"to":"Respiratory embryology (PU-1)","how":"Type II pneumocytes mature late in gestation, so surfactant deficiency in premature infants shows the law of Laplace in action."},{"to":"Pulmonary blood flow and airflow (PU-5)","how":"Dynamic airway compression and airway resistance explain why obstructive disease limits expiratory flow on spirometry."},{"to":"Gas exchange and blood gas transport (PU-7, PU-8)","how":"Alveolar ventilation sets alveolar PCO2 and PO2, and the shape of the oxyhemoglobin curve explains the 60 mmHg chemoreceptor threshold."},{"to":"Renal and acid-base physiology","how":"Renal bicarbonate retention resets CSF pH in chronic hypercapnia and blunts the central drive to breathe."},{"to":"Pulmonary vascular disease (PU-14-E)","how":"Repeated hypoxemia in sleep-disordered breathing causes hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction, pulmonary hypertension, and cor pulmonale."}],"terms":[{"term":"Functional residual capacity (FRC)","def":"The volume of gas left in the lungs at the end of a quiet expiration, where the inward recoil of the lung equals the outward recoil of the chest wall."},{"term":"Residual volume (RV)","def":"The volume of gas that remains in the lungs after a maximal expiration and cannot be exhaled."},{"term":"Anatomic dead space","def":"The volume of the conducting airways, about 150 mL in an adult, which fills with gas that never reaches alveoli."},{"term":"Physiologic dead space","def":"Anatomic dead space plus alveolar dead space (alveoli that are ventilated but not perfused); the total wasted ventilation."},{"term":"Alveolar ventilation","def":"The volume of fresh gas reaching perfused alveoli each minute; it sets alveolar and arterial PCO2."},{"term":"Compliance","def":"The change in volume produced by a change in distending pressure (delta V divided by delta P); the inverse of elastance or stiffness."},{"term":"Transpulmonary pressure","def":"Alveolar pressure minus intrapleural pressure; the pressure that holds the lung open."},{"term":"Surfactant","def":"A phospholipid and protein mixture, mainly dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine, secreted by type II pneumocytes that lowers alveolar surface tension."},{"term":"FEV1/FVC ratio","def":"The fraction of the forced vital capacity exhaled in the first second; normally about 0.75 to 0.85 in young adults."},{"term":"Hering-Breuer inflation reflex","def":"A vagal reflex in which slowly adapting airway stretch receptors terminate inspiration when the lung is markedly inflated."},{"term":"Glomus (type I) cell","def":"The oxygen-sensing cell of the carotid and aortic bodies, which releases transmitters onto afferent nerve endings when PaO2 falls."},{"term":"Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction","def":"Contraction of pulmonary arterioles in response to low alveolar PO2, which diverts blood to better ventilated regions but raises pulmonary vascular resistance when hypoxia is widespread."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-06-01-C01","obj":"PU-6.1","title":"Lung volumes and capacities, and why residual volume needs special methods","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The lung is never empty. Its volume at any moment is described with four non-overlapping volumes and four capacities, and each capacity is a sum of two or more volumes. Values depend on height, age, and sex; those below are typical for a young adult man and are roughly 20% to 25% lower in women."},{"type":"table","title":"Lung volumes and capacities","cols":["Measure","Definition","Typical value"],"rows":[["Tidal volume (TV)","Air moved in a quiet breath","About 500 mL"],["Inspiratory reserve volume (IRV)","Extra air that can be inhaled after a normal inspiration","About 3000 mL"],["Expiratory reserve volume (ERV)","Extra air that can be exhaled after a normal expiration","About 1100 mL"],["Residual volume (RV)","Air left after maximal expiration","About 1200 mL"],["Inspiratory capacity (IC)","TV + IRV","About 3500 mL"],["Functional residual capacity (FRC)","ERV + RV","About 2300 mL"],["Vital capacity (VC)","IRV + TV + ERV","About 4600 mL"],["Total lung capacity (TLC)","VC + RV","About 5800 mL"]],"caption":"Any capacity that contains RV (FRC and TLC) cannot be measured by spirometry alone."},{"type":"p","text":"TLC is reached when the inspiratory muscles can no longer overcome the inward recoil of the lung and chest wall. RV remains because, in young people, the chest wall becomes too stiff for the expiratory muscles to compress further; with age and in obstructive disease, small airways close near the bottom of expiration and trap gas. FRC is not a muscular limit at all: it is the resting volume where the lung and chest wall recoils balance (C03). FRC also serves as a gas reservoir that buffers alveolar PO2 and PCO2 between breaths."},{"type":"p","text":"A spirometer records only the gas that moves through the mouth. Residual volume never leaves the lung, so spirometry cannot measure RV or any capacity that contains it. Two indirect methods are used. Helium dilution has the subject breathe from a closed spirometer containing a known concentration of helium, an inert gas that is barely absorbed into blood. After equilibration, conservation of helium gives C1 x V1 = C2 x (V1 + FRC). Body plethysmography seats the subject in a sealed box; when the subject breathes against a closed shutter at FRC, the change in mouth pressure and the reciprocal change in box pressure give thoracic gas volume through Boyle law (P1V1 = P2V2). RV is then FRC minus ERV, and TLC is RV plus VC."},{"type":"p","text":"The methods differ in what they count. Helium reaches only gas in communication with open airways, so it underestimates FRC when gas is trapped behind closed or narrowed airways, as in emphysema or bullous disease. Plethysmography compresses all gas in the thorax, trapped or not. In obstructive disease the plethysmographic value therefore exceeds the helium value, and the difference estimates trapped gas."},{"type":"example","title":"Helium dilution and trapped gas","text":"A spirometer holds 3.0 L of gas with 10% helium. The patient, a heavy smoker, is connected at the end of a quiet expiration and breathes until the helium reads 5.0%. Helium is conserved: 0.10 x 3.0 L = 0.05 x (3.0 L + FRC), so 0.30 = 0.15 + 0.05 FRC, and FRC is 3.0 L. Body plethysmography gives an FRC of 4.0 L. The extra 1.0 L is gas in poorly communicating regions, such as bullae, that helium never reached. If ERV is 0.8 L, plethysmographic RV is 3.2 L, well above normal, which is the air trapping expected in emphysema."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-06-01-C02","obj":"PU-6.2","title":"Minute ventilation, alveolar ventilation, and dead space","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Minute ventilation (VE) is the total gas moved per minute: tidal volume times respiratory rate, about 500 mL x 12 = 6 L/min at rest. Not all of it reaches gas exchange. At the end of each expiration the conducting airways are full of alveolar gas, and the first part of the next breath simply pushes that stale gas back into the alveoli. The last part of each inspiration stays in the conducting airways and is exhaled unchanged. This anatomic dead space is about 150 mL in an adult (often estimated as about 1 mL per pound of ideal body weight), so alveolar ventilation (VA) is (TV minus VD) x rate, about (500 minus 150) x 12 = 4.2 L/min."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiologic dead space adds alveolar dead space, alveoli that are ventilated but not perfused. Normally alveolar dead space is negligible, so physiologic and anatomic dead space nearly match, but in pulmonary embolism or emphysema alveolar dead space grows. The Bohr method measures total physiologic dead space. Dead space gas contributes essentially no CO2, so it dilutes the CO2 coming from perfused alveoli. Using arterial PCO2 as the estimate of alveolar PCO2: VD/VT = (PaCO2 minus PECO2) / PaCO2, where PECO2 is the PCO2 of mixed expired gas. With PaCO2 40 mmHg and PECO2 28 mmHg, VD/VT is 12/40 = 0.30, the normal value of about 0.2 to 0.35."},{"type":"p","text":"Alveolar ventilation, not minute ventilation, sets PaCO2. The alveolar ventilation equation states that PaCO2 is proportional to CO2 production divided by alveolar ventilation (PaCO2 = VCO2 x 0.863 / VA, with VCO2 in mL/min and VA in L/min). At a steady CO2 production of about 200 mL/min, halving VA roughly doubles PaCO2. Hypoventilation is defined by a raised PaCO2, and hyperventilation by a low one, regardless of how fast or deep the breathing looks."},{"type":"table","title":"Same minute ventilation, different alveolar ventilation","cols":["Pattern","TV x rate","Minute ventilation","Alveolar ventilation (VD 150 mL)"],"rows":[["Normal","500 mL x 12","6.0 L/min","4.2 L/min"],["Slow and deep","1000 mL x 6","6.0 L/min","5.1 L/min"],["Rapid and shallow","250 mL x 24","6.0 L/min","2.4 L/min"]],"caption":"Dead space is paid once per breath, so small fast breaths waste a larger fraction of every breath."},{"type":"p","text":"Anatomic dead space is fixed by airway size, so each breath pays the same 150 mL cost. At a tidal volume of 250 mL, 60% of each breath is dead space, and alveolar ventilation falls from 4.2 to 2.4 L/min even though minute ventilation is unchanged. If CO2 production is constant, PaCO2 rises toward about 70 mmHg. This explains why patients with fatiguing respiratory muscles, opioid toxicity, or stiff lungs that force small tidal volumes can retain CO2 while appearing to breathe quickly."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient's PaCO2 is 40 mmHg and mixed expired PCO2 falls from 28 to 20 mmHg after a pulmonary embolus, with no change in tidal volume or rate. What happened to VD/VT, and what will PaCO2 do if ventilation stays the same?","explain":"VD/VT rises from 0.30 to 0.50 because embolized alveoli are ventilated but unperfused and add CO2-free gas to the exhalate. Alveolar ventilation of perfused units falls, so PaCO2 would rise unless total ventilation increases, which in practice it usually does.","check":"The Bohr equation and the alveolar ventilation equation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-06-01-C03","obj":"PU-6.3","title":"Compliance, elastic recoil, intrapleural pressure, and surfactant","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The lung and the chest wall are two elastic structures coupled by the thin pleural fluid layer. The lung always tends to recoil inward, toward a small collapsed volume. The chest wall, at volumes below about two thirds of TLC, tends to spring outward. Compliance (delta V divided by delta P) describes how easily each is stretched: the normal lung alone is about 200 mL/cmH2O, and lung plus chest wall together is about 110 mL/cmH2O, because two springs in series are stiffer than either alone."},{"type":"p","text":"FRC is the volume at which these opposite recoils are equal, so the respiratory muscles are relaxed. The pleural space, pulled from both sides, sits below atmospheric pressure, about -5 cmH2O at FRC. Transpulmonary pressure (alveolar minus pleural pressure) is therefore about +5 cmH2O, which holds the lung open. During quiet inspiration the diaphragm makes pleural pressure more negative, to about -7.5 cmH2O, alveolar pressure falls slightly below atmospheric, and air flows in. Quiet expiration is passive recoil. If air enters the pleural space (pneumothorax), pleural pressure becomes atmospheric, the coupling is lost, the lung collapses inward, and the chest wall on that side springs outward."},{"type":"table","title":"Conditions that change compliance","cols":["Change","Example","Effect on FRC"],"rows":[["Lung compliance increased","Emphysema (elastin destroyed), aging","FRC rises; chest becomes barrel shaped"],["Lung compliance decreased","Pulmonary fibrosis, pulmonary edema, surfactant deficiency","FRC falls"],["Chest wall compliance decreased","Obesity, kyphoscoliosis","FRC falls"]],"caption":"FRC moves to the new volume where the inward lung recoil again equals the outward chest wall recoil."},{"type":"p","text":"About two thirds of the lung's recoil comes not from tissue elastin but from surface tension at the air-liquid interface of the alveoli. This is shown by the classic observation that a saline-filled lung inflates at much lower pressure than an air-filled one. The law of Laplace for a sphere states that the pressure needed to keep it open is P = 2T/r, where T is surface tension and r is radius. If all alveoli had the same surface tension, a small alveolus would have a higher internal pressure than a large neighbor and would empty into it, and the lung would trend toward collapse of small units (atelectasis)."},{"type":"p","text":"Type II pneumocytes secrete surfactant from lamellar bodies. It is mostly phospholipid, chiefly dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine, with surfactant proteins (SP-A, SP-B, SP-C, SP-D) that help it spread, recycle, and defend the alveolus. Surfactant lowers surface tension from about 72 dynes/cm for pure water to about 5 to 30 dynes/cm. Its key property is that as an alveolus shrinks, the surfactant molecules are compressed into a denser film and surface tension falls further, so T falls along with r and small alveoli are stabilized. Lower surface tension also increases compliance, reducing the work of breathing, and reduces the inward pull on interstitial fluid that would otherwise draw fluid into the alveoli. Deficient surfactant, as in the premature infant, produces stiff lungs, widespread atelectasis, and alveolar edema (neonatal respiratory distress syndrome)."},{"type":"flow","title":"Surfactant deficiency and lung failure","steps":["Immature type II pneumocytes secrete too little surfactant","Surface tension stays high, especially in small alveoli","Laplace: small alveoli collapse into larger ones","Compliance falls; work of breathing rises","Atelectasis creates shunt; hypoxemia worsens","High surface tension draws fluid into alveoli"],"caption":"Laplace physics links surfactant deficiency to collapse, stiffness, shunt, and edema."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-06-01-C04","obj":"PU-6.4","title":"Spirometry and flow-volume loops: obstructive, restrictive, and upper airway patterns","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In forced spirometry the subject inhales to TLC and exhales as hard and fast as possible to RV. The forced vital capacity (FVC) is the total volume exhaled, FEV1 is the volume exhaled in the first second, and their ratio shows whether flow is limited out of proportion to volume. A healthy young adult exhales about 75% to 85% of the FVC in the first second."},{"type":"p","text":"After the first part of a forced expiration, flow becomes effort independent. Rising pleural pressure squeezes the alveoli and drives air out, but it also compresses the airways. Along the airway, pressure falls from alveolus to mouth, and where airway pressure equals pleural pressure (the equal pressure point) the airway downstream is compressed, so pushing harder does not raise flow. Anything that reduces elastic recoil (emphysema), narrows the lumen (asthma, chronic bronchitis), or weakens the radial traction that tethers airways open moves the equal pressure point toward the alveoli so flow becomes limited over more of the expiration."},{"type":"p","text":"Obstructive disease therefore lowers FEV1 much more than FVC, so FEV1/FVC falls below about 0.70. Airways close early during expiration, trapping gas, so RV rises and TLC is normal or increased (hyperinflation). On the flow-volume loop the expiratory limb is scooped (concave) because flow falls disproportionately as volume declines, and the loop is shifted toward higher volumes. Restrictive disease reduces how much the lung can hold, not how fast it empties. FEV1 and FVC fall together, so the ratio is normal or high. In fibrosis the ratio may exceed normal because increased radial traction from stiff tissue holds airways open and strong recoil drives flow. TLC is the defining measure and is reduced. The loop is narrow, shifted to lower volumes, with expiratory flow near or above normal for the volume."},{"type":"table","title":"Spirometry and lung volume patterns","cols":["Measure","Obstructive","Restrictive (parenchymal)","Restrictive (chest wall or neuromuscular)"],"rows":[["FEV1","Low","Low","Low"],["FVC","Normal or low","Low","Low"],["FEV1/FVC","Below about 0.70","Normal or high","Normal or high"],["TLC","Normal or high","Low","Low"],["RV","High (air trapping)","Low","Normal or high if expiratory muscles are weak"],["Loop shape","Scooped expiratory limb, shifted to high volume","Narrow, shifted to low volume","Narrow; peak flow may be reduced by weakness"],["Examples","COPD, asthma, bronchiectasis","Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, sarcoidosis, pneumoconioses","Obesity, kyphoscoliosis, myasthenia gravis, ALS"]],"caption":"The ratio sorts obstructive from restrictive; TLC confirms restriction. Diffusing capacity (PU-7) separates parenchymal from extrapulmonary restriction."},{"type":"p","text":"Large airway lesions are sorted by whether the narrowing changes with the phase of breathing. A fixed obstruction, such as tracheal stenosis after intubation or a rigid tumor, cannot change caliber, so both inspiratory and expiratory limbs are flattened into plateaus. A variable lesion moves with transmural pressure. Outside the thorax, airway pressure falls below atmospheric during forced inspiration, so a variable extrathoracic lesion (vocal fold paralysis, laryngeal tumor) collapses inward and flattens the inspiratory limb. Inside the thorax, pleural pressure surrounds the airway, rises above airway pressure during forced expiration, and compresses a variable intrathoracic lesion (tracheomalacia, a tumor of the lower trachea), flattening the expiratory limb."},{"type":"table","title":"Upper airway obstruction on the flow-volume loop","cols":["Lesion","Inspiratory limb","Expiratory limb","Reason"],"rows":[["Fixed (either location)","Flattened","Flattened","Caliber cannot change with pressure"],["Variable extrathoracic","Flattened","Normal","Airway pressure below atmospheric during inspiration"],["Variable intrathoracic","Normal","Flattened","Pleural pressure exceeds airway pressure during expiration"]],"caption":"Large airway lesions may leave FEV1/FVC only mildly affected, so the loop shape, not the ratio, identifies them."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient has FEV1 1.8 L, FVC 2.0 L, and TLC 60% of predicted. Is this obstruction?","explain":"No. FEV1/FVC is 0.90, which is normal or high, and TLC is reduced, so this is restriction. A low FEV1 alone means little until it is compared with FVC and TLC.","check":"The spirometry pattern table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-06-01-C05","obj":"PU-6.5","title":"Brainstem control of breathing and the Hering-Breuer reflex","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Breathing continues during sleep and anesthesia because its rhythm is generated automatically in the brainstem. The medulla contains the rhythm generator and the main premotor neurons; the pons and vagal afferents shape the timing of each breath; and the cortex can override the system for speech, breath holding, or voluntary hyperventilation through corticospinal pathways that bypass the medulla."},{"type":"p","text":"The dorsal respiratory group (DRG) lies in the dorsal medulla within the nucleus tractus solitarius. It is mainly inspiratory and receives visceral afferents from the glossopharyngeal (CN IX) and vagus (CN X) nerves, including chemoreceptor and lung stretch input. During quiet breathing its output is a ramp: inspiratory neuron firing rises steadily for about 2 seconds, so the diaphragm contracts smoothly through the phrenic nerve (C3 to C5), then stops abruptly, and recoil carries out expiration for about 3 seconds. The ventral respiratory group (VRG), in the ventrolateral medulla around the nucleus ambiguus, contains both inspiratory and expiratory neurons. It is nearly silent during quiet breathing, when expiration is passive, but it is recruited when ventilation must rise, driving accessory inspiratory muscles and the internal intercostal and abdominal muscles of active expiration. The VRG includes the pre-Botzinger complex, which most current physiology texts identify as the core rhythm generator."},{"type":"p","text":"The pneumotaxic center (pontine respiratory group) in the upper pons sends signals that switch off the inspiratory ramp. Strong pneumotaxic output shortens inspiration and therefore increases rate; weak output lengthens each breath. The apneustic center in the lower pons does the opposite, promoting prolonged inspiration. Normally the pneumotaxic center and vagal stretch input override it; when both are removed, the result is apneusis, long sustained inspiratory gasps with brief expirations."},{"type":"table","title":"Breathing after brainstem transection or lesion","cols":["Level of section","Remaining control","Breathing pattern"],"rows":[["Above the pons","Medulla and entire pons","Normal automatic rhythm"],["Upper pons, vagi intact","Apneustic center, medulla, vagal reflex","Slower, deeper breaths"],["Upper pons, vagi cut","Apneustic center and medulla only","Apneusis"],["Pontomedullary junction","Medulla only","Rhythmic but irregular breathing persists"],["Below the medulla (high cervical cord)","Spinal cord only","Apnea"]],"caption":"The medulla can generate rhythm on its own; the pons and vagus refine it."},{"type":"p","text":"The Hering-Breuer inflation reflex begins in slowly adapting stretch receptors in the smooth muscle of bronchi and bronchioles. Inflation stretches them, and their signals travel in the vagus to the DRG, where they act as an inspiratory off-switch, like the pneumotaxic center. In adults the reflex is weak during quiet breathing and becomes active only when tidal volume exceeds about 1.5 L, about three times normal, so it mainly protects against overinflation. It is more prominent in newborns."},{"type":"flow","title":"Hering-Breuer inflation reflex","steps":["Large inspiration stretches the airways","Slowly adapting stretch receptors fire","Vagal afferents reach the dorsal respiratory group","Inspiratory ramp switches off early","Expiration begins; overinflation is prevented"],"caption":"The reflex shares its output, earlier termination of inspiration, with the pneumotaxic center."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-06-01-C06","obj":"PU-6.6","title":"Central and peripheral chemoreceptors and the response to hypercapnia and hypoxemia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Ventilation is adjusted mainly to hold PaCO2 near 40 mmHg, with PaO2 as a backup signal, and the two sets of chemoreceptors sense different things. Central chemoreceptors lie just beneath the ventral surface of the medulla, bathed in brain interstitial fluid and CSF. They respond to H+, not directly to CO2, but in practice they track arterial PCO2. The blood-brain barrier is relatively impermeable to H+ and HCO3- but CO2 crosses it freely. In CSF, CO2 combines with water to form carbonic acid, and because CSF contains little protein its buffering is weak, so a small rise in PaCO2 produces a relatively large fall in CSF pH. They produce most of the steady-state response to CO2, and ventilation rises steeply and nearly linearly as PaCO2 climbs above about 40 mmHg."},{"type":"flow","title":"Central chemoreceptor response to hypercapnia","steps":["PaCO2 rises","CO2 diffuses across the blood-brain barrier","CO2 + H2O forms H+ and HCO3- in poorly buffered CSF","CSF pH falls; central chemoreceptors fire","Respiratory centers increase rate and depth","Alveolar ventilation rises; PaCO2 returns toward 40 mmHg"],"caption":"Arterial H+ crosses the barrier poorly, so the central response follows CO2."},{"type":"p","text":"Peripheral chemoreceptors are the carotid bodies at the carotid bifurcations, whose afferents run in the carotid sinus nerve of CN IX, and the aortic bodies along the aortic arch, served by CN X. Their glomus (type I) cells contain O2-sensitive K+ channels. When PO2 falls these channels close, the cell depolarizes, voltage-gated Ca2+ channels open, and transmitters (including dopamine and ATP) are released onto afferent endings. Carotid body blood flow is extremely high relative to its metabolism, so the receptor senses dissolved O2, the PaO2, not O2 content. Anemia and carbon monoxide poisoning lower content while PaO2 stays normal, so they stimulate these receptors little."},{"type":"p","text":"Peripheral receptor firing increases only modestly until PaO2 falls below about 60 mmHg, then rises steeply. This matches the oxyhemoglobin curve, whose flat upper portion keeps saturation at about 90% or more until PaO2 falls to about 60 mmHg. The carotid bodies also respond quickly to rises in PaCO2 and arterial H+, and they are the main sensors of arterial H+, so they drive the immediate hyperventilation of metabolic acidosis. Hypoxemia and hypercapnia act synergistically: low PaO2 steepens the CO2 response. The ventilatory response to hypoxemia alone is braked because hyperventilation lowers PaCO2 and raises pH, removing central drive."},{"type":"table","title":"Central versus peripheral chemoreceptors","cols":["Feature","Central","Peripheral"],"rows":[["Location","Ventral surface of the medulla","Carotid bodies (CN IX); aortic bodies (CN X)"],["Primary stimulus","CSF H+ generated from arterial CO2","PaO2 below about 60 mmHg"],["Other stimuli","None of clinical importance","Rising PaCO2; arterial H+ (mainly carotid)"],["Anemia or CO poisoning","No response","Little response, because PaO2 is normal"],["Chronic hypercapnia","Adapts as CSF HCO3- rises","Does not adapt; becomes a larger share of drive"]],"caption":"Central receptors guard PaCO2; peripheral receptors guard PaO2 and arterial pH."},{"type":"p","text":"With sustained hypercapnia, as in severe COPD, the kidneys retain HCO3-, and within 1 to 2 days CSF HCO3- also rises and restores CSF pH. Central drive then weakens at the same PaCO2, and carotid body hypoxic drive becomes a larger share of the stimulus. When such patients breathe high concentrations of O2, PaCO2 can rise, mainly because relief of hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction worsens V/Q matching and the Haldane effect releases CO2 from hemoglobin; loss of hypoxic drive contributes less."},{"type":"example","title":"Predicting the response to two blood gases","text":"Patient A has opioid toxicity: PaCO2 60 mmHg, PaO2 60 mmHg. CSF acidosis should raise ventilation severalfold, but the opioid depresses the medullary centers, so CO2 accumulates. Patient B is at high altitude: PaO2 50 mmHg, PaCO2 normal at first. Carotid bodies fire and ventilation rises, but the resulting fall in PaCO2 raises CSF pH and restrains central drive. Over several days, as renal HCO3- excretion lowers plasma and CSF HCO3-, the brake is released and ventilation climbs further."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-06-01-C07","obj":"PU-6.7","title":"Sleep apnea and obesity hypoventilation, with their cardiopulmonary and hematologic consequences","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Sleep removes the wakefulness drive to breathe, lowers chemoreceptor sensitivity, and reduces tone in the pharyngeal dilator muscles such as the genioglossus. The pharynx has no rigid support, so its patency depends on that tone. An apnea is cessation of airflow for at least 10 seconds; the disorders differ in whether the brain is still trying to breathe."},{"type":"p","text":"In obstructive sleep apnea (OSA), the pharynx collapses during inspiration because negative airway pressure exceeds the reduced dilator tone, and the respiratory muscles keep working against the closed airway. Chest and abdominal effort therefore continue while airflow stops, often with paradoxical motion. Risk factors are those that narrow the airway: obesity with fat around the pharynx, a large neck, tonsillar hypertrophy (the main cause in children), retrognathia, and macroglossia, as in hypothyroidism or acromegaly. Each event ends with a brief arousal that restores tone, so sleep is fragmented, causing loud snoring, witnessed apneas, daytime sleepiness, and morning headache from nocturnal CO2 retention. Repeated arousals and hypoxemia cause sympathetic surges that contribute to systemic hypertension."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The obstructive apnea cycle","steps":["Sleep onset lowers pharyngeal dilator tone","Pharynx collapses; effort continues without airflow","PaO2 falls and PaCO2 rises","Chemoreceptor drive provokes brief arousal","Dilator tone returns; airway opens; sleep resumes"],"caption":"Hundreds of cycles a night fragment sleep and expose the pulmonary vessels to repeated hypoxemia."},{"type":"p","text":"In central sleep apnea, airflow stops because the medullary drive stops, so there is no chest or abdominal effort during the pause. Causes include opioids, brainstem lesions, and heart failure. In heart failure, Cheyne-Stokes breathing results from an unstable feedback loop: prolonged circulation time delays the arrival of changed blood gases at the chemoreceptors, and heightened CO2 sensitivity makes the response overshoot, so hyperventilation drives PaCO2 below the apneic threshold, breathing stops, CO2 accumulates, and the cycle waxes again."},{"type":"p","text":"Obesity hypoventilation syndrome (OHS) is defined by BMI 30 kg/m2 or more and awake daytime PaCO2 above 45 mmHg, after other causes of hypoventilation are excluded; most patients also have OSA. The heavy chest wall and abdomen lower respiratory system compliance and FRC, increase the work of breathing, and favor small tidal volumes, while the CO2 response is blunted. Nightly CO2 retention raises serum HCO3-, which blunts central drive further and carries hypercapnia into the day. Elevated serum bicarbonate is therefore a typical clue."},{"type":"table","title":"Sleep-related breathing disorders","cols":["Feature","Obstructive sleep apnea","Central sleep apnea","Obesity hypoventilation"],"rows":[["Respiratory effort during events","Present","Absent","Reduced; hypoventilation persists while awake"],["Core mechanism","Pharyngeal collapse","Loss of medullary drive","Mechanical load plus blunted CO2 response"],["Daytime PaCO2","Usually normal","Normal or low (heart failure)","Above 45 mmHg"],["Typical associations","Obesity, tonsils, retrognathia, hypertension","Heart failure, opioids, brainstem lesion","BMI 30 or more, coexisting OSA, raised HCO3-"]],"caption":"Effort sorts obstructive from central apnea; daytime hypercapnia defines obesity hypoventilation."},{"type":"p","text":"Chronic or repeated hypoxemia affects the pulmonary circulation and the marrow. In pulmonary arteriolar smooth muscle, low alveolar PO2 inhibits K+ channels, depolarizes the cell, and opens Ca2+ channels, causing vasoconstriction; acidosis from hypercapnia enhances it. When hypoxia is global rather than regional, pulmonary vascular resistance rises throughout the lung, and with time the vessels remodel. The right ventricle hypertrophies against the higher afterload (cor pulmonale). Hypoxia also stabilizes hypoxia-inducible factor in renal peritubular interstitial cells, raising erythropoietin and producing secondary polycythemia, which increases blood viscosity and adds further to pulmonary vascular resistance. These complications are most marked in OHS and in OSA with daytime hypoxemia, such as coexisting COPD."},{"type":"flow","title":"From nocturnal hypoxemia to cor pulmonale and polycythemia","steps":["Repeated or sustained alveolar hypoxia","Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction, enhanced by acidosis","Vascular remodeling; pulmonary hypertension","Right ventricular hypertrophy (cor pulmonale)","Renal hypoxia raises erythropoietin","Secondary polycythemia raises viscosity and resistance"],"caption":"Two hypoxia responses that are adaptive locally become harmful when hypoxia is global and repeated."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and physics set the limits of ventilation. The rigid thoracic cage and diaphragm create the outward chest wall recoil, elastin and surface tension create the inward lung recoil, and their balance fixes FRC; type II pneumocytes, which mature late in gestation, supply the surfactant that keeps small alveoli open as Laplace predicts they otherwise would not. Pathology changes these forces in predictable directions: emphysema destroys elastin and traps gas, fibrosis stiffens the lung and shrinks TLC, and obesity loads the chest wall. Spirometry and lung volumes detect the result, but they describe a pattern rather than a diagnosis, and a mixed obstructive and restrictive picture requires full lung volumes to interpret."},{"type":"p","text":"Control of breathing links the lung to the brain, kidney, and blood. Central chemoreceptors respond to CO2 through CSF chemistry, so renal bicarbonate handling resets them in chronic hypercapnia; peripheral receptors respond to PaO2 because of the shape of the oxyhemoglobin curve. Drugs that depress the medulla, such as opioids, and diseases that slow circulation, such as heart failure, therefore change the pattern of breathing, and sustained hypoxemia from any cause recruits the same pulmonary vascular and erythropoietin responses that produce cor pulmonale and polycythemia."}],"summary":["Capacities are sums of volumes; RV, FRC, and TLC cannot be measured by spirometry and need helium dilution or body plethysmography, which also counts trapped gas.","Alveolar ventilation is (TV minus dead space) x rate, and PaCO2 varies inversely with it at constant CO2 production.","The Bohr equation, VD/VT = (PaCO2 minus PECO2) / PaCO2, measures physiologic dead space, normally about 0.3.","Rapid shallow breathing wastes a larger fraction of each breath on fixed dead space, lowering alveolar ventilation and raising PaCO2 at the same minute ventilation.","FRC is where inward lung recoil equals outward chest wall recoil, giving an intrapleural pressure of about -5 cmH2O.","By Laplace (P = 2T/r) small alveoli would collapse; surfactant lowers surface tension most in small alveoli, stabilizing them and raising compliance.","Obstruction: FEV1/FVC below about 0.70 with normal or high TLC and a scooped loop; restriction: normal or high ratio with low TLC.","Fixed upper airway obstruction flattens both limbs; variable extrathoracic flattens inspiration; variable intrathoracic flattens expiration.","The medulla generates rhythm (DRG for inspiration, VRG for forced breathing); the pneumotaxic center and Hering-Breuer reflex end inspiration; the apneustic center prolongs it.","Central chemoreceptors track PaCO2 through CSF H+; carotid bodies respond to PaO2 below about 60 mmHg and to arterial H+; OSA (effort present), central apnea (effort absent), and OHS (daytime PaCO2 above 45 mmHg) lead to pulmonary hypertension and polycythemia through hypoxemia."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pulmonary Ventilation"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Regulation of Respiration"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Respiratory physiology: mechanics of ventilation and control of breathing"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lung"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Pulmonary Disease"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Respiratory System, maturation of the lungs"}]},"PU-7":{"unit":"PU-7","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-07","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-07-01","title":"Gas Exchange and Tissue Perfusion","subject":"Respiratory System (RESP)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows oxygen from inspired air into alveolar gas, across the alveolar-capillary barrier, and out to the tissues. It covers the alveolar gas equation and A-a gradient, the five mechanisms of hypoxemia, the ventilation-perfusion spectrum from dead space to shunt, diffusion and DLCO, the four types of tissue hypoxia, and adaptation to high altitude. Board questions usually give an arterial blood gas, a pulmonary function result, or a setting such as a mountain or a house fire and ask you to locate the failing step. If you can calculate PAO2 and say which step of oxygen delivery is broken, most of these questions become straightforward.","prerequisites":["Dalton law of partial pressures and the composition of air","Minute ventilation, alveolar ventilation, and the PaCO2 relationship (PU-6)","Regional distribution of pulmonary blood flow and ventilation (PU-5)","The oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve and factors that shift it (PU-8)","Basic acid-base physiology: the bicarbonate buffer and renal compensation"],"connections":[{"to":"Blood gas transport (PU-8)","how":"Hemoglobin binding and the shape of the dissociation curve explain why shunt cannot be offset and why carbon monoxide is so harmful."},{"to":"Energy production and the respiratory system (PU-11)","how":"Cyanide blocks cytochrome c oxidase in the electron transport chain, producing histotoxic hypoxia with lactic acidosis."},{"to":"Restrictive and obstructive pulmonary diseases (PU-14-A, PU-14-B)","how":"Fibrosis and emphysema lower DLCO by different mechanisms, and both widen the A-a gradient through V/Q mismatch."},{"to":"Pulmonary vascular disease (PU-14-E)","how":"Pulmonary embolism creates dead space, and chronic hypoxic vasoconstriction at altitude causes pulmonary hypertension."},{"to":"Renal acid-base physiology and hematology","how":"Renal bicarbonate excretion and erythropoietin release are the slow arms of altitude acclimatization."}],"terms":[{"term":"Alveolar gas equation","def":"PAO2 = FiO2 x (Patm minus PH2O) minus PaCO2/R, which predicts alveolar PO2 from inspired O2 and arterial PCO2."},{"term":"A-a gradient","def":"The difference between calculated alveolar PO2 and measured arterial PO2, a measure of how well the lung transfers oxygen."},{"term":"Respiratory quotient (R)","def":"The ratio of CO2 produced to O2 consumed, about 0.8 on a mixed diet."},{"term":"Ventilation-perfusion ratio (V/Q)","def":"The ratio of alveolar ventilation to capillary blood flow in a lung unit or the whole lung, about 0.8 overall."},{"term":"Physiological dead space","def":"The part of each breath that does not take part in gas exchange: anatomic dead space plus alveoli that are ventilated but not perfused."},{"term":"Shunt","def":"Blood that reaches the systemic arteries without passing a ventilated alveolus, so it keeps mixed venous gas tensions."},{"term":"Perfusion-limited exchange","def":"Gas transfer in which the gas equilibrates early in the capillary, so uptake rises only if blood flow rises."},{"term":"Diffusion-limited exchange","def":"Gas transfer in which the gas has not equilibrated by the end of the capillary, so uptake depends on the barrier."},{"term":"DLCO","def":"Diffusing capacity of the lung for carbon monoxide, the uptake of CO per minute per mmHg of driving pressure."},{"term":"Oxygen content (CaO2)","def":"Total oxygen carried per deciliter of blood, bound to hemoglobin plus dissolved, normally about 20 mL/dL."},{"term":"Histotoxic hypoxia","def":"Hypoxia in which oxygen is delivered normally but cells cannot use it, as when cyanide blocks cytochrome c oxidase."},{"term":"Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction","def":"Contraction of pulmonary arterioles in response to low alveolar PO2, which diverts blood away from poorly ventilated alveoli."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-07-01-C01","obj":"PU-7.1","title":"The alveolar gas equation and the A-a gradient","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Arterial PO2 can never exceed alveolar PO2, so the first question in any hypoxemia problem is what the alveolar PO2 should be. Inspired gas is warmed to 37 °C (98.6 °F) and fully humidified in the airways, and water vapor at that temperature exerts 47 mmHg. The gas available for oxygen is therefore barometric pressure minus 47, and inspired PO2 (PIO2) is FiO2 times that value. At sea level on room air, PIO2 = 0.21 x (760 minus 47), or about 150 mmHg. FiO2 is 0.21 at every altitude; only barometric pressure changes."},{"type":"p","text":"In the alveolus, oxygen is continuously removed by capillary blood and CO2 is added. Alveolar PCO2 equals arterial PCO2 for practical purposes because CO2 equilibrates completely, so PaCO2 tells us how much CO2 has diluted the alveolar gas. The body consumes more O2 than it produces CO2 (respiratory quotient about 0.8), so alveolar PO2 falls by more than the PCO2 added: by PaCO2 divided by 0.8. This gives the alveolar gas equation: PAO2 = FiO2 x (Patm minus 47) minus PaCO2/0.8. With a PaCO2 of 40 mmHg, PAO2 is about 150 minus 50, or 100 mmHg. The equation shows two ways to lower alveolar PO2 without any lung disease: lower the inspired PO2 or let PaCO2 rise."},{"type":"p","text":"The A-a gradient is PAO2 minus measured PaO2. Even healthy lungs have a small gradient, because bronchial venous blood enters the pulmonary veins and thebesian veins empty into the left ventricle (a small anatomic shunt) and because V/Q varies from apex to base. On room air the normal gradient is about 5 to 15 mmHg in young adults and widens with age as V/Q matching worsens; a common estimate of the upper limit is age divided by 4, plus 4. Normal PaO2 at sea level is about 80 to 100 mmHg."},{"type":"p","text":"Interpretation follows from the logic of the equation. If the gradient is normal, the lung is transferring oxygen well and the low PaO2 comes from a low PAO2, meaning low inspired O2 or hypoventilation. If the gradient is widened, oxygen is failing to move from alveolus to blood, which means diffusion limitation, V/Q mismatch, or shunt. The simplified equation is most reliable on room air; on high FiO2 the normal gradient itself becomes larger, so gradients measured on supplemental oxygen must be read with care."},{"type":"example","title":"Two hypoxemic patients at sea level on room air","text":"Patient A after an opioid overdose: PaO2 45, PaCO2 80 mmHg. PAO2 = 150 minus 80/0.8 = 150 minus 100 = 50 mmHg. The A-a gradient is 50 minus 45 = 5 mmHg, which is normal, so hypoventilation fully explains the hypoxemia. Patient B with severe COPD: PaO2 50, PaCO2 60 mmHg. PAO2 = 150 minus 75 = 75 mmHg, and the gradient is 25 mmHg, which is widened. Hypoventilation is present, but it cannot account for the whole fall in PaO2; V/Q mismatch within the lung adds to it."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-07-01-C02","obj":"PU-7.2","title":"The five mechanisms of hypoxemia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hypoxemia means a low arterial PO2, generally below about 80 mmHg at sea level. There are five mechanisms, and each breaks a different step between inspired air and arterial blood. Two lower alveolar PO2 itself and leave the gradient normal; three impair transfer within the lung and widen it."},{"type":"p","text":"Low inspired O2, as at altitude, lowers PIO2 and therefore PAO2 and PaO2 in parallel. The hypoxemia stimulates the peripheral chemoreceptors, so PaCO2 is usually low. Hypoventilation from opioids, sedatives, brainstem injury, neuromuscular weakness, or chest wall disease reduces alveolar ventilation; PaCO2 rises in inverse proportion to alveolar ventilation, and by the alveolar gas equation PAO2 falls. A high PaCO2 with a normal gradient is the signature."},{"type":"p","text":"Diffusion limitation occurs when the barrier is thickened or the capillary transit time is too short for equilibration (C04). V/Q mismatch, the most common mechanism in lung disease (COPD, asthma, pneumonia, embolism), mixes blood from low V/Q units, which leave the capillary with low PO2 and saturation, with blood from high V/Q units. Because the oxyhemoglobin curve is flat near the top, the high V/Q units add little extra oxygen, so they cannot balance the desaturated blood. Right-to-left shunt is the extreme of V/Q mismatch, with V/Q of zero (C03), and also includes intracardiac shunts such as an atrial or ventricular septal defect after the pressure gradient reverses."},{"type":"p","text":"Response to supplemental oxygen separates shunt from the other two lung mechanisms. In a low V/Q unit, even a little ventilation with enriched gas gradually washes out nitrogen and raises alveolar PO2 greatly; in diffusion limitation, a higher alveolar PO2 increases the driving gradient across the barrier. In a true shunt, the blood never contacts the added oxygen. The ventilated units add only dissolved oxygen (0.003 mL/dL per mmHg) to blood that is already saturated, so when the shunt fraction exceeds about 30%, PaO2 rises very little even on 100% O2."},{"type":"table","title":"Mechanisms of hypoxemia","cols":["Mechanism","PaCO2","A-a gradient","PaO2 on 100% O2","Examples"],"rows":[["Low inspired O2","Low (hyperventilation)","Normal","Corrects","High altitude"],["Hypoventilation","High","Normal","Corrects (PaCO2 stays high)","Opioid overdose, myasthenic crisis, severe kyphoscoliosis"],["Diffusion limitation","Normal or low","Widened","Corrects","Interstitial fibrosis, especially on exercise"],["V/Q mismatch","Low, normal, or high","Widened","Corrects","COPD, asthma, pulmonary embolism"],["Right-to-left shunt","Normal or low","Widened","Rises little","Lobar consolidation, ARDS, atelectasis, reversed septal defect"]],"caption":"The gradient places the problem inside or outside the lung; the response to oxygen identifies shunt."},{"type":"flow","title":"Sorting hypoxemia from the blood gas","steps":["Confirm low PaO2 and calculate PAO2","Normal A-a gradient: problem is outside the lung","High PaCO2 means hypoventilation; low PaCO2 means low PIO2","Widened A-a gradient: problem is in gas transfer","Corrects with 100% O2: V/Q mismatch or diffusion","Little change with 100% O2: shunt"],"caption":"A widened gradient with a high PaCO2 means lung disease with or without added hypoventilation."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with lobar pneumonia has a PaO2 of 55 mmHg on room air and 65 mmHg on 100% O2. Which mechanism dominates?","explain":"Shunt. Alveoli filled with exudate receive no gas, so the blood passing them keeps mixed venous values no matter what the patient breathes. The small rise comes from dissolved O2 added in the ventilated units and from correction of coexisting low V/Q units.","check":"The paragraph on response to supplemental oxygen and the mechanisms table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-07-01-C03","obj":"PU-7.3","title":"Dead space versus shunt","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Overall alveolar ventilation is about 4 L/min and pulmonary blood flow about 5 L/min, giving a whole-lung V/Q of about 0.8. In the upright lung, blood flow increases toward the base more steeply than ventilation does, so V/Q is about 3 at the apex and about 0.6 at the base. Every lung unit lies somewhere on a spectrum whose two ends are dead space and shunt."},{"type":"p","text":"Dead space is ventilation without perfusion, so V/Q approaches infinity. Alveolar gas in such a unit is never changed by blood and stays close to inspired gas (PO2 about 150, PCO2 near 0 mmHg). Physiological dead space is anatomic dead space (the conducting airways, about 150 mL in an adult) plus alveolar dead space, which is normally negligible. The Bohr equation (Enghoff form) measures it: VD/VT = (PaCO2 minus mixed expired PCO2) divided by PaCO2, normally about 0.2 to 0.35. Pulmonary embolism is the classic cause, and alveolar dead space also rises with low cardiac output and in emphysema."},{"type":"p","text":"Shunt is perfusion without ventilation, so V/Q is zero. Blood leaves the unit unchanged, with mixed venous gas (PO2 about 40, PCO2 about 46 mmHg). Causes are alveoli filled by pus, edema fluid, or blood, collapsed alveoli (atelectasis, as distal to an obstructed bronchus), and anatomic right-to-left shunts."},{"type":"p","text":"The two gases respond differently because their dissociation curves differ. The CO2 curve is nearly linear across the physiological range, so when chemoreceptors raise ventilation, the healthy units unload extra CO2 and keep PaCO2 normal or low despite a shunt. The O2 curve is flat above a PO2 of about 60 mmHg, so hyperventilated units cannot add enough oxygen to make up for shunted blood, and PaO2 stays low."},{"type":"p","text":"In a pure dead space problem, wasted ventilation would raise PaCO2 if total ventilation stayed fixed. In pulmonary embolism, however, pain, hypoxemia, and stimulation of pulmonary receptors make the patient breathe faster, so PaCO2 is usually low. Hypoxemia in embolism comes from blood diverted to the remaining vessels, which overperfuses their alveoli and creates low V/Q units, with atelectasis adding some shunt. Because unperfused alveoli contribute CO2-free gas to the exhaled breath, end-tidal PCO2 falls below PaCO2. The lung partly corrects both extremes locally: hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction diverts blood away from shunt units, and low alveolar PCO2 in unperfused units constricts their airways."},{"type":"table","title":"Dead space and shunt compared","cols":["Feature","Dead space","Shunt"],"rows":[["Defect","Ventilated, not perfused","Perfused, not ventilated"],["V/Q","Approaches infinity","Zero"],["Gas in the affected unit","Like inspired air (PO2 about 150, PCO2 near 0)","Like mixed venous blood (PO2 about 40, PCO2 about 46)"],["Classic causes","Pulmonary embolism, low cardiac output","Lobar pneumonia, pulmonary edema, atelectasis, airway obstruction"],["PaO2","Falls from overperfusion of the remaining units","Falls"],["PaCO2","Would rise, but tachypnea usually makes it low","Normal or low"],["Response to 100% O2","Improves","Little change"]],"caption":"Both ends of the V/Q spectrum lower PaO2; neither usually raises PaCO2, because the patient increases ventilation."},{"type":"stop","predict":"If a patient with a large pulmonary embolism could not increase total ventilation (for example, during fixed mechanical ventilation), what would happen to PaCO2?","explain":"It would rise. Part of each breath now ventilates unperfused alveoli, so alveolar ventilation of perfused units falls. The usual low PaCO2 in embolism reflects the compensating tachypnea, not the dead space itself.","check":"The paragraph on dead space and PaCO2 and the comparison table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-07-01-C04","obj":"PU-7.4","title":"Diffusion, perfusion limitation, and DLCO","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Gases cross the alveolar-capillary barrier by passive diffusion. Fick law states that the rate of transfer equals (A/T) x D x (P1 minus P2), where A is surface area, T is barrier thickness, P1 minus P2 is the partial pressure difference, and D is a diffusion constant proportional to solubility divided by the square root of molecular weight. The barrier is very thin (well under 1 micrometer in most places) and its area is large (about 50 to 100 square meters). CO2 is about 20 times more diffusible than O2, mainly because it is far more soluble, so CO2 transfer is rarely limited by diffusion."},{"type":"p","text":"Whether uptake of a gas depends on the barrier or on blood flow is decided by how quickly its partial pressure in capillary blood rises toward the alveolar value. Nitrous oxide does not bind hemoglobin, so its plasma partial pressure rises almost immediately and equilibrates within the first tenth of the capillary length; after that no more crosses, and uptake can rise only if more blood flows through. This is perfusion limitation. Carbon monoxide binds hemoglobin so avidly that its plasma partial pressure stays near zero along the whole capillary, so the gradient never collapses and uptake is set by the barrier. This is diffusion limitation."},{"type":"p","text":"Oxygen normally behaves like a perfusion-limited gas. At rest a red cell spends about 0.75 s in the pulmonary capillary, and capillary PO2 reaches alveolar PO2 in about 0.25 s, leaving a large reserve. During heavy exercise transit time can fall to about a third of that, yet a healthy lung still equilibrates. Oxygen becomes diffusion-limited when the reserve is used up: a thickened barrier slows the rate of rise, a low alveolar PO2 at altitude shrinks the driving gradient, and exercise shortens the time available. This is why fibrosis patients and healthy climbers at high altitude desaturate on exertion though they may be adequately saturated at rest."},{"type":"p","text":"DLCO uses a small, safe concentration of CO to measure the transfer capacity of the barrier, normally about 25 mL/min/mmHg. It depends on the membrane and on the volume of capillary blood and hemoglobin available to bind CO, so it is reported corrected for hemoglobin. In emphysema, destruction of alveolar walls removes surface area and capillaries, so DLCO falls; in chronic bronchitis and asthma the alveolar walls are intact, so DLCO is normal (sometimes high in asthma). Interstitial fibrosis lowers DLCO by thickening the barrier and destroying capillaries, while pulmonary vascular disease lowers it by reducing capillary blood volume. Polycythemia and alveolar hemorrhage raise it because extra hemoglobin binds CO."},{"type":"table","title":"Perfusion-limited and diffusion-limited exchange","cols":["Feature","Perfusion-limited","Diffusion-limited"],"rows":[["Binding in blood","None (N2O) or sites fill quickly (O2 at rest)","Strong binding holds plasma partial pressure low (CO)"],["Equilibration","Early in the capillary","Not reached by the end of the capillary"],["Uptake raised by","More blood flow","More area, thinner barrier, larger gradient"],["Examples","N2O; O2 and CO2 in healthy lungs","CO; O2 in fibrosis or strenuous exercise at altitude"]],"caption":"The same gas can switch category when disease, altitude, or exercise removes the equilibration reserve."},{"type":"table","title":"DLCO in common disorders","cols":["Disorder","DLCO","Reason"],"rows":[["Emphysema","Low","Loss of alveolar walls and capillary surface area"],["Interstitial fibrosis","Low","Thickened barrier and loss of capillaries"],["Pulmonary embolism or pulmonary hypertension","Low","Reduced capillary blood volume"],["Anemia (uncorrected value)","Low","Less hemoglobin to bind CO"],["Chronic bronchitis, asthma","Normal or high","Alveolar walls intact; disease is in the airways"],["Polycythemia, alveolar hemorrhage","High","Extra hemoglobin in or near the alveoli binds CO"]],"caption":"DLCO separates emphysema from chronic bronchitis and asthma, and fibrosis from extrapulmonary restriction such as obesity or neuromuscular weakness."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-07-01-C05","obj":"PU-7.5","title":"Hypoxemia versus tissue hypoxia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hypoxemia is a low PaO2 in arterial blood. Hypoxia is an inadequate supply or use of oxygen in tissue. They often occur together but can be separated, because tissues depend on oxygen delivery, which is cardiac output times arterial oxygen content (about 5 L/min x 200 mL/L, or about 1,000 mL/min at rest)."},{"type":"p","text":"Oxygen content is CaO2 = (1.34 x Hb x SaO2) + (0.003 x PaO2), in mL O2/dL. With Hb 15 g/dL, SaO2 0.98, and PaO2 100 mmHg, bound O2 is about 19.7 and dissolved O2 only 0.3, for about 20 mL/dL. PaO2 reflects only the dissolved fraction, while content depends mostly on how much hemoglobin there is and how saturated it is. At rest tissues extract about 5 mL/dL (about 25%), so mixed venous blood returns with PO2 about 40 mmHg and saturation about 75%."},{"type":"p","text":"Hypoxemic hypoxia lowers PaO2, SaO2, and content together. In anemic hypoxia hemoglobin is low, so PaO2 and SaO2 are normal but content falls. Carbon monoxide poisoning is a form of anemic hypoxia: CO binds hemoglobin about 200 to 250 times more avidly than O2, so true SaO2 and content fall while PaO2 stays normal; CO also shifts the curve left, so the remaining O2 unloads poorly. Standard two-wavelength pulse oximeters read carboxyhemoglobin as oxyhemoglobin, so only co-oximetry shows the true saturation. Methemoglobin (iron in the ferric state) behaves similarly, and pulse oximetry tends to read about 85% regardless of the true value."},{"type":"p","text":"In circulatory (stagnant) hypoxia, from shock, heart failure, or arterial obstruction, arterial blood is normal but flow is too low. Tissues compensate by extracting more oxygen, so venous O2 is low and the arteriovenous difference widens. In histotoxic hypoxia, cyanide binds the ferric iron of cytochrome a3 in cytochrome c oxidase (complex IV) and stops electron transport. Oxygen is delivered but not used, so venous O2 is abnormally high, the arteriovenous difference narrows, and cells switch to anaerobic glycolysis, producing a high anion gap lactic acidosis. Venous oxygen therefore separates the two types that have normal arterial values."},{"type":"table","title":"Types of hypoxia","cols":["Type","PaO2","SaO2","CaO2","Venous O2","Example"],"rows":[["Hypoxemic","Low","Low","Low","Low","Altitude, lung disease"],["Anemic","Normal","Normal","Low","Low","Iron deficiency anemia"],["Carbon monoxide","Normal","Low by co-oximetry; pulse oximetry falsely normal","Low","Low","Smoke inhalation, faulty heater"],["Circulatory","Normal","Normal","Normal","Low","Cardiogenic shock, arterial occlusion"],["Histotoxic","Normal","Normal","Normal","High","Cyanide poisoning"]],"caption":"Ask which of PaO2, saturation, and content is low; if none is, venous O2 separates circulatory from histotoxic hypoxia."},{"type":"example","title":"Why anemia lowers content more than moderate hypoxemia","text":"Patient A has Hb 15 g/dL and PaO2 40 mmHg with SaO2 75%. Content = 1.34 x 15 x 0.75 + 0.003 x 40 = 15.1 + 0.1, about 15.2 mL/dL. Patient B has Hb 7.5 g/dL, PaO2 95 mmHg, and SaO2 98%. Content = 1.34 x 7.5 x 0.98 + 0.003 x 95 = 9.8 + 0.3, about 10.1 mL/dL. Patient B has a normal blood gas but about a third less oxygen per deciliter than patient A. A patient with Hb 15 and 50% carboxyhemoglobin has a content near 10 mL/dL like patient B, but is worse off because the left shift also impairs unloading."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-07-01-C06","obj":"PU-7.6","title":"Acclimatization to high altitude and altitude illness","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Barometric pressure falls with altitude while FiO2 stays 0.21. At about 3,000 m, pressure is about 525 mmHg, so PIO2 is about 0.21 x (525 minus 47), or about 100 mmHg, and PAO2 falls to roughly 60 mmHg or less, depending on how much the person hyperventilates. This is hypoxemia from low inspired O2 with a normal A-a gradient. Each acclimatization response improves oxygen delivery but carries a cost."},{"type":"p","text":"Ventilation rises within minutes because the carotid bodies respond sharply once PaO2 falls below about 60 mmHg. The fall in PaCO2 produces respiratory alkalosis, and the alkalosis and low PCO2 brake the central chemoreceptors, which limits the first rise. Over the next several days the kidneys excrete bicarbonate by reducing H+ secretion and bicarbonate reabsorption, and bicarbonate in cerebrospinal fluid also falls. As pH returns toward normal, the brake is released and ventilation climbs further; carotid body sensitivity to hypoxia also increases. Carbonic anhydrase inhibition illustrates the mechanism, since the bicarbonate diuresis it causes produces a metabolic acidosis that reproduces this renal step in advance."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Ventilatory acclimatization","steps":["Low PaO2 stimulates the carotid bodies","Ventilation rises; PaCO2 falls; pH rises","Alkalosis brakes the central chemoreceptors","Kidneys excrete bicarbonate over several days","pH normalizes; brake released; ventilation rises further"],"caption":"Renal compensation for respiratory alkalosis is what allows ventilation to reach its acclimatized level."},{"type":"p","text":"Hypoxia stabilizes hypoxia-inducible factor (its prolyl hydroxylases need O2 to mark it for degradation), so renal interstitial cells release erythropoietin within hours, and red cell mass rises over weeks. Hematocrit rises sooner from plasma volume contraction. Red cell 2,3-BPG rises, shifting the curve to the right and favoring unloading. Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction, in which low alveolar PO2 inhibits K+ channels in pulmonary arterial smooth muscle and causes depolarization and Ca2+ entry, becomes generalized at altitude. Pulmonary artery pressure rises, and over months to years right ventricular hypertrophy can follow."},{"type":"p","text":"Acute mountain sickness begins about 6 to 12 hours after a fast ascent, usually above about 2,500 m, with headache, nausea, fatigue, dizziness, and poor sleep. Hypoxic cerebral vasodilation raises cerebral blood flow and capillary permeability. High altitude cerebral edema is the severe end of the same process, with vasogenic edema causing ataxia and confusion that can progress to coma. High altitude pulmonary edema usually appears 2 to 4 days after arrival. Vasoconstriction is uneven, so the less constricted capillaries receive high pressure and flow, their walls undergo stress failure, and protein- and red cell-rich fluid floods the alveoli. Left atrial pressure is normal, so this is noncardiogenic edema. Dyspnea at rest, cough, and a widened A-a gradient follow, because flooded alveoli now add shunt."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of high altitude pulmonary edema","steps":["Low alveolar PO2 throughout the lung","Exaggerated and uneven hypoxic vasoconstriction","High pressure and flow in unconstricted capillaries","Stress failure of the capillary wall","Protein-rich fluid and red cells fill alveoli","Shunt widens the A-a gradient; hypoxemia worsens"],"caption":"People with a strong pulmonary pressor response to hypoxia are most susceptible."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A climber at 4,000 m has a PaO2 of 45 mmHg and a normal A-a gradient on day 1. On day 3 PaO2 is 35 mmHg with a widened gradient. What changed?","explain":"Day 1 hypoxemia is explained by low inspired O2 alone. A newly widened gradient means the lung itself now fails to transfer oxygen, most likely from high altitude pulmonary edema creating shunt and low V/Q units.","check":"The altitude illness paragraph and the A-a gradient logic in C01."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Oxygen delivery is a chain, and each discipline explains a different link. Anatomy sets the area and thickness of the barrier and the small normal shunt through bronchial and thebesian veins. Physiology supplies the alveolar gas equation, V/Q matching, and Fick law, which decide alveolar and arterial PO2. Biochemistry explains why hemoglobin, not PaO2, determines content, how CO and methemoglobin disable it, and how cyanide stops oxygen use at complex IV. Pathology maps diseases onto the chain: emphysema removes surface area, fibrosis thickens the barrier, pneumonia and edema create shunt, and embolism creates dead space."},{"type":"p","text":"The working method is to locate the failing step. A low PaO2 with a normal gradient points before the alveolus; a widened gradient points to the alveolar-capillary unit, and the response to oxygen separates shunt from V/Q mismatch and diffusion. A normal PaO2 with hypoxia points to hemoglobin, flow, or the mitochondria. The limits are real: reference values vary with age and source, real patients usually have mixed mechanisms, and the A-a gradient is least reliable on high FiO2."}],"summary":["PAO2 = FiO2 x (Patm minus 47) minus PaCO2/0.8, about 100 mmHg at sea level on room air; normal A-a gradient is about 5 to 15 mmHg and widens with age.","Hypoxemia with a normal gradient comes from low inspired O2 (low PaCO2) or hypoventilation (high PaCO2); a widened gradient means diffusion limitation, V/Q mismatch, or shunt.","V/Q mismatch is the most common mechanism; shunt is the one that barely improves on 100% O2 because shunted blood never meets the added oxygen.","Dead space (V/Q approaching infinity, classically embolism) and shunt (V/Q of zero, as in consolidation or edema) both lower PaO2, while PaCO2 is usually normal or low because ventilation rises.","The flat top of the O2 curve prevents healthy units from offsetting shunt; the nearly linear CO2 curve lets them unload extra CO2.","N2O and resting O2 are perfusion-limited; CO is diffusion-limited, and O2 becomes diffusion-limited in fibrosis or with exercise at altitude.","DLCO is low in emphysema, fibrosis, and pulmonary vascular disease, and normal in chronic bronchitis and asthma.","CaO2 = 1.34 x Hb x SaO2 + 0.003 x PaO2; anemia and CO lower content with a normal PaO2, and pulse oximetry misses CO.","Circulatory hypoxia has low venous O2 from increased extraction; cyanide gives high venous O2 and lactic acidosis.","Altitude acclimatization: hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis, renal bicarbonate excretion, more EPO and 2,3-BPG, and pulmonary vasoconstriction; uneven vasoconstriction causes HAPE."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Principles of Gas Exchange; Diffusion of Oxygen and Carbon Dioxide Through the Respiratory Membrane"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Transport of Oxygen and Carbon Dioxide in Blood and Tissue Fluids"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Aviation, High Altitude, and Space Physiology"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Respiratory system: gas exchange, ventilation-perfusion matching, and high altitude"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Electron transport chain and oxidative phosphorylation, inhibitors of cytochrome c oxidase"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Pulmonary Disease"}]},"PU-8":{"unit":"PU-8","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-08","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-08-01","title":"Blood Gas Transport","subject":"Respiratory System (RESP)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how blood carries oxygen and carbon dioxide between the lungs and the tissues and how breathing sets arterial acid-base status. It starts with oxygen content and the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve, follows CO2 as bicarbonate, carbamino compounds, and dissolved gas, and shows how the Bohr and Haldane effects link the two gases. It then applies these ideas to carbon monoxide poisoning and methemoglobinemia, where PaO2 is normal yet tissues are hypoxic, and to arterial blood gases in respiratory acidosis and alkalosis. Board questions test whether you can keep partial pressure, saturation, and content apart, predict curve shifts, and decide whether compensation is appropriate or a second disorder is present.","prerequisites":["Partial pressures, Dalton's law, and Henry's law for gases in solution","Alveolar ventilation and its relation to PaCO2 (PU-6)","Hemoglobin structure: globin chains, heme, and ferrous iron (HE-5)","Buffers and the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation","Red cell glycolysis and the origin of 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate"],"connections":[{"to":"Gas exchange and tissue perfusion (PU-7)","how":"Alveolar diffusion and ventilation-perfusion matching set PaO2, which this chapter converts into saturation, content, and delivery."},{"to":"Hemoglobin and hematologic biochemistry (HE-5)","how":"Globin chain composition, heme iron state, and red cell enzymes determine O2 affinity and explain fetal hemoglobin, methemoglobin, and 2,3-BPG effects."},{"to":"Kidney in acid-base and blood pressure control (UR-7)","how":"Renal H+ secretion, ammoniagenesis, and new bicarbonate generation produce the slow compensation for respiratory acid-base disorders."},{"to":"Energy production and the respiratory system (PU-11)","how":"Carbon monoxide inhibits cytochrome c oxidase, and any failure of O2 delivery shifts cells to anaerobic glycolysis and lactate production."},{"to":"Restrictive and obstructive pulmonary diseases (PU-14-A, PU-14-B)","how":"Diseases that limit ventilation produce the chronic respiratory acidosis whose renal compensation is analyzed here."}],"terms":[{"term":"Oxygen content (CaO2)","def":"The total volume of O2 carried per deciliter of arterial blood, bound to hemoglobin plus dissolved in plasma."},{"term":"Saturation (SaO2)","def":"The percentage of available hemoglobin heme sites in arterial blood that carry O2."},{"term":"P50","def":"The PO2 at which hemoglobin is 50% saturated, normally about 26 to 27 mmHg; a higher P50 means lower affinity."},{"term":"Cooperative binding","def":"The property by which O2 binding at one heme of hemoglobin increases the O2 affinity of the remaining hemes."},{"term":"T and R states","def":"The tense (low affinity, favored by deoxyhemoglobin) and relaxed (high affinity, favored by oxyhemoglobin) conformations of hemoglobin."},{"term":"2,3-Bisphosphoglycerate (2,3-BPG)","def":"A red cell glycolytic byproduct that binds deoxyhemoglobin and lowers its O2 affinity."},{"term":"Carbaminohemoglobin","def":"Hemoglobin carrying CO2 bound to the terminal amino groups of its globin chains."},{"term":"Chloride shift","def":"Exchange of red cell bicarbonate for plasma chloride through band 3 as CO2 is loaded, reversed in the lung."},{"term":"Bohr effect","def":"The decrease in hemoglobin O2 affinity caused by a rise in H+ or CO2."},{"term":"Haldane effect","def":"The increase in blood CO2 carrying capacity when hemoglobin is deoxygenated, and the release of CO2 when it is oxygenated."},{"term":"Methemoglobin","def":"Hemoglobin whose heme iron has been oxidized to the ferric (Fe3+) state, which cannot bind O2."},{"term":"Co-oximetry","def":"Multiwavelength measurement of a blood sample that quantifies oxyhemoglobin, deoxyhemoglobin, carboxyhemoglobin, and methemoglobin directly."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-08-01-C01","obj":"PU-8.1","title":"Arterial oxygen content and how anemia, polycythemia, and CO change it","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Oxygen travels in blood in two forms. A small amount is physically dissolved in plasma in proportion to its partial pressure (Henry's law), about 0.003 mL O2 per dL of blood for each mmHg of PO2. Normally about 98% is bound to the ferrous (Fe2+) iron of hemoglobin, and each gram of fully saturated hemoglobin binds about 1.34 mL O2. Arterial O2 content (CaO2, in mL O2 per dL) is therefore CaO2 = (1.34 x Hb x SaO2) + (0.003 x PaO2), with Hb in g/dL and SaO2 as a fraction."},{"type":"p","text":"With Hb 15 g/dL, SaO2 0.98, and PaO2 100 mmHg, bound O2 is about 19.7 mL/dL and dissolved O2 only 0.3 mL/dL, for a total of about 20 mL/dL. Mixed venous blood (PO2 about 40 mmHg, saturation about 75%) holds about 15 mL/dL, so resting tissues extract about 5 mL/dL, roughly a quarter of what is delivered. Breathing 100% O2 raises PaO2 to about 600 mmHg but raises dissolved O2 only to about 1.8 mL/dL, which shows that hemoglobin, not plasma, sets content."},{"type":"h","text":"Pressure, saturation, and content are separate measurements"},{"type":"p","text":"PaO2 is the partial pressure of dissolved O2 in arterial plasma. It reflects alveolar PO2 and how well the lung transfers O2 (diffusion and ventilation-perfusion matching), and it is independent of the amount of hemoglobin. SaO2 is the fraction of available heme sites carrying O2; for normal hemoglobin it follows PaO2 along the dissociation curve. Content depends on both, and O2 delivery equals cardiac output times CaO2, about 5 L/min times 200 mL/L, or about 1,000 mL/min at rest against consumption of about 250 mL/min."},{"type":"p","text":"Anemia lowers content in proportion to the fall in hemoglobin while PaO2 and SaO2 stay normal: the lungs and each hemoglobin molecule work normally, but there is less hemoglobin. The carotid bodies sense PaO2, not content, so anemia does not drive ventilation at rest. Compensation comes from higher cardiac output, greater tissue extraction (lower venous PO2), and a rise in red cell 2,3-BPG that shifts the curve right. Polycythemia raises content with normal PaO2 and SaO2, at the cost of higher viscosity. Carbon monoxide leaves PaO2 normal but occupies heme sites, so true SaO2 and content fall (C05). Only low inspired PO2, hypoventilation, diffusion limitation, ventilation-perfusion mismatch, or shunt lowers PaO2."},{"type":"table","title":"How each condition changes the oxygen numbers","cols":["Condition","PaO2","True SaO2","Pulse oximeter (SpO2)","O2 content"],"rows":[["Anemia","Normal","Normal","Normal","Low"],["Polycythemia","Normal","Normal","Normal","High"],["Carbon monoxide poisoning","Normal","Low","Falsely normal","Low"],["Methemoglobinemia","Normal","Low","Drifts toward about 85%","Low"],["Hypoventilation or high altitude","Low","Low; falls steeply below about 60 mmHg","Low","Low"]],"caption":"Low alveolar PO2 or poor transfer across the lung lowers PaO2; abnormal hemoglobin lowers true SaO2 with a normal PaO2; too little hemoglobin lowers only content."},{"type":"example","title":"Same content, different tissue oxygenation","text":"Patient A has Hb 7.5 g/dL, SaO2 0.98, and PaO2 100 mmHg: CaO2 = (1.34 x 7.5 x 0.98) + 0.3 = 9.8 + 0.3, about 10.1 mL/dL. Patient B has Hb 15 g/dL, PaO2 100 mmHg, and 50% carboxyhemoglobin, so only about 48% of hemes carry O2: CaO2 = (1.34 x 15 x 0.48) + 0.3 = 9.6 + 0.3, about 9.9 mL/dL. Content is nearly identical, yet patient A may be ambulatory while patient B may be comatose. In anemia the curve is normal or right shifted, so tissues can draw O2 down to a low PO2. In CO poisoning the remaining hemes are held in the high-affinity state (left shift), so O2 is released only at a very low tissue PO2, and CO also inhibits cytochrome c oxidase. Content alone does not predict tissue oxygenation; unloading matters too."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-08-01-C02","obj":"PU-8.2","title":"The oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve, cooperativity, and curve shifts","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve plots hemoglobin saturation against PO2. It is sigmoid, unlike the hyperbolic curve of single-chain myoglobin, because hemoglobin binds O2 cooperatively. Adult hemoglobin (HbA) is a tetramer of two alpha and two beta chains, each holding one heme. In the T (tense) state, typical of deoxyhemoglobin, salt bridges between subunits constrain the molecule and O2 affinity is low. Binding of O2 pulls the iron into the plane of the porphyrin ring, and the change spreads to the other subunits as shown below."},{"type":"flow","title":"Cooperative binding from T state to R state","steps":["Deoxyhemoglobin held in the low-affinity T state by salt bridges","First O2 binds one heme with low affinity","Iron moves into the porphyrin plane, pulling the proximal histidine","Movement shifts subunit interfaces and breaks salt bridges","Tetramer converts toward the high-affinity R state","Remaining hemes bind O2 more readily"],"caption":"Each O2 bound makes the next easier to bind, which converts a hyperbolic binding curve into a sigmoid one."},{"type":"p","text":"The shape has a purpose. The upper plateau keeps saturation above about 90% as long as PaO2 stays above about 60 mmHg, so loading in the lung tolerates a moderate fall in alveolar PO2. The steep middle portion, roughly 15 to 40 mmHg, lies at tissue PO2, so a small fall in tissue PO2 releases a large amount of O2. Landmarks are about 97 to 98% saturation at 100 mmHg (arterial), about 75% at 40 mmHg (mixed venous), and 50% at about 26 to 27 mmHg, the P50, which is the standard index of affinity."},{"type":"p","text":"A right shift raises P50, meaning lower affinity and easier unloading; a left shift lowers P50, meaning tighter binding and poorer unloading. Right-shift factors stabilize the T state. H+ protonates histidine residues that then form salt bridges, CO2 forms carbamino groups on the amino terminals of the globin chains, and 2,3-BPG, made in red cells from the glycolytic intermediate 1,3-BPG, binds one molecule per tetramer in the central cavity between the beta chains of deoxyhemoglobin. Heat lowers affinity directly. All of these rise in working muscle, so O2 is released where demand is highest."},{"type":"p","text":"Left shifts occur when the T state is destabilized or the R state is locked in. Fetal hemoglobin (two alpha and two gamma chains) binds 2,3-BPG poorly because the gamma chain lacks a positively charged histidine at the binding site, so its P50 is about 19 to 20 mmHg and O2 moves from maternal to fetal blood across the placenta. CO and methemoglobin hold the remaining hemes in a high-affinity state (C05). Alkalosis, hypothermia, and low 2,3-BPG (stored blood, hypophosphatemia) also shift the curve left. Chronic hypoxemia, anemia, and altitude raise 2,3-BPG within hours, helping unloading."},{"type":"table","title":"Factors that shift the curve","cols":["Direction","Causes","P50","Effect on O2"],"rows":[["Right","More H+ (lower pH), higher PCO2, higher temperature, more 2,3-BPG, exercise","Higher than about 27 mmHg","Lower affinity; more unloading in tissues"],["Left","Fetal hemoglobin, carbon monoxide, methemoglobin, alkalosis, hypothermia, less 2,3-BPG","Lower than about 26 mmHg","Higher affinity; less unloading in tissues"]],"caption":"Right-shift factors stabilize the T state and are all increased by tissue metabolism; left-shift factors favor or lock in the R state."},{"type":"stop","predict":"In pyruvate kinase deficiency the glycolytic block lies below 1,3-BPG. Predict the P50 and why exercise tolerance is often better than the hemoglobin level suggests.","explain":"Upstream intermediates accumulate, so more 1,3-BPG is diverted to 2,3-BPG. The curve shifts right and P50 rises, so tissues extract more O2 from each deciliter. Hexokinase deficiency does the opposite and shifts the curve left.","check":"The role of 2,3-BPG in stabilizing the T state and the shift table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-08-01-C03","obj":"PU-8.3","title":"Carbon dioxide transport and the chloride shift","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Resting metabolism produces about 200 mL CO2 per minute. Mixed venous blood carries about 52 mL CO2 per dL and arterial blood about 48 mL/dL, so each deciliter unloads only about 4 mL in the lung. CO2 is about 20 times more soluble than O2, so dissolved CO2 is a meaningful fraction, but most CO2 is carried only after chemical conversion inside the red cell."},{"type":"table","title":"Forms of CO2 in blood","cols":["Form","Share of CO2 exchanged","Where formed","Key point"],"rows":[["Bicarbonate (HCO3-)","About 70%","Made in red cells by carbonic anhydrase; carried mostly in plasma","Depends on the chloride shift and on H+ buffering by hemoglobin"],["Carbamino compounds","About 20 to 25%","Terminal amino groups of globin chains, and plasma proteins","Deoxyhemoglobin forms more than oxyhemoglobin"],["Dissolved CO2","About 5 to 10%","Plasma and red cell water","Proportional to PCO2; about 0.03 mmol/L per mmHg"]],"caption":"Shares refer to the CO2 added in tissues and removed in the lungs; exact percentages differ slightly between texts."},{"type":"p","text":"Hydration of CO2 to carbonic acid is slow in plasma but very fast in red cells, which contain abundant carbonic anhydrase (mainly the CA II isoform), a zinc enzyme that speeds the reaction several thousandfold. Carbonic acid dissociates almost instantly into H+ and HCO3-. Two processes keep the reaction moving forward. First, hemoglobin buffers the H+, and deoxyhemoglobin is the better buffer because its histidines have a higher pKa. Second, HCO3- leaves the cell through band 3 (anion exchanger 1), an electroneutral exchanger that brings Cl- in, the chloride shift."},{"type":"flow","title":"CO2 loading in a systemic capillary","steps":["Tissue CO2 diffuses into plasma and then red cells","Carbonic anhydrase converts CO2 and H2O to H2CO3","H2CO3 dissociates into H+ and HCO3-","Deoxyhemoglobin buffers the H+, pulling the reaction forward","HCO3- exits through band 3 in exchange for Cl-","HCO3- travels in plasma to the lungs"],"caption":"In the pulmonary capillary every step runs in reverse and CO2 is exhaled."},{"type":"p","text":"Because hemoglobin absorbs most of the H+, venous pH is only about 0.03 to 0.04 units lower than arterial pH despite the CO2 load. The chloride shift has a visible consequence: venous red cells gain Cl- and osmotically active particles, take up water, and swell slightly, so venous hematocrit is about 3% higher than arterial. In the pulmonary capillary Cl- leaves, HCO3- re-enters, combines with H+ released from newly oxygenated hemoglobin, and carbonic anhydrase converts carbonic acid back to CO2, which diffuses into the alveolus. Inhibiting carbonic anhydrase slows both loading and unloading, so tissue PCO2 rises. Carbamino CO2 needs no enzyme; it forms on uncharged amino groups and is released when hemoglobin is oxygenated (C04)."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-08-01-C04","obj":"PU-8.4","title":"The Bohr effect in tissues and the Haldane effect in the lungs","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The Bohr and Haldane effects are two views of one property of hemoglobin: binding of O2 and binding of H+ or CO2 favor opposite conformations. Deoxyhemoglobin (T state) binds H+ and forms carbamino groups more readily, while oxyhemoglobin (R state) releases them. Whichever ligand rises pushes the molecule toward the conformation that releases the other."},{"type":"p","text":"The Bohr effect is the fall in O2 affinity caused by H+ and CO2. In a systemic capillary, CO2 enters the red cell and carbonic anhydrase generates H+, which binds hemoglobin and stabilizes the T state; CO2 also binds directly as carbamino groups. The curve shifts right, so at the same tissue PO2 more O2 is released. Working muscle, with high PCO2, low pH, and high temperature, therefore receives more O2 from each deciliter of blood. In the lung the reverse occurs: CO2 leaves, pH rises, affinity increases, and loading is helped slightly."},{"type":"p","text":"The Haldane effect is the change in CO2 carriage caused by O2. At any PCO2, deoxygenated blood holds more CO2 because deoxyhemoglobin buffers more H+, which lets more HCO3- form, and forms more carbamino compounds. In the pulmonary capillary, O2 loading converts hemoglobin to the R state, which releases H+ and carbamino CO2; the released H+ combines with HCO3- to form CO2, which is exhaled. By Guyton and Hall's estimate, the Haldane effect roughly doubles the CO2 released in the lung, making it quantitatively more important for CO2 transport than the Bohr effect is for O2 transport. In tissues it runs the other way: as O2 unloads, the new deoxyhemoglobin takes up more CO2 and H+."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Reciprocal exchange in a systemic capillary","steps":["Tissue CO2 enters red cell; carbonic anhydrase forms H+","H+ and CO2 bind hemoglobin, stabilizing the T state (Bohr)","O2 is released to the tissue","More deoxyhemoglobin is present","Deoxyhemoglobin takes up more H+ and CO2 (Haldane)"],"caption":"Each gas helps unload the other; in the pulmonary capillary the same loop runs in reverse, with O2 loading driving CO2 release."},{"type":"table","title":"Bohr versus Haldane","cols":["Feature","Bohr effect","Haldane effect"],"rows":[["What changes","H+ and CO2 alter O2 binding","O2 alters H+ and CO2 binding"],["Where it matters most","Systemic capillaries","Pulmonary capillaries"],["Main result","O2 unloading to tissues","CO2 release into alveoli (and uptake in tissues)"],["Curve that moves","O2 dissociation curve shifts right","CO2 dissociation curve shifts down when blood is oxygenated"]],"caption":"Bohr: CO2 and H+ push O2 off hemoglobin. Haldane: O2 pushes CO2 and H+ off hemoglobin."},{"type":"p","text":"The Haldane effect has a practical consequence in chronic hypercapnia. When a patient with severe COPD breathes high inspired O2, PaCO2 can rise; oxygenating venous hemoglobin releases CO2 it had been carrying, and relief of hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction worsens ventilation-perfusion matching, while the fall in hypoxic drive contributes less than once thought."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A red cell enters a capillary in exercising muscle. Predict which way each effect operates there.","explain":"Rising CO2, H+, and temperature lower affinity and unload O2 (Bohr). As hemoglobin deoxygenates, it binds more H+ and carbamino CO2, so the blood loads more CO2 at a given PCO2 (Haldane in reverse).","check":"The cycle figure and the Bohr versus Haldane table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-08-01-C05","obj":"PU-8.5","title":"Carbon monoxide, methemoglobin, and misleading pulse oximetry","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Carbon monoxide (CO) comes from incomplete combustion, such as vehicle exhaust, faulty heaters, and smoke inhalation. It binds the same ferrous heme site as O2 with about 200 to 250 times greater affinity, so an alveolar PCO below 1 mmHg can occupy a large fraction of hemoglobin. CO impairs O2 delivery in three ways: it removes heme sites, so content falls; it holds the remaining subunits in the R state, so the curve shifts left and O2 is not released at normal tissue PO2; and it binds cytochrome c oxidase and myoglobin, impairing O2 use in cells. Brain and heart, with high O2 demand, show early effects: headache, confusion, seizures, myocardial ischemia, and lactic acidosis. Cherry-red skin is classic but uncommon."},{"type":"flow","title":"Carbon monoxide poisoning to tissue hypoxia","steps":["CO binds heme with about 200 times the affinity of O2","Fewer heme sites carry O2, so content falls","Remaining hemes hold O2 tightly (left shift)","Unloading at tissue PO2 falls","Tissue hypoxia with normal PaO2 and normal SpO2","Anaerobic glycolysis raises lactate"],"caption":"Low content and poor unloading combine, and cytochrome c oxidase inhibition adds a cellular defect."},{"type":"p","text":"Methemoglobin contains ferric (Fe3+) iron, which cannot bind O2. Red cells normally keep it below about 1 to 2% by reducing it with NADH-cytochrome b5 reductase, using NADH from glycolysis; an NADPH-dependent reductase is a minor pathway. Oxidizing agents overwhelm this system, including nitrites and nitrates, dapsone, topical anesthetics such as benzocaine, and primaquine. Congenital forms arise from cytochrome b5 reductase deficiency and hemoglobin M variants. A ferric heme raises the O2 affinity of the remaining ferrous hemes in the same tetramer, so the curve also shifts left. Blood is chocolate brown and stays brown on exposure to air, and cyanosis appears at about 1.5 g/dL of methemoglobin, compared with about 5 g/dL of deoxyhemoglobin for ordinary cyanosis."},{"type":"h","text":"Why the usual measurements mislead"},{"type":"p","text":"PaO2 is normal in both disorders because ventilation and the alveolar-capillary membrane are intact and PaO2 measures only dissolved O2. A blood gas analyzer that calculates saturation from PaO2 therefore reports a falsely normal SaO2. A standard pulse oximeter compares absorbance at about 660 nm (red) and 940 nm (infrared) and assumes only oxyhemoglobin and deoxyhemoglobin are present. Carboxyhemoglobin absorbs red light much like oxyhemoglobin, so it is counted as oxygenated and SpO2 reads falsely normal. Methemoglobin absorbs strongly at both wavelengths, pushing the ratio toward 1, which the device reads as about 85%, whatever the true saturation. Co-oximetry measures each hemoglobin species directly, and a gap between calculated or pulse oximeter saturation and the co-oximetry value points to an abnormal hemoglobin."},{"type":"table","title":"Carbon monoxide poisoning versus methemoglobinemia","cols":["Feature","Carbon monoxide","Methemoglobin"],"rows":[["Defect","CO occupies ferrous heme","Heme iron oxidized to ferric"],["Typical causes","Combustion, smoke inhalation","Nitrites, dapsone, benzocaine, primaquine; b5 reductase deficiency"],["Curve","Left shift","Left shift"],["PaO2","Normal","Normal"],["SpO2","Falsely normal","About 85% regardless of true value"],["Appearance","Cherry red (uncommon)","Cyanosis; chocolate-brown blood"],["Confirmed by","Co-oximetry carboxyhemoglobin","Co-oximetry methemoglobin"]],"caption":"Both lower true saturation and content with a normal PaO2; they differ in iron state and in how they fool the pulse oximeter."},{"type":"stop","predict":"In methemoglobinemia, why does breathing supplemental O2 raise PaO2 yet leave the cyanosis and SpO2 nearly unchanged?","explain":"PaO2 was already normal, so the ferrous hemes were already nearly saturated, and ferric hemes cannot bind O2 at any PO2. The extra O2 adds only a little dissolved O2, and the oximeter ratio is still dominated by methemoglobin.","check":"The methemoglobin paragraph and the pulse oximetry explanation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-08-01-C06","obj":"PU-8.6","title":"Arterial blood gases in respiratory acidosis and alkalosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"PaCO2 is proportional to CO2 production divided by alveolar ventilation, so a primary respiratory acid-base disorder is a disorder of ventilation. pH follows the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation, pH = 6.1 + log [HCO3- / (0.03 x PaCO2)]. Normal arterial values are pH 7.35 to 7.45, PaCO2 35 to 45 mmHg, HCO3- 22 to 26 mEq/L, and PaO2 about 80 to 100 mmHg on room air at sea level. Respiratory acidosis is a primary rise in PaCO2 (hypoventilation); respiratory alkalosis is a primary fall (hyperventilation)."},{"type":"p","text":"Compensation works on two time scales. Within minutes, non-bicarbonate buffers, chiefly hemoglobin and intracellular proteins, take up or release H+, changing HCO3- only slightly; pH falls about 0.08 per 10 mmHg acute rise in PaCO2. Renal compensation starts within hours and is complete in 3 to 5 days. With high PaCO2, tubular cells secrete more H+, increase ammoniagenesis and NH4+ excretion, and add new HCO3- to the blood while Cl- is excreted, so chronic respiratory acidosis has low plasma Cl-. With low PaCO2 they reabsorb less HCO3-. Compensation moves pH toward normal but rarely all the way, except sometimes in chronic respiratory alkalosis."},{"type":"table","title":"Expected HCO3- in respiratory disorders","cols":["Disorder","HCO3- change per 10 mmHg change in PaCO2","Example: PaCO2, HCO3-, pH"],"rows":[["Acute respiratory acidosis","Up about 1 mEq/L","60 mmHg, 26 mEq/L, about 7.26"],["Chronic respiratory acidosis","Up about 3 to 4 mEq/L","60 mmHg, 31 mEq/L, about 7.34"],["Acute respiratory alkalosis","Down about 2 mEq/L","30 mmHg, 22 mEq/L, about 7.49"],["Chronic respiratory alkalosis","Down about 4 to 5 mEq/L","30 mmHg, 19 to 20 mEq/L, about 7.44"]],"caption":"Start from HCO3- 24 mEq/L and PaCO2 40 mmHg. A measured HCO3- clearly outside the expected value means a second, metabolic disorder."},{"type":"flow","title":"Reading a blood gas with a respiratory problem","steps":["pH below 7.35 is acidemia; above 7.45 is alkalemia","Primary change is the value that explains the pH","If PaCO2 is primary, decide acute or chronic","Calculate expected HCO3- from a baseline of 24","Measured HCO3- outside expected: add a metabolic disorder","Use PaO2 and the A-a gradient to locate the cause"],"caption":"Judge compensation against a calculated value, not a normal pH."},{"type":"p","text":"Respiratory acidosis follows failure anywhere in the ventilatory chain: depressed respiratory centers (opioids, sedatives), neuromuscular weakness (Guillain-Barre syndrome, myasthenia gravis), chest wall restriction or obesity hypoventilation, airway obstruction, and advanced COPD. Pure hypoventilation lowers PaO2 with a normal A-a gradient. CO2 crosses the blood-brain barrier and dilates cerebral vessels, causing headache, raised intracranial pressure, drowsiness, and at high levels CO2 narcosis. Respiratory alkalosis follows increased drive: anxiety, hypoxemia (altitude, pulmonary embolism, pneumonia), pregnancy (progesterone), early salicylate toxicity, sepsis, and liver failure. Alkalemia increases Ca2+ binding to albumin, lowering ionized Ca2+ and causing perioral numbness, paresthesias, and carpopedal spasm, while hypocapnic cerebral vasoconstriction causes lightheadedness."},{"type":"p","text":"Mixed disorders appear when HCO3- departs from the expected value. A normal pH with clearly abnormal PaCO2 and HCO3- moving in opposite directions, as in salicylate toxicity (respiratory alkalosis plus anion gap metabolic acidosis), indicates two disorders. When metabolic acidosis is primary, expected PaCO2 is 1.5 x HCO3- + 8 (plus or minus 2), the Winter formula; a higher PaCO2 means a coexisting respiratory acidosis."},{"type":"example","title":"Is the bicarbonate appropriate?","text":"A patient with long-standing stable COPD has pH 7.21, PaCO2 70 mmHg, and HCO3- 27 mEq/L. The pH is acidemic and PaCO2 is high, so respiratory acidosis is primary. PaCO2 is 30 mmHg above 40. If chronic, expected HCO3- is 24 + (3 x 3 to 4), about 33 to 36 mEq/L, and pH would be about 7.32. The measured 27 mEq/L is far too low for a known chronic state, so a metabolic acidosis, such as lactic acidosis, coexists. Had the hypercapnia been new, 27 would fit acute compensation (24 + 3). Conversely, PaCO2 60 mmHg with HCO3- 38 mEq/L exceeds even chronic compensation (about 30 to 32) and indicates an added metabolic alkalosis."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Biochemistry and hematology explain carrying capacity and affinity. Globin chain composition sets 2,3-BPG binding and fetal O2 transfer, the iron oxidation state decides whether heme can bind O2 at all, and red cell glycolytic enzymes and NADH-cytochrome b5 reductase link enzyme defects to curve shifts and methemoglobinemia. Anemia, polycythemia, and abnormal hemoglobins all alter content or unloading without touching the lung, which is why PaO2 can be normal in a hypoxic patient. Mitochondrial biochemistry completes the picture: CO and cyanide block cytochrome c oxidase, and lactic acidosis signals failed O2 use whatever its cause."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology and pathology join at the blood gas. Alveolar ventilation sets PaCO2, red cell carbonic anhydrase and band 3 convert CO2 into a transportable buffer, and the kidney supplies slow compensation, so a single ABG reflects lungs, blood, and kidney together. The expected compensation values are empirical averages with real scatter, the shares of CO2 transport and the P50 vary slightly by source, and pulse oximetry is reliable only when hemoglobin is normal, so each number is interpreted with the history and, when needed, co-oximetry and the anion gap."}],"summary":["CaO2 = (1.34 x Hb x SaO2) + (0.003 x PaO2), about 20 mL/dL normally; nearly all O2 is bound to hemoglobin.","PaO2 reflects gas exchange, SaO2 reflects how full hemoglobin is, and content reflects both plus the amount of hemoglobin; anemia lowers only content.","Cooperative T to R transition makes the curve sigmoid, with a protective plateau above PaO2 60 mmHg and a steep segment at tissue PO2; P50 is about 26 to 27 mmHg.","H+, CO2, temperature, and 2,3-BPG shift the curve right; fetal hemoglobin, CO, methemoglobin, alkalosis, and cold shift it left.","About 70% of CO2 is carried as bicarbonate made by red cell carbonic anhydrase and exported through band 3 in exchange for Cl- (chloride shift); the rest is carbamino and dissolved.","Bohr: CO2 and H+ lower O2 affinity and unload O2 in tissues. Haldane: oxygenation releases H+ and CO2 in the lungs, and deoxygenation increases CO2 uptake in tissues.","CO and methemoglobin lower true saturation and content and shift the curve left while PaO2 stays normal; pulse oximetry reads falsely normal in CO and about 85% in methemoglobinemia; co-oximetry measures both.","In respiratory acidosis HCO3- rises about 1 mEq/L acutely and 3 to 4 mEq/L chronically per 10 mmHg PaCO2; in respiratory alkalosis it falls about 2 acutely and 4 to 5 chronically.","A measured HCO3- outside the expected range, or a normal pH with clearly abnormal PaCO2 and HCO3-, signals a mixed disorder."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Transport of Oxygen and Carbon Dioxide in Blood and Tissue Fluids"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Acid-Base Regulation"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Transport of oxygen and carbon dioxide in the blood"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Globular Proteins"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Pulmonary Disease"}]},"PU-9":{"unit":"PU-9","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-09","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-09-01","title":"Pulmonary Biochemistry and Nutrition","subject":"Respiratory System (RESP)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"The lung stays open, elastic, clean, and intact because of a handful of molecules: surfactant, elastin and collagen, mucins, alpha-1 antitrypsin, and the antioxidant enzymes, supported by vitamin A, the fat-soluble vitamins, and selenium. This chapter explains how each molecule is built and what it does, then follows each failure to a lung disease: neonatal respiratory distress, emphysema, fibrosis, oxygen toxicity, chronic bronchitis, and cystic fibrosis. Board questions usually describe a patient or an infant and ask for the molecule, enzyme, cofactor, or hormone responsible. If you can explain why a molecular defect changes compliance, recoil, or mucus clearance, you can reason through most of them.","prerequisites":["Alveolar histology: type I and type II pneumocytes, alveolar macrophages, and the airway epithelium (PU-2)","Lung compliance, elastic recoil, and the pressure-volume curve (PU-6)","Phospholipid structure and the CDP-choline pathway of phosphatidylcholine synthesis","Collagen synthesis and post-translational modification of proteins in the ER and Golgi","Fat digestion, micelle formation, and chylomicron transport"],"connections":[{"to":"Respiratory embryology (PU-1)","how":"Surfactant production depends on the saccular and alveolar stages of lung development, which sets the gestational age at which a newborn can breathe without collapse."},{"to":"Pulmonary genetics (PU-10)","how":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency and cystic fibrosis are the inherited versions of the protease and mucus failures taught here."},{"to":"Obstructive pulmonary diseases (PU-14-B)","how":"Emphysema and chronic bronchitis are the structural results of protease excess, elastin loss, and mucous gland hyperplasia."},{"to":"Restrictive pulmonary diseases (PU-14-A)","how":"Collagen deposition and loss of surfactant both lower compliance, which is the mechanical basis of restriction."},{"to":"Energy production and the respiratory system (PU-11)","how":"Mitochondrial electron leak and the pentose phosphate pathway supply, respectively, the superoxide and the NADPH that drive oxidant injury and its repair."}],"terms":[{"term":"Surfactant","def":"A lipid and protein film secreted by type II pneumocytes that lowers alveolar surface tension, most strongly as the alveolus shrinks."},{"term":"Dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine (DPPC)","def":"A phosphatidylcholine (lecithin) with two saturated palmitate chains, the main surface-active lipid of surfactant."},{"term":"Lecithin to sphingomyelin (L/S) ratio","def":"The amniotic fluid ratio of phosphatidylcholine to sphingomyelin, which rises as fetal surfactant production increases."},{"term":"Serpin","def":"A serine protease inhibitor that traps its target protease in a covalent complex after the protease cleaves the inhibitor's reactive loop."},{"term":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin (AAT)","def":"A serpin made mainly by hepatocytes that is the main inhibitor of neutrophil elastase in the lower respiratory tract."},{"term":"Lysyl oxidase","def":"A copper-dependent extracellular enzyme that converts lysine side chains to aldehydes, starting the cross-links of elastin and collagen."},{"term":"Desmosine","def":"A cross-link formed from four lysine side chains that is found only in elastin and lets elastin fibers stretch and recoil."},{"term":"Compliance","def":"The change in lung volume per unit change in transpulmonary pressure; the reciprocal of elastance."},{"term":"Squamous metaplasia","def":"Reversible replacement of ciliated columnar epithelium by stratified squamous epithelium in response to chronic injury or vitamin A deficiency."},{"term":"Reactive oxygen species (ROS)","def":"Partially reduced oxygen products, including superoxide, hydrogen peroxide, and the hydroxyl radical, that oxidize lipids, proteins, and DNA."},{"term":"Mucin","def":"A very large glycoprotein whose dense O-linked sugar chains bind water and form the mucus gel."},{"term":"Reid index","def":"The ratio of submucosal gland thickness to bronchial wall thickness between epithelium and cartilage; above 0.4 indicates the gland hyperplasia of chronic bronchitis."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-09-01-C01","obj":"PU-9.1","title":"Surfactant composition, function, and hormonal control of its synthesis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A thin layer of water lines every alveolus, and surface tension at that air-water interface pulls the alveolus toward collapse. By the law of Laplace, collapsing pressure equals 2T/r (T is surface tension, r is radius). With equal T everywhere, small alveoli would empty into large ones and collapse at end expiration, and surface tension would also draw interstitial fluid into the airspace. Surfactant prevents both."},{"type":"p","text":"Surfactant is about 90% lipid and 10% protein. Type II pneumocytes synthesize it, store it in lamellar bodies, and secrete it by exocytosis; it unfolds into tubular myelin and spreads as a film. Type II cells recycle most of it and alveolar macrophages degrade the rest, so defective macrophage clearance (pulmonary alveolar proteinosis) fills alveoli with surfactant."},{"type":"table","title":"Components of surfactant","cols":["Component","Approximate share","Role"],"rows":[["Dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine (DPPC)","About 40% to 50% of surfactant by weight","Main surface-active lipid; packs tightly when compressed"],["Other phosphatidylcholines","Most of the remaining phosphatidylcholine","Keep the film fluid enough to spread"],["Phosphatidylglycerol (PG)","About 5% to 10% of the lipid; appears late in gestation","Helps film spreading; its presence marks a mature lung"],["Cholesterol and neutral lipids","Small fraction","Modulate film fluidity"],["SP-B and SP-C (hydrophobic)","Small fraction","Insert lipid into the interface and stabilize the film; SP-B deficiency is lethal at birth"],["SP-A and SP-D (hydrophilic collectins)","Small fraction","Bind microbial carbohydrates and act as opsonins; SP-A also aids tubular myelin formation"]],"caption":"Lipids lower surface tension; the hydrophobic proteins spread the film; the collectins add innate immune defense."},{"type":"p","text":"DPPC's two saturated palmitate chains are straight and pack tightly. As the alveolus shrinks in expiration, the film is compressed, DPPC crowds together, and surface tension falls from about 70 mN/m (water) toward near zero. Because the fall is greatest where surface area is smallest, T drops with r, the 2T/r pressures equalize, and alveoli of different sizes stay open together. Phosphatidylcholine is made by the CDP-choline pathway (CTP:phosphocholine cytidylyltransferase is rate limiting), and an unsaturated chain is then exchanged for palmitate to make DPPC."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: why small alveoli need surfactant most","text":"Two connected alveoli have radii of 50 and 100 micrometers. With equal T, collapsing pressure is 2T/50 in the small one and 2T/100 in the large one, so the small alveolus has twice the pressure and empties into the large one. With surfactant, the small alveolus is compressed more and its T falls further; if T falls to half that of the large one, pressures become 2(T/2)/50 and 2T/100, which are equal, and both stay open. Without surfactant, as in a preterm infant, the result is diffuse atelectasis, stiff lungs, and shunt."},{"type":"p","text":"Type II pneumocytes appear around 20 to 24 weeks of gestation, but enough surfactant to prevent collapse is usually present only after about 34 to 35 weeks; an amniotic L/S ratio above 2.0 and the presence of PG indicate maturity. Fetal cortisol is the main driver: through the glucocorticoid receptor it induces type II cell differentiation, fatty acid synthase and cytidylyltransferase activity for DPPC synthesis, and the SP-A, SP-B, and SP-C genes, which is why fetal stress or exogenous glucocorticoids accelerate maturity. Thyroid hormone acts synergistically, mainly by increasing phosphatidylcholine synthesis."},{"type":"flow","title":"Maternal diabetes and delayed surfactant","steps":["Maternal hyperglycemia; glucose crosses the placenta","Fetal beta cells secrete excess insulin","Insulin antagonizes cortisol-driven type II cell maturation","Surfactant protein and DPPC synthesis lag; PG appears late","Respiratory distress at a gestational age usually safe"],"caption":"Insulin does not cross the placenta; the fetus makes it in response to maternal glucose."},{"type":"p","text":"Fetal hyperinsulinemia also drives macrosomia, so the infant of a diabetic mother may be large yet surfactant deficient. Deficiency of any cause produces neonatal respiratory distress syndrome: atelectasis lowers compliance and creates shunt, hypoxemia injures the alveolar capillary barrier, and leaked plasma proteins form hyaline membranes that further inactivate surfactant."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-09-01-C02","obj":"PU-9.2","title":"Protease and antiprotease balance and how smoking tips it","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Alveolar walls are built on elastin, and neutrophils that enter the lung carry neutrophil elastase, a serine protease that can digest it. Normal lungs survive this exposure because an excess of antiproteases surrounds every neutrophil. Alpha-1 antitrypsin (AAT) is the most important: a roughly 52 kDa glycoprotein made mainly by hepatocytes, it diffuses from plasma into the interstitium and epithelial lining fluid and supplies most of the antielastase protection of the alveoli. Secretory leukocyte protease inhibitor guards the larger airways, and tissue inhibitors of metalloproteinases (TIMPs) restrain matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs)."},{"type":"p","text":"AAT is a serpin and works as a suicide substrate. Elastase attacks a peptide bond at methionine 358 in AAT's exposed reactive center loop. Once cut, the loop snaps into the body of AAT and carries the still-attached elastase to the opposite pole, distorting its active site so the acyl-enzyme intermediate cannot be hydrolyzed. Both molecules are consumed in a stable complex that is cleared by the liver. Inhibition is therefore stoichiometric, one AAT for one elastase, and depends on enough AAT reaching the tissue."},{"type":"p","text":"Methionine is the weak point. Oxidants convert the methionine 358 side chain to methionine sulfoxide, which elastase no longer recognizes, and AAT's rate of association with elastase falls by orders of magnitude. AAT is present but functionally inactive."},{"type":"flow","title":"Cigarette smoke tips the balance toward destruction","steps":["Smoke activates alveolar macrophages and epithelium","Chemokines such as IL-8 and leukotriene B4 recruit neutrophils","Neutrophils release elastase; macrophages release MMPs such as MMP-12","Smoke and respiratory burst oxidants oxidize AAT methionine 358","Unopposed proteases digest alveolar wall elastin","Airspaces merge, recoil falls, and small airways collapse"],"caption":"Smoking acts on both sides at once: more protease and less functional antiprotease."},{"type":"p","text":"The resulting emphysema in smokers is centriacinar and worst in the upper lobes, where smoke particles deposit and respiratory bronchioles are destroyed first. In inherited AAT deficiency (most often the PiZZ genotype, PU-10), the misfolded Z protein polymerizes in hepatocyte endoplasmic reticulum, plasma AAT falls to about 10% to 15% of normal, and the whole acinus is unprotected. The result is panacinar emphysema, worst in the lower lobes, possibly because they receive more blood flow and therefore more neutrophils, appearing in early middle age and much earlier in smokers. Retained Z polymers form PAS-positive, diastase-resistant globules in hepatocytes and can cause cirrhosis, so a patient with both emphysema and liver disease suggests AAT deficiency."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A nonsmoker with PiZZ AAT deficiency and a smoker with normal AAT levels both develop emphysema. Which has the lower plasma AAT, and which has more functionally inactive AAT in the alveolus?","explain":"The PiZZ patient has low plasma AAT because it is retained in the liver. The smoker has normal plasma levels but oxidized, inactive AAT locally, plus more recruited neutrophils.","check":"Compare with the paragraphs on methionine oxidation and on PiZZ panacinar emphysema."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-09-01-C03","obj":"PU-9.3","title":"Elastin, collagen, and opposite changes in compliance","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Elastic recoil, the tendency of the stretched lung to return to its resting volume, has two sources: surface tension at the alveolar lining, which accounts for roughly half to two-thirds of recoil, and the elastic fiber network of the alveolar walls and airways. Collagen in the same walls provides tensile strength and limits overdistension."},{"type":"p","text":"Fibroblasts secrete elastin as soluble tropoelastin, a protein rich in glycine, alanine, proline, and valine, with little hydroxyproline, no hydroxylysine, and no glycine-X-Y repeats, which distinguishes it from collagen. Hydrophobic domains alternate with short lysine-rich domains, and tropoelastin is deposited on a fibrillin microfibril scaffold. Lysyl oxidase, a copper-dependent extracellular enzyme, oxidatively deaminates selected lysine side chains to aldehydes (allysine). Three allysines and one unmodified lysine then condense spontaneously into desmosine or isodesmosine, cross-links unique to elastin that join four chains into a rubber-like network. Lysyl oxidase also starts the cross-links that give collagen its strength."},{"type":"p","text":"Elastin recoils because of entropy: stretching forces its hydrophobic domains into ordered extended chains, and on release they return to a disordered state, pulling the fiber back. Cross-links prevent the chains from slipping apart. Elastin is synthesized mainly during development and has a half-life measured in decades, so fibers destroyed in adulthood are poorly replaced. Inadequate copper (as in Menkes disease, a defect of copper transport) or lysyl oxidase inhibition (lathyrism) weakens both elastin and collagen."},{"type":"table","title":"Emphysema versus pulmonary fibrosis","cols":["Feature","Emphysema (elastin loss)","Fibrosis (collagen excess)"],"rows":[["Molecular change","Protease digestion of elastin","Myofibroblasts deposit collagen types I and III, driven by TGF-beta"],["Compliance","Increased (floppy lung)","Decreased (stiff lung)"],["Elastic recoil","Decreased","Increased"],["Pressure-volume curve","Shifted up and left, steeper","Shifted down and right, flatter"],["Lung volumes","TLC, FRC, and RV increased","TLC, FRC, and RV decreased"],["FEV1/FVC","Decreased (obstructive)","Normal or increased (restrictive)"],["DLCO","Decreased (lost surface area)","Decreased (thick membrane, lost capillaries)"]],"caption":"Opposite changes in compliance and recoil, but both diseases lower DLCO."},{"type":"p","text":"In emphysema, the lung expands easily but recoils weakly. Recoil is the force that drives expiratory flow, and the elastic attachments of alveolar walls normally hold small airways open by radial traction. When both are lost, small airways collapse during forced expiration, air is trapped, and the chest becomes hyperinflated. In fibrosis, the stiff lung pulls inward strongly, resting volumes shrink, and each breath needs a larger pressure change, so patients take rapid shallow breaths. High recoil also holds airways wide open, so flow is normal or high for the small lung volume."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: calculating compliance","text":"Compliance is the change in volume divided by the change in transpulmonary pressure. In a healthy adult, a 500 mL breath needs a pressure change of about 2.5 cm H2O, so compliance is 500 / 2.5 = 200 mL/cm H2O. In emphysema the same 500 mL needs only about 1.5 cm H2O: 500 / 1.5 = about 330 mL/cm H2O, high compliance with little stored recoil to drive exhalation. In fibrosis it needs about 7 cm H2O: 500 / 7 = about 70 mL/cm H2O, low compliance and more work per breath. The numbers are illustrative."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-09-01-C04","obj":"PU-9.4","title":"Vitamin A and airway epithelium; fat-soluble vitamin loss in cystic fibrosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The conducting airways are lined by pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells, the working surface of the mucociliary escalator. Keeping that lining differentiated requires retinoic acid, the active acid form of vitamin A. Retinoic acid enters the nucleus and binds retinoic acid receptors (RARs), which pair with retinoid X receptors (RXRs) on DNA response elements and switch on genes for ciliated and mucus-secreting differentiation while suppressing genes for keratinizing squamous cells."},{"type":"flow","title":"Vitamin A deficiency and respiratory infection","steps":["Low retinol intake or absorption","Too little retinoic acid for RAR and RXR signaling","Basal cells differentiate into keratinizing squamous cells","Cilia and goblet cells are lost","Mucociliary clearance fails and mucosal immunity weakens","More frequent and more severe respiratory infections"],"caption":"The same switch to keratinizing epithelium in the conjunctiva produces xerophthalmia and Bitot spots."},{"type":"p","text":"Squamous metaplasia trades function for toughness: the new epithelium resists abrasion but cannot move mucus, so bacteria and debris stay in the airways. Vitamin A deficiency also impairs mucosal IgA responses and other immune functions, which is why it raises the severity and mortality of measles and lower respiratory infections in children. The metaplasia is reversible when retinoic acid is restored. Note that cigarette smoke causes a similar squamous metaplasia by chronic injury, not by vitamin deficiency."},{"type":"p","text":"Vitamins A, D, E, and K are absorbed only with dietary fat. Pancreatic lipase and colipase digest triglycerides, bile salts gather the products and the vitamins into micelles, enterocytes absorb them, and they leave in chylomicrons through the lymphatics. Anything that blocks fat digestion therefore blocks all four vitamins at once."},{"type":"p","text":"In cystic fibrosis, defective CFTR in pancreatic duct cells cannot secrete chloride and bicarbonate, so ductal secretions become thick, protein-rich, and acidic. They plug the ducts, the acini are destroyed and replaced by fibrosis and fat, and most patients (about 85% to 90%) have exocrine pancreatic insufficiency from infancy. The enzymes that do reach the duodenum work poorly, because without bicarbonate the duodenal pH stays low, which inactivates lipase and precipitates bile acids. The result is steatorrhea and malabsorption of fat-soluble vitamins, sometimes compounded by CF liver disease."},{"type":"table","title":"Fat-soluble vitamin deficiencies and their mechanisms","cols":["Vitamin","Key function","Deficiency findings"],"rows":[["A (retinol)","11-cis retinal in rhodopsin; retinoic acid for epithelial differentiation","Night blindness, xerophthalmia, squamous metaplasia, infections"],["D (calciferol)","Calcitriol raises intestinal calcium and phosphate absorption","Rickets in children, osteomalacia in adults, low bone density"],["E (tocopherol)","Chain-breaking antioxidant in membranes","Hemolytic anemia, spinocerebellar ataxia, peripheral neuropathy"],["K (phylloquinone)","Cofactor for gamma-carboxylation of factors II, VII, IX, X and proteins C and S","Bleeding, prolonged PT"]],"caption":"All four fall together whenever fat digestion or absorption fails."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-09-01-C05","obj":"PU-9.5","title":"Oxidant lung injury from hyperoxia and smoke, and the antioxidant enzymes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Complete reduction of O2 to water takes four electrons, delivered together by cytochrome c oxidase. When O2 accepts electrons one at a time, it forms reactive oxygen species (ROS): one electron gives superoxide, two give hydrogen peroxide, and three give the hydroxyl radical, usually produced when hydrogen peroxide reacts with ferrous iron (the Fenton reaction). The hydroxyl radical is the most damaging and no enzyme removes it, so cells protect themselves by removing its precursors and keeping iron bound."},{"type":"p","text":"The lung faces more oxygen than any other tissue. Normally, a small fraction of electrons leak from mitochondrial complexes I and III to form superoxide, and phagocyte NADPH oxidase generates ROS on purpose to kill microbes. Breathing a high inspired O2 fraction (FiO2) raises tissue PO2, and mitochondrial superoxide production rises with it. Cigarette smoke delivers very large numbers of free radicals in its gas phase, and quinones in its tar phase cycle continuously to make superoxide and hydrogen peroxide; smoke also recruits neutrophils whose respiratory burst adds more."},{"type":"p","text":"ROS attack polyunsaturated fatty acids in membranes, starting chain reactions of lipid peroxidation; they oxidize proteins (including methionine 358 of AAT, C02) and damage DNA. The most vulnerable cells are the thin type I pneumocytes and the capillary endothelium. Their injury opens the alveolar capillary barrier, protein-rich fluid floods the alveoli, hyaline membranes form, and surfactant is inactivated: diffuse alveolar damage. If exposure continues, type II cells proliferate and fibroblasts lay down collagen. Prolonged high FiO2 can cause this pattern in adults, and in preterm infants, whose antioxidant enzymes mature late in gestation along with surfactant, oxygen injury contributes to bronchopulmonary dysplasia. High FiO2 also causes absorption atelectasis, a separate mechanism in which O2 replacing nitrogen is absorbed from poorly ventilated alveoli until they collapse."},{"type":"table","title":"Antioxidant defenses of the lung","cols":["Defense","Reaction or action","Cofactor or source"],"rows":[["Superoxide dismutase (SOD)","2 superoxide + 2 H+ to hydrogen peroxide + O2","Copper and zinc (cytosolic SOD1, extracellular SOD3); manganese (mitochondrial SOD2)"],["Catalase","2 hydrogen peroxide to 2 H2O + O2","Heme iron; located mainly in peroxisomes"],["Glutathione peroxidase","Hydrogen peroxide or lipid peroxides + 2 GSH to water or alcohols + GSSG","Selenium as selenocysteine in the active site"],["Glutathione reductase","GSSG + NADPH to 2 GSH","FAD (riboflavin); NADPH from glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase"],["Vitamin E","Breaks lipid peroxidation chains in membranes","Diet; regenerated by vitamin C"],["Vitamin C and glutathione","Water-soluble scavengers in epithelial lining fluid","Diet; glutathione made by cells"]],"caption":"GSH is reduced glutathione; GSSG is its oxidized disulfide form."},{"type":"flow","title":"Removing superoxide and hydrogen peroxide","steps":["O2 gains one electron, forming superoxide","SOD converts superoxide to hydrogen peroxide","Catalase or glutathione peroxidase converts hydrogen peroxide to water","Glutathione peroxidase oxidizes GSH to GSSG","Glutathione reductase regenerates GSH using NADPH"],"caption":"Removing hydrogen peroxide quickly also denies iron the substrate for hydroxyl radical formation."},{"type":"p","text":"Selenium deficiency lowers glutathione peroxidase activity, and copper or zinc deficiency lowers SOD1 and SOD3. NADPH supply ties the system to the pentose phosphate pathway, so G6PD deficiency limits glutathione regeneration. Catalase and glutathione peroxidase both remove hydrogen peroxide, but only glutathione peroxidase needs selenium and only it reduces lipid peroxides in membranes."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with selenium deficiency is exposed to a high FiO2. Which reaction slows, and which product accumulates?","explain":"Glutathione peroxidase activity falls, so hydrogen peroxide and lipid peroxides are cleared more slowly, more hydroxyl radical forms through Fenton chemistry, and lipid peroxidation spreads. Catalase still works but cannot reduce lipid peroxides.","check":"Compare with the antioxidant table and the flow of peroxide removal."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-RESP-09-01-C06","obj":"PU-9.6","title":"Mucin structure, mucus clearance, chronic bronchitis, and cystic fibrosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Airway mucus is a gel that traps inhaled particles and microbes so cilia can carry them up to the pharynx. Its gel-forming molecules are mucins, very large glycoproteins of which up to about 80% of the mass is carbohydrate. The main airway mucins are MUC5AC, made mainly by goblet cells of the surface epithelium, and MUC5B, made mainly by mucous cells of the submucosal glands."},{"type":"p","text":"The mucin protein core contains long tandem repeat domains rich in serine, threonine, and proline. In the Golgi apparatus, N-acetylgalactosamine is attached to the hydroxyl groups of serine and threonine (O-linked glycosylation, in contrast to the N-linked glycosylation on asparagine that starts in the ER), and hundreds of branching sugar chains are built outward, many ending in negatively charged sialic acid or sulfate. The result is a stiff bottlebrush that binds large amounts of water. Cysteine-rich ends join mucins end to end by disulfide bonds into long polymers. Mucins are stored in secretory granules in a compact form in which calcium shields their negative charges; at release, calcium is exchanged and the molecules expand many hundredfold into gel."},{"type":"p","text":"Clearance depends on two layers of airway surface liquid. A watery periciliary layer, about the height of an extended cilium, lets cilia beat freely, and the mucus gel rides on top. Hydration of both is controlled by ion transport across the epithelium: CFTR secretes chloride and bicarbonate, water follows, and the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC) absorbs sodium, which pulls water back. Too much mucus or too little water stops the escalator."},{"type":"table","title":"Two ways mucus clearance fails","cols":["Feature","Chronic bronchitis","Cystic fibrosis"],"rows":[["Primary problem","Too much mucus","Too little water in the mucus and periciliary layer"],["Cause","Chronic irritation, mainly smoking","Loss-of-function CFTR mutations (PU-10)"],["Structural change","Submucosal gland hyperplasia (Reid index above 0.4); goblet cell increase in small airways","Dehydrated, adherent mucus plugs; later bronchiectasis"],["Ciliary function","Cilia damaged by smoke; ciliated cells reduced","Cilia normal but flattened by a collapsed periciliary layer"],["Infection pattern","Recurrent bacterial exacerbations","Staphylococcus aureus early; Pseudomonas aeruginosa later"],["Clinical definition or clue","Productive cough at least 3 months in each of 2 consecutive years","Onset in childhood with pancreatic insufficiency"]],"caption":"Chronic bronchitis is a clinical diagnosis; the Reid index is its pathologic correlate."},{"type":"p","text":"In chronic bronchitis, smoke and inflammatory mediators induce mucin gene expression and gland enlargement, and neutrophil elastase (C02) is itself a strong stimulus for mucus secretion. Excess mucus plugs small airways, causing ventilation-perfusion mismatch, hypoxemia, and often CO2 retention."},{"type":"flow","title":"CFTR loss and mucus plugging","steps":["Defective CFTR: chloride and bicarbonate secretion lost","Sodium absorption through ENaC continues unopposed","Airway surface liquid shrinks; periciliary layer collapses","Low bicarbonate leaves mucins poorly expanded and sticky","Mucus adheres to the epithelium; cilia cannot move it","Bacteria colonize; neutrophil DNA adds viscosity"],"caption":"Infection and inflammation then cause the airway wall destruction of bronchiectasis."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"One cell type and one exposure tie this chapter together. The type II pneumocyte makes surfactant, renews type I cells after oxidant injury, and its maturity depends on endocrine signals, linking embryology and endocrinology to neonatal lung disease. Cigarette smoke links nearly every other section: it recruits neutrophils whose elastase digests elastin and stimulates mucus secretion, it oxidizes AAT, it overwhelms antioxidant enzymes, and it causes squamous metaplasia. The two main inherited disorders, AAT deficiency and cystic fibrosis, show the same failures without smoke, and cystic fibrosis also connects the lung to the pancreas, the gut, and vitamin status."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology follows mechanics. Loss of elastin raises compliance and causes obstruction; excess collagen or loss of surfactant lowers compliance and causes restriction; failed clearance causes infection and bronchiectasis. The limits are worth stating: emphysema and chronic bronchitis usually coexist in smokers, and the hormonal control of surfactant is better established for cortisol than for thyroid hormone."}],"summary":["Surfactant is mostly lipid; DPPC packs tightly when compressed, so surface tension falls most in small alveoli and the 2T/r pressures equalize.","Fetal cortisol, with thyroid hormone, drives type II cell maturation and surfactant synthesis; fetal hyperinsulinemia in maternal diabetes antagonizes it.","An L/S ratio above 2.0 and the presence of phosphatidylglycerol in amniotic fluid indicate fetal lung maturity.","AAT is a hepatic serpin that traps neutrophil elastase one to one; oxidation of its methionine 358 inactivates it.","Smoking adds protease and removes functional antiprotease, giving centriacinar upper lobe emphysema; PiZZ AAT deficiency gives panacinar lower lobe emphysema and liver disease.","Copper-dependent lysyl oxidase starts desmosine cross-links in elastin; elastin loss raises compliance and lowers recoil, and collagen deposition does the opposite.","Retinoic acid maintains ciliated and goblet cell epithelium through RAR and RXR; deficiency causes squamous metaplasia and more respiratory infections.","CF pancreatic insufficiency blocks fat digestion and causes deficiency of vitamins A, D, E, and K.","SOD (copper, zinc, manganese), catalase (heme), and glutathione peroxidase (selenium) remove ROS, with NADPH regenerating glutathione.","Mucins are heavily O-glycosylated on serine and threonine; chronic bronchitis makes too much mucus and cystic fibrosis makes mucus too dry to clear."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pulmonary Ventilation, section on surfactant, surface tension, and lung compliance"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Mechanics of ventilation and airway surface liquid"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Fibrous Proteins; Phospholipid, Glycosphingolipid, and Eicosanoid Metabolism; Glycosaminoglycans, Proteoglycans, and Glycoproteins"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Vitamins; pentose phosphate pathway and NADPH"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lung (obstructive lung diseases, acute lung injury); Diseases of Infancy and Childhood (neonatal respiratory distress syndrome)"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Respiratory System, maturation of the lungs"}]}});
